const e=[{title:"-(으)ㄹ걸요 — A Soft but Confident Guess",func:`Use -(으)ㄹ걸요 when you make a guess you're fairly sure about, but want to sound gentle instead of flat-out telling someone. It feels like "I'd say..." or "probably..." based on what you know.`,formTable:[{when:"Verb/adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ걸요",ex:"가다 → 갈걸요; 비싸다 → 비쌀걸요"},{when:"Verb/adjective stem ends in a consonant",add:"-을걸요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을걸요; 작다 → 작을걸요"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"drop ㄹ, then add -ㄹ걸요",ex:"살다 → 살걸요; 멀다 → 멀걸요"},{when:"Past (any stem)",add:"-았/었을걸요",ex:"가다 → 갔을걸요; 먹다 → 먹었을걸요"},{when:"Noun (guess about a fact)",add:"N일걸요",ex:"학생 → 학생일걸요"}],examples:[{ko:"친구는 지금 집에 있을걸요.",romanization:"chinguneun jigeum jibe isseulgeolyo.",en:"My friend is probably home right now.",note:"A guess you're fairly sure of, softened so it doesn't sound like a flat claim."},{ko:"그 영화 재미있을걸요.",romanization:"geu yeonghwa jaemiisseulgeolyo.",en:"That movie's probably good.",note:"있다 ends in a consonant, so it takes -을걸요."},{ko:"이 책은 좀 비쌀걸요.",romanization:"i chaegeun jom bissalgeolyo.",en:"This book's probably a bit pricey.",note:"비싸- ends in a vowel, so it takes -ㄹ걸요."},{ko:"부모님은 벌써 주무실걸요.",romanization:"bumonimeun beolsseo jumusilgeolyo.",en:"My parents are probably asleep already.",note:"Uses the honorific 주무시다 (to sleep) for parents, while still keeping the guessing feel."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ걸요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요",body:`Both say something is likely. -(으)ㄹ 거예요 sounds like a plain prediction; -(으)ㄹ걸요 adds a softer "I'd guess" feeling, often when the other person might be thinking otherwise. Save the finer back-vowel rules for later — just match vowel vs. consonant stems for now.`},pronunciation:"걸요 sounds like [거료], so 갈걸요 comes out [갈꺼료] — the ㄱ tenses and the ㄹ links smoothly into 요.",drill:{instruction:"Answer each question with a soft guess using -(으)ㄹ걸요.",model:"친구 지금 뭐 해요? → 아마 자고 있을걸요.",items:["그 식당 지금 문 열었어요? → 아마 ___.","이 옷 비싸요? → 글쎄요, ___.","선생님 지금 학교에 계세요? → 아마 ___."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"내일 비가 올 거 같을걸요.",right:"내일 비가 올걸요.",explanation:`English speakers stack "probably" on top of another guess word. -(으)ㄹ걸요 already means "probably," so don't combine it with -(으)ㄹ 것 같다. Attach it straight to the plain stem: 올걸요.`}},{title:"-(으)면 좋겠어요 — A Hope or Gentle Wish",func:`Use -(으)면 좋겠어요 to say "I hope..." or "I wish...". It literally means "it'd be nice if...," and it's the everyday way to say what you'd like to happen.`,formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-면 좋겠어요",ex:"오다 → 오면 좋겠어요; 싸다 → 싸면 좋겠어요"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으면 좋겠어요",ex:"먹다 → 먹으면 좋겠어요; 많다 → 많으면 좋겠어요"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-면 좋겠어요 (keep ㄹ)",ex:"살다 → 살면 좋겠어요"},{when:"Wish about now / something not true",add:"-았/었으면 좋겠어요",ex:"오다 → 왔으면 좋겠어요; 있다 → 있었으면 좋겠어요"},{when:"Noun",add:"N(이)면 좋겠어요",ex:"주말 → 주말이면 좋겠어요"}],examples:[{ko:"내일 날씨가 좋으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"naeil nalssiga joeumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I hope the weather's nice tomorrow.",note:"A simple hope about the future; 좋다 ends in a consonant, so -으면."},{ko:"시간이 좀 더 있으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"sigani jom deo isseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I wish I had a little more time.",note:"Everyday wish; 있다 takes -으면 좋겠어요."},{ko:"이번 시험이 쉬웠으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"ibeon siheomi swiwosseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I hope this test is easy.",note:"The -었으면 form here makes the hope feel stronger, even though the test hasn't happened yet."},{ko:"우리 집이 학교에서 가까웠으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"uri jibi hakgyoeseo gakkawosseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I wish our place were closer to school.",note:"A wish about how things are now; 가깝다 is ㅂ-irregular, so 가까웠으면."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 좋겠어요 vs. -고 싶어요",body:`-고 싶어요 means "I want to (do it myself)." -(으)면 좋겠어요 means "I hope (it happens)," so it can be about other people, the weather, or things you don't control. The contrary-to-fact nuance of the -았/었으면 form gets fuller treatment in a later chapter.`},pronunciation:"좋겠어요 sounds like [조케써요]: ㅎ + ㄱ blend into an aspirated [ㅋ], and 겠 tenses the next ㅅ into [써].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each situation into a wish using -(으)면 좋겠어요.",model:"비가 와요. (싫어요) → 비가 안 오면 좋겠어요.",items:["돈이 없어요. → 돈이 좀 ___.","주말이 짧아요. → 주말이 ___.","친구가 한국에 안 와요. → 친구가 ___."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"한국에 가고 싶으면 좋겠어요.",right:"한국에 가면 좋겠어요.",explanation:`Don't slip 싶다 in. "I hope I go to Korea" is just 가면 좋겠어요. Adding 싶다 doubles the wanting and sounds off. Keep 가고 싶어요 for a plain "I want to go."`}},{title:"-겠- — Intention, a Good Guess, or a Set Polite Phrase",func:`-겠- does three everyday jobs: stating your own plan ("I'll..."), making a guess about a situation ("that must be..."), and forming a few set polite phrases. You read which one it is from the context and the subject.`,formTable:[{when:'Intention (your own plan, "I")',add:"stem + -겠-",ex:"하다 → 하겠어요; 먹다 → 먹겠습니다"},{when:"Guess (about something you can see/know)",add:"stem + -겠-",ex:"맛있다 → 맛있겠어요; 춥다 → 춥겠어요"},{when:"Guess about the past",add:"-았/었겠-",ex:"힘들다 → 힘들었겠어요"},{when:"Set polite phrases",add:"fixed forms",ex:"알겠어요 (got it); 모르겠어요 (I don't know); 잘 먹겠습니다"},{when:"Noun + guess",add:"N(이)겠-",ex:"학생 → 학생이겠어요"}],examples:[{ko:"제가 내일 다시 오겠습니다.",romanization:"jega naeil dasi ogesseumnida.",en:"I'll come back tomorrow.",note:"Your own plan; -겠습니다 sounds firm and polite, common when serving someone or in formal speech."},{ko:"이 음식 정말 맛있겠어요!",romanization:"i eumsik jeongmal masitgesseoyo!",en:"This food looks so good!",note:"A guess from what you see — you haven't tasted it yet, but you can tell it must be good."},{ko:"숙제가 많아서 힘들었겠어요.",romanization:"sukjega manaseo himdeureotgesseoyo.",en:"That must have been rough with so much homework.",note:"-었겠어요 guesses about someone else's past and shows you feel for them."},{ko:"무슨 말인지 잘 모르겠어요.",romanization:"museun marinji jal moreugesseoyo.",en:"I don't really get what you mean.",note:"모르겠어요 is a set, softened way to say it; plain 몰라요 can sound more blunt."}],keyPoint:{label:"Intention -겠- vs. Guess -겠-",body:`With "I" as the subject and an action verb, -겠- usually means a plan ("I'll"). With someone else, or with an adjective describing a situation, it means a guess ("must be / looks like"). The subject and the kind of verb tell you which. The polite "future" feel of -겠- in set phrases comes later.`},pronunciation:"-겠- tenses the next consonant: 먹겠어요 → [먹께써요], 맛있겠어요 → [마싣께써요].",drill:{instruction:"Decide if it's a plan or a guess, then finish it with -겠-.",model:"(친구가 밤새 일했어요) → 정말 피곤하겠어요.",items:["(내 차례예요) 제가 먼저 ___ (하다).","(친구가 시험에 떨어졌어요) 많이 ___ (속상하다).","(처음 듣는 설명이에요) 잘 ___ (모르다)."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"그 사람 학생이에요? — said as 그 사람 학생이겠어요?",right:"그 사람 학생이에요?",explanation:`Don't use -겠- for a plain yes/no question. -겠- adds "must be / I'm guessing," so 학생이겠어요? asks "I suppose he's a student?" To simply ask whether someone is a student, use 학생이에요?`}},{title:"-다면 / N(이)라면 — If That Were the Case",func:`Use -다면 / N(이)라면 to set up a "what if" — an imagined or supposed situation, often one you don't really expect or that isn't true right now: "if (let's say)...".`,formTable:[{when:"Verb stem",add:"-ㄴ/는다면",ex:"가다 → 간다면; 먹다 → 먹는다면"},{when:"Adjective stem",add:"-다면",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘다면; 많다 → 많다면"},{when:"Past (any stem)",add:"-았/었다면",ex:"왔다면; 먹었다면"},{when:"Noun ending in a consonant",add:"N이라면",ex:"학생 → 학생이라면"},{when:"Noun ending in a vowel",add:"N라면",ex:"부자 → 부자라면; 의사 → 의사라면"}],examples:[{ko:"돈이 많다면 큰 집을 사고 싶어요.",romanization:"doni mantamyeon keun jibeul sago sipeoyo.",en:"If I had a lot of money, I'd want to buy a big house.",note:`A "what if" the speaker doesn't really expect; 많다 is an adjective, so -다면.`},{ko:"내가 너라면 그 사람한테 전화할 거예요.",romanization:"naega neoramyeon geu saramhante jeonhwahal geoyeyo.",en:"If I were you, I'd call him.",note:'Noun + 라면 sets up an imagined "if I were" situation.'},{ko:"시간이 있다면 같이 영화 봐요.",romanization:"sigani ittamyeon gachi yeonghwa bwayo.",en:"If you happen to have time, let's catch a movie.",note:"있다 takes -다면; here it frames a supposed condition before a suggestion."},{ko:"그때 일찍 출발했다면 안 늦었을 거예요.",romanization:"geuttae iljjik chulbalhaetdamyeon an neujeosseul geoyeyo.",en:"If we'd left early back then, we wouldn't have been late.",note:"-았/었다면 looks back at something that didn't happen — a regret about the past."}],keyPoint:{label:"-다면 vs. -(으)면",body:'-(으)면 is the all-purpose "if/when," fine for real, likely things (밥 먹으면 = when/if I eat). -다면 puts more weight on "just supposing," so it suits imagined or unlikely cases. For everyday real conditions, -(으)면 is still your default. The full -았/었다면 "would have" pattern is explored more later.'},pronunciation:"많다면 sounds like [만타면]: the ㅎ in 많 blends with the following ㄷ into an aspirated [ㅌ].",drill:{instruction:'Make a "what if" sentence using -다면 / N(이)라면.',model:"(복권 당첨) 복권에 당첨된다면 여행을 갈 거예요.",items:["(내가 새라면) ___ 하늘을 날고 싶어요.","(시간이 많다) ___ 책을 더 읽을 거예요.","(다시 태어난다) ___ 뭘 하고 싶어요?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"학생다면 할인을 받아요.",right:"학생이라면 할인을 받아요.",explanation:"After a noun, you can't just add -다면. Nouns use (이)라면: 학생이라면 (if you're a student), 부자라면 (if you're rich). Only verb and adjective stems take -다면 directly."}},{title:"-(으)ㄴ/는데요 — Softening and Leaving Room",func:`Ending a sentence with -(으)ㄴ/는데요 softens it. Instead of a hard statement, it sets something up and leaves the door open — for the other person to react, for a "but...," or for a gentle pushback. It's one of the most common ways Koreans avoid sounding too direct.`,formTable:[{when:"Action verb (present)",add:"-는데요",ex:"가다 → 가는데요; 먹다 → 먹는데요"},{when:"Adjective, vowel stem (present)",add:"-ㄴ데요",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁜데요; 크다 → 큰데요"},{when:"Adjective, consonant stem (present)",add:"-은데요",ex:"좋다 → 좋은데요; 작다 → 작은데요"},{when:"Past (any stem)",add:"-았/었는데요",ex:"가다 → 갔는데요; 먹다 → 먹었는데요"},{when:"Noun (present)",add:"N인데요",ex:"학생 → 학생인데요"}],examples:[{ko:"지금 좀 바쁜데요.",romanization:"jigeum jom bappeundeyo.",en:"I'm kind of busy right now... (so maybe later?)",note:'A soft "no" — it states the situation and lets the other person draw the conclusion, instead of flatly refusing.'},{ko:"이거 제 자린데요.",romanization:"igeo je jarindeyo.",en:"Um, this is my seat... (could you move?)",note:"Noun + 인데요 makes a gentle correction without sounding harsh."},{ko:"저는 매운 거 잘 못 먹는데요.",romanization:"jeoneun maeun geo jal mot meongneundeyo.",en:"I'm not so good with spicy food, actually...",note:"-는데요 softly raises a concern and leaves room for the other person to respond."},{ko:"어제는 비가 왔는데요.",romanization:"eojeneun biga watneundeyo.",en:"It rained yesterday, though...",note:'Past -았/었는데요 gently offers a fact, often hinting at a "but" the listener can pick up on.'}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄴ/는데요 vs. a plain -아/어요 ending",body:`좀 바빠요 just states "I'm busy." 좀 바쁜데요 says the same thing but trails off, inviting the other person to fill in the rest — softer and less final. Use it to hedge, hint, or set up a contrast. Its longer use for connecting two clauses ("~는데, …") comes in a separate point.`},pronunciation:"In 먹는데요, the ㄱ before ㄴ turns nasal: it sounds like [멍는데요]. 좋은데요 sounds like [조은데요], with the ㅎ dropping out.",drill:{instruction:"Soften each blunt sentence by re-ending it with -(으)ㄴ/는데요.",model:"지금 바빠요. → 지금 좀 바쁜데요.",items:["그건 제 책이에요. → 그건 제 ___.","저는 그 영화 별로예요. → 저는 그 영화 ___.","어제 거기 갔어요. → 어제 거기 ___."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저 지금 바쁜데요 그래서 못 가요.",right:"저 지금 바쁜데요. (and let the meaning land on its own)",explanation:`English speakers spell out the whole "so I can't go." The point of -(으)ㄴ/는데요 is to leave that part unsaid and let the listener get it. Tacking on the blunt conclusion undoes the softening the ending is there to create.`}}],o=[{title:"ㅂ Irregular: 춥다 → 추워요",func:"A lot of describing words (and a few action words) whose stem ends in ㅂ change that ㅂ to 우 when a vowel ending comes next. That's why 춥다 (to be cold) becomes 추워요, not 춥어요.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅂ, before a vowel ending (-아/어요, -았/었-, -으니까)",add:"ㅂ → 우, then add 어요 (우+어 = 워)",ex:"춥다 → 추+우+어요 → 추워요"},{when:"Stem ends in ㅂ, before a consonant ending (-고, -지만, -습니다)",add:"no change, ㅂ stays",ex:"춥다 → 춥고, 춥지만"},{when:"돕다 / 곱다 only",add:"ㅂ → 오 (오+아 = 와)",ex:"돕다 → 도+오+아요 → 도와요"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘 날씨가 너무 추워요.",romanization:"oneul nalssiga neomu chuwoyo.",en:"The weather is so cold today.",note:"춥다 + 아/어요: ㅂ turns to 우, then 우+어 joins into 워."},{ko:"이 가방은 정말 가벼워요.",romanization:"i gabangeun jeongmal gabyeowoyo.",en:"This bag is really light.",note:"가볍다 follows the same ㅂ → 우 → 워 pattern."},{ko:"친구가 제 일을 도와줬어요.",romanization:"chinguga je ireul dowajwosseoyo.",en:"My friend helped me with my work.",note:"돕다 is special: ㅂ → 오, so 도+오+아 = 도와요/도와줬어요."},{ko:"한국어 공부는 어렵지만 재미있어요.",romanization:"hangugeo gongbuneun eoryeopjiman jaemiisseoyo.",en:"Studying Korean is hard but fun.",note:"Before a consonant ending (-지만), ㅂ does NOT change: 어렵지만."}],keyPoint:{label:"Vowel ending vs consonant ending",body:"The ㅂ only changes before a vowel: 춥다 → 추워요 (vowel) but 춥다 → 춥고 (consonant). Almost all ㅂ-irregular words use 우; only 돕다 and 곱다 use 오. For now, just learn 추워요 as a set phrase and you'll see the pattern."},pronunciation:"추워요 is said smoothly as [추워요]. The 우+어 → 워 is a real spelling change, not just a sound, so you actually write 워.",drill:{instruction:"Change each ㅂ-irregular dictionary form to the polite -아/어요 form.",model:"춥다 → 추워요",items:["덥다 → ____ (be hot → 더워요)","맵다 → ____ (be spicy → 매워요)","돕다 → ____ (help → 도와요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"날씨가 춥어요.",right:"날씨가 추워요.",explanation:"It feels natural to just add the ending and say 춥어요, but ㅂ-irregular stems change the ㅂ to 우 before a vowel, giving 추워요."}},{title:"ㄷ Irregular: 듣다 → 들어요",func:"A small group of action words whose stem ends in ㄷ change that ㄷ to ㄹ when a vowel ending follows. So 듣다 (to hear/listen) becomes 들어요, not 듣어요.",formTable:[{when:"ㄷ-irregular stem + vowel ending (-아/어요, -았/었-, -으면)",add:"ㄷ → ㄹ, then add 어요",ex:"듣다 → 들+어요 → 들어요"},{when:"ㄷ-irregular stem + consonant ending (-고, -지만, -습니다)",add:"no change, ㄷ stays",ex:"듣다 → 듣고, 듣습니다"},{when:"Regular ㄷ verbs (받다, 닫다, 믿다)",add:"ㄷ never changes",ex:"받다 → 받아요 (not 발아요)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 음악을 자주 들어요.",romanization:"jeoneun eumageul jaju deureoyo.",en:"I listen to music a lot.",note:"듣다 + 아/어요: ㄷ becomes ㄹ before the vowel, so 들어요."},{ko:"매일 학교까지 걸어요.",romanization:"maeil hakgyokkaji georeoyo.",en:"I walk to school every day.",note:"걷다 (to walk) is ㄷ-irregular: 걷 → 걸 → 걸어요."},{ko:"선생님한테 질문을 물어봤어요.",romanization:"seonsaengnimhante jilmuneul mureobwasseoyo.",en:"I asked the teacher a question.",note:"묻다 (to ask) → 물어봤어요; ㄷ → ㄹ before the past-tense vowel."},{ko:"친구한테서 선물을 받았어요.",romanization:"chinguhanteseo seonmureul badasseoyo.",en:"I got a present from my friend.",note:"받다 is a REGULAR ㄷ verb, so ㄷ stays: 받았어요, never 발았어요."}],keyPoint:{label:"Not every ㄷ verb is irregular",body:"Only a few verbs change (듣다, 걷다, 묻다 'ask', 싣다 'load'). Everyday verbs like 받다 (get), 닫다 (close), 믿다 (believe) keep their ㄷ: 받아요, 닫아요, 믿어요. You just have to remember which ones are irregular."},pronunciation:"들어요 links as [드러요] — the ㄹ slides into the next syllable. Don't worry about the rule mid-conversation; learn 들어요 as a chunk.",drill:{instruction:"Conjugate these ㄷ-irregular verbs into the -아/어요 form.",model:"듣다 → 들어요",items:["걷다 → ____ (walk → 걸어요)","묻다 → ____ (ask → 물어요)","싣다 → ____ (load → 실어요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"음악을 듣어요.",right:"음악을 들어요.",explanation:"It's tempting to keep the ㄷ and say 듣어요, but ㄷ-irregular verbs change ㄷ to ㄹ before a vowel ending, giving 들어요."}},{title:"ㅅ Irregular: 낫다 → 나아요",func:"Some action/describing words whose stem ends in ㅅ drop the ㅅ before a vowel ending — but unlike ㄷ and ㅂ, the two vowels do NOT join up. So 낫다 (to get better) becomes 나아요, keeping both vowels.",formTable:[{when:"ㅅ-irregular stem + vowel ending (-아/어요, -았/었-, -으면)",add:"ㅅ drops; vowels stay separate (no joining)",ex:"낫다 → 나+아요 → 나아요"},{when:"ㅅ-irregular stem + consonant ending (-고, -지만, -습니다)",add:"no change, ㅅ stays",ex:"낫다 → 낫고, 낫습니다"},{when:"Regular ㅅ verbs (웃다, 씻다, 벗다)",add:"ㅅ never drops",ex:"웃다 → 웃어요 (not 우어요)"}],examples:[{ko:"감기가 다 나았어요.",romanization:"gamgiga da naasseoyo.",en:"My cold is all better now.",note:"낫다 → 나았어요; ㅅ drops but 나+았 stays as two parts, no joining."},{ko:"이 식당이 저 식당보다 나아요.",romanization:"i sikdangi jeo sikdangboda naayo.",en:"This restaurant is better than that one.",note:"낫다 'be better' → 나아요; the two ㅏ's stay separate, written 나아."},{ko:"물을 컵에 부어 주세요.",romanization:"mureul keobe bueo juseyo.",en:"Please pour the water into the cup.",note:"붓다 (to pour) → 부어 주세요; ㅅ drops, vowels do not merge."},{ko:"아이가 크게 웃어요.",romanization:"aiga keuge useoyo.",en:"The child laughs loudly.",note:"웃다 is a REGULAR ㅅ verb, so ㅅ stays: 웃어요, never 우어요."}],keyPoint:{label:"ㅅ drops but the vowels stay apart",body:"Unlike 추워요 or 들어요, the ㅅ-irregular leaves the two vowels apart: 낫다 → 나아요 (not 나요). Compare regular 웃다 → 웃어요, where the ㅅ stays. The irregular ones to know: 낫다, 붓다, 젓다, 짓다."},pronunciation:"나아요 keeps two clear ㅏ sounds [나아요]; don't shorten it to [나요]. That held, two-beat vowel is the giveaway of the ㅅ-irregular.",drill:{instruction:"Conjugate these ㅅ-irregular verbs into the -아/어요 form (keep the vowels separate).",model:"낫다 → 나아요",items:["붓다 → ____ (pour → 부어요)","젓다 → ____ (stir → 저어요)","짓다 → ____ (build → 지어요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"감기가 다 나요.",right:"감기가 다 나았어요.",explanation:"Learners drop the ㅅ and then wrongly squeeze the vowels into 나요. The ㅅ-irregular keeps both vowels: present 나아요, past 나았어요."}},{title:"르 Irregular: 모르다 → 몰라요",func:"Words ending in 르 add an extra ㄹ to the syllable before 르 and drop the ㅡ before -아/어. So 모르다 (to not know) becomes 몰라요, with a doubled ㄹ.",formTable:[{when:"르 stem + vowel ending, when the syllable before 르 has ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"drop ㅡ, add ㄹ to the syllable before, then 라",ex:"모르다 → 몰ㄹ + 아 → 몰라요"},{when:"르 stem + vowel ending, when the syllable before 르 has any other vowel",add:"drop ㅡ, add ㄹ to the syllable before, then 러",ex:"부르다 → 불ㄹ + 어 → 불러요"},{when:"르 stem + consonant ending (-고, -지만)",add:"no change",ex:"모르다 → 모르고, 모르지만"}],examples:[{ko:"그 사람 이름을 잘 몰라요.",romanization:"geu saram ireumeul jal mollayo.",en:"I don't really know that person's name.",note:"모르다 → 몰라요; 모 before 르 has ㅗ, so the ending is 라."},{ko:"친구가 노래를 불렀어요.",romanization:"chinguga noraereul bulleosseoyo.",en:"My friend sang a song.",note:"부르다 → 불렀어요; 부 has ㅜ (not ㅏ/ㅗ), so the ending is 러/렀."},{ko:"택시를 빨리 불러 주세요.",romanization:"taeksireul ppalli bulleo juseyo.",en:"Please call a taxi quickly.",note:"부르다 'call' → 불러; same doubled-ㄹ rule with 러."},{ko:"시간이 너무 빨라요.",romanization:"sigani neomu ppallayo.",en:"Time goes by so fast.",note:"빠르다 → 빨라요; 빠 has ㅏ, so it takes 라."}],keyPoint:{label:"라 vs 러 depends on the vowel before 르",body:"If the syllable before 르 has ㅏ or ㅗ, use 라 (모르다 → 몰라요, 빠르다 → 빨라요). Otherwise use 러 (부르다 → 불러요, 누르다 → 눌러요). It's the same ㅏ/ㅗ vs everything-else choice as -아요/-어요."},pronunciation:"몰라요 has a strong doubled ㄹ [몰라요] — the first ㄹ closes 몰 and the second opens 라.",drill:{instruction:"Conjugate these 르-irregular words into the -아/어요 form (watch 라 vs 러).",model:"모르다 → 몰라요",items:["부르다 → ____ (call/sing → 불러요)","빠르다 → ____ (be fast → 빨라요)","다르다 → ____ (be different → 달라요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"그 사람을 몰라요 → 모르아요",right:"그 사람을 몰라요.",explanation:"Learners try to add the ending straight onto 모르 and get 모르아요. The 르-irregular drops the ㅡ and doubles the ㄹ, so it's 몰라요."}},{title:"ㅡ Irregular: 바쁘다 → 바빠요",func:"When a stem ends in ㅡ (like 쓰다, 바쁘다), the ㅡ drops out before -아/어. Then the syllable before it decides whether you add 아 or 어. So 바쁘다 (to be busy) becomes 바빠요.",formTable:[{when:"ㅡ stem + vowel ending, when the syllable before ㅡ has ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"drop ㅡ, add 아 → 빠",ex:"바쁘다 → 바ㅃ + 아 → 바빠요"},{when:"ㅡ stem + vowel ending, other vowel (or no syllable before, like 쓰다)",add:"drop ㅡ, add 어 → 써/뻐",ex:"쓰다 → ㅆ + 어 → 써요"},{when:"ㅡ stem + consonant ending (-고, -지만)",add:"no change, ㅡ stays",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘고, 바쁘지만"}],examples:[{ko:"요즘 일이 너무 바빠요.",romanization:"yojeum iri neomu bappayo.",en:"I'm so busy these days.",note:"바쁘다 → 바빠요; 바 has ㅏ, so the dropped ㅡ takes 아 → 빠."},{ko:"지금 편지를 써요.",romanization:"jigeum pyeonjireul sseoyo.",en:"I'm writing a letter right now.",note:"쓰다 → 써요; nothing before ㅡ, so it defaults to 어 → 써."},{ko:"동생이 정말 예뻐요.",romanization:"dongsaengi jeongmal yeppeoyo.",en:"My younger sibling is really pretty.",note:"예쁘다 → 예뻐요; 예 is not ㅏ/ㅗ, so it takes 어 → 뻐."},{ko:"머리가 조금 아파요.",romanization:"meoriga jogeum apayo.",en:"My head hurts a little.",note:"아프다 → 아파요; 아 has ㅏ, so the dropped ㅡ takes 아 → 파."}],keyPoint:{label:"Drop the ㅡ, then pick 아 or 어",body:"The ㅡ always disappears before -아/어. To choose, look at the syllable before it: ㅏ/ㅗ → 아 (바빠요, 아파요), anything else (or nothing) → 어 (써요, 예뻐요). This is the same vowel-harmony idea as regular -아요/-어요."},pronunciation:"바빠요 has a tense ㅃ [바빠요], and 써요 starts with a tense ㅆ [써요]. The dropped ㅡ leaves a strong, double consonant sound.",drill:{instruction:"Conjugate these ㅡ-irregular words into the -아/어요 form (watch 아 vs 어).",model:"바쁘다 → 바빠요",items:["크다 → ____ (be big → 커요)","아프다 → ____ (hurt → 아파요)","슬프다 → ____ (be sad → 슬퍼요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"요즘 너무 바쁘어요.",right:"요즘 너무 바빠요.",explanation:"It feels natural to keep the ㅡ and add the ending, giving 바쁘어요. But ㅡ-stems drop the ㅡ first, so it's 바빠요 (and 쓰다 → 써요, not 쓰어요)."}},{title:"ㅎ Irregular: 빨갛다 → 빨개요",func:"Most color words and a few pointing words (그렇다, 어떻다) end in ㅎ. Before -아/어, the ㅎ drops AND the vowel changes to ㅐ. So 빨갛다 (to be red) becomes 빨개요. Note: 좋다 is regular — 좋아요.",formTable:[{when:"ㅎ-irregular stem + -아/어요",add:"drop ㅎ, vowel becomes ㅐ, add 요",ex:"빨갛다 → 빨가 → 빨개요"},{when:"ㅎ-irregular stem + 어떻다 / 이렇다 etc.",add:"same: drop ㅎ, vowel → ㅐ",ex:"어떻다 → 어때요 / 이렇다 → 이래요"},{when:"ㅎ-irregular stem + consonant ending (-고, -지만)",add:"no change, ㅎ stays",ex:"빨갛다 → 빨갛고, 빨갛지만"}],examples:[{ko:"사과가 정말 빨개요.",romanization:"sagwaga jeongmal ppalgaeyo.",en:"The apple is really red.",note:"빨갛다 → 빨개요; ㅎ drops and the vowel turns into ㅐ."},{ko:"하늘이 너무 파래요.",romanization:"haneuri neomu paraeyo.",en:"The sky is so blue.",note:"파랗다 → 파래요; same ㅎ-drop with the vowel becoming ㅐ."},{ko:"저도 그래요.",romanization:"jeodo geuraeyo.",en:"Me too / Same here.",note:"그렇다 → 그래요; a super common one to just memorize."},{ko:"날씨가 어때요?",romanization:"nalssiga eottaeyo?",en:"How's the weather?",note:"어떻다 → 어때요; you'll hear 어때요 constantly, so learn it as a set phrase."}],keyPoint:{label:"ㅎ drops and the vowel turns into ㅐ",body:"With ㅎ-irregulars, two things happen at once before -아/어: the ㅎ disappears and the vowel becomes ㅐ (빨갛다 → 빨개요, 그렇다 → 그래요). Watch out: 좋다 (be good) is NOT irregular — it's just 좋아요. Learn 그래요 and 어때요 as ready-made phrases first."},pronunciation:"빨개요 is said [빨개요] with the ㅐ vowel; 그래요 is [그래요]. Don't try to keep the ㅎ sound — it's gone before the ending.",drill:{instruction:"Conjugate these ㅎ-irregular words into the -아/어요 form (ㅎ drops, vowel → ㅐ).",model:"빨갛다 → 빨개요",items:["노랗다 → ____ (be yellow → 노래요)","어떻다 → ____ (be how → 어때요)","그렇다 → ____ (be so → 그래요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"사과가 빨갛아요.",right:"사과가 빨개요.",explanation:"It seems logical to keep the ㅎ and add 아요, giving 빨갛아요. But ㅎ-irregulars drop the ㅎ and change the vowel to ㅐ, so it's 빨개요. (And don't overcorrect 좋다 — that one really is 좋아요.)"}}],n=[{title:"에게 / 한테 / 께 — to a person (께 = honorific)",func:"Marks the person who receives or is the target of an action: giving, sending, asking, telling, lending. English uses 'to (a person)'.",formTable:[{when:"Neutral or written, after any person noun",add:"noun + 에게",ex:"친구 → 친구에게 (to a friend)"},{when:"Casual spoken, after any person noun",add:"noun + 한테",ex:"동생 → 동생한테 (to my younger sibling)"},{when:"Honorific, a person you respect",add:"noun + 께",ex:"선생님 → 선생님께 (to the teacher)"},{when:"'from a person' (source)",add:"noun + 에게서 / 한테서",ex:"친구한테서 들었어요 (I heard it from a friend)"}],examples:[{ko:"친구에게 선물을 줬어요.",romanization:"chinguege seonmureul jwosseoyo.",en:"I gave a gift to a friend.",note:"친구 is the receiver of 주다, so the person-target 에게 fits."},{ko:"동생한테 책을 빌려줬어요.",romanization:"dongsaenghante chaegeul billyeojwosseoyo.",en:"I lent a book to my younger sibling.",note:"한테 is the everyday spoken choice in casual talk."},{ko:"선생님께 메일을 드렸어요.",romanization:"seonsaengnimkke meireul deuryeosseoyo.",en:"I sent an email to the teacher.",note:"께 raises the receiver and pairs naturally with the humble verb 드리다."},{ko:"회사에 메일을 보냈어요.",romanization:"hoesae meireul bonaesseoyo.",en:"I sent an email to the company.",note:"A place or organization takes 에, not 에게 — 에게 is only for people (and animals)."}],keyPoint:{label:"에게/한테 vs 께",body:"에게 and 한테 mean the same thing (한테 just sounds more casual). 께 is the honorific version for a respected person and usually rides with humble verbs like 드리다 and 여쭤보다."},pronunciation:"께 is a tense [kk] sound, like the kk in 'bookkeeper' — 선생님께 is [seon-saeng-nim-kke], never a plain [g].",drill:{instruction:"Fill the blank with 에게/한테 or 께, then read the whole sentence aloud.",model:"친구___ 선물을 줬어요. → 친구에게 선물을 줬어요.",items:["선생님___ 메일을 드렸어요. (respected receiver)","동생___ 책을 빌려줬어요. (casual, spoken)","친구___ 전화했어요. (neutral)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"선물에게 줬어요.",right:"친구에게 선물을 줬어요.",explanation:"English 'gave to the gift' is obviously wrong, but learners attach 에게 to whatever follows 'to'. 에게 marks only the person receiver; the thing given takes the object particle 을/를."}},{title:"와/과 · 하고 · (이)랑 — and / with",func:"Links two nouns ('A and B') or marks the companion you do something with ('with A'). All three mean the same; they differ only in register.",formTable:[{when:"After a vowel-final noun (written/tidy)",add:"noun + 와",ex:"커피 → 커피와 물 (coffee and water)"},{when:"After a consonant-final noun (written/tidy)",add:"noun + 과",ex:"책 → 책과 가방 (a book and a bag)"},{when:"Any noun (neutral spoken)",add:"noun + 하고",ex:"민수하고 (with Minsu)"},{when:"Vowel-final / consonant-final (casual spoken)",add:"noun + 랑 / 이랑",ex:"친구 → 친구랑 / 동생 → 동생이랑"}],examples:[{ko:"친구랑 영화를 봐요.",romanization:"chingurang yeonghwareul bwayo.",en:"I watch a movie with a friend.",note:"친구 ends in a vowel, so 랑; casual speech prefers (이)랑."},{ko:"민수하고 같이 갔어요.",romanization:"minsuhago gachi gasseoyo.",en:"I went together with Minsu.",note:"하고 plus 같이 makes the 'with / together' reading unmistakable."},{ko:"커피와 물을 샀어요.",romanization:"keopiwa mureul sasseoyo.",en:"I bought coffee and water.",note:"커피 ends in a vowel → 와; this sounds tidy and slightly written."},{ko:"책과 가방만 가져왔어요.",romanization:"chaekgwa gabangman gajyeowasseoyo.",en:"I brought only a book and a bag.",note:"책 ends in a consonant → 과; the linker comes before the next particle 만."}],keyPoint:{label:"와/과 vs 하고 vs (이)랑",body:"Meaning is identical; register differs. 와/과 = neutral-to-written, 하고 = neutral spoken, (이)랑 = casual spoken. Add 같이 when you want 'with' to be clear over 'and'."},pronunciation:"과 after a consonant tenses: 책과 sounds like [chaek-kkwa]; 와 after a vowel stays light, 커피와 [keo-pi-wa].",drill:{instruction:"Choose 와/과 or (이)랑 to match the final sound, then read it aloud.",model:"커피 ___ 물 → 커피와 물",items:["책 ___ 가방 (written, consonant-final)","친구 ___ 영화를 봐요 (casual, vowel-final)","동생 ___ 같이 갔어요 (casual, consonant-final)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구랑 영화를 보고 팝콘이랑 먹었어요.",right:"영화를 보고 팝콘을 먹었어요.",explanation:"와/과·하고·(이)랑 connect nouns, not actions. To join two verbs ('watch and eat'), use the connective -고, not these particles."}},{title:"밖에 + negative — nothing but / only (and it's not enough)",func:"Marks a limit that always needs a negative predicate. It frames the amount as scarce or insufficient: 'nothing but X', 'only X (and that's all there is)'.",formTable:[{when:"noun + 밖에 with 없다",add:"noun + 밖에 + 없어요",ex:"한 시간밖에 없어요 (I only have one hour)"},{when:"noun + 밖에 with 못",add:"noun + 밖에 + 못 V",ex:"물밖에 못 마셔요 (I can drink nothing but water)"},{when:"noun + 밖에 with 안 / -지 않다",add:"noun + 밖에 + 안 V",ex:"공부밖에 안 해요 (I do nothing but study)"},{when:"Replaces 이/가/을/를",add:"no subject/object particle before 밖에",ex:"이것밖에 없어요 (✓), 이것을밖에 (✗)"}],examples:[{ko:"공부밖에 못 해요.",romanization:"gongbubakke mot haeyo.",en:"I can do nothing but study.",note:"못 supplies the required negative; 밖에 alone can't stand with a positive verb."},{ko:"한 시간밖에 없어요.",romanization:"han siganbakke eopseoyo.",en:"I only have one hour.",note:"없어요 carries the negative and signals the time feels too short."},{ko:"이 방법밖에 없어요.",romanization:"i bangbeopbakke eopseoyo.",en:"There's no way except this one.",note:"밖에 plus 없어요 = a forced, no-other-choice frame."},{ko:"물밖에 못 마셔요.",romanization:"mulbakke mot masyeoyo.",en:"I can only drink water.",note:"물 takes 밖에 with no extra particle, paired with 못."}],keyPoint:{label:"밖에 vs 만",body:"Both translate as 'only', but 밖에 demands a negative predicate and feels scarce/insufficient (공부밖에 못 해요), while 만 is neutral and takes a positive verb (공부만 해요). If you see a positive verb, you need 만, not 밖에."},pronunciation:"밖에 links across the ㄲ: [ba-kke], so 공부밖에 is [gong-bu-ba-kke] and 한 시간밖에 is [han si-gan-ba-kke].",drill:{instruction:"Complete each sentence so the predicate is negative (못/안/없어요).",model:"공부밖에 ___ . → 공부밖에 못 해요.",items:["한 시간밖에 ___ . (there is none left)","물밖에 ___ 마셔요. (cannot)","이 방법밖에 ___ . (there is none)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"공부밖에 해요.",right:"공부밖에 못 해요. / 공부만 해요.",explanation:"Because English 'only' goes with positive verbs, learners drop the negative. 밖에 grammatically requires 못/안/없어요. If you want a plain positive 'only', switch to 만."}},{title:"(이)나 — as many as / or",func:"After a number or amount it adds 'surprisingly much' (as many as); between two nouns it offers a loose choice ('A or B'). This chapter centers on the 'as many as' use.",formTable:[{when:"After a consonant-final number/noun",add:"+ 이나",ex:"다섯 잔 → 다섯 잔이나 (as many as 5 cups)"},{when:"After a vowel-final number/noun",add:"+ 나",ex:"세 개 → 세 개나 (as many as 3)"},{when:"'A or B' (rough choice)",add:"noun(이)나 noun",ex:"커피나 물 (coffee or water)"},{when:"Loose suggestion ('or something')",add:"noun + (이)나",ex:"물이나 마실까요? (shall we just drink water or something?)"}],examples:[{ko:"문제가 세 개나 남았어요.",romanization:"munjega se gaena namasseoyo.",en:"As many as three questions are left.",note:"개 ends in a vowel → 나; the speaker feels three is a lot."},{ko:"커피를 다섯 잔이나 마셨어요.",romanization:"keopireul daseot jan-ina masyeosseoyo.",en:"I drank as many as five cups of coffee.",note:"잔 ends in a consonant → 이나; emphasizes the surprisingly high count."},{ko:"한 시간이나 기다렸어요.",romanization:"han siganina gidaryeosseoyo.",en:"I waited a whole hour.",note:"시간 ends in a consonant → 이나; the wait felt long."},{ko:"커피나 물 주세요.",romanization:"keopina mul juseyo.",en:"Give me coffee or water (either is fine).",note:"Here (이)나 means a loose 'or' between two acceptable options."}],keyPoint:{label:"(이)나 vs 밖에",body:"Both attach to amounts but pull opposite ways. 다섯 잔이나 = the amount feels big (more than expected); 다섯 잔밖에 못 마셨어요 = the amount feels small (less than wanted). 나 says 'wow, that much'; 밖에 says 'only that little'."},pronunciation:"이나/나 trigger no special tensing; just match the final sound: 개나 [gae-na], 잔이나 [jan-i-na].",drill:{instruction:"Add 이나 or 나 to the number to mean 'as many as'.",model:"세 개 ___ 남았어요. → 세 개나 남았어요.",items:["다섯 잔 ___ 마셨어요. (consonant-final)","한 시간 ___ 기다렸어요. (consonant-final)","열 명 ___ 왔어요. (consonant-final)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"세 개나밖에 남았어요.",right:"세 개나 남았어요. / 세 개밖에 안 남았어요.",explanation:"Learners blend the two because both attach to numbers. Pick one stance: 나 = surprisingly many (positive verb), 밖에 = surprisingly few (negative verb). They cannot stack."}},{title:"(이)라도 — at least; even if only",func:"Offers a minimum or fallback option when the ideal one isn't available: 'at least X', 'even if only X', 'X if nothing else'.",formTable:[{when:"After a vowel-final noun",add:"noun + 라도",ex:"커피 → 커피라도 (coffee, if nothing else)"},{when:"After a consonant-final noun",add:"noun + 이라도",ex:"물 → 물이라도 (at least water)"},{when:"With a suggestion (-(으)ㄹ까요? / -읍시다)",add:"noun(이)라도 + suggestion",ex:"물이라도 마실까요? (shall we at least drink some water?)"},{when:"'even a little' with time/amount",add:"amount + (이)라도",ex:"잠깐이라도 쉬세요 (rest, even for a moment)"}],examples:[{ko:"시간 없으면 물이라도 마셔요.",romanization:"sigan eopseumyeon murirado masyeoyo.",en:"If you don't have time, at least drink some water.",note:"물 is consonant-final → 이라도; water is the fallback when there's no time for more."},{ko:"커피라도 한잔할까요?",romanization:"keopirado hanjanhalkkayo?",en:"Shall we at least grab a coffee?",note:"커피 is vowel-final → 라도; offers coffee as a modest plan."},{ko:"잠깐이라도 쉬세요.",romanization:"jamkkanirado swiseyo.",en:"Rest, even for just a moment.",note:"잠깐 is consonant-final → 이라도; 'even a little' time is better than none."},{ko:"버스라도 타고 가요.",romanization:"beoseurado tago gayo.",en:"Let's at least take the bus (go by bus).",note:"버스 is vowel-final → 라도; the bus is the second-best option."}],keyPoint:{label:"(이)라도 vs 만",body:"만 picks X as the exclusive choice ('only X'); (이)라도 picks X as a settling-for second choice ('X if nothing better'). 물만 마셔요 = I drink only water (by choice); 물이라도 마셔요 = at least drink water (since there's nothing else)."},pronunciation:"(이)라도 is light with no tensing; just link smoothly: 물이라도 [mu-ri-ra-do], 커피라도 [keo-pi-ra-do].",drill:{instruction:"Add 라도 or 이라도 to the noun to mean 'at least / if nothing else'.",model:"커피 ___ 마실까요? → 커피라도 마실까요?",items:["물 ___ 드세요. (consonant-final)","버스 ___ 타고 가요. (vowel-final)","잠깐 ___ 쉬세요. (consonant-final)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"물이라도만 마셔요.",right:"물이라도 마셔요. / 물만 마셔요.",explanation:"(이)라도 and 만 express opposite attitudes (settling vs. exclusive), so they don't stack. Choose 이라도 for 'at least, if nothing else' or 만 for 'only, by choice'."}},{title:"의 — 's / of (noun-to-noun link)",func:`의 links two nouns to show possession or relationship, like English "'s" or "of": 친구의 가방 (a friend's bag). In speech it is usually dropped (친구 가방), and 저의/나의 contract to 제/내.`,formTable:[{when:"noun + 의 + noun (possession)",add:"의",ex:"친구의 책 (a friend's book) · 한국의 음식 (Korea's food)"},{when:"저/나 + 의 contract",add:"제 / 내",ex:"저의 → 제 · 나의 → 내"},{when:"Usually dropped in speech",add:"(no 의)",ex:"친구 가방 = 친구의 가방"}],examples:[{ko:"이건 친구의 책이에요.",romanization:"igeon chinguui chaegieyo.",en:"This is my friend's book.",note:"친구 + 의 + 책 — the first noun owns the second."},{ko:"제 가방이 어디 있어요?",romanization:"je gabangi eodi isseoyo?",en:"Where is my bag?",note:'저의 contracts to 제 — the everyday way to say "my."'},{ko:"한국의 음식을 좋아해요.",romanization:"han-gugui eumsigeul joahaeyo.",en:"I like Korean food.",note:'의 links 한국 to 음식 ("of Korea").'},{ko:"동생 방이 정말 커요.",romanization:"dongsaeng bangi jeongmal keoyo.",en:"My sibling's room is really big.",note:"In speech the 의 is dropped — 동생 방 = 동생의 방."}],keyPoint:{label:"Drop 의 in speech; contract 저의 → 제",body:"Written Korean keeps 의 (친구의 책), but spoken Korean usually drops it (친구 책). The one place it survives is the contractions 제 (저의), 내 (나의), 네 (너의) — always say 제 책, not 저의 책 in conversation."},pronunciation:"The possessive particle 의 may be pronounced [의] or, very commonly in conversation, [에]: 친구의 → [친구의] or [친구에]. Inside a word, non-initial 의 may also be heard as [이] in permitted pronunciations. Treat [에] as a useful spoken option, not the only possible sound.",drill:{instruction:"Link the two nouns with 의 (or its contraction).",model:"(친구 / 책) → 친구의 책 · (저 / 가방) → 제 가방",items:["(동생 / 방) → ______","(저 / 친구) → ______","(한국 / 음식) → ______"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저의 가방",right:"제 가방",explanation:"In speech, 저의 always shrinks to 제. Say 제 가방 (my bag), not 저의 가방 — the full form sounds stiff and written."}},{title:"(으)로 — by means of / toward (direction)",func:"Marks how you do something (tool, means, transport) or which direction you head: 'by/with X', 'toward X'.",formTable:[{when:"After a vowel-final noun",add:"noun + 로",ex:"버스 → 버스로 (by bus)"},{when:"After a consonant-final noun",add:"noun + 으로",ex:"손 → 손으로 (with one's hand)"},{when:"After a ㄹ-final noun (no 으)",add:"noun + 로",ex:"지하철 → 지하철로 (by subway)"},{when:"Direction (toward)",add:"place + (으)로",ex:"오른쪽으로 가세요 (go to the right)"}],examples:[{ko:"버스로 학교에 가요.",romanization:"beoseuro hakgyoe gayo.",en:"I go to school by bus.",note:"버스 is vowel-final → 로; (으)로 marks the means of transport."},{ko:"손으로 먹었어요.",romanization:"soneuro meogeosseoyo.",en:"I ate with my hands.",note:"손 is consonant-final → 으로; the hand is the means/tool."},{ko:"지하철로 갈아탔어요.",romanization:"jihacheollo garatasseoyo.",en:"I transferred to the subway.",note:"지하철 ends in ㄹ, so just 로 (no 으); pronounced [ji-ha-cheol-lo]."},{ko:"이쪽으로 오세요.",romanization:"ijjogeuro oseyo.",en:"Come this way.",note:"이쪽 is consonant-final → 으로; here it marks direction, not means."}],keyPoint:{label:"(으)로 vs 에 (destination)",body:"에 marks the exact destination you arrive at (학교에 가요 = go to school); (으)로 marks the direction you face or the means you use (학교로 가요 = head toward school; 버스로 가요 = go by bus). For transport and tools, always (으)로, never 에."},pronunciation:"After ㄹ the 로 doubles the ㄹ: 지하철로 is [ji-ha-cheol-lo], not [ji-ha-cheol-ro].",drill:{instruction:"Add 로 or 으로 to match the final sound, then read it aloud.",model:"버스 ___ 가요. → 버스로 가요.",items:["손 ___ 먹어요. (consonant-final)","지하철 ___ 가요. (ㄹ-final, just 로)","오른쪽 ___ 가세요. (consonant-final, direction)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"버스에 학교에 가요.",right:"버스로 학교에 가요.",explanation:"English uses 'by bus' / 'to school' with no particle clash, but in Korean the means takes (으)로 and the destination takes 에. Don't put 에 on the transport — 버스에 가요 would mean 'go onto the bus'."}},{title:"마다 — every / each",func:"Attaches to a noun to mean 'every X' or 'each X', often for repeated times or all members of a group: 'every morning', 'each person'.",formTable:[{when:"After a time noun",add:"noun + 마다",ex:"아침 → 아침마다 (every morning)"},{when:"After a counted unit",add:"unit + 마다",ex:"세 시간 → 세 시간마다 (every three hours)"},{when:"After a person/thing noun",add:"noun + 마다",ex:"사람 → 사람마다 (each person)"},{when:"With a place noun",add:"place + 마다",ex:"나라 → 나라마다 (every country)"}],examples:[{ko:"아침마다 커피를 마셔요.",romanization:"achimmada keopireul masyeoyo.",en:"I drink coffee every morning.",note:"아침 + 마다 = 'every morning'; no extra time particle is needed."},{ko:"주말마다 친구를 만나요.",romanization:"jumalmada chingureul mannayo.",en:"I meet my friends every weekend.",note:"마다 attaches straight to 주말; it already means 'every', so don't add 에."},{ko:"사람마다 생각이 달라요.",romanization:"sarammada saenggagi dallayo.",en:"Each person thinks differently.",note:"사람마다 = 'each/every person'; great for talking about variation."},{ko:"세 시간마다 약을 먹어요.",romanization:"se siganmada yageul meogeoyo.",en:"I take medicine every three hours.",note:"마다 after a counted unit gives regular intervals."}],keyPoint:{label:"마다 vs 매-",body:"마다 is a particle stuck onto a noun (아침마다, 날마다). 매 is a prefix glued in front (매일 = every day, 매주 = every week). Both mean 'every', but 매 forms set words while 마다 attaches freely to almost any noun."},pronunciation:"마다 is plain and light: 아침마다 [a-chim-ma-da], 주말마다 [ju-mal-ma-da]. No tensing or linking surprises.",drill:{instruction:"Add 마다 to the noun to mean 'every / each'.",model:"아침 ___ 커피를 마셔요. → 아침마다 커피를 마셔요.",items:["주말 ___ 친구를 만나요. (every weekend)","사람 ___ 생각이 달라요. (each person)","세 시간 ___ 약을 먹어요. (every three hours)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"아침마다에 커피를 마셔요.",right:"아침마다 커피를 마셔요.",explanation:"마다 already carries the 'at every' meaning, so adding the time particle 에 is redundant and wrong. Use 마다 alone — never 마다에."}}],t=[{title:"Counting Word Order: Noun + Number + Counter",func:"To count things in Korean, you name the thing first, then say how many. The pattern is NOUN + NUMBER + COUNTER, with no particle between the number and the counter. English says 'three apples'; Korean says 'apple three (units)' — 사과 세 개.",formTable:[{when:"Basic count of a thing",add:"noun + (space) number + (space) counter",ex:"사과 세 개 = three apples (apple / three / units)"},{when:"Add a verb after the count",add:"keep noun + number + counter, then verb",ex:"커피 두 잔 주세요 = please give me two coffees"},{when:"Mark the counted noun as object",add:"the object particle 을/를 goes on the NOUN, not on the counter",ex:"빵을 네 개 샀어요 = I bought four pieces of bread"}],examples:[{ko:"사과 세 개 주세요.",romanization:"sagwa se gae juseyo.",en:"Please give me three apples.",note:"Order is noun (사과) → number (세) → counter (개); no particle joins 세 and 개."},{ko:"고양이 두 마리가 있어요.",romanization:"goyangi du mari-ga isseoyo.",en:"There are two cats.",note:"The subject particle 가 attaches to the counter 마리 at the end of the count phrase, not to 고양이."},{ko:"물 한 병 살게요.",romanization:"mul han byeong salgeyo.",en:"I'll buy one bottle of water.",note:"한 (from 하나) sits before the counter 병; the noun 물 comes first as always."},{ko:"표를 두 장 샀어요.",romanization:"pyo-reul du jang sasseoyo.",en:"I bought two tickets.",note:"The object particle 를 stays on the noun 표; the count 두 장 follows it directly."}],keyPoint:{label:"Korean reverses the English order",body:"English usually uses number + noun ('three apples'). Standard Korean counting uses noun + number + counter (사과 세 개). In short lists or elliptical speech you may also hear a counter omitted, but noun + number + counter is the reliable full pattern for learners."},pronunciation:"",drill:{instruction:"Put the words in the correct Korean counting order: noun + number + counter. Say the full phrase out loud.",model:"(빵 / 두 / 개) → 빵 두 개",items:["(우유 / 세 / 병) → ___","(책 / 네 / 권) → ___","(사람 / 다섯 / 명) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"세 사과 (se sagwa)",right:"사과 세 개 (sagwa se gae)",explanation:"English speakers often copy 'three apples' and put the number first with no counter. The reliable full Korean pattern puts the noun first and a counter word after the number: 사과 세 개."}},{title:"Native-Number Contractions Before a Counter",func:"Korean native numbers 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷 and 스물 change shape when a counter follows them. They drop their final part: 하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네, 스물→스무. Use the contracted form only when a counter comes right after.",formTable:[{when:"하나 + counter",add:"하나 → 한",ex:"한 개 = one item (NOT 하나 개)"},{when:"둘 + counter",add:"둘 → 두",ex:"두 잔 = two cups"},{when:"셋 + counter",add:"셋 → 세",ex:"세 명 = three people"},{when:"넷 + counter",add:"넷 → 네",ex:"네 마리 = four animals"},{when:"스물 + counter",add:"스물 → 스무",ex:"스무 살 = twenty years old"}],examples:[{ko:"커피 한 잔 마셨어요.",romanization:"keopi han jan masyeosseoyo.",en:"I drank one cup of coffee.",note:"하나 contracts to 한 before the counter 잔; saying 하나 잔 is wrong."},{ko:"연필 두 자루 빌려주세요.",romanization:"yeonpil du jaru billyeo-juseyo.",en:"Please lend me two pencils.",note:"둘 becomes 두 before 자루, the counter for pencils and pen-shaped things."},{ko:"친구 네 명이 왔어요.",romanization:"chingu ne myeong-i wasseoyo.",en:"Four friends came.",note:"넷 contracts to 네 before 명; 넷 명 would be incorrect."},{ko:"우리 형은 스무 살이에요.",romanization:"uri hyeong-eun seumu sal-ieyo.",en:"My older brother is twenty years old.",note:"스물 drops its ㄹ to become 스무 before the age counter 살."}],keyPoint:{label:"Contract only before a counter",body:"하나/둘/셋/넷/스물 keep their full form when counting alone ('하나, 둘, 셋…') but contract to 한/두/세/네/스무 the moment a counter follows. Compare: 사과가 하나 있어요 (one apple, no counter) vs. 사과 한 개 있어요 (one apple, with counter 개)."},pronunciation:"",drill:{instruction:"Contract the native number to its pre-counter form and read the phrase aloud.",model:"하나 + 개 → 한 개",items:["둘 + 명 → ___","넷 + 잔 → ___","스물 + 살 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"하나 개, 둘 잔, 넷 마리",right:"한 개, 두 잔, 네 마리",explanation:"Learners memorize 하나/둘/셋/넷 and use them unchanged. Before any counter they MUST contract to 한/두/세/네. This is one of the most common beginner mistakes."}},{title:"Counting People and Animals: 명 / 분 / 마리",func:"People are counted with 명 (plain) or 분 (honorific, for people you respect). Animals — pets, fish, birds, insects — are all counted with 마리.",formTable:[{when:"Counting people (neutral/plain)",add:"native number (contracted) + 명",ex:"학생 세 명 = three students"},{when:"Counting people politely (honorific)",add:"native number (contracted) + 분",ex:"손님 두 분 = two guests"},{when:"Counting any animal",add:"native number (contracted) + 마리",ex:"개 한 마리 = one dog"}],examples:[{ko:"교실에 학생 다섯 명이 있어요.",romanization:"gyosil-e haksaeng daseot myeong-i isseoyo.",en:"There are five students in the classroom.",note:"명 is the neutral counter for people: classmates, friends, ordinary people."},{ko:"할머니 두 분이 오셨어요.",romanization:"halmeoni du bun-i osyeosseoyo.",en:"Two grandmothers came.",note:"분 is the honorific counter, right for elders; the verb is also honorific 오셨어요."},{ko:"우리 집에 강아지 세 마리가 있어요.",romanization:"uri jib-e gangaji se mari-ga isseoyo.",en:"We have three puppies at home.",note:"마리 counts animals of any kind, including pets like puppies."},{ko:"어항에 물고기 네 마리가 있어요.",romanization:"eohang-e mulgogi ne mari-ga isseoyo.",en:"There are four fish in the tank.",note:"Even fish use 마리 — it covers all animals, not just four-legged ones."}],keyPoint:{label:"명 vs. 분 = respect level",body:"명 and 분 both count people; the difference is politeness. Use 분 for guests, elders, teachers, and customers; use 명 for friends, kids, or a general headcount. Never use 분 for yourself. Animals always take 마리, never 명."},pronunciation:"",drill:{instruction:"Choose the right person/animal counter (명, 분, or 마리) and complete the phrase.",model:"(고양이 / 두) → 고양이 두 마리",items:["(선생님 / 세, honorific) → ___","(아이 / 네) → ___","(새 / 다섯) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"손님 두 명이 왔어요 (said to a customer's face)",right:"손님 두 분이 오셨어요",explanation:"English has no built-in 'respect counter,' so learners default to 명 for everyone. For guests, elders, and customers, use the honorific 분 to sound polite."}},{title:"Counting Objects, Drinks, Books, and Sheets: 개 / 잔 / 권 / 장",func:"Different kinds of things take different counters. 개 = general objects, 잔 = cups/glasses of a drink, 권 = books and notebooks, 장 = flat sheets (paper, tickets, photos).",formTable:[{when:"General object (default counter)",add:"number + 개",ex:"사탕 두 개 = two candies"},{when:"A drink served in a cup or glass",add:"number + 잔",ex:"물 한 잔 = one glass of water"},{when:"A book or notebook",add:"number + 권",ex:"책 세 권 = three books"},{when:"A thin flat sheet",add:"number + 장",ex:"종이 네 장 = four sheets of paper"}],examples:[{ko:"지우개 두 개만 주세요.",romanization:"jiugae du gae-man juseyo.",en:"Please give me just two erasers.",note:"개 is the all-purpose counter for everyday objects like erasers."},{ko:"녹차 한 잔 더 주세요.",romanization:"nokcha han jan deo juseyo.",en:"One more cup of green tea, please.",note:"잔 counts drinks served in a cup or glass — tea, coffee, water, juice."},{ko:"이번 달에 책 두 권을 읽었어요.",romanization:"ibeon dar-e chaek du gwon-eul ilgeosseoyo.",en:"I read two books this month.",note:"권 is the counter for books and notebooks."},{ko:"사진 다섯 장을 뽑았어요.",romanization:"sajin daseot jang-eul ppobasseoyo.",en:"I printed five photos.",note:"장 counts flat, thin things: photos, paper, tickets."}],keyPoint:{label:"개 is the safe default",body:"When you don't know the right counter, 개 usually works for objects. But the specific counters are expected for their categories: drinks → 잔, books → 권, flat sheets → 장. Saying 책 세 개 is understandable but sounds like a learner; 책 세 권 is correct."},pronunciation:"권 ends in a clear final ㄴ; before 을 it links as 권을 [궈늘 / gwo-neul]. 장을 links as [장을 / jang-eul] with the ㅇ carrying over.",drill:{instruction:"Pick the correct counter (개, 잔, 권, or 장) for each noun and complete the phrase.",model:"(커피 / 두) → 커피 두 잔",items:["(공책 / 세) → ___","(우표 / 네) → ___","(가방 / 한) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"책 세 개, 커피 두 개",right:"책 세 권, 커피 두 잔",explanation:"English uses 'three books / two coffees' with no special unit, so learners overuse 개. Match the counter to the category: 권 for books, 잔 for cups of a drink."}},{title:"Counting Clothes and Machines: 벌 / 대",func:"벌 counts sets of clothing — an outfit, a suit, pajamas. 대 counts machines and vehicles — cars, phones, computers, TVs, anything mechanical.",formTable:[{when:"A set or suit of clothes",add:"number + 벌",ex:"옷 한 벌 = one set of clothes"},{when:"Another outfit or suit",add:"number + 벌",ex:"양복 두 벌 = two suits"},{when:"A car or vehicle",add:"number + 대",ex:"차 한 대 = one car"},{when:"A machine or appliance",add:"number + 대",ex:"컴퓨터 두 대 = two computers"}],examples:[{ko:"새 옷 한 벌 샀어요.",romanization:"sae ot han beol sasseoyo.",en:"I bought one new set of clothes.",note:"벌 counts a full outfit, not single garments; 한 from 하나 before the counter."},{ko:"양복 두 벌이 필요해요.",romanization:"yangbok du beol-i piryohaeyo.",en:"I need two suits.",note:"벌 also counts suits and other matched sets of clothing."},{ko:"우리 집에 차가 한 대 있어요.",romanization:"uri jib-e cha-ga han dae isseoyo.",en:"We have one car at home.",note:"대 is the counter for vehicles; 가 attaches after the counter phrase."},{ko:"교실에 컴퓨터가 세 대 있어요.",romanization:"gyosil-e keompyuteo-ga se dae isseoyo.",en:"There are three computers in the classroom.",note:"대 counts machines and appliances — computers, phones, washing machines."}],keyPoint:{label:"벌 = clothing sets, 대 = machines",body:"Use 벌 for clothes you wear as a set (옷 한 벌, 양복 두 벌) and 대 for anything mechanical (차 한 대, 핸드폰 두 대, 세탁기 한 대). Don't reach for 개 here — 차 한 개 or 옷 한 개 sounds clearly wrong to native ears."},pronunciation:"",drill:{instruction:"Pick the correct counter (벌 or 대) for each noun and complete the phrase.",model:"(차 / 두) → 차 두 대",items:["(옷 / 한) → ___","(핸드폰 / 세) → ___","(양복 / 두) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"차 한 개, 옷 두 개",right:"차 한 대, 옷 두 벌",explanation:"Learners fall back on the all-purpose 개, but vehicles and machines need 대 and sets of clothing need 벌. 차 한 개 and 옷 한 개 sound clearly wrong to native speakers."}},{title:"Counting Time and Frequency: 번 / 시간 / 살",func:"번 counts the number of times something happens (once, twice). 시간 counts hours of duration. 살 counts years of age. All three take native numbers, contracted before the counter.",formTable:[{when:"Number of times (occurrences)",add:"number + 번",ex:"한 번 = one time / once"},{when:"Repeating an action",add:"number + 번",ex:"세 번 봤어요 = I saw it three times"},{when:"Duration in hours",add:"number + 시간",ex:"두 시간 = two hours"},{when:"A person's age",add:"number + 살",ex:"스무 살 = twenty years old"}],examples:[{ko:"그 영화를 두 번 봤어요.",romanization:"geu yeonghwa-reul du beon bwasseoyo.",en:"I watched that movie twice.",note:"번 counts how many times; 둘 contracts to 두 before it."},{ko:"하루에 세 번 약을 먹어요.",romanization:"haru-e se beon yag-eul meogeoyo.",en:"I take medicine three times a day.",note:"번 with a native number gives the frequency of an action."},{ko:"어제 다섯 시간 잤어요.",romanization:"eoje daseot sigan jasseoyo.",en:"I slept five hours yesterday.",note:"시간 counts a span of hours; it is different from 시 (clock o'clock)."},{ko:"우리 딸은 일곱 살이에요.",romanization:"uri ttar-eun ilgop sal-ieyo.",en:"My daughter is seven years old.",note:"살 is the everyday counter for age, always with native numbers."}],keyPoint:{label:"시간 (duration) vs. 시 (o'clock)",body:"세 시간 means 'three hours' (how long), but 세 시 means 'three o'clock' (what time). Both use native numbers but answer different questions. 번 counts occurrences (한 번 = once), and 살 counts age (열 살 = ten years old)."},pronunciation:"",drill:{instruction:"Choose the right counter (번, 시간, or 살) and complete the phrase.",model:"(영화 / 두, times) → 두 번",items:["(잠 / 여덟, hours) → ___","(아들 / 다섯, age) → ___","(여행 / 세, times) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"세 시간 (used for 'at three o'clock')",right:"세 시에 (at three o'clock) vs. 세 시간 (for three hours)",explanation:"English 'three hours' and 'three o'clock' look alike, so learners mix up 시간 and 시. Use 시간 for how long something lasts and 시 for the time on the clock."}}],a=[{title:"연음 (Linking): A Final Consonant Slides onto the Next Vowel",func:"When a syllable ends in a 받침 (final consonant) and the next syllable starts with ㅇ (a silent placeholder before a vowel), the 받침 jumps over and is pronounced as the first sound of that next syllable. The spelling never changes — only the sound. This is why 한국어 is said [한구거]: the ㄱ of 국 links onto the 어.",formTable:[{when:"받침 + a vowel starting with ㅇ",add:"the 받침 moves into the empty ㅇ slot",ex:"한국어 → [한구거]"},{when:"a single 받침 (ㄱ/ㄴ/ㄹ/ㅁ/ㅂ/ㅅ etc.) before a vowel",add:"that consonant becomes the next syllable's onset",ex:"음악 → [으막], 옷이 → [오시]"},{when:"a double 받침 (겹받침) before a vowel",add:"the first consonant stays, the second links over",ex:"읽어요 → [일거요], 앉아요 → [안자요]"},{when:"받침 ㅇ before a vowel",add:"no linking; ㅇ [ŋ] stays where it is",ex:"고양이 → [고양이] (the ㅇ does not move)"}],examples:[{ko:"한국어 발음이 어려워요.",romanization:"han-gu-geo ba-reu-mi eo-ryeo-wo-yo",en:"Korean pronunciation is hard.",note:"한국어 → [한구거] (국's ㄱ links to 어); 발음이 → [바르미] (음's ㅁ links to 이)."},{ko:"이 단어를 읽어 보세요.",romanization:"i da-neo-reul il-geo bo-se-yo",en:"Try reading this word.",note:"단어 → [다너] (단's ㄴ links to 어); 읽어 → [일거] (겹받침 ㄺ splits: ㄹ stays, ㄱ links)."},{ko:"제 이름이 뭐예요?",romanization:"je i-reu-mi mwo-ye-yo",en:"What is my name? (lit.)",note:"이름이 → [이르미]: 름's ㅁ links onto 이. Compare 이름 alone, said [이름]."},{ko:"옷이 정말 예뻐요.",romanization:"o-si jeong-mal ye-ppeo-yo",en:"The clothes are really pretty.",note:"옷이 → [오시]: the ㅅ 받침 links onto 이 and is pronounced as a clear ㅅ, not [옫이]."}],keyPoint:{label:"Spelling vs. sound",body:"Linking changes only the sound, never the spelling. You always write 한국어, but you say [한구거]. Reading each syllable as written (han-guk-eo) sounds robotic; native speakers link automatically."},pronunciation:"한국어 is [한구거], not [한국어]. Listen for the 받침 sliding right: 옷이 [오시], 꽃이 [꼬치], 밥을 [바블].",drill:{instruction:"Say each phrase aloud, then write the linked pronunciation in brackets.",model:"음악을 들어요 → [으마글 드러요]",items:["옷이 예뻐요 → [____]","책을 읽어요 → [____]","이름이 뭐예요 → [____]"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"han-guk · eo (a hard stop between the syllables)",right:"han-gu-geo (the ㄱ glides into the next syllable)",explanation:"English speakers tend to pronounce every syllable as written and insert a small gap before a vowel. In Korean the 받침 must flow onto the following vowel with no break, so 한국어 sounds like [한구거]."}},{title:"경음화 (Tensing): A Plain Consonant Tenses After a Stop",func:"When a 받침 pronounced as a stop [ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅂ] is immediately followed by a plain consonant ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ, ㅅ, or ㅈ, that following consonant becomes its tense (된소리) version: ㄱ→ㄲ, ㄷ→ㄸ, ㅂ→ㅃ, ㅅ→ㅆ, ㅈ→ㅉ. This is why 학교 is said [학꾜].",formTable:[{when:"[ㄱ] 받침 + ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅂ/ㅅ/ㅈ",add:"the following consonant tenses",ex:"학교 → [학꾜], 책상 → [책쌍]"},{when:"[ㄷ] 받침 (ㄷ,ㅅ,ㅈ,ㅊ,ㅌ all sound [ㄷ]) + a plain consonant",add:"the following consonant tenses",ex:"숟가락 → [숟까락], 듣고 → [듣꼬]"},{when:"[ㅂ] 받침 (ㅂ,ㅍ sound [ㅂ]) + a plain consonant",add:"the following consonant tenses",ex:"입국 → [입꾹], 잡지 → [잡찌]"},{when:"a plain consonant after ㄴ/ㄹ/ㅁ/ㅇ",add:"usually NO tensing (those are not stops)",ex:"안경 → [안경] (stays plain)"}],examples:[{ko:"학교에서 한국어를 배워요.",romanization:"hak-kkyo-e-seo han-gu-geo-reul bae-wo-yo",en:"I learn Korean at school.",note:"학교 → [학꾜]: the [ㄱ] of 학 tenses the following ㄱ into ㄲ."},{ko:"책상 위에 책이 있어요.",romanization:"chaek-ssang wi-e chae-gi i-sseo-yo",en:"There is a book on the desk.",note:"책상 → [책쌍]: the [ㄱ] 받침 tenses ㅅ to ㅆ. (책이 → [채기] is linking, a separate rule.)"},{ko:"식당에서 같이 먹어요.",romanization:"sik-ttang-e-seo ga-chi meo-geo-yo",en:"Let's eat together at the restaurant.",note:"식당 → [식땅]: the [ㄱ] 받침 tenses ㄷ to ㄸ."},{ko:"이 발음을 잡지 못했어요.",romanization:"i ba-reu-meul jap-jji mo-tae-sseo-yo",en:"I couldn't catch this pronunciation.",note:"잡지 → [잡찌]: the [ㅂ] 받침 tenses ㅈ to ㅉ."}],keyPoint:{label:"Tense only after a stop",body:"Tensing happens after the three stop sounds [ㄱ][ㄷ][ㅂ], not after the sonorants ㄴ/ㄹ/ㅁ/ㅇ. Compare 학교 [학꾜] (tensing) with 안경 [안경] (no tensing) — the 받침 in 안경 is ㄴ, which is not a stop."},pronunciation:"학교 is [학꾜], not [학교]. The ㄲ is the same tense sound as in 꼬리/꽃 — tighten the throat, no puff of air.",drill:{instruction:"Read each word and write the tensed pronunciation in brackets.",model:"학생 → [학쌩]",items:["식당 → [____]","입구 → [____]","듣고 → [____]"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"hak-gyo (a soft, voiced 'g' after the k)",right:"hak-kkyo (a sharp, tense ㄲ)",explanation:"English speakers often voice the consonant after a stop, turning ㄱ into a soft 'g'. Korean does the opposite: the consonant tenses to ㄲ. So 학교 is [학꾜], with a tight, unaspirated ㄲ, never a relaxed 'g'."}},{title:"비음화 (Nasalization): A Stop Becomes Nasal Before ㄴ/ㅁ",func:"When a 받침 pronounced as a stop [ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅂ] is followed by the nasal consonant ㄴ or ㅁ, the stop changes into the matching nasal: ㄱ→ㅇ[ŋ], ㄷ→ㄴ, ㅂ→ㅁ. The mouth assimilates to the nasal that follows. This is why 한국말 is said [한궁말].",formTable:[{when:"[ㄱ] 받침 + ㄴ/ㅁ",add:"ㄱ → ㅇ [ŋ]",ex:"한국말 → [한궁말], 작년 → [장년]"},{when:"[ㄷ] 받침 + ㄴ/ㅁ",add:"ㄷ → ㄴ",ex:"듣는 → [든는], 끝나다 → [끈나다]"},{when:"[ㅂ] 받침 + ㄴ/ㅁ",add:"ㅂ → ㅁ",ex:"입니다 → [임니다], 감사합니다 → [감사함니다]"},{when:"a stop + a non-nasal consonant",add:"no nasalization (a different rule applies)",ex:"학교 → [학꾜] (tensing, not nasal)"}],examples:[{ko:"한국말 발음이 자연스러워요.",romanization:"han-gung-mal ba-reu-mi ja-yeon-seu-reo-wo-yo",en:"Your Korean pronunciation is natural.",note:"한국말 → [한궁말]: the [ㄱ] of 국 becomes ㅇ before ㅁ."},{ko:"감사합니다.",romanization:"gam-sa-ham-ni-da",en:"Thank you.",note:"합니다 → [함니다]: the [ㅂ] of 합 becomes ㅁ before ㄴ — the single most common nasalization learners hear."},{ko:"저는 학생입니다.",romanization:"jeo-neun hak-ssaeng-im-ni-da",en:"I am a student.",note:"입니다 → [임니다]: [ㅂ] → ㅁ before ㄴ; this is how every formal '-입니다' ending is actually said. (학생 → [학쌩] is tensing.)"},{ko:"지금 노래를 듣는 중이에요.",romanization:"ji-geum no-rae-reul deun-neun jung-i-e-yo",en:"I'm listening to a song right now.",note:"듣는 → [든는]: the [ㄷ] of 듣 becomes ㄴ before ㄴ."}],keyPoint:{label:"-입니다 / -습니다",body:"The polite formal endings -ㅂ니다 / -습니다 are ALWAYS nasalized in speech: 입니다 [임니다], 습니다 [슴니다]. Saying [입니다] with a real ㅂ sounds unnatural — the ㅂ always becomes ㅁ before the ㄴ."},pronunciation:"한국말 is [한궁말]: the ㄱ disappears into a nasal ㅇ. Hum it — the air should go through your nose on the 궁.",drill:{instruction:"Read each word and write its nasalized pronunciation in brackets.",model:"국물 → [궁물]",items:["감사합니다 → [____]","끝나요 → [____]","작년 → [____]"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"hap-ni-da (a hard 'p' before the n)",right:"ham-ni-da (the ㅂ turns into ㅁ)",explanation:"English keeps the stop and nasal separate ('hap-nip'). Korean blends them: a stop before ㄴ/ㅁ assimilates into the matching nasal. So 합니다 is [함니다] and 한국말 is [한궁말], never with the original stop intact."}},{title:"격음화 (Aspiration): ㅎ Merges with a Plain Stop into an Aspirate",func:"When ㅎ meets a plain consonant ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ, or ㅈ (in either order — ㅎ before the consonant, or a [ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅂ] 받침 before ㅎ), the two merge into a single aspirated consonant: ㄱ→ㅋ, ㄷ→ㅌ, ㅂ→ㅍ, ㅈ→ㅊ. This is why 좋다 is said [조타].",formTable:[{when:"ㅎ 받침 + ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅈ",add:"merge into ㅋ/ㅌ/ㅊ",ex:"좋다 → [조타], 좋고 → [조코], 좋지 → [조치]"},{when:"[ㄱ] 받침 + ㅎ",add:"ㄱ + ㅎ → ㅋ",ex:"축하 → [추카], 백화점 → [배콰점]"},{when:"[ㄷ] 받침 + ㅎ",add:"ㄷ + ㅎ → ㅌ",ex:"맏형 → [마텽]"},{when:"[ㅂ] 받침 + ㅎ",add:"ㅂ + ㅎ → ㅍ",ex:"입학 → [이팍], 잡화 → [자퐈]"}],examples:[{ko:"발음이 좋다고 들었어요.",romanization:"ba-reu-mi jo-ta-go deu-reo-sseo-yo",en:"I heard your pronunciation is good.",note:"좋다 → [조타]: ㅎ 받침 + ㄷ merge into the aspirate ㅌ."},{ko:"입학을 축하해요!",romanization:"i-pa-geul chu-ka-hae-yo",en:"Congratulations on starting school!",note:"입학 → [이팍]: [ㅂ] + ㅎ → ㅍ; 축하 → [추카]: [ㄱ] + ㅎ → ㅋ. Two aspirations in one sentence."},{ko:"이 단어를 어떻게 읽어요?",romanization:"i da-neo-reul eo-tteo-ke il-geo-yo",en:"How do you read this word?",note:"어떻게 → [어떠케]: ㅎ 받침 + ㄱ → ㅋ. (읽어요 → [일거요] is separate linking.)"},{ko:"발음이 정말 좋고 자연스러워요.",romanization:"ba-reu-mi jeong-mal jo-ko ja-yeon-seu-reo-wo-yo",en:"The pronunciation is really good and natural.",note:"좋고 → [조코]: ㅎ 받침 + ㄱ merge into the aspirate ㅋ."}],keyPoint:{label:"Aspiration vs. tensing",body:"Both reshape a plain stop, but the trigger differs. ㅎ + ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅂ/ㅈ gives an ASPIRATED consonant (ㅋ/ㅌ/ㅍ/ㅊ, with a puff of air): 좋다 [조타]. A stop + plain consonant gives a TENSE consonant (ㄲ/ㄸ/ㅃ, no air): 학교 [학꾜]. Hold your hand to your mouth — aspiration puffs, tensing doesn't."},pronunciation:"좋다 is [조타], not [졷다] or [조따]. The ㅌ is aspirated like the ㅌ in 타요 — release a clear puff of air, not a tense ㄸ.",drill:{instruction:"Read each word and write the aspirated pronunciation in brackets.",model:"좋다 → [조타]",items:["축하 → [____]","입학 → [____]","어떻게 → [____]"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"jot-da (keeping the ㅎ and ㄷ as two separate hard sounds)",right:"jo-ta (the ㅎ and ㄷ merge into one aspirated ㅌ)",explanation:"English speakers try to pronounce both the ㅎ and the following stop, or drop the ㅎ entirely as a tense [조따]. In Korean the two fuse into a single aspirated consonant with a puff of air, so 좋다 is [조타] and 축하 is [추카]."}}],i=[{title:"안 + verb/adjective — simple negation",func:`Put 안 directly in front of a verb or adjective to make it negative. This is the everyday spoken negation: "I don't / it isn't." It states a choice or a plain fact, with no sense of being blocked or unable.`,formTable:[{when:"Most verbs and adjectives",add:"안 in front of the word",ex:"가요 → 안 가요 (don't go)"},{when:"Adjective",add:"안 in front",ex:"바빠요 → 안 바빠요 (not busy)"},{when:"하다 action verbs (공부하다 등)",add:"안 splits IN: noun + 안 + 해요",ex:"공부해요 → 공부 안 해요 (don't study)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 술을 안 마셔요.",romanization:"jeoneun sureul an masyeoyo.",en:"I don't drink alcohol.",note:"안 sits right before 마셔요; a stated habit, not an inability. Pronounced [an ma.syeo.yo], no tensing."},{ko:"오늘은 학교에 안 가요.",romanization:"oneureun hakgyoe an gayo.",en:"I'm not going to school today.",note:"A simple choice or plan; 안 negates 가요 directly."},{ko:"이 영화는 안 무서워요.",romanization:"i yeonghwaneun an museowoyo.",en:"This movie isn't scary.",note:"안 also negates adjectives (무섭다 = scary), describing a plain quality."},{ko:"저는 운동 안 해요.",romanization:"jeoneun undong an haeyo.",en:"I don't exercise.",note:"With 운동하다, 안 goes BETWEEN the noun and 해요: 운동 안 해요, not 안 운동해요."}],keyPoint:{label:"안 vs 못",body:"안 = don't or won't, by choice or as a plain fact (안 가요, I'm not going). 못 = can't, blocked by an outside obstacle (못 가요, I can't go). If you simply chose not to, use 안; if something stops you, use 못."},pronunciation:"안 is pronounced [an] and, unlike 못, it does NOT tense the next sound. 안 가요 stays [an ga.yo], 안 봐요 stays [an bwa.yo]. Before a verb starting with ㅁ/ㄴ the nasal flows smoothly: 안 마셔요 → [an ma.syeo.yo]. No double-consonant tensing anywhere.",drill:{instruction:"Make each sentence negative using 안. Watch the 하다 verbs, where 안 goes between the noun and 해요.",model:"매워요 (spicy) → 안 매워요",items:["커피를 마셔요 → ____","요리해요 → ____","집에 가요 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"안 공부해요",right:"공부 안 해요",explanation:"English speakers treat 공부하다 as one block and put 안 in front of the whole thing. But noun + 하다 verbs split: the noun stays, then 안, then 해요. So it's 공부 안 해요, 운동 안 해요, 요리 안 해요."}},{title:"못 + verb — cannot, because something blocks you",func:"Put 못 in front of a verb to say you CANNOT do it — not because you don't want to, but because something blocks or prevents you (no time, no skill, no permission, an obstacle). 못 is used with action verbs, not with descriptive adjectives.",formTable:[{when:"Most action verbs",add:"못 in front of the verb",ex:"가요 → 못 가요 (can't go)"},{when:"하다 action verbs",add:"못 splits IN: noun + 못 + 해요",ex:"수영해요 → 수영 못 해요 (can't swim)"},{when:"NOT with plain adjectives",add:"use 안 instead",ex:"안 바빠요 (○) / 못 바빠요 (✗)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘 너무 바빠서 못 가요.",romanization:"oneul neomu bappaseo mot gayo.",en:"I can't go today because I'm too busy.",note:"못 = blocked by an outside reason (being busy), not a choice. Pronounced [몯 까요]."},{ko:"저는 매운 음식을 못 먹어요.",romanization:"jeoneun maeun eumsigeul mot meogeoyo.",en:"I can't eat spicy food.",note:"My body can't handle it — an inability, so 못, not 안."},{ko:"저는 수영 못 해요.",romanization:"jeoneun suyeong mot haeyo.",en:"I can't swim.",note:"하다 verb: 못 goes between 수영 and 해요. It's a lack of skill, hence 못."},{ko:"시끄러워서 잠을 못 잤어요.",romanization:"sikkeureowoseo jameul mot jasseoyo.",en:"I couldn't sleep because it was noisy.",note:"An external obstacle (noise) prevented the action — a classic 못 situation, here in the past."}],keyPoint:{label:"못 + verb only (never adjectives)",body:"못 means 'unable to DO an action,' so it attaches to action verbs (못 가요, 못 먹어요). You can't be 'unable to be tall' or 'unable to be busy' — for adjectives use 안 (안 커요, 안 바빠요). 못 바빠요 is wrong."},pronunciation:"못 is written with final ㅅ but pronounced [몯]. Before a vowel it links: 못 와요 → [모 돠요]. Before ㄱ/ㄷ/ㅂ/ㅈ it TENSES the next sound: 못 가요 → [몯 까요], 못 봐요 → [몯 빠요]. That tensing is the audible signal of 못 — and the clearest way to tell it apart from 안.",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite using 못 to mean 'cannot.' For 하다 verbs, put 못 between the noun and 해요.",model:"가요 → 못 가요",items:["매운 거를 먹어요 → ____","운전해요 → ____","피아노를 쳐요 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 안 수영해요 (when you mean you literally can't swim)",right:"저는 수영 못 해요",explanation:"English 'I don't swim' and 'I can't swim' look similar, so learners default to 안. But when an ability or obstacle is involved, Korean wants 못. 안 수영해요 means you choose not to; 수영 못 해요 means you're unable."}},{title:"-지 않아요 — the longer / written negation",func:"Attach -지 않아요 to a verb or adjective stem for the 'long form' of negation. It means the same as 안, but is a little more formal and is common in writing, careful speech, and longer sentences. It works smoothly with every verb and adjective, including 하다 verbs (no splitting).",formTable:[{when:"Verb or adjective stem",add:"stem + -지 않아요",ex:"가다 → 가지 않아요 (don't go)"},{when:"Adjective",add:"stem + -지 않아요",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘지 않아요 (not busy)"},{when:"하다 verbs (no splitting!)",add:"공부하 + -지 않아요",ex:"공부하다 → 공부하지 않아요 (don't study)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 고기를 먹지 않아요.",romanization:"jeoneun gogireul meokji anayo.",en:"I don't eat meat.",note:"먹다 → 먹지 않아요; same meaning as 안 먹어요 but sounds a touch more formal or written."},{ko:"그 가게는 비싸지 않아요.",romanization:"geu gageneun bissaji anayo.",en:"That shop isn't expensive.",note:"Works with adjectives too: 비싸다 → 비싸지 않아요 (= 안 비싸요)."},{ko:"저는 담배를 피우지 않아요.",romanization:"jeoneun dambaereul piuji anayo.",en:"I don't smoke.",note:"A clean way to state a habit you don't do; common on forms and in polite explanations."},{ko:"요즘 운동하지 않아요.",romanization:"yojeum undonghaji anayo.",en:"I don't exercise these days.",note:"With 하다 verbs there is NO split: 운동하 + 지 않아요. Compare the spoken 운동 안 해요."}],keyPoint:{label:"안 vs -지 않아요",body:"They mean the same thing. 안 먹어요 = 먹지 않아요 (I don't eat). 안 is short and spoken; -지 않아요 is longer and slightly more formal or written. Bonus: 하다 verbs DON'T split with -지 않아요 (운동하지 않아요), unlike 운동 안 해요."},pronunciation:"않아요 is pronounced [아나요] — the ㅎ in 않 is silent and the ㄴ links into the following vowel: 가지 않아요 → [가지 아나요]. The ㅎ never makes its own sound here.",drill:{instruction:"Convert each verb or adjective to the long negative with -지 않아요.",model:"가다 → 가지 않아요",items:["먹다 (eat) → ____","바쁘다 (busy) → ____","청소하다 (clean) → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"먹지 안 아요 / 먹지 않어요",right:"먹지 않아요",explanation:"Two errors are common: splitting 안 out as a separate word, and misspelling the ending. The fixed ending is one unit, -지 않아요 [지 아나요]. Don't insert 안 separately, and don't write 않어요."}},{title:`N이/가 없어요 — absence / "don't have"`,func:"없어요 is the verb for 'there isn't / I don't have.' It is the opposite of 있어요 (there is / have). The noun takes the subject marker 이/가, and 없어요 covers both 'don't possess' and 'isn't present / doesn't exist.'",formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"noun + 이 없어요",ex:"시간 → 시간이 없어요 (no time)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"noun + 가 없어요",ex:"차 → 차가 없어요 (no car)"},{when:"Opposite (positive)",add:"있어요",ex:"시간이 있어요 (have time)"}],examples:[{ko:"지금 시간이 없어요.",romanization:"jigeum sigani eopseoyo.",en:"I don't have time right now.",note:"시간 ends in a consonant, so 시간이; 없어요 = don't have. Pronounced [시가니 업써요]."},{ko:"저는 차가 없어요.",romanization:"jeoneun chaga eopseoyo.",en:"I don't have a car.",note:"차 ends in a vowel, so the marker is 가. 없어요 here means 'don't possess.'"},{ko:"냉장고에 우유가 없어요.",romanization:"naengjanggoe uyuga eopseoyo.",en:"There's no milk in the fridge.",note:"Same 없어요 also means 'isn't there / doesn't exist' — the absence of a thing in a place. 우유 ends in a vowel → 우유가."},{ko:"오늘은 약속이 없어요.",romanization:"oneureun yaksogi eopseoyo.",en:"I don't have any plans today.",note:"없어요 negates having something (약속 = plans/an appointment); 약속 ends in a consonant → 약속이."}],keyPoint:{label:"없어요 vs 안 + verb",body:"For NOT HAVING or NOT EXISTING, use 없어요, never 안 있어요. 안 있어요 is wrong. So 'no time' = 시간이 없어요 (○), not 시간이 안 있어요 (✗). 안 negates actions and qualities; 없어요 negates existence and possession."},pronunciation:"없어요 is pronounced [업써요]: the ㅂㅅ cluster keeps the ㅂ [p] sound, and the ㅅ links to the next vowel as a tense [ss]. So 시간이 없어요 → [시가니 업써요].",drill:{instruction:"Say you don't have each thing using 이/가 없어요. Choose the right marker by the noun's last sound.",model:"시간 → 시간이 없어요",items:["돈 (money) → ____","우산 (umbrella) → ____","친구 (friend) → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"시간이 안 있어요",right:"시간이 없어요",explanation:"English 'I don't have time' tempts learners to negate 있어요 with 안. Korean has a dedicated negative verb 없어요 for this; 안 있어요 sounds wrong to native ears. Memorize 있어요 ↔ 없어요 as a pair."}},{title:"Boundary check — 안 vs 못 vs 아니에요 vs 싫어요",func:"Korean splits the English word 'not' across several forms, and choosing the wrong one sounds off. This note sorts them: 안 = don't/won't (choice or fact), 못 = can't (blocked), 아니에요 = 'it's not [a noun]' (identity), 싫어요 = 'I don't like it / I don't want to' (dislike or refusal). Match the form to the meaning, not to the English word.",formTable:[{when:"Choice / plain fact about an action or quality",add:"안 + verb/adjective",ex:"안 가요 (I'm not going)"},{when:"Inability / outside obstacle",add:"못 + verb",ex:"못 가요 (I can't go)"},{when:"'It is NOT [a noun]' (identity)",add:"noun + 이/가 아니에요",ex:"학생이 아니에요 (not a student)"},{when:"Dislike / 'I don't want to'",add:"싫어요 (or 안 좋아요)",ex:"숙제가 싫어요 (I hate homework)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘은 안 가요.",romanization:"oneureun an gayo.",en:"I'm not going today.",note:"A choice or plan — plain negation of the action, so 안."},{ko:"바빠서 오늘은 못 가요.",romanization:"bappaseo oneureun mot gayo.",en:"I can't go today because I'm busy.",note:"Something blocks the action (being busy), so 못, not 안."},{ko:"이건 제 가방이 아니에요.",romanization:"igeon je gabangi anieyo.",en:"This isn't my bag.",note:"Saying a noun is NOT something uses 아니에요, never 안 — 안 가방이에요 is wrong."},{ko:"저는 매운 음식이 싫어요.",romanization:"jeoneun maeun eumsigi sireoyo.",en:"I don't like spicy food.",note:"Dislike, not inability: 싫어요. (못 먹어요 would mean 'I can't eat it.')"}],keyPoint:{label:"One English 'not,' four Korean choices",body:"Ask what kind of 'not' it is. Action by choice → 안. Action blocked → 못. 'It's not [a noun]' → 아니에요. 'I don't like / don't want' → 싫어요. Picking by feeling, not by the English word, is the whole skill of this chapter."},pronunciation:"아니에요 → [아니에요], four clear syllables, no linking trick. 싫어요 → [시러요]: the ㅎ in 싫 is silent and the ㄹ links into the vowel, so it sounds like [시러요], not [실허요].",drill:{instruction:"Choose 안, 못, 아니에요, or 싫어요 for each meaning.",model:"'I can't swim' → 수영 못 해요",items:["'I'm not going today (I just won't)' → 오늘은 ____ 가요","'This isn't my phone' → 이건 제 폰이 ____","'I don't like vegetables' → 저는 채소가 ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이건 제 가방 안 이에요 / 저는 그게 안 좋아요 (meaning 'I hate it')",right:"이건 제 가방이 아니에요 / 저는 그게 싫어요",explanation:"Two traps: (1) you can't negate 이에요/예요 with 안 — to say a noun is NOT something, use 아니에요. (2) For strong dislike or refusal, 싫어요 is the natural word; 안 좋아요 just means 'not good,' which is milder and can sound evasive."}}],s=[{title:'N이었어요 / N였어요 — "It was an N" (past of 이다)',func:'Says what something was in the past — an identity, role, or category that was true before now: "It was my birthday," "She was a student," "Yesterday was the weekend." It is just 이다 put into the past polite form.',formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"N + 이었어요",ex:"학생 → 학생이었어요 (was a student)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 였어요 (shortened from 이었어요)",ex:"친구 → 친구였어요 (was a friend)"},{when:"Casual 반말 past",add:"N이었어 / N였어",ex:"주말이었어 / 친구였어 (was the weekend / was a friend)"},{when:"Negative past (was not N)",add:"N이/가 아니었어요",ex:"학생이 아니었어요 (wasn't a student)"}],examples:[{ko:"어제는 제 생일이었어요.",romanization:"eojeneun je saengirieosseoyo.",en:"Yesterday was my birthday.",note:"생일 ends in a consonant (ㄹ), so it takes the full 이었어요."},{ko:"그 사람은 제 친구였어요.",romanization:"geu sarameun je chinguyeosseoyo.",en:"That person was my friend.",note:"친구 ends in a vowel, so 이었어요 shortens to 였어요."},{ko:"작년에는 저도 학생이었어요.",romanization:"jangnyeoneneun jeodo haksaengieosseoyo.",en:"Last year I was a student too.",note:"학생 ends in a consonant → 이었어요; 작년 (last year) sets the past time."},{ko:"그건 제 우산이 아니었어요.",romanization:"geugeon je usani anieosseoyo.",en:"That wasn't my umbrella.",note:"Past negative uses 아니다 → 아니었어요, never 였어요."}],keyPoint:{label:"이었어요 vs 였어요 — just a sound rule",body:"Both are the same past form of 이다. After a consonant, keep the full 이었어요 (학생이었어요). After a vowel, 이었어요 shrinks to 였어요 (친구였어요). The meaning is identical — only the spelling and sound change, based on the noun's final letter."},pronunciation:"학생이었어요 links across the boundary and sounds like [학쌩이어써요]; 친구였어요 sounds like [친구여써요].",drill:{instruction:"Add the correct past form (이었어요 or 였어요) to each noun.",model:"학생 → 학생이었어요",items:["가수 → ____ (was a singer)","선생님 → ____ (was a teacher)","주말 → ____ (was the weekend)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"학생였어요",right:"학생이었어요",explanation:"Learners grab 였어요 because it is shorter, but 였어요 only attaches to vowel-final nouns. 학생 ends in a consonant, so it needs the full 이었어요. Choose the ending by the noun's last letter, not by what is easier to say."}},{title:`N인데 / N인데도 — "it's N, and… / even though it's N"`,func:`Attaches to a noun to set the scene before the main clause: "It's the weekend, so…/and…" (인데), or to mark a mild contrast "even though it's N" (인데도). 인데 softens a statement and leads into the rest; 인데도 adds "despite that."`,formTable:[{when:"Noun (any ending) + background / lead-in",add:"N + 인데",ex:"학생인데… (I'm a student, so/and…)"},{when:`Noun + "even though it's N"`,add:"N + 인데도",ex:"주말인데도 (even though it's the weekend)"},{when:"Past noun background",add:"N + 이었는데 / 였는데",ex:"친구였는데 (we were friends, but…)"},{when:`Polite sentence-ender (soft "it's N, but…")`,add:"N + 인데요",ex:"여기 제 자리인데요. (Um, this is my seat…)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘은 주말인데 어디 갈까요?",romanization:"oneureun jumarinde eodi galkkayo?",en:"It's the weekend today, so where shall we go?",note:"인데 gives the noun (주말) as background leading into a suggestion."},{ko:"저는 처음인데 잘 부탁해요.",romanization:"jeoneun cheoeuminde jal butakaeyo.",en:"It's my first time, so please be kind to me.",note:"처음 (first time) + 인데 sets up the polite request."},{ko:"주말인데도 회사에 가요.",romanization:"jumarindedo hoesae gayo.",en:"Even though it's the weekend, I go to work.",note:'인데도 = 인데 + 도, marking contrast: "despite it being the weekend."'},{ko:"같은 반인데 이름을 몰라요.",romanization:"gateun baninde ireumeul mollayo.",en:"We're in the same class, but I don't know their name.",note:`인데 here leans contrastive — "it's the case that…, yet…"`}],keyPoint:{label:"인데 vs 인데도",body:`인데 is neutral: it just hands you a noun-fact as a lead-in, and context decides whether it means "so" or "but." 인데도 is clearly concessive — it always means "even though it's N," and the next clause is something surprising. Add 도 only when you want the firm "despite" feeling.`},pronunciation:"인데 sounds like [인데] with no change; 인데도 sounds like [인데도], the 도 stays plain.",drill:{instruction:"Turn each noun into a background clause with 인데, then say the 인데도 version.",model:"방학 → 방학인데 / 방학인데도",items:["생일 → ____ / ____","월요일 → ____ / ____","처음 → ____ / ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"학생는데",right:"학생인데",explanation:"인데 comes from 이다, so a noun needs the 이 (학생 + 이 + ㄴ데 → 학생인데). Learners drop the 인 and stick 는데 straight onto the noun as if it were a verb. 는데 is for verbs and adjectives; 인데 is the noun version."}},{title:'N이라서 / 라서 — "because it is N"',func:`Gives a reason that is a noun fact: "Because it's the weekend, the shop is closed," "Because I'm a beginner, it's hard." It is the 이다 version of the reason ending -아서/어서.`,formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"N + 이라서",ex:"학생이라서 (because I'm a student)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 라서",ex:"친구라서 (because we're friends)"},{when:"More formal / written-sounding reason",add:"N + 이어서 / 여서",ex:"주말이어서 (because it's the weekend)"},{when:"Reason for a noun negative",add:"N이/가 아니라서",ex:"가족이 아니라서 (because I'm not family)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘은 주말이라서 가게가 쉬어요.",romanization:"oneureun jumariraseo gagega swieoyo.",en:"Because it's the weekend today, the shop is closed.",note:"주말 ends in a consonant → 이라서."},{ko:"저는 초보라서 천천히 할게요.",romanization:"jeoneun chobolaseo cheoncheonhi halgeyo.",en:"Because I'm a beginner, I'll go slowly.",note:"초보 ends in a vowel → 라서."},{ko:"평일이라서 사람이 없어요.",romanization:"pyeongiriraseo sarami eopseoyo.",en:"Because it's a weekday, there's nobody around.",note:"평일 (consonant-final) + 이라서 gives the reason."},{ko:"가족이 아니라서 못 들어가요.",romanization:"gajogi aniraseo mot deureogayo.",en:"Because I'm not family, I can't go in.",note:"Noun negative reason: 아니다 → 아니라서."}],keyPoint:{label:"이라서 vs verb -아서/어서",body:`For verbs and adjectives you say -아서/어서 (먹어서, 좋아서). For nouns you must run the reason through 이다 → 이라서/라서. So "because it's a student" is 학생이라서, never 학생어서. Reach for the noun form whenever the reason is a noun.`},pronunciation:"학생이라서 sounds like [학쌩이라서]. The [쌩] is just how 학생 is always said — the ㄱ받침 of 학 tenses the 생; 이라서 itself adds no new tensing.",drill:{instruction:"Give the reason form (이라서 or 라서) for each noun.",model:"주말 → 주말이라서",items:["겨울 → ____ (because it's winter)","친구 → ____ (because we're friends)","처음 → ____ (because it's the first time)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"주말이어서 가게가 쉬어요 (said casually)",right:"주말이라서 가게가 쉬어요",explanation:"이어서/여서 is correct grammar but sounds formal or bookish in speech. In everyday conversation Koreans overwhelmingly say 이라서/라서. Both are right; pick 이라서/라서 to sound natural when you're talking."}},{title:'N이니까 / 니까 — "since it is N, so…"',func:`Gives a reason with a "since you know this, therefore let's…" feel — strong for backing up a command, suggestion, or decision: "Since it's the weekend, let's study," "Since it's my birthday, I'll treat you." It is the 이다 version of -(으)니까.`,formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"N + 이니까",ex:"주말이니까 (since it's the weekend)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 니까",ex:"친구니까 (since we're friends)"},{when:"Leading a suggestion / command",add:"N이니까 + ~해요/하세요",ex:"주말이니까 같이 공부해요 (it's the weekend, so let's study together)"},{when:"Noun negative reason",add:"N이/가 아니니까",ex:"애기가 아니니까 (since you're not a baby)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘은 주말이니까 같이 공부해요.",romanization:"oneureun jumarinikka gachi gongbuhaeyo.",en:"Since it's the weekend today, let's study together.",note:"주말 (consonant-final) + 이니까 backs up the suggestion."},{ko:"우리는 친구니까 괜찮아요.",romanization:"urineun chingunikka gwaenchanayo.",en:"Since we're friends, it's okay.",note:"친구 ends in a vowel → 니까."},{ko:"오늘은 제 생일이니까 제가 살게요.",romanization:"oneureun je saengirinikka jega salgeyo.",en:"Since it's my birthday today, I'll treat you.",note:"이니까 supports the speaker's decision (제가 살게요)."},{ko:"평일이니까 일찍 자세요.",romanization:"pyeongirinikka iljjik jaseyo.",en:"Since it's a weekday, please go to bed early.",note:"이니까 + command 자세요 — a natural pairing that 이라서 avoids."}],keyPoint:{label:"이니까 vs 이라서",body:`Both mean "because it's N," but 이니까 is the one you use before commands and suggestions (조용히 하세요, 같이 공부해요) and when the reason is shared or obvious. 이라서 is more about plain cause and sounds off before a command. Rule of thumb: order or invite → 이니까; just explain → 이라서.`},pronunciation:"주말이니까 sounds like [주마리니까]; 까 is written with ㄲ, so it is always tense.",drill:{instruction:`Make a "since it's N, so…" clause using 이니까 / 니까.`,model:"주말 → 주말이니까",items:["방학 → ____ (since it's vacation)","가수 → ____ (since she's a singer)","오늘 → ____ (since it's today)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"주말이라서 같이 공부해요 (before a let's-suggestion)",right:"주말이니까 같이 공부해요",explanation:`Before a suggestion (같이 공부해요) or command (하세요), Koreans use 이니까, not 이라서. 이라서 is for plainly stating a cause; it sounds unnatural right before "let's…" or an order. When your sentence ends in a suggestion or command, switch the noun reason to 이니까/니까.`}},{title:'N이라도 / 라도 — "even just an N / at least N"',func:`Marks a noun as a not-ideal-but-acceptable fallback: "Let's study even just one hour," "Have at least some water." It says "this isn't the best option, but it'll do." It is the 이다 form behind the 라도 fallback marker.`,formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"N + 이라도",ex:"한 시간이라도 (even just one hour)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 라도",ex:"커피라도 (coffee, at least)"},{when:"Offering a fallback choice",add:"N이라도 + 할까요? / 드세요",ex:"물이라도 드세요 (at least have some water)"},{when:'"even on/at N" (time noun)',add:"시간 noun + 이라도/라도",ex:"주말이라도 (even on the weekend, at least)"}],examples:[{ko:"주말이니까 한 시간이라도 같이 공부해요.",romanization:"jumarinikka han siganirado gachi gongbuhaeyo.",en:"Since it's the weekend, let's study together even if just for one hour.",note:"시간 ends in a consonant → 이라도; one hour isn't much, but it's still worth it."},{ko:"바쁘면 커피라도 한잔해요.",romanization:"bappeumyeon keopirado hanjanhaeyo.",en:"If you're busy, let's at least grab a coffee.",note:"커피 ends in a vowel → 라도; coffee as a quick fallback plan."},{ko:"배고프면 빵이라도 드세요.",romanization:"baegopeumyeon ppangirado deuseyo.",en:"If you're hungry, at least have some bread.",note:"빵 (consonant-final) + 이라도 offers a fallback food."},{ko:"시간 없으면 아침에라도 조금 해요.",romanization:"sigan eopseumyeon achimerado jogeum haeyo.",en:"If you have no time, do a little even just in the morning.",note:'아침에 + 라도 — "even at/in the morning" as a fallback time.'}],keyPoint:{label:'라도 = "not the best, but okay"',body:"이라도/라도 picks a noun that isn't your first choice but is good enough: 한 시간이라도 (even just one hour), 커피라도 (at least a coffee). Use 이라도 after a consonant and 라도 after a vowel — the same sound split you see in 이라서/라서 and 이니까/니까."},pronunciation:"한 시간이라도 sounds like [한 시가니라도]; 커피라도 sounds like [커피라도], no special change.",drill:{instruction:"Add the fallback form (이라도 or 라도) to each noun.",model:"한 시간 → 한 시간이라도",items:["물 → ____ (at least some water)","빵 → ____ (at least some bread)","커피 → ____ (at least a coffee)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"한 시간라도",right:"한 시간이라도",explanation:"시간 ends in a consonant (ㄴ), so it needs 이라도, not 라도. Learners default to the shorter 라도 everywhere, but 라도 only goes on vowel-final nouns (커피라도). Check the noun's last letter first, just like with 이라서 and 이니까."}}],r=[{title:"저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요 — Saying How Many People Are in Your Family",func:'To tell someone how big your family is, politely. 저희 is the humble form of 우리 ("our"), used to be modest in front of a listener, and 명 is the counter for people. You name the group (저희 가족은 = "my family"), then give the count with a native-Korean number + 명 + 이에요.',formTable:[{when:"Counting people, use a native-Korean number + 명",add:"한/두/세/네/다섯… + 명",ex:"네 명 (four people), 다섯 명 (five people)"},{when:"Numbers 1–4 change shape right before a counter",add:"하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네",ex:"두 명, 세 명, 네 명 (NOT 둘 명 / 셋 명)"},{when:"Closing the sentence after 명",add:"명 + 이에요 (명 ends in a consonant, so 이에요)",ex:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요."}],examples:[{ko:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",romanization:"jeohui gajogeun ne myeong-ieyo.",en:"There are four people in my family.",note:'저희 (humble "our") + 명 is the standard polite way to give family size; the native number 네 is used before 명.'},{ko:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요. 부모님, 형, 누나, 그리고 저요.",romanization:"jeohui gajogeun daseot myeong-ieyo. bumonim, hyeong, nuna, geurigo jeoyo.",en:"There are five people in my family: my parents, older brother, older sister, and me.",note:'Shows how to break the number down into members; 저요 ("and me") naturally closes the list.'},{ko:"우리 가족은 세 명이에요.",romanization:"uri gajogeun se myeong-ieyo.",en:"There are three people in my family.",note:'우리 (plain "our") is fine with friends; 저희 is the more polite choice with strangers or elders.'},{ko:"가족이 몇 명이에요?",romanization:"gajogi myeot myeong-ieyo?",en:"How many people are in your family?",note:'몇 명 ("how many people?") is the question your answer responds to.'}],keyPoint:{label:"명 vs. 분 for counting people",body:"명 is the neutral people-counter and is correct for your own family: 저희 가족은 네 명이에요. When you respectfully count other people (elders, guests), use 분 instead: 손님 세 분 (three guests). You always count your OWN family with 명, never 분, because you don't elevate your own in-group when speaking to others."},pronunciation:"가족은 links across the 받침: 가족 + 은 → [가조근]. The same happens in 가족이 → [가조기]. The final ㄱ of 가족 slides onto the following vowel.",drill:{instruction:"Say your family size. Fill in the native number that matches the count in parentheses, keeping 명이에요.",model:"(3) → 저희 가족은 세 명이에요.",items:["(4) 저희 가족은 ___ 명이에요.","(2) 저희 가족은 ___ 명이에요.","(5) 저희 가족은 ___ 명이에요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저희 가족은 넷 명이에요.",right:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",explanation:"Native numbers 1–4 take a special shortened shape before a counter: 하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네. English speakers tend to keep the standalone form (넷) and say 넷 명, but before 명 it must become 네 명."}},{title:"형 / 오빠 / 누나 / 언니 — Older-Sibling Words That Depend on YOUR Gender",func:'To name an older brother or older sister. Korean has no single word for "older brother" or "older sister" — the word you pick depends on whether YOU (the speaker) are male or female. A man and a woman use completely different words for the very same person.',formTable:[{when:"A male speaker's older brother",add:"형",ex:"(남자가) 우리 형 (my older brother)"},{when:"A female speaker's older brother",add:"오빠",ex:"(여자가) 우리 오빠 (my older brother)"},{when:"A male speaker's older sister",add:"누나",ex:"(남자가) 우리 누나 (my older sister)"},{when:"A female speaker's older sister",add:"언니",ex:"(여자가) 우리 언니 (my older sister)"}],examples:[{ko:"(남자) 우리 형은 회사에 다녀요.",romanization:"(namja) uri hyeong-eun hoesa-e danyeoyo.",en:"(male speaker) My older brother works at a company.",note:"A boy calls his older brother 형; 오빠 would be wrong here for a male speaker."},{ko:"(여자) 우리 오빠는 키가 커요.",romanization:"(yeoja) uri oppa-neun kiga keoyo.",en:"(female speaker) My older brother is tall.",note:"A girl calls her older brother 오빠 — same person, different word from a boy's 형."},{ko:"(남자) 누나가 두 명 있어요.",romanization:"(namja) nuna-ga du myeong isseoyo.",en:"(male speaker) I have two older sisters.",note:"A boy's older sister is 누나; the counter 명 is used, with 두 (not 둘) before it."},{ko:"(여자) 언니랑 같이 살아요.",romanization:"(yeoja) eonni-rang gachi sarayo.",en:"(female speaker) I live with my older sister.",note:`A girl's older sister is 언니; ~랑 ("with") is common in casual talk about family.`}],keyPoint:{label:"The word encodes the SPEAKER's gender, not the sibling's",body:"Picture a 2×2 grid. Older brother: 형 (said by a male) vs. 오빠 (said by a female). Older sister: 누나 (said by a male) vs. 언니 (said by a female). The same older brother is 형 to his younger brother and 오빠 to his younger sister at the same time. Younger siblings have no gendered split — they are just 동생 (남동생 = younger brother, 여동생 = younger sister)."},pronunciation:"오빠's ㅃ is a tense, slightly held stop. 언니 → [언니] keeps both ㄴ sounds clear. 형은 → [형은], with the ㅇ (ng) carrying over softly; no tensing here.",drill:{instruction:"Choose the correct older-sibling word for the speaker's gender given in parentheses.",model:"(female, older brother) → 오빠",items:["(male, older brother) → 우리 ___은/는 의사예요.","(female, older sister) → 우리 ___이/가 요리해요.","(male, older sister) → 우리 ___은/는 학생이에요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"(a male speaker) 우리 오빠는 키가 커요.",right:"(a male speaker) 우리 형은 키가 커요.",explanation:'English has one word, "older brother," so learners grab whichever Korean word they heard first — often 오빠 from K-pop. But 오빠 is only for FEMALE speakers. A male speaker must say 형. Always ask first: who is talking, a man or a woman?'}},{title:"부모님 / 아버지 / 어머니 — Respectful Words for Parents",func:'To talk about your parents respectfully. These are the polite nouns for family elders. 부모님 = "parents" (with the honorific suffix ~님), 아버지 = "father," 어머니 = "mother." They contrast with the casual 아빠 (dad) and 엄마 (mom) that children use at home.',formTable:[{when:"Both parents together, respectfully",add:"부모 + 님 (honorific suffix)",ex:"부모님 (one's parents)"},{when:"Father — respectful vs. casual",add:"아버지 (respectful) / 아빠 (casual)",ex:"저희 아버지 vs. 우리 아빠"},{when:"Mother — respectful vs. casual",add:"어머니 (respectful) / 엄마 (casual)",ex:"저희 어머니 vs. 우리 엄마"},{when:"Someone else's parents (extra polite)",add:"아버님 / 어머님 (add ~님)",ex:"친구 어머님 (a friend's mother)"}],examples:[{ko:"저희 부모님은 부산에 사세요.",romanization:"jeohui bumonim-eun busan-e saseyo.",en:"My parents live in Busan.",note:"부모님 + the honorific verb 사세요 (from 살다) elevate the parents — the natural pairing for elders."},{ko:"저희 아버지는 선생님이세요.",romanization:"jeohui abeoji-neun seonsaengnim-iseyo.",en:"My father is a teacher.",note:"아버지 (not 아빠) is the respectful choice when speaking to people outside the family; ~세요 honors him."},{ko:"어머니께서 김치를 만드세요.",romanization:"eomeoni-kkeseo gimchi-reul mandeuseyo.",en:"My mother makes kimchi.",note:"어머니 pairs naturally with the honorific subject marker 께서 and 만드세요 (from 만들다)."},{ko:"아빠, 우리 어디 가요?",romanization:"appa, uri eodi gayo?",en:"Dad, where are we going?",note:"A child speaking straight to their own father at home uses casual 아빠 — natural in-family address, unlike the formal 아버지."}],keyPoint:{label:"아버지/어머니 (respectful) vs. 아빠/엄마 (casual)",body:'Use 아버지/어머니 when you describe your parents TO other people, in writing, or in any polite setting — they show respect. Use 아빠/엄마 to ADDRESS your own parents at home or among close family, like English "Dad/Mom" vs. "father/mother." 부모님 always carries ~님 and has no casual short form.'},pronunciation:"부모님은 links the 받침: 부모님 + 은 → [부모니믄], the ㅁ carrying onto 은. 선생님이세요 → [선생니미세요] in the same way.",drill:{instruction:"Replace the casual word with the respectful word to make each sentence polite for outsiders.",model:"우리 아빠는 회사원이에요. → 저희 아버지는 회사원이세요.",items:["우리 엄마는 간호사예요. → 저희 ___는 간호사세요.","우리 아빠는 운동을 좋아해요. → 저희 ___는 운동을 좋아하세요.","우리 엄마 아빠는 집에 있어요. → 저희 ___은 집에 계세요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저희 엄마는 의사예요. (in a polite self-introduction)",right:"저희 어머니는 의사세요.",explanation:'English uses "my mom" comfortably even in semi-formal speech, so learners say 엄마 everywhere. In Korean, 엄마/아빠 sound childish when you introduce your family to strangers or elders. Switch to 어머니/아버지 (and 부모님 with ~님) to be polite.'}},{title:"N께서 V-(으)시- — Honoring a Family Elder Who Does the Action",func:"To honor the person doing the action — usually a family elder like a parent or grandparent. Two pieces work together: 께서 replaces the subject marker 이/가 (or 은/는) to lift up the subject, and the verb takes the honorific infix ~(으)시~, which surfaces as ~세요/~으세요 in polite speech.",formTable:[{when:"Marking an honored subject",add:"이/가 → 께서 (은/는 → 께서는)",ex:"아버지가 → 아버지께서"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel + honorific",add:"stem + ~세요",ex:"가다 → 가세요, 오다 → 오세요"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant + honorific",add:"stem + ~으세요",ex:"읽다 → 읽으세요, 앉다 → 앉으세요"},{when:"Special honorific verbs (must be memorized)",add:"있다→계시다, 먹다→드시다/잡수시다, 자다→주무시다",ex:"할머니께서 주무세요 (Grandma is sleeping)"}],examples:[{ko:"할아버지께서 신문을 읽으세요.",romanization:"harabeoji-kkeseo sinmun-eul ilgeuseyo.",en:"My grandfather reads the newspaper.",note:"할아버지께서 marks the honored subject; 읽다 (consonant stem) → 읽으세요 with the ~으세요 form."},{ko:"어머니께서 방에 계세요.",romanization:"eomeoni-kkeseo bang-e gyeseyo.",en:"My mother is in the room.",note:"있다 has the special honorific 계시다, not 있으세요 — use 계세요 for an elder's location/presence."},{ko:"아버지께서 저녁을 드세요.",romanization:"abeoji-kkeseo jeonyeok-eul deuseyo.",en:"My father is eating dinner.",note:"드시다 → 드세요 is the conventional lexical honorific of 먹다. 먹으세요 also contains the honorific -(으)시-, but 드세요 is the more respectful default in this family context."},{ko:"할머니께서 일찍 주무세요.",romanization:"halmeoni-kkeseo iljjik jumuseyo.",en:"My grandmother goes to bed early.",note:"자다 becomes the honorific 주무시다 → 주무세요; a fully irregular special form to memorize."}],keyPoint:{label:"Honor the subject in the verb",body:"The verb ending ~(으)시~ is the core way to honor the person doing the action. The honorific subject marker 께서 often accompanies it in careful speech, while ordinary 이/가 can also appear with an honorific verb depending on context. You can be humble about yourself with 저희 while honoring an elder: 저희 어머니가 만드세요 and 저희 어머니께서 만드세요 are both possible."},pronunciation:"읽으세요 → [일그세요]: the ㄺ cluster splits, ㄱ moving onto the 으. 계세요 → [게세요] in casual speech (the ㅖ often flattens to ㅔ). 께서 keeps its tense ㄲ at the start.",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each plain sentence to honor the elder: change 이/가 to 께서 and put the verb in the honorific ~(으)세요 form (watch for special verbs).",model:"아버지가 신문을 읽어요. → 아버지께서 신문을 읽으세요.",items:["어머니가 방에 있어요. → 어머니___ 방에 ___.","할아버지가 저녁을 먹어요. → 할아버지___ 저녁을 ___.","할머니가 일찍 자요. → 할머니___ 일찍 ___."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"아버지께서 저녁을 먹어요.",right:"아버지께서 저녁을 드세요.",explanation:"Learners may add 께서 but leave the verb plain. The verb should honor the elder too, and conventional lexical honorifics such as 드시다, 계시다, and 주무시다 are the natural choices in careful family descriptions."}}],h=[{title:"Symptom frame — body part + 이/가 아프다",func:"Say what hurts at the clinic. Korean makes the body part the grammatical subject, marked with 이/가, and 아프다 (to hurt / be sore) describes its state. This is your answer when the doctor asks 어디가 아파요? (Where does it hurt?).",formTable:[{when:"Body part ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 이 아파요",ex:"목 → 목이 아파요 (my throat hurts)"},{when:"Body part ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 아파요",ex:"머리 → 머리가 아파요 (my head hurts)"},{when:"Asking 'where does it hurt?'",add:"어디 + 가 아파요?",ex:"어디가 아파요? (where does it hurt?)"},{when:"Past tense (it hurt)",add:"아프 → 아팠어요",ex:"어제부터 배가 아팠어요 (my stomach has hurt since yesterday)"}],examples:[{ko:"머리가 아파요.",romanization:"meoriga apayo.",en:"My head hurts.",note:"머리 ends in a vowel, so it takes 가; 아프 + 아요 → 아파요."},{ko:"목이 아프고 열도 나요.",romanization:"mogi apeugo yeoldo nayo.",en:"My throat hurts and I also have a fever.",note:"목 has a 받침, so it takes 이; 열이 나다 is the natural way to say 'have a fever.'"},{ko:"어디가 아파요?",romanization:"eodiga apayo?",en:"Where does it hurt?",note:"The doctor's standard question; 어디 + 가 makes the sore spot the subject."},{ko:"배가 너무 아파서 왔어요.",romanization:"baega neomu apaseo wasseoyo.",en:"My stomach hurts so much that I came in.",note:"배 + 가; 너무 (so / too much) intensifies the pain."}],keyPoint:{label:"The body part is the SUBJECT, not the object",body:"Use 아프다 for physical body pain (머리가 아파요). Korean makes the body part the subject with 이/가 — it 'hurts' by itself. There is no 'I have a pain' verb that takes the body part as an object, so never mark it with 을/를 here."},drill:{instruction:"Make a sentence saying this body part hurts. Choose 이 or 가 by the final sound.",model:"목 → 목이 아파요.",items:["머리 → ___","배 → ___","이 (tooth) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"나는 머리를 아파요.",right:"(저는) 머리가 아파요.",explanation:"English says 'I hurt my head' with the head as object (를). In Korean the body part is the SUBJECT (가), and 아프다 describes its state. Never mark the body part with 를 here."}},{title:"V-아/어야 해요 — must / have to (medical instruction)",func:"Give or receive a medical instruction: something you must do. The doctor uses it for what's necessary (약을 먹어야 해요 — you have to take the medicine), and you use it for what you must do (쉬어야 해요 — I have to rest).",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem has ㅏ/ㅗ vowel",add:"+ 아야 해요",ex:"받다 → 받아야 해요 (have to get / receive)"},{when:"Verb stem has other vowel",add:"+ 어야 해요",ex:"먹다 → 먹어야 해요 (have to take / eat)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 → 해야 해요",ex:"검사하다 → 검사해야 해요 (have to get tested)"},{when:"Casual spoken alternative",add:"-아/어야 돼요",ex:"쉬어야 돼요 (have to rest)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 약을 하루에 세 번 먹어야 해요.",romanization:"i yageul harue se beon meogeoya haeyo.",en:"You have to take this medicine three times a day.",note:"먹다 → 먹어야 해요; the doctor's dosage instruction (하루에 세 번 = three times a day)."},{ko:"푹 쉬어야 해요.",romanization:"puk swieoya haeyo.",en:"You have to rest well.",note:"쉬다 → 쉬어야 해요; 푹 is the set adverb that pairs with resting."},{ko:"내일 다시 와야 해요?",romanization:"naeil dasi waya haeyo?",en:"Do I have to come back tomorrow?",note:"오다 → 와야 해요; a yes/no question about a required visit."},{ko:"피 검사를 받아야 해요.",romanization:"pi geomsareul badaya haeyo.",en:"You have to get a blood test.",note:"받다 has ㅏ, so 받아야 해요; 피 검사 = blood test."}],keyPoint:{label:"해요 vs 돼요",body:"-아/어야 해요 and -아/어야 돼요 both mean 'must / have to.' 돼요 is a touch more conversational and very common in speech; 해요 feels slightly more standard. In a clinic you will hear both."},drill:{instruction:"Turn the verb into a 'have to ___' medical instruction with -아/어야 해요.",model:"약을 먹다 → 약을 먹어야 해요.",items:["푹 쉬다 → ___","물을 많이 마시다 → ___","검사하다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"약을 먹어 해요.",right:"약을 먹어야 해요.",explanation:"Learners drop the 야. The obligation meaning lives entirely in -야; without it the sentence is broken. Always keep -아/어 + 야 + 해요/돼요 together."}},{title:"A/V-아서/어서 — symptom causing a result",func:"Link a symptom to its consequence: 'because X, Y happened.' This is your go-to for explaining the visit: 배가 아파서 왔어요 (my stomach hurt, so I came). The 아서/어서 clause is the cause; the second clause is the result.",formTable:[{when:"Stem has ㅏ/ㅗ vowel",add:"+ 아서",ex:"아프다 → 아파서 (because it hurts)"},{when:"Stem has other vowel",add:"+ 어서",ex:"먹다 → 먹어서 (because I ate)"},{when:"하다 words",add:"하 → 해서",ex:"심하다 → 심해서 (because it's bad / severe)"},{when:"Symptom 나다 (fever / cough)",add:"나 + 서 → 나서",ex:"열이 나서 (because I have a fever)"}],examples:[{ko:"배가 아파서 병원에 왔어요.",romanization:"baega apaseo byeongwone wasseoyo.",en:"My stomach hurt, so I came to the clinic.",note:"아프다 → 아파서; the classic reason-for-visit sentence."},{ko:"열이 나서 학교에 못 갔어요.",romanization:"yeori naseo hakgyoe mot gasseoyo.",en:"I had a fever, so I couldn't go to school.",note:"열이 나다 → 나서; a symptom leading to an inability."},{ko:"기침이 심해서 잠을 못 잤어요.",romanization:"gichimi simhaeseo jameul mot jasseoyo.",en:"My cough was bad, so I couldn't sleep.",note:"심하다 → 심해서; severity causing a result."},{ko:"콧물이 나서 휴지가 필요해요.",romanization:"konmuri naseo hyujiga piryohaeyo.",en:"I have a runny nose, so I need tissues.",note:"콧물이 나다 → 나서; an everyday cold symptom."}],keyPoint:{label:"아서/어서 vs 때문에",body:"Both mean 'because.' Use V-아서/어서 to chain a state or action straight into its result in one flowing clause. Use N 때문에 when the cause is a NOUN. With 아서/어서 you cannot put a command or suggestion in the result clause — for that use -(으)니까."},drill:{instruction:"Join the symptom and result with -아서/어서.",model:"배가 아프다 / 병원에 오다 → 배가 아파서 병원에 왔어요.",items:["열이 나다 / 회사에 못 가다 → ___","목이 아프다 / 말을 못 하다 → ___","기침이 심하다 / 병원에 가다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"열이 나서 약을 드세요.",right:"열이 나니까 약을 드세요.",explanation:"You can't end an 아서/어서 result clause with a command or suggestion. 'You have a fever, so take medicine' needs -(으)니까, not 아서/어서."}},{title:"N 때문에 — because of (a symptom / illness)",func:"State a NOUN cause: 'because of my cold / fever / headache.' When the reason is a noun — a named symptom or illness — use N 때문에: 감기 때문에 일을 못 했어요 (I couldn't work because of a cold).",formTable:[{when:"Cause is a noun",add:"N + 때문에",ex:"감기 → 감기 때문에 (because of a cold)"},{when:"Sentence-initial 'that's why'",add:"그래서 / 그것 때문에",ex:"그래서 왔어요 (that's why I came)"},{when:"Person as cause",add:"사람 N + 때문에",ex:"동생 때문에 (because of my sibling)"},{when:"To say 'thanks to' (positive)",add:"N + 덕분에",ex:"약 덕분에 (thanks to the medicine)"}],examples:[{ko:"감기 때문에 목이 아파요.",romanization:"gamgi ttaemune mogi apayo.",en:"My throat hurts because of a cold.",note:"감기 is a noun, so 때문에 fits perfectly."},{ko:"열 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",romanization:"yeol ttaemune jameul mot jasseoyo.",en:"I couldn't sleep because of the fever.",note:"열 (fever) as a noun cause of a result."},{ko:"두통 때문에 일을 못 했어요.",romanization:"dutong ttaemune ireul mot haesseoyo.",en:"I couldn't work because of a headache.",note:"두통 (headache) noun + 때문에."},{ko:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 자요.",romanization:"gichim ttaemune jameul mot jayo.",en:"I can't sleep because of the cough.",note:"기침 (cough) noun + 때문에; a line straight from the clinic."}],keyPoint:{label:"때문에 (negative cause) vs 덕분에 (positive cause)",body:"Both attach to a noun. 때문에 marks a cause, usually neutral or negative (감기 때문에 — because of a cold). 덕분에 means 'thanks to' and is positive (약 덕분에 나았어요 — I got better thanks to the medicine)."},drill:{instruction:"Make a 'because of N' sentence with N 때문에.",model:"감기 / 목이 아프다 → 감기 때문에 목이 아파요.",items:["열 / 잠을 못 자다 → ___","두통 / 일을 못 하다 → ___","감기 / 학교에 못 가다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"아파서 때문에 병원에 왔어요.",right:"아파서 병원에 왔어요. / 통증 때문에 병원에 왔어요.",explanation:"때문에 attaches to a NOUN only. You cannot stack a verb-form (아파서) in front of 때문에. Either use 아파서 alone, or put a noun like 통증 (pain) before 때문에."}},{title:"A/V-(으)니까 — since this is so, do this (advice)",func:"Give advice or instruction grounded in a fact: 'since X is the case, do / let's do Y.' Unlike 아서/어서, the second clause CAN be a command or suggestion — perfect for a doctor's advice: 열이 나니까 약을 드세요 (since you have a fever, take the medicine).",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ 으니까",ex:"먹다 → 먹으니까 (since you eat / ate)"},{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ 니까",ex:"아프다 → 아프니까 (since it hurts)"},{when:"ㄹ stem (ㄹ drops)",add:"ㄹ 빠짐 + 니까",ex:"만들다 → 만드니까 (since you make it)"},{when:"나다 (fever / cough)",add:"나 + 니까 → 나니까",ex:"열이 나니까 (since you have a fever)"}],examples:[{ko:"열이 계속 나니까 물을 많이 마시세요.",romanization:"yeori gyesok nanikka mureul mani masiseyo.",en:"Since the fever keeps up, drink a lot of water.",note:"나다 → 나니까; result clause is a command (마시세요) — only 니까 allows this."},{ko:"목이 아프니까 따뜻한 물을 드세요.",romanization:"mogi apeunikka ttatteuthan mureul deuseyo.",en:"Since your throat hurts, drink warm water.",note:"아프다 → 아프니까; advice based on a stated fact."},{ko:"약을 먹었으니까 좀 쉬세요.",romanization:"yageul meogeosseunikka jom swiseyo.",en:"Since you've taken the medicine, get some rest.",note:"Past tense + 으니까 → 먹었으니까; the result is a suggestion."},{ko:"오늘은 병원이 문을 닫았으니까 내일 가요.",romanization:"oneureun byeongwoni muneul dadasseunikka naeil gayo.",en:"Since the clinic is closed today, let's go tomorrow.",note:"닫다 → 닫았으니까; result is a suggestion (가요), which 니까 permits."}],keyPoint:{label:"니까 lets you give a command; 아서/어서 doesn't",body:"The big difference from 아서/어서 is the second clause. With -(으)니까 the result can be a command (-세요), a suggestion (-아요/-(으)ㅂ시다), or a request. So whenever a doctor's 'because…, do this' sentence ends in an instruction, you must use 니까, not 아서/어서."},drill:{instruction:"Join the fact and the advice with -(으)니까, keeping the command in the second clause.",model:"열이 나다 / 약을 먹다 → 열이 나니까 약을 드세요.",items:["목이 아프다 / 말을 많이 하지 않다 → ___","기침이 심하다 / 병원에 가다 → ___","약을 먹었다 / 쉬다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"열이 나서 약을 드세요.",right:"열이 나니까 약을 드세요.",explanation:"Both 아서/어서 and 니까 mean 'because,' so learners reach for 아서/어서 by habit. But when the result is a command or suggestion (약을 드세요), only -(으)니까 is grammatical."}},{title:"열이 나다 / 기침이 나다 — symptom 'comes out' with 나다",func:"Describe symptoms that arise — fever, cough, runny nose — with the symptom noun + 이/가 + 나다. This is the most common way to list these cold symptoms at the clinic; forms such as 열이 있어요 can also describe their presence.",formTable:[{when:"Symptom noun ends in a consonant",add:"+ 이 나다",ex:"열 → 열이 나요 (I have a fever)"},{when:"Symptom noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 나다",ex:"재채기 → 재채기가 나요"},{when:"Cough",add:"기침 + 이 나다",ex:"기침이 나요 (I have a cough)"},{when:"Past tense (had the symptom)",add:"나 → 났어요",ex:"어제 열이 났어요 (I had a fever yesterday)"}],examples:[{ko:"아침부터 열이 나요.",romanization:"achimbuteo yeori nayo.",en:"I've had a fever since this morning.",note:"열 + 이 나다; 아침부터 (since this morning) sets the start time."},{ko:"기침이 나고 콧물도 나요.",romanization:"gichimi nago konmuldo nayo.",en:"I have a cough and a runny nose too.",note:"기침이 나다 + 콧물이 나다 chained with -고; the classic cold combo."},{ko:"열도 나고 목도 아파요.",romanization:"yeoldo nago mokdo apayo.",en:"I have a fever and my throat also hurts.",note:"나다 for fever, but 아프다 for the throat — different verbs."},{ko:"어제 열이 많이 났어요.",romanization:"eoje yeori mani nasseoyo.",en:"I had a high fever yesterday.",note:"나다 → 났어요 (past); 많이 = a lot / high."}],keyPoint:{label:"나다 symptoms vs 아프다 pain",body:"Symptoms that 'come out' — 열(fever), 기침(cough), 콧물(runny nose) — use 이/가 나다. A body part that aches — 머리, 목, 배 — uses 이/가 아프다. Mixing them up (열이 아파요 ✗, 머리가 나요 ✗) is the giveaway, so memorize which noun pairs with which verb.",label2:""},drill:{instruction:"Pick the right verb: symptom that 'comes out' → 나요, body part that aches → 아파요.",model:"열 → 열이 나요. / 머리 → 머리가 아파요.",items:["기침 → ___","목 → ___","콧물 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"열이 있어요 is always wrong.",right:"열이 나요. / 열도 있어요?",explanation:"열이 나요 is the most useful default when reporting that a fever has developed. 열이 있어요 or 열도 있어요? can naturally describe or ask about the presence of a fever, especially during a medical check. Teach the preferred symptom pairing without banning the presence expression."}},{title:"못 + V — can't do it (symptom blocking an action)",func:"Say you were unable to do something because of a symptom: 'I couldn't ___.' Put 못 right before the verb: 잠을 못 자요 (I can't sleep), 학교에 못 갔어요 (I couldn't go to school). This is how you describe what the illness stopped you from doing.",formTable:[{when:"Plain verb",add:"못 + 동사",ex:"자다 → 못 자요 (can't sleep)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"못 + V-았/었어요",ex:"가다 → 못 갔어요 (couldn't go)"},{when:"하다 verb (못 goes inside)",add:"N + 못 + 해요",ex:"일하다 → 일을 못 해요 (can't work)"},{when:"Written / formal alternative",add:"V + -지 못해요",ex:"자다 → 자지 못해요 (cannot sleep)"}],examples:[{ko:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 자요.",romanization:"gichim ttaemune jameul mot jayo.",en:"I can't sleep because of the cough.",note:"못 sits right before 자요; 잠을 자다 → 잠을 못 자요."},{ko:"열이 나서 학교에 못 갔어요.",romanization:"yeori naseo hakgyoe mot gasseoyo.",en:"I had a fever, so I couldn't go to school.",note:"Past: 가다 → 못 갔어요."},{ko:"목이 아파서 밥을 못 먹어요.",romanization:"mogi apaseo babeul mot meogeoyo.",en:"My throat hurts, so I can't eat.",note:"못 + 먹어요; a common reason given at the clinic."},{ko:"어제 일을 못 했어요.",romanization:"eoje ireul mot haesseoyo.",en:"I couldn't work yesterday.",note:"일하다 splits: 일을 못 했어요 — 못 goes before 하다."}],keyPoint:{label:"못 = couldn't (blocked); 안 = didn't (chose not to)",body:"못 means you were UNABLE — something (a symptom) prevented you: 못 잤어요 (couldn't sleep). 안 means you simply didn't / chose not to: 안 잤어요 (didn't sleep). At the clinic you almost always want 못, because the illness is what stopped you."},drill:{instruction:"Say you couldn't do this because of a symptom, using 못 + verb.",model:"자다 → 잠을 못 자요.",items:["밥을 먹다 → ___","학교에 가다 (past) → ___","일하다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"잠을 못 안 자요.",right:"잠을 못 자요.",explanation:"Don't stack 못 and 안 together — pick one. Use 못 for 'unable to' (a symptom blocked you). Also keep 못 directly before the verb (못 자요), and for 하다 verbs split the noun off: 일을 못 해요, not 못 일해요."}}],l=[{title:"N이/가 있어요 — saying what a home has",func:"Use 있어요 to say a room or feature exists in a home. It is the everyday way to describe what an apartment or house comes with: a balcony, an elevator, parking, and so on.",formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 이 있어요",ex:"방 → 방이 있어요 (there is a room)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 있어요",ex:"엘리베이터 → 엘리베이터가 있어요 (there is an elevator)"},{when:"Yes/no question",add:"있어요? (rising tone)",ex:"주차장이 있어요? (Is there parking?)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 집에 방이 세 개 있어요.",romanization:"i jibe bangi se gae isseoyo.",en:"This house has three rooms.",note:"방 ends in a consonant, so it takes 이; 있어요 says the rooms exist."},{ko:"우리 아파트에 엘리베이터가 있어요.",romanization:"uri apateue ellibeiteoga isseoyo.",en:"Our apartment has an elevator.",note:"엘리베이터 ends in a vowel, so it takes 가."},{ko:"집 근처에 큰 마트가 있어요.",romanization:"jip geuncheoe keun mateuga isseoyo.",en:"There is a big supermarket near the house.",note:"있어요 also covers 'there is X nearby,' a common housing detail."},{ko:"베란다가 있어요?",romanization:"berandaga isseoyo?",en:"Is there a balcony?",note:"A rising tone turns the statement into a yes/no question renters actually ask."}],keyPoint:{label:"있어요 vs 이에요/예요",body:"있어요 means 'there is / it has' (existence). 이에요/예요 means 'it is' (identity). 방이 있어요 = 'there is a room'; 방이에요 = 'it is a room.' For listing what a home has, you almost always want 있어요."},pronunciation:"있어요 links the 받침 ㅆ into the next vowel: [이써요]. So 방이 있어요 runs together as [방이 이써요].",drill:{instruction:"Add 이 or 가 to the noun and finish with 있어요 to say the home has it.",model:"주차장 → 주차장이 있어요.",items:["화장실 → ____","에어컨 → ____","마당 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이 집은 방 있어요.",right:"이 집은 방이 있어요.",explanation:"English drops the subject marker ('the house has rooms'), but Korean normally needs 이/가 on the thing that exists. Don't omit the particle before 있어요 in careful speech."}},{title:"N이/가 없어요 — saying a home lacks something",func:"없어요 is the negative of 있어요: it says a feature does NOT exist. In housing talk it covers a missing room, no parking, no elevator, or 'there is no extra fee.'",formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 이 없어요",ex:"창문 → 창문이 없어요 (there is no window)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 없어요",ex:"엘리베이터 → 엘리베이터가 없어요 (there is no elevator)"},{when:"Yes/no question",add:"없어요? (rising tone)",ex:"엘리베이터가 없어요? (Is there no elevator?)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 방에는 창문이 없어요.",romanization:"i bangeneun changmuni eopseoyo.",en:"This room has no window.",note:"창문 ends in a consonant → 이 없어요; says the feature is absent."},{ko:"이 건물에는 엘리베이터가 없어요.",romanization:"i geonmureneun ellibeiteoga eopseoyo.",en:"This building has no elevator.",note:"엘리베이터 ends in a vowel → 가 없어요."},{ko:"보증금 외에 추가 비용이 없어요.",romanization:"bojeunggeum oee chuga biyongi eopseoyo.",en:"There's no extra cost besides the deposit.",note:"A reassuring rental phrase: 없어요 = 'there is no (extra fee).'"},{ko:"지금은 빈방이 없어요.",romanization:"jigeumeun binbangi eopseoyo.",en:"There are no empty rooms right now.",note:"빈방 'empty room' + 이 없어요 = a common reply from a landlord."}],keyPoint:{label:"있어요 ↔ 없어요",body:"These are a fixed pair, not 있어요 + 안. To say a home lacks something, use 없어요, never 안 있어요. 방이 있어요 (has a room) ↔ 방이 없어요 (has no room)."},pronunciation:"없어요 links the cluster ㅄ so only ㅅ carries over: [업써요]. So 창문이 없어요 sounds like [창무니 업써요].",drill:{instruction:"Use 이/가 없어요 to say the home lacks the feature.",model:"엘리베이터 → 엘리베이터가 없어요.",items:["주차장 → ____","에어컨 → ____","추가 비용 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이 집은 주차장이 안 있어요.",right:"이 집은 주차장이 없어요.",explanation:"There is no '안 있어요.' The negative of 있어요 is the separate word 없어요. Reach for 없어요 directly instead of negating 있어요 with 안."}},{title:"A-(으)ㄴ N — describing a home (넓은 방)",func:"Turn a descriptive adjective into a word that modifies a noun, like 'a spacious room' or 'a clean house.' This is how you describe homes: 넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네.",formTable:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 은 + N",ex:"넓다 → 넓은 방 (a spacious room)"},{when:"Adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄴ + N",ex:"깨끗하다 → 깨끗한 집 (a clean house)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop ㄹ, add ㄴ)",add:"ㄹ→ㄴ + N",ex:"멀다 → 먼 곳 (a faraway place)"}],examples:[{ko:"넓은 방을 찾고 있어요.",romanization:"neolbeun bangeul chatgo isseoyo.",en:"I'm looking for a spacious room.",note:"넓다 (consonant stem) → 넓은 modifies 방; the adjective comes before the noun."},{ko:"깨끗한 집이 좋아요.",romanization:"kkaekkeutan jibi joayo.",en:"I like a clean house.",note:"하다-adjective 깨끗하다 → 깨끗한 (drop the vowel-ending 하, add ㄴ)."},{ko:"조용한 동네에서 살고 싶어요.",romanization:"joyonghan dongneeseo salgo sipeoyo.",en:"I want to live in a quiet neighborhood.",note:"조용하다 → 조용한; describes the kind of 동네 (neighborhood)."},{ko:"회사에서 가까운 집을 찾고 있어요.",romanization:"hoesaeeseo gakkaun jibeul chatgo isseoyo.",en:"I'm looking for a house close to work.",note:"가깝다 is ㅂ-irregular → 가까운 집; a very common housing criterion."}],keyPoint:{label:"넓은 방 vs 방이 넓어요",body:"Modifier form goes BEFORE the noun: 넓은 방 ('a spacious room' — naming a thing). Predicate form ends the sentence: 방이 넓어요 ('the room is spacious' — stating a fact). Use 넓은 방 when the adjective is labeling the noun."},pronunciation:"넓다 is pronounced [널따], and 넓은 links the ㄼ cluster as [널븐]. 가까운 and 깨끗한 link smoothly.",drill:{instruction:"Make the adjective modify the noun with -(으)ㄴ.",model:"조용하다 / 동네 → 조용한 동네",items:["넓다 / 거실 → ____","깨끗하다 / 화장실 → ____","가깝다 / 지하철역 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"나는 넓어요 방을 원해요.",right:"나는 넓은 방을 원해요.",explanation:"Don't put a sentence-ending form in the modifier slot. 넓어요 ends a sentence; to attach it to 방 you must change it to the modifier form 넓은. English uses 'wide' in both spots, but Korean changes the ending."}},{title:"A/V-(으)면 — stating a housing condition",func:"-(으)면 means 'if / when.' Use it to set a condition about a home: 'if it's too expensive...', 'if I like the place...', 'when I sign the contract...'",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 으면",ex:"좋다 → 좋으면 (if it's good)"},{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ 면",ex:"비싸다 → 비싸면 (if it's expensive)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (keep ㄹ, add 면)",add:"+ 면",ex:"멀다 → 멀면 (if it's far)"}],examples:[{ko:"방이 너무 비싸면 다른 집을 볼게요.",romanization:"bangi neomu bissamyeon dareun jibeul bolgeyo.",en:"If the room is too expensive, I'll look at another place.",note:"비싸다 (vowel stem) → 비싸면; sets the condition before the result clause."},{ko:"지하철역에서 가까우면 좋아요.",romanization:"jihacheoryeogeseo gakkaumyeon joayo.",en:"It'd be nice if it's close to the subway station.",note:"가깝다 → 가까우면 (ㅂ-irregular); '(으)면 좋아요' = 'it'd be nice if.'"},{ko:"계약하면 보증금을 내요.",romanization:"gyeyakhamyeon bojeunggeumeul naeyo.",en:"When you sign the contract, you pay the deposit.",note:"계약하다 → 계약하면; here -(으)면 means 'when/once' for a routine step."},{ko:"집이 마음에 들면 계약할 거예요.",romanization:"jibi maeume deulmyeon gyeyakhal geoyeyo.",en:"If I like the house, I'll sign the contract.",note:"들다 is ㄹ-final → 마음에 들면 (keep ㄹ, add 면); 마음에 들다 = 'to like.'"}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 vs -아/어서",body:"-(으)면 is a hypothetical 'if/when' — the result depends on the condition. -아/어서 gives an actual reason that already holds. 비싸면 안 사요 = 'if it's expensive, I won't buy.' 비싸서 안 사요 = 'I'm not buying because it's (actually) expensive.'"},pronunciation:"After a 받침, 으면 links: 좋으면 sounds like [조으면] (ㅎ weakens between vowels). The ㄹ-stem 들면 is simply [들면].",drill:{instruction:"Attach -(으)면 to make the housing condition.",model:"비싸다 → 비싸면",items:["멀다 → ____","좋다 → ____","계약하다 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"집이 마음에 들으면 계약할 거예요.",right:"집이 마음에 들면 계약할 거예요.",explanation:"ㄹ-final stems do NOT add 으; they keep ㄹ and add 면 directly: 들다 → 들면. Learners over-apply 으면 to every consonant ending, but ㄹ is special here."}},{title:"V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요 — what's possible/allowed at home",func:"Express that something is possible or allowed in a home: you can park, you can keep a pet, you can move in next week. The opposite, 'cannot,' is V-(으)ㄹ 수 없어요.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 을 수 있어요",ex:"살다 → 살 수 있어요 (can live)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄹ 수 있어요",ex:"주차하다 → 주차할 수 있어요 (can park)"},{when:"Negative 'cannot'",add:"(으)ㄹ 수 없어요",ex:"키우다 → 키울 수 없어요 (cannot keep a pet)"}],examples:[{ko:"여기에 주차할 수 있어요?",romanization:"yeogie juchahal su isseoyo?",en:"Can I park here?",note:"주차하다 (vowel stem) → 주차할 수 있어요; a yes/no question about what's allowed."},{ko:"이 집에서 강아지를 키울 수 있어요.",romanization:"i jibeseo gangajireul kiul su isseoyo.",en:"You can keep a dog in this house.",note:"키우다 (vowel stem) → 키울 수 있어요; asking about pets is a common rental question."},{ko:"다음 주에 이사할 수 있어요.",romanization:"daeum jue isahal su isseoyo.",en:"I can move in next week.",note:"이사하다 → 이사할 수 있어요; states a possible move-in time."},{ko:"여기에서는 반려동물을 키울 수 없어요.",romanization:"yeogieseoneun ballyeodongmureul kiul su eopseoyo.",en:"You can't keep pets here.",note:"The negative uses 없어요: 키울 수 없어요 = 'cannot keep.'"}],keyPoint:{label:"있어요 → 없어요 for 'cannot'",body:"To make V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요 negative, swap 있어요 for 없어요, not 안: 살 수 있어요 (can live) → 살 수 없어요 (cannot live). The 수 (way/possibility) stays; only the existence verb flips.",drill:{instruction:"Make 'can do' with V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요.",model:"주차하다 → 주차할 수 있어요",items:["살다 → ____","키우다 → ____","이사하다 → ____"]}},pronunciation:"수 있어요 is pronounced [수 이써요], and the ㄹ before 수 stays light: 살 수 → [살 쑤] (the ㅅ tenses to ㅆ after the ㄹ).",drill:{instruction:"Make 'can do' with V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요.",model:"주차하다 → 주차할 수 있어요",items:["살다 → ____","키우다 → ____","이사하다 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"여기에서 주차 안 할 수 있어요.",right:"여기에서 주차할 수 없어요.",explanation:"To say 'cannot park,' don't insert 안 into the can-phrase. The negative of (으)ㄹ 수 있어요 is (으)ㄹ 수 없어요. 안 주차하다 would mean 'choose not to park,' which is different from 'not allowed/able to park.'"}},{title:"N에 살아요 — saying where you live",func:"Use 에 + 살아요 to say where you live: in a city, a neighborhood, an apartment, or on a particular floor. 에 marks the place of residence.",formTable:[{when:"Place + 에 살아요",add:"N + 에 살아요",ex:"부산에 살아요 (I live in Busan)"},{when:"Building/room type",add:"N + 에 살아요",ex:"아파트에 살아요 (I live in an apartment)"},{when:"Floor (N층)",add:"숫자 + 층에 살아요",ex:"3층에 살아요 (I live on the 3rd floor)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 부산에 살아요.",romanization:"jeoneun busane sarayo.",en:"I live in Busan.",note:"부산 (place) + 에 살아요; 에 marks where you live."},{ko:"지금 원룸에 살아요.",romanization:"jigeum wollume sarayo.",en:"I live in a studio apartment right now.",note:"원룸 'one-room/studio' is a very common type of home in Korea."},{ko:"저는 5층에 살아요.",romanization:"jeoneun ocheunge sarayo.",en:"I live on the 5th floor.",note:"층 'floor' + 에 살아요; 5층 is read 오층."},{ko:"어느 동네에 살아요?",romanization:"eoneu dongnee sarayo?",en:"Which neighborhood do you live in?",note:"어느 'which' + 동네 + 에 살아요? — a natural getting-to-know-you question."}],keyPoint:{label:"에 살아요 vs 에서 + action",body:"For living (a state), use 에: 서울에 살아요. For an action that happens at a place, use 에서: 집에서 일해요 ('I work at home'). With 살다 specifically, Korean uses 에, not 에서."},pronunciation:"살아요 links the ㄹ into the next vowel: [사라요]. 부산에 살아요 runs together as [부사네 사라요].",drill:{instruction:"Add 에 살아요 to say where you live.",model:"서울 → 서울에 살아요.",items:["아파트 → ____","2층 → ____","조용한 동네 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 부산에서 살아요.",right:"저는 부산에 살아요.",explanation:"With 살다 (to live/reside), the standard particle is 에, not 에서. English 'live in/at' tempts learners toward 에서, but residence as a state takes 에."}},{title:"N(으)로 이사 가요/와요 — moving house",func:"Use (으)로 to mark the destination you move TO when changing homes: 이사 가다 'to move (away to)' and 이사 오다 'to move (here to).' (으)로 means 'to / toward.'",formTable:[{when:"Place ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 으로 이사 가요",ex:"부산 → 부산으로 이사 가요"},{when:"Place ends in a vowel or ㄹ",add:"+ 로 이사 가요",ex:"제주 → 제주로… / 서울 → 서울로…"},{when:"Moving here (toward speaker)",add:"+ (으)로 이사 와요",ex:"새집으로 이사 와요 (move into a new house)"}],examples:[{ko:"다음 달에 새집으로 이사 가요.",romanization:"daeum dare saejibeuro isa gayo.",en:"I'm moving to a new house next month.",note:"새집 'new house' + 으로 (받침 ㅂ) + 이사 가요; (으)로 marks the destination."},{ko:"부산으로 이사 갈 거예요.",romanization:"busaneuro isa gal geoyeyo.",en:"I'm going to move to Busan.",note:"부산 ends in ㄴ → 으로; 이사 갈 거예요 = future 'will move.'"},{ko:"우리 옆집에 새 이웃이 이사 왔어요.",romanization:"uri yeopjibe sae iusi isa wasseoyo.",en:"A new neighbor moved in next door.",note:"이사 오다 → 이사 왔어요 (past); 옆집 'next-door house,' 이웃 'neighbor.'"},{ko:"언제 이사 가요?",romanization:"eonje isa gayo?",en:"When are you moving out?",note:"언제 'when' + 이사 가요? — a natural question to a friend who's moving."}],keyPoint:{label:"이사 가요 vs 이사 와요",body:"이사 가다 = move away (the speaker is not at the destination). 이사 오다 = move here (toward the speaker's location). 서울로 이사 가요 = 'I'm moving to Seoul'; 여기로 이사 왔어요 = 'I moved here.'"},pronunciation:"이사 가요 is [이사 가요]; 으로 links its ㄹ into 이사: 새집으로 → [새지브로]. 이사 왔어요 sounds like [이사 와써요].",drill:{instruction:"Add (으)로 이사 가요 to say where you're moving to.",model:"새집 → 새집으로 이사 가요.",items:["부산 → ____","서울 → ____","더 넓은 집 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 새집에 이사 가요.",right:"저는 새집으로 이사 가요.",explanation:"For the destination of a move, Korean prefers (으)로 ('toward/to') with 이사 가다, not 에. English 'move to a new house' suggests 에, but the directional (으)로 is the natural choice here."}}],d=[{title:`A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 — "I think it'll..." (a soft guess about the weather)`,func:`Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 to make a soft prediction about the weather from signs you can see — clouds, a dark sky, a dropping temperature. It is the everyday way to say "I think it's going to rain / snow / be cold." Because it is a guess, not a fact, it sounds careful and polite rather than absolute.`,formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel (오다)",add:"-ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"오다 → 올 것 같아요 (it looks like it'll rain)"},{when:"Verb/adjective stem ends in a consonant (맑다, 좋다)",add:"-을 것 같아요",ex:"맑다 → 맑을 것 같아요 (I think it'll be clear)"},{when:"ㅂ-irregular adjective (춥다 → 추우-, 덥다 → 더우-)",add:"-ㄹ 것 같아요 (on the 우 form)",ex:"춥다 → 추울 것 같아요 (I think it'll be cold)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (얼다, 불다) — ㄹ stays, no extra 으",add:"-ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"불다 → 불 것 같아요 (it looks like the wind will blow)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘 오후에 비가 올 것 같아요.",romanization:"oneul ohue biga ol geot gatayo.",en:"I think it's going to rain this afternoon.",note:"오다 is a vowel-stem, so just -ㄹ; the model weather-guess sentence."},{ko:"하늘을 보니까 눈이 올 것 같아요.",romanization:"haneureul bonikka nuni ol geot gatayo.",en:"Looking at the sky, I think it's going to snow.",note:"Pairs the guess with visible evidence (하늘을 보니까), the natural reason for a forecast."},{ko:"내일은 좀 추울 것 같아요.",romanization:"naeireun jom chuul geot gatayo.",en:"I think it'll be a bit cold tomorrow.",note:"춥다 is ㅂ-irregular → 추우- + ㄹ; 좀 softens the guess even more."},{ko:"주말에는 날씨가 맑을 것 같아요.",romanization:"jumareneun nalssiga malgeul geot gatayo.",en:"I think the weather will be clear this weekend.",note:"맑다 ends in a consonant, so it takes -을; everyday seasons vocabulary."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 vs. -아/어요",body:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 is a guess about something not yet certain (비가 올 것 같아요 = I think it'll rain), while plain 비가 와요 states it as a present fact (it's raining). Reach for 것 같아요 when you're reading the signs, not reporting what's already true."},pronunciation:'것 같아요 is pronounced [걷 까타요]: the final ㅅ of 것 becomes a [t] stop, and the following ㄱ tenses to [ㄲ], so it sounds like "geot kkatayo."',drill:{instruction:"Make a soft weather guess with -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요. Follow the model.",model:"비가 오다 → 비가 올 것 같아요.",items:["바람이 불다 → ___","날씨가 춥다 → ___","눈이 그치다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"비가 올 거예요 같아요.",right:"비가 올 것 같아요.",explanation:'English speakers try to glue "will" (-ㄹ 거예요) onto "seems" (같아요). In Korean the single chunk is -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 — the noun 것, not the sentence-ending 거예요, attaches before 같아요.'}},{title:`V/A-(으)ㄹ 텐데 — "it'll probably... so" (a likely situation plus concern)`,func:`Use -(으)ㄹ 텐데 to predict a likely situation and then add worry, advice, or a suggestion about it. It is the warm "it'll probably rain, so (take an umbrella)" pattern — you forecast something and immediately care about its effect on the listener. The 텐데 part leaves the sentence half-open, inviting a follow-up clause.`,formTable:[{when:"Verb/adjective, vowel-ending stem (오다)",add:"-ㄹ 텐데",ex:"오다 → 올 텐데 (it'll probably rain, so...)"},{when:"Verb/adjective, consonant-ending stem (좋다, 많다)",add:"-을 텐데",ex:"많다 → 많을 텐데 (there'll probably be a lot, so...)"},{when:"ㅂ-irregular adjective (춥다 → 추우-, 덥다 → 더우-)",add:"-ㄹ 텐데 (on the 우 form)",ex:"춥다 → 추울 텐데 (it'll probably be cold, so...)"},{when:"Past situation (already likely happened)",add:"-았/었을 텐데",ex:"얼다 → 얼었을 텐데 (it must have frozen, so...)"}],examples:[{ko:"밖에 추울 텐데 코트를 입으세요.",romanization:"bakke chuul tende koteureul ibeuseyo.",en:"It'll probably be cold outside, so please wear a coat.",note:"춥다 → 추우- + ㄹ 텐데; forecast (cold) + caring advice (wear a coat), the core use."},{ko:"오후에 비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요.",romanization:"ohue biga ol tende usaneul gajyeogaseyo.",en:"It'll probably rain in the afternoon, so take an umbrella.",note:'The everyday "take an umbrella" sentence; 올 텐데 sets up the practical suggestion.'},{ko:"눈이 많이 올 텐데 운전 조심하세요.",romanization:"nuni mani ol tende unjeon josimhaseyo.",en:"It'll probably snow a lot, so drive carefully.",note:"Shows 텐데 carrying genuine concern (조심하세요) about a weather outcome."},{ko:"내일은 더울 텐데 물을 많이 드세요.",romanization:"naeireun deoul tende mureul mani deuseyo.",en:"It'll probably be hot tomorrow, so drink a lot of water.",note:"덥다 → 더우- + ㄹ 텐데; summer-heat advice, everyday seasonal vocabulary."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 텐데 vs. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"Both can present an expectation. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 comfortably ends after the guess: 비가 올 것 같아요. -(으)ㄹ 텐데 often leads into a consequence, concern, or advice: 비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요. In real conversation it can also end the turn by itself when the missing thought is obvious, such as 비가 올 텐데… ('But it may rain…')."},pronunciation:'올 텐데 links smoothly into one breath group — "ol-ten-de," no pause — so the forecast flows straight into the advice. 텐데 itself is read [텐데] as written.',drill:{instruction:"Predict the weather and add caring advice with -(으)ㄹ 텐데. Follow the model.",model:"비가 오다 / 우산을 가져가세요 → 비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요.",items:["날씨가 춥다 / 따뜻하게 입으세요 → ___","눈이 오다 / 일찍 출발하세요 → ___","바람이 많이 불다 / 모자를 쓰세요 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"Sentence-final -(으)ㄹ 텐데 is always incomplete and wrong.",right:"비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요. / 비가 올 텐데…",explanation:"Include the following clause when you need to state the advice clearly. In conversation, a final 텐데 can deliberately leave concern, hesitation, or disagreement unstated because the listener can infer it. The trailing form is a real conversational tool, not a grammar error."}},{title:'A/V-(으)면 — "if / when the weather is like this" (condition)',func:`Use -(으)면 to set up a weather condition and its result: "if it rains, I'll stay home," "when spring comes, flowers bloom." It covers both English "if" (a possibility) and "when" (a regular trigger) — exactly how Koreans talk about weather shaping everyday decisions.`,formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel (오다, 따뜻하다)",add:"-면",ex:"오다 → 오면 (if/when it rains)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (좋다, 많다)",add:"-으면",ex:"좋다 → 좋으면 (if the weather is good)"},{when:"ㅂ-irregular adjective (덥다 → 더우-, 춥다 → 추우-)",add:"-면 (on the 우 form)",ex:"덥다 → 더우면 (if it's hot)"},{when:"Past/completed condition",add:"-았/었으면",ex:"그치다 → 그쳤으면 (if the rain/snow has stopped)"}],examples:[{ko:"비가 오면 집에 있을 거예요.",romanization:"biga omyeon jibe isseul geoyeyo.",en:"If it rains, I'll stay home.",note:'오다 vowel-stem → 오면; the model "if it rains" sentence with a plan as the result.'},{ko:"봄이 오면 꽃이 펴요.",romanization:"bomi omyeon kkochi pyeoyo.",en:"When spring comes, flowers bloom.",note:'Here -면 = "when" for a regular seasonal pattern, not a one-time "if."'},{ko:"날씨가 더우면 에어컨을 켜요.",romanization:"nalssiga deoumyeon eeokeoneul kyeoyo.",en:"When it's hot, I turn on the air conditioner.",note:"덥다 → 더우- + 면; a habitual weather-triggered action."},{ko:"눈이 많이 오면 학교에 안 가요.",romanization:"nuni mani omyeon hakgyoe an gayo.",en:"If it snows a lot, we don't go to school.",note:"Shows a weather condition deciding an everyday outcome (no school)."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 vs. -(으)니까",body:"-(으)면 introduces a condition that may or may not happen (비가 오면 = if/when it rains — uncertain). -(으)니까 gives a reason that is already true (비가 오니까 = since it's raining — a fact right now). Use -면 for forecasts and rules, -니까 for what's actually happening."},pronunciation:`좋으면 is pronounced [조으면]: the ㅎ of 좋 goes silent (ㅎ-탈락) before the vowel 으, so it's "jo-eu-myeon," not "joh-eu-myeon."`,drill:{instruction:"Join the weather condition to its result with -(으)면. Follow the model.",model:"비가 오다 / 집에 있어요 → 비가 오면 집에 있어요.",items:["날씨가 춥다 / 두꺼운 옷을 입어요 → ___","봄이 오다 / 꽃구경을 가요 → ___","날씨가 좋다 / 산책을 해요 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"비가 오면 and 비가 올 거면 are interchangeable in every context.",right:"비가 오면 집에 있을 거예요. (neutral condition) / 비가 올 거면 일정을 바꿔요. (if rain is what is expected)",explanation:"Plain V-(으)면 is the neutral everyday conditional. V-(으)ㄹ 거면 is also grammatical and sets up an expected plan or premise: 'if it is going to V / if that is what will happen.' Use it when that future premise itself matters."}},{title:'N 때문에 — "because of..." (a weather cause)',func:'Use N 때문에 to name a noun — usually a weather event — as the cause of something, especially something inconvenient: "because of the rain," "because of the heat." It attaches straight to a noun and is the everyday way to blame the weather for a delay, a cancellation, or how you feel.',formTable:[{when:"Noun ending in a vowel (비, 더위)",add:"+ 때문에",ex:"비 → 비 때문에 (because of the rain)"},{when:"Noun ending in a consonant (태풍, 눈)",add:"+ 때문에 (no change)",ex:"태풍 → 태풍 때문에 (because of the typhoon)"},{when:'"because it is N" (noun as reason-state)',add:"+ (이)기 때문에",ex:"여름 → 여름이기 때문에 (because it's summer)"},{when:"Adjective/verb cause (the related form)",add:"-기 때문에",ex:"춥다 → 춥기 때문에 (because it's cold)"}],examples:[{ko:"비 때문에 소풍이 취소됐어요.",romanization:"bi ttaemune sopungi chwisodwaesseoyo.",en:"The picnic was cancelled because of the rain.",note:'비 + 때문에; the classic "weather ruined the plan" sentence.'},{ko:"더위 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",romanization:"deowi ttaemune jameul mot jasseoyo.",en:"I couldn't sleep because of the heat.",note:"더위 (the heat) as a cause of how you feel — very common in summer."},{ko:"태풍 때문에 비행기가 늦어요.",romanization:"taepung ttaemune bihaenggiga neujeoyo.",en:"The plane is late because of the typhoon.",note:"태풍 ends in a consonant; 때문에 attaches with no change."},{ko:"눈 때문에 길이 많이 막혀요.",romanization:"nun ttaemune giri mani makhyeoyo.",en:"The roads are really backed up because of the snow.",note:"Everyday winter complaint; 눈 (snow) named as the cause of traffic."}],keyPoint:{label:"N 때문에 vs. N 덕분에",body:`때문에 is neutral-to-negative — you usually blame it for trouble (비 때문에 취소됐어요 = cancelled because of the rain). For a good outcome, Koreans switch to 덕분에 "thanks to" (좋은 날씨 덕분에 = thanks to the nice weather). Don't use 때문에 to thank the weather.`},pronunciation:'때문에 is pronounced [때무네]: the ㄴ of 문 links into the following 에 (연음), so it sounds like "ttae-mu-ne," run together as one word after the noun.',drill:{instruction:"Name the weather cause with N 때문에. Follow the model.",model:"비 / 소풍이 취소됐어요 → 비 때문에 소풍이 취소됐어요.",items:["눈 / 길이 막혀요 → ___","더위 / 힘들어요 → ___","태풍 / 학교에 안 가요 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"비 때문에 좋은 날씨를 즐겼어요.",right:"좋은 날씨 덕분에 산책했어요.",explanation:'English "because of" is neutral, so learners use 때문에 for good things too. But 때문에 carries a blaming nuance; for a positive cause Korean uses 덕분에 (thanks to). Saving 때문에 for problems keeps you natural.'}},{title:"V/A-(으)ㄹ 때 — when (a time or situation)",func:`-(으)ㄹ 때 marks the time or situation when something happens: "when it rains," "when it's cold." Attach it to a verb or adjective stem; for a past time, use -았/었을 때.`,formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ 때",ex:"오다 → 올 때 (when it comes/rains)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-을 때",ex:"춥다 → 추울 때 (when it's cold)"},{when:"A past time",add:"-았/었을 때",ex:"비가 왔을 때 (when it rained)"}],examples:[{ko:"비가 올 때 우산을 써요.",romanization:"biga ol ttae usaneul sseoyo.",en:"When it rains, I use an umbrella.",note:"오다 → 올 때. The 때 clause sets the time for the main action."},{ko:"날씨가 추울 때 집에 있어요.",romanization:"nalssiga chuul ttae jibe isseoyo.",en:"When the weather is cold, I stay home.",note:"춥다 (ㅂ-irregular) → 추울 때."},{ko:"더울 때 물을 많이 마셔요.",romanization:"deoul ttae mureul mani masyeoyo.",en:"When it's hot, I drink a lot of water.",note:"덥다 → 더울 때."},{ko:"눈이 왔을 때 사진을 찍었어요.",romanization:"nuni wasseul ttae sajineul jjigeosseoyo.",en:"When it snowed, I took photos.",note:"Past time → 왔을 때 (오다 + 았을 때)."}],keyPoint:{label:"때 is a noun, so use the -(으)ㄹ modifier",body:'때 means "time," so the verb in front must be in the noun-modifying -(으)ㄹ form: 올 때, 추울 때 — never 와 때 or 춥어 때. For a finished past time, use -았/었을 때 (왔을 때).'},pronunciation:"때 begins with the tense consonant ㄸ — say it hard: 올 때 is [올 때], not a soft [대].",drill:{instruction:'Make a "when …" clause with -(으)ㄹ 때.',model:"(비가 오다) → 비가 올 때 · (춥다) → 추울 때",items:["(덥다) → ______","(눈이 오다) → ______","(날씨가 좋다) → ______"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"비가 와 때 / 비가 오을 때",right:"비가 올 때",explanation:"때 needs the -(으)ㄹ modifier form. 오다 ends in a vowel, so it's 올 때 (not 와 때, not 오을 때). A consonant stem takes -을 때 (추울 때)."}}],u=[{title:"V-아/어 보세요 — Try doing it",func:"Suggest that the listener try an action to see how it goes — perfect for inviting someone to try a piece of clothing on, try a color, or give a style a go. It softens a plain command into friendly encouragement: go ahead and try it.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-아 보세요",ex:"사다 → 사 보세요 (try buying it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in any other vowel or a consonant",add:"-어 보세요",ex:"입다 → 입어 보세요 (try wearing it), 신다 → 신어 보세요 (try them on)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"해 보세요",ex:"착용하다 → 착용해 보세요 (try wearing it on)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 원피스 한번 입어 보세요.",romanization:"i wonpiseu hanbeon ibeo boseyo.",en:"Try this dress on.",note:"한번 (once / give it a go) commonly pairs with -아/어 보세요 to mean a light, no-pressure try."},{ko:"이 모자도 써 보세요.",romanization:"i moja-do sseo boseyo.",en:"Try this hat on too.",note:"쓰다 (to wear on the head) is a ㅡ-irregular: ㅡ drops before -어, giving 써 보세요."},{ko:"이 신발 신어 보세요.",romanization:"i sinbal sineo boseyo.",en:"Try these shoes on.",note:"신다 = to wear on the feet. Each clothing verb is different, but all combine the same way with 보세요."},{ko:"빨간색으로 한번 골라 보세요.",romanization:"ppalgansaegeuro hanbeon golla boseyo.",en:"Try picking the red one.",note:"고르다 is a 르-irregular, so 고르 → 골라 before -아; you are inviting them to try choosing."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)세요 vs -아/어 보세요",body:"입으세요 = Put it on (a plain polite command). 입어 보세요 = Try it on (and see) — it adds the nuance of testing or experiencing, which is why a shop clerk says 입어 보세요, not 입으세요."},drill:{instruction:"Invite the customer to try each item, using V-아/어 보세요.",model:"치마 → 이 치마 입어 보세요.",items:["안경 (write: 안경 써 보세요)","장갑 (write: 장갑 껴 보세요)","목도리 (write: 목도리 해 보세요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이 코트 시도해 보세요.",right:"이 코트 입어 보세요.",explanation:"English try on makes learners reach for a word meaning try/attempt (시도하다). In Korean you keep the real clothing verb (입다/신다/쓰다) and just attach 보세요; the try meaning comes entirely from 보세요."}},{title:"V-아/어 주시겠어요? — Could you please do it for me?",func:"A very polite request asking someone to do something for your benefit. In a shop or fitting room it is how you ask the clerk to bring another size, show you a color, or give you a hand. -주다 adds for me, and -시겠어요? makes it gentle and respectful.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-아 주시겠어요?",ex:"찾다 → 찾아 주시겠어요? (could you find it for me?)"},{when:"Stem ends in another vowel or consonant",add:"-어 주시겠어요?",ex:"보이다 → 보여 주시겠어요? (could you show me?)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"해 주시겠어요?",ex:"추천하다 → 추천해 주시겠어요? (could you recommend one?)"}],examples:[{ko:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요?",romanization:"deo keun saijeuro gajyeoda jusigesseoyo?",en:"Could you bring me a bigger size?",note:"가져다주다 (bring for someone) is the go-to fitting-room request; -주시겠어요? keeps it polite to the clerk."},{ko:"다른 색깔도 보여 주시겠어요?",romanization:"dareun saekkkaldo boyeo jusigesseoyo?",en:"Could you show me another color too?",note:"보이다 → 보여; 보여 주다 literally show for me, the natural way to ask to be shown an item."},{ko:"이 셔츠 좀 찾아 주시겠어요?",romanization:"i syeocheu jom chaja jusigesseoyo?",en:"Could you find this shirt for me?",note:"좀 softens the request even more — a near-obligatory politeness word in spoken requests."},{ko:"이 바지 잘 어울리는지 봐 주시겠어요?",romanization:"i baji jal eoullineunji bwa jusigesseoyo?",en:"Could you check whether these pants suit me?",note:"보다 → 봐; asking someone to look or check for your benefit, a common appearance question to a friend or clerk."}],keyPoint:{label:"-아/어 주세요 vs -아/어 주시겠어요?",body:"가져다 주세요 = Please bring it (polite request). 가져다 주시겠어요? = Could you bring it? — the -시겠어요? question form is one step more deferential and indirect, the safest choice with a stranger or staff."},drill:{instruction:"Make each request polite using V-아/어 주시겠어요?",model:"보이다 → 색깔 보여 주시겠어요?",items:["작은 사이즈 / 찾다 (write: 작은 사이즈 찾아 주시겠어요?)","거울 / 보이다 (write: 거울 보여 주시겠어요?)","이 옷 / 추천하다 (write: 이 옷 추천해 주시겠어요?)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요 나를 위해?",right:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요?",explanation:"Learners add for me as a separate phrase (나를 위해) by analogy with English. The benefit is already built into 주다, so adding 나를 위해 is redundant and unnatural — just use 주시겠어요?."}},{title:"A-아/어 보이다 — It looks / appears",func:"Describe how something or someone appears to the observer. Attached to an adjective it means looks (pretty/big/expensive); it is the everyday way to compliment an outfit or comment on how clothes look on someone.",formTable:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-아 보이다",ex:"좋다 → 좋아 보여요 (looks good)"},{when:"Stem ends in another vowel or consonant",add:"-어 보이다",ex:"예쁘다 → 예뻐 보여요 (looks pretty)"},{when:"하다 adjectives",add:"-해 보이다",ex:"깔끔하다 → 깔끔해 보여요 (looks neat)"}],examples:[{ko:"그 원피스 정말 예뻐 보여요.",romanization:"geu wonpiseu jeongmal yeppeo boyeoyo.",en:"That dress really looks pretty.",note:"예쁘다 → 예뻐 (ㅡ drops before -어); the headline example for this pattern."},{ko:"이 코트는 좀 비싸 보여요.",romanization:"i koteuneun jom bissa boyeoyo.",en:"This coat looks a bit expensive.",note:"비싸다 ends in ㅏ, so the -아 merges in: 비싸 보여요; commenting on apparent price."},{ko:"그 색깔이 잘 어울려 보여요.",romanization:"geu saekkkari jal eoullyeo boyeoyo.",en:"That color looks like it suits you well.",note:"어울리다 → 어울려; pairing 잘 어울리다 with 보이다 is the standard it looks good on you comment."},{ko:"이 신발을 신으니까 키가 커 보여요.",romanization:"i sinbareul sineunikka kiga keo boyeoyo.",en:"With these shoes on, you look taller.",note:"크다 → 커; appearance changes caused by clothing are exactly what -아/어 보이다 expresses."}],keyPoint:{label:"예뻐요 vs 예뻐 보여요",body:"예뻐요 = It is pretty (your judgment of the thing itself). 예뻐 보여요 = It looks pretty (how it appears, possibly more than it really is). Use 보이다 when you are describing an impression or the effect an outfit gives."},pronunciation:"보여요 is pronounced with a smooth y-glide [bo-yeo-yo]; learners often clip it to [bo-eo-yo] — keep the ㅕ. In 좋아 보여요, ㅎ is silent before a vowel, so 좋아 links as [jo-a].",drill:{instruction:"Say how each looks using A-아/어 보여요.",model:"치마 / 예쁘다 → 치마가 예뻐 보여요.",items:["코트 / 따뜻하다 (write: 코트가 따뜻해 보여요.)","가방 / 비싸다 (write: 가방이 비싸 보여요.)","셔츠 / 작다 (write: 셔츠가 작아 보여요.)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"그 원피스는 예쁜 것처럼 보여요.",right:"그 원피스는 예뻐 보여요.",explanation:"English looks like it is pretty tempts learners to build a heavy ~것처럼 보이다 clause. For a simple adjective impression, Korean just fuses the adjective with 보이다: 예뻐 보여요."}},{title:"A-(으)ㄴ N — An adjective before a noun",func:"Turn a descriptive adjective into a modifier that sits directly in front of a noun — the basic way to say a pretty dress, a long skirt, black shoes. Essential for describing clothing and appearance with detail.",formTable:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄴ + N",ex:"예쁘다 → 예쁜 원피스 (a pretty dress)"},{when:"Adjective stem ends in a consonant",add:"-은 + N",ex:"작다 → 작은 신발 (small shoes)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (ㄹ drops)",add:"-ㄴ + N",ex:"길다 → 긴 치마 (a long skirt)"},{when:"있다/없다 type",add:"-는 + N",ex:"멋있다 → 멋있는 옷 (cool-looking clothes)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 긴 치마를 좋아해요.",romanization:"jeoneun gin chimareul joahaeyo.",en:"I like long skirts.",note:"길다 is a ㄹ-irregular: ㄹ drops and -ㄴ attaches → 긴. A classic irregular to flag for clothing length."},{ko:"검은색 구두를 신었어요.",romanization:"geomeunsaek gudureul sineosseoyo.",en:"I wore black dress shoes.",note:"검다 → 검은; consonant-final stems take -은. 검은색 (black color) is everyday clothing vocabulary."},{ko:"이건 작은 모자예요.",romanization:"igeon jageun mojayeyo.",en:"This is a small hat.",note:"작다 → 작은; the modifier comes directly before the noun with no extra particle — unlike English, no is needed."},{ko:"새 운동화를 샀어요.",romanization:"sae undonghwareul sasseoyo.",en:"I bought new sneakers.",note:"새 (new) is a determiner that takes no ending at all — a common exception worth memorizing alongside the rule."}],keyPoint:{label:"치마가 길어요 vs 긴 치마",body:"치마가 길어요 = The skirt is long (adjective as predicate, sentence-final). 긴 치마 = a long skirt (adjective modifying the noun, mid-sentence). The same adjective takes -아/어요 to end a sentence but -(으)ㄴ to sit before a noun."},pronunciation:"작은 links as [ja-geun] — the final ㄱ of 작 moves onto the -은 (resyllabification). Likewise 검은 → [geo-meun], 좋은 → [jo-eun] (ㅎ silent).",drill:{instruction:"Combine the adjective and noun into A-(으)ㄴ N.",model:"예쁘다 + 가방 → 예쁜 가방",items:["짧다 + 바지 (write: 짧은 바지)","하얗다 + 셔츠 (write: 하얀 셔츠 — ㅎ-irregular: ㅎ drops)","멋있다 + 코트 (write: 멋있는 코트 — 있다 type takes -는)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"긴은 치마",right:"긴 치마",explanation:"With ㄹ-irregular adjectives like 길다, learners try to add -은 to a stem that already kept ㄹ. Instead the ㄹ drops and only -ㄴ attaches: 길다 → 긴 치마, not 길은/긴은 치마."}},{title:"N보다 (더) — More ... than N",func:"Compare two things: mark the standard of comparison (the thing you measure against) with 보다, and add 더 (more) for emphasis. Ideal for clothing and appearance — saying one skirt is longer than another, or one coat is more expensive than another.",formTable:[{when:"After any noun (the thing compared against)",add:"N보다",ex:"이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요 (this skirt is longer than that one)"},{when:"Add 더 before the adjective for emphasis",add:"N보다 더 + A",ex:"이 코트가 저 코트보다 더 비싸요 (this coat is more expensive than that one)"},{when:"Opposite direction uses 덜 (less)",add:"N보다 덜 + A",ex:"이 신발이 저 신발보다 덜 편해요 (these shoes are less comfortable than those)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 원피스가 저 원피스보다 예뻐요.",romanization:"i wonpiseuga jeo wonpiseuboda yeppeoyo.",en:"This dress is prettier than that one.",note:"보다 attaches to 저 원피스, the thing being compared against — no separate word for than is needed."},{ko:"검은색 구두가 흰색보다 더 잘 어울려요.",romanization:"geomeunsaek guduga huinsaekboda deo jal eoullyeoyo.",en:"Black shoes suit you better than white ones.",note:"더 (more) plus 잘 어울리다 is the natural way to say something suits you better."},{ko:"이 바지가 저 바지보다 짧아요.",romanization:"i bajiga jeo bajiboda jjalbayo.",en:"These pants are shorter than those.",note:"짧다 → 짧아요; comparing length is a very common clothing comment."},{ko:"이 모자가 저 모자보다 더 커요.",romanization:"i mojaga jeo mojaboda deo keoyo.",en:"This hat is bigger than that one.",note:"크다 → 커요; 더 reinforces the comparison but the sentence is already comparative thanks to 보다."}],keyPoint:{label:"Where 보다 attaches",body:"보다 attaches to the standard (the thing you measure against), not the subject. 이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요 = this skirt is longer than that skirt. The 가-marked noun is the winner; the 보다-marked noun is what it beats."},pronunciation:"보다 is always pronounced [bo-da] and attaches directly with no space in writing: 저 치마보다. 더 [deo] is a separate word with a clear ㅓ vowel — do not reduce it to [deu].",drill:{instruction:"Make a comparison with N보다 (더) using the cue.",model:"이 치마 / 저 치마 / 길다 → 이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요.",items:["이 코트 / 저 코트 / 비싸다 (write: 이 코트가 저 코트보다 비싸요.)","이 신발 / 저 신발 / 더 편하다 (write: 이 신발이 저 신발보다 더 편해요.)","빨간색 / 파란색 / 잘 어울리다 (write: 빨간색이 파란색보다 잘 어울려요.)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이 치마가 길어요 저 치마보다.",right:"이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요.",explanation:"English puts than that one at the end, so learners tack 저 치마보다 on after the adjective. In Korean the 보다 phrase must come before the adjective: subject + 보다-phrase + 더 + adjective."}}],c=[{title:"N 주세요 — Ordering an Item",func:'Use N 주세요 to ask someone to give you a thing. In a restaurant or café this is your basic ordering phrase: name the item, optionally with a number, then add 주세요 ("please give me"). It is polite and works with any server.',formTable:[{when:"Item alone",add:"N + 주세요",ex:"물 → 물 주세요 (Water, please.)"},{when:"Item + amount",add:"N + 수량 + 주세요",ex:"커피 두 잔 → 커피 두 잔 주세요 (Two coffees, please.)"},{when:"Pointing at the menu",add:"이거 + 주세요",ex:"이거 → 이거 주세요 (This one, please.)"},{when:'Softer "could you give me"',add:"N + 좀 + 주세요",ex:"물 → 물 좀 주세요 (Some water, please.)"}],examples:[{ko:"여기 김치찌개 하나 주세요.",romanization:"yeogi gimchijjigae hana juseyo.",en:"One kimchi stew here, please.",note:`여기 ("here") flags you're ordering for this table; 하나 is the casual way to count one dish.`},{ko:"물 좀 주세요.",romanization:"mul jom juseyo.",en:"Some water, please.",note:"좀 softens the request and is almost automatic with 물 in a restaurant."},{ko:"이거 주세요.",romanization:"igeo juseyo.",en:"This one, please.",note:'이거 ("this thing") lets you point at the menu without knowing the dish name.'},{ko:"콜라 두 개 주세요.",romanization:"kolla du gae juseyo.",en:"Two colas, please.",note:"개 is the all-purpose counter for canned/bottled drinks; 잔 is for glasses you pour."}],keyPoint:{label:"주세요 vs. 주문할게요",body:`주세요 literally means "give me," so you attach it to the THING (커피 주세요). If you want to say "I'll order," that's the verb 주문하다 → 주문할게요. Beginners only need N 주세요 to order; save 주문할게요 for flagging the server that you're ready.`},pronunciation:"주세요 is read [주세요], no special linking. Watch the counter 한 개: say 한 / 개 as two clear beats — learners squash it into 항개. Keep the ㄴ of 한 crisp before 개.",drill:{instruction:"You are at a restaurant. Order each item by putting it into the N (수량) 주세요 frame.",model:"비빔밥 (one) → 비빔밥 하나 주세요.",items:["냉면 (one) → ____","맥주 (two glasses) → ____","이거 (this one) → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 커피 주세요.",right:"커피 주세요.",explanation:'English speakers add "I" (저는) out of habit, but 주세요 already means "give ME," so 저는 is redundant and sounds odd. Just name the item + 주세요.'}},{title:"V-아/어 주세요 — Asking for an Action",func:"Use V-아/어 주세요 to ask someone to DO something for you, not just hand you a thing. Take a verb stem, add the 아/어 connector, then 주세요. In a restaurant this is how you ask the server to bring, change, cut, or wrap something.",formTable:[{when:"Stem vowel ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"stem + 아 주세요",ex:"닦다 → 닦아 주세요 (Please wipe it.)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"stem + 어 주세요",ex:"바꾸다 → 바꿔 주세요 (Please change it.)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"해 주세요",ex:"계산하다 → 계산해 주세요 (Please ring it up.)"},{when:"ㅂ irregular",add:"ㅂ→우, then 어",ex:"굽다 → 구워 주세요 (Please grill it.)"}],examples:[{ko:"반찬 좀 더 갖다 주세요.",romanization:"banchan jom deo gatda juseyo.",en:"Could you bring more side dishes, please.",note:'갖다 주세요 ("bring and give") is the set phrase for asking a server to fetch something; 좀 더 = a bit more.'},{ko:"고기 좀 잘라 주세요.",romanization:"gogi jom jalla juseyo.",en:"Please cut the meat for us.",note:"At a Korean BBQ you ask staff to cut the meat; 자르다 → 잘라 (르 irregular)."},{ko:"이거 포장해 주세요.",romanization:"igeo pojanghae juseyo.",en:"Please wrap this to go.",note:'포장하다 ("to pack") → 포장해 주세요 is the standard way to ask for takeout.'},{ko:"덜 맵게 해 주세요.",romanization:"deol maepge hae juseyo.",en:"Please make it less spicy.",note:'하다 → 해 주세요; 덜 맵게 ("less spicily") tells the kitchen how to cook it.'}],keyPoint:{label:"N 주세요 vs. V-아/어 주세요",body:`N 주세요 asks for a THING (물 주세요 = give me water). V-아/어 주세요 asks for an ACTION (포장해 주세요 = pack it). If the English word is a noun ("water," "the menu"), use N 주세요; if it's a verb ("cut it," "wrap it," "bring it"), use V-아/어 주세요.`},pronunciation:"After a stem ending in ㄹ like 만들다, the form is 만들어 주세요 [만드러 주세요] — the ㄹ links into the next syllable. Say it smoothly as 만드러, not 만들-어.",drill:{instruction:"Turn each verb into a polite request with V-아/어 주세요.",model:"포장하다 (this) → 이거 포장해 주세요.",items:["바꾸다 (the spoon) → 숟가락 ____","굽다 (the meat) → 고기 ____","갖다주다 (water) → 물 ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"포장하 주세요.",right:"포장해 주세요.",explanation:"Learners attach 주세요 to the bare stem (포장하-), but you must first conjugate with 아/어: 하다 → 해. The connector vowel is not optional — 하 주세요 is ungrammatical; it must be 해 주세요."}},{title:"V-고 — Linking Food Actions in Order",func:`Use V-고 to chain two verbs done by the same person, usually in sequence: "do A and (then) B." Attach 고 directly to the verb stem — no vowel changes, no tense on the first verb. It's the everyday way to describe eating-and-doing routines.`,formTable:[{when:"Vowel-ending stem",add:"stem + 고",ex:"마시다 → 마시고 (drink and...)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem",add:"stem + 고",ex:"먹다 → 먹고 (eat and...)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 + 고",ex:"요리하다 → 요리하고 (cook and...)"},{when:"Tense goes on the LAST verb only",add:"V-고 + V-past/future",ex:"먹고 잤어요 (ate and slept)"}],examples:[{ko:"밥을 먹고 커피를 마셔요.",romanization:"babeul meokgo keopireul masyeoyo.",en:"I eat a meal and (then) drink coffee.",note:"Classic sequence; tense (present) appears only on the final verb 마셔요."},{ko:"손을 씻고 밥을 먹어요.",romanization:"soneul ssitgo babeul meogeoyo.",en:"I wash my hands and eat.",note:"Natural order-of-events use; 씻고 stays plain even though the action is habitual."},{ko:"라면을 끓이고 김치를 꺼냈어요.",romanization:"ramyeoneul kkeurigo gimchireul kkeonaesseoyo.",en:"I cooked ramyeon and took out the kimchi.",note:"Past tense 꺼냈어요 sits only on the last verb; 끓이고 carries no -었-."},{ko:"고기를 굽고 상추에 싸 먹어요.",romanization:"gogireul gupgo sangchue ssa meogeoyo.",en:"We grill the meat and wrap it in lettuce to eat.",note:"Describes the two-step BBQ routine; 굽고 links the grilling to the eating."}],keyPoint:{label:"V-고 vs. V-아서/어서 (sequence)",body:'Both can mean "and then," but 고 just lists separate actions in order (먹고 마셔요 = eat, then drink — different objects). 아서/어서 is used when action B builds on A with the same object (사서 먹어요 = buy it and eat it). For loosely sequenced food steps, 고 is the safe default.'},pronunciation:"After a stem ending in ㄱ, like 먹다, 먹고 is pronounced [먹꼬] — the 고 tenses to 꼬. Likewise 씻고 [씯꼬]. The 고 itself never changes spelling.",drill:{instruction:"Join the two actions with V-고. For a simple sequence, tense on the final verb is enough.",model:"손을 씻다 / 밥을 먹다 → 손을 씻고 밥을 먹어요.",items:["커피를 마시다 / 신문을 읽다 → ____","밥을 먹다 / 이를 닦다 → ____","라면을 끓이다 / 계란을 넣다 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"Past tense may appear only on the final verb with -고.",right:"밥을 먹고 커피를 마셨어요. / 밥을 먹었고 커피도 마셨어요.",explanation:"Both are grammatical. In a simple sequence, the final past ending can set the time for both actions, so 먹고 … 마셨어요 is concise and common. When listing completed facts separately or emphasizing each event, 먹었고 … 마셨어요 is also natural."}},{title:"A/V-아서/어서 — Reason and Result",func:`Use A/V-아서/어서 to give a reason: "because A, B." The first clause is the cause, the second is the result. Attach the 아/어 connector + 서 to the stem. With food you use it to explain why you can or can't eat something, or why you like a dish.`,formTable:[{when:"Stem vowel ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"stem + 아서",ex:"비싸다 → 비싸서 (because it's expensive)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"stem + 어서",ex:"맛있다 → 맛있어서 (because it's tasty)"},{when:"하다 words",add:"해서",ex:"매콤하다 → 매콤해서 (because it's nicely spicy)"},{when:"Past/future reason",add:"NO tense on the -아서/어서 clause",ex:"배가 고파서 먹었어요 (not 고팠어서)"}],examples:[{ko:"너무 매워서 못 먹겠어요.",romanization:"neomu maewoseo mot meokgesseoyo.",en:"It's so spicy that I can't eat it.",note:"맵다 → 매워서 (ㅂ irregular); the spiciness is the reason for not eating."},{ko:"이 집 김치찌개가 맛있어서 자주 와요.",romanization:"i jip gimchijjigaega masisseoseo jaju wayo.",en:"This place's kimchi stew is delicious, so I come often.",note:"맛있어서 explains the reason for the result 자주 와요 (come often)."},{ko:"배가 고파서 라면을 끓였어요.",romanization:"baega gopaseo ramyeoneul kkeuryeosseoyo.",en:"I was hungry, so I made ramyeon.",note:"고프다 → 고파서; even though the result is past, the reason clause stays plain (no 고팠어서)."},{ko:"커피를 많이 마셔서 잠이 안 와요.",romanization:"keopireul mani masyeoseo jami an wayo.",en:"I drank a lot of coffee, so I can't sleep.",note:"마시다 → 마셔서; everyday cause-and-result about a drink."}],keyPoint:{label:"-아서/어서 vs. -(으)니까 (reason)",body:`Both mean "because," but -아서/어서 cannot be followed by a command or suggestion, and never takes past/future tense in the reason clause. Use -아서/어서 for a plain reason → result (매워서 못 먹어요). When you want to follow with a command or suggestion ("It's spicy, so order something else"), switch to -(으)니까.`},pronunciation:"맛있어서 is pronounced [마시써서] — the ㅅ of 맛 links forward and the double ㅆ stays tense. Say it as one smooth 마시써서, not 맛-있-어-서.",drill:{instruction:"Combine the two clauses with -아서/어서. Keep the reason clause plain (no past tense on it).",model:"맛있다 / 자주 와요 → 맛있어서 자주 와요.",items:["배가 고프다 / 라면을 끓였어요 → ____","너무 맵다 / 못 먹어요 → ____","커피가 진하다 / 잠이 안 와요 → ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"배가 고팠어서 라면을 먹었어요.",right:"배가 고파서 라면을 먹었어요.",explanation:'English puts past tense on both clauses ("was hungry, so ate"), so learners write 고팠어서. The -아서/어서 reason clause never carries -었-; the tense lives only in the result clause (먹었어요).'}},{title:"V-(으)ㄹ게요 — Saying What You'll Have",func:`Use V-(으)ㄹ게요 to announce a decision you're making on the spot — "I'll have / I'll do ___." At the table it's how you tell the server or your friends what you've chosen, with a nuance of committing to it right now. Add -ㄹ게요 to a vowel-ending stem, -을게요 to a consonant-ending stem.`,formTable:[{when:"Vowel-ending stem",add:"stem + ㄹ게요",ex:"시키다 → 시킬게요 (I'll order it.)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem",add:"stem + 을게요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을게요 (I'll have it.)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"할게요",ex:"주문하다 → 주문할게요 (I'll order.)"},{when:"ㄹ-ending stem (drop nothing extra)",add:"stem + 게요",ex:"만들다 → 만들게요 (I'll make it.)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",romanization:"jeoneun bibimbap meogeulgeyo.",en:"I'll have bibimbap.",note:'Announcing your own choice at the table; 저는 marks "as for me" when each person picks a dish.'},{ko:"저기요, 주문할게요.",romanization:"jeogiyo, jumunhalgeyo.",en:"Excuse me, we're ready to order.",note:"저기요 calls the server; 주문할게요 signals you've decided and want to order now."},{ko:"그럼 저는 냉면 시킬게요.",romanization:"geureom jeoneun naengmyeon sikilgeyo.",en:"Then I'll order naengmyeon.",note:'시키다 ("to order/make happen") → 시킬게요; 그럼 ("then") reacts to what others chose.'},{ko:"제가 계산할게요.",romanization:"jega gyesanhalgeyo.",en:"I'll pay.",note:`Offering to pay the bill; -ㄹ게요 carries the "I'll take care of it" promise to the listener.`}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ게요 vs. -(으)ㄹ래요",body:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is you committing to do something, often for the listener's sake (저는 비빔밥 먹을게요 = I've decided, I'll have bibimbap). -(으)ㄹ래요 is more about your wish/preference and can sound like you're asking or stating want (비빔밥 먹을래요 = I want bibimbap / shall I?). To simply state your order, -(으)ㄹ게요 is the smoother choice."},pronunciation:"-ㄹ게요 is pronounced [ㄹ께요] — the ㄱ tenses to ㄲ. So 먹을게요 is [머글께요] and 갈게요 is [갈께요]. The spelling stays 게, but you say 께.",drill:{instruction:"Say what you'll have using V-(으)ㄹ게요.",model:"비빔밥 / 먹다 → 저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",items:["냉면 / 시키다 → 저는 냉면 ____","커피 / 마시다 → 저는 커피 ____","계산 / 하다 → 제가 ____"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 비빔밥 먹을 거예요.",right:"저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",explanation:`-(으)ㄹ 거예요 states a future plan as information ("I'm going to eat bibimbap") and sounds detached when ordering. -(으)ㄹ게요 directs the decision at the listener — the server or your friends — which is what ordering needs. For "I'll have ___" at the table, use -(으)ㄹ게요.`}}],m=[{title:"V-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요 — Have you ever…? (past experience)",func:"Says you have had the experience of doing something at least once. The negative, V-(으)ㄴ 적이 없어요, means you have never done it. It is about whether something has ever happened, not about one specific past event.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -은 적이 있어요",ex:"듣다 → 들은 적이 있어요 (have heard it before)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -ㄴ 적이 있어요",ex:"보다 → 본 적이 있어요 (have seen it before)"},{when:"Negative (never done it)",add:"replace 있어요 with 없어요",ex:"가다 → 간 적이 없어요 (have never gone)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 그 드라마를 본 적이 있어요.",romanization:"jeoneun geu deuramareul bon jeogi isseoyo.",en:"I have seen that drama before.",note:"보다 ends in a vowel, so just -ㄴ 적이 있어요 — talking about a past viewing experience."},{ko:"그 가수 콘서트에 간 적이 있어요?",romanization:"geu gasu konseoteue gan jeogi isseoyo?",en:"Have you ever been to that singer's concert?",note:"As a question it asks about life experience, not one specific event you both know about."},{ko:"이 게임은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없어요.",romanization:"i geimeun han beondo hae bon jeogi eopseoyo.",en:"I have never played this game even once.",note:"없어요 makes it the 'never' negative; 한 번도 reinforces 'not even once.'"},{ko:"유튜브에 영상을 올린 적이 있어요.",romanization:"yutyubeue yeongsangeul ollin jeogi isseoyo.",en:"I have uploaded a video to YouTube before.",note:"올리다 ends in a vowel → 올린; the experience of having uploaded at some point."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요 vs. plain past -았/었어요",body:"Plain past 봤어요 reports one specific event ('I watched it yesterday'). 본 적이 있어요 reports the experience itself ('I have watched it at some point'). Use -(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요 when the time doesn't matter, only whether it has ever happened.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"적이 is pronounced [저기] (jeogi): the final ㄱ of 적 links onto the 이 of the next syllable.",drill:{instruction:"Turn each verb into a 'have you ever…?' question using -(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요.",model:"라이브 방송을 보다 → 라이브 방송을 본 적이 있어요?",items:["케이팝 노래를 듣다 → ___","웹툰을 그리다 → ___","온라인 게임에서 지다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 그 영화를 봤은 적이 있어요.",right:"저는 그 영화를 본 적이 있어요.",explanation:"English speakers add 었 because the meaning feels 'past.' But -(으)ㄴ already carries the past sense; attach it straight to the plain stem (보 → 본), never to the -았/었 form."}},{title:"A/V-는 것 같아요 — I think… / It seems… (soft impression)",func:"Softens a statement into an impression or guess: 'it seems,' 'I think,' 'probably.' It makes you sound less blunt and more polite, which is why Korean speakers use it constantly even when fairly sure.",formTable:[{when:"Verb, present",add:"+ -는 것 같아요",ex:"보다 → 보는 것 같아요 (seems to be watching)"},{when:"Adjective, present",add:"+ -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있는 것 같아요 / 좋다 → 좋은 것 같아요 (seems good)"},{when:"Verb, past",add:"+ -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",ex:"끝나다 → 끝난 것 같아요 (seems to have ended)"},{when:"Any, future/guess",add:"+ -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있을 것 같아요 (looks like it'll be fun)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 드라마가 요즘 제일 인기 있는 것 같아요.",romanization:"i deuramaga yojeum jeil ingi inneun geot gatayo.",en:"I think this drama is the most popular these days.",note:"있다 takes the verb-style -는 것 같아요; softens a strong claim into an opinion."},{ko:"새 앨범이 정말 좋은 것 같아요.",romanization:"sae aelbeomi jeongmal joeun geot gatayo.",en:"I think the new album is really good.",note:"좋다 is an adjective → -은 것 같아요; gives a gentle personal opinion."},{ko:"댓글을 보니까 사람들이 화난 것 같아요.",romanization:"daetgeureul bonikka saramdeuri hwanan geot gatayo.",en:"Judging by the comments, people seem to be angry.",note:"화나다 here is a completed state → past -ㄴ 것 같아요 ('have gotten angry')."},{ko:"이번 주말에 영화관이 붐빌 것 같아요.",romanization:"ibeon jumare yeonghwagwani bumbil geot gatayo.",en:"The movie theater will probably be crowded this weekend.",note:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 is a guess about the future you can't see yet."}],keyPoint:{label:"Present -는 것 같아요 vs. future -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"Use -는 것 같아요 for something happening or true now ('seems to be ~ing'). Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 for a prediction about something not yet visible ('will probably ~'). Same softener, different time: 보는 것 같아요 = 'seems to be watching it'; 볼 것 같아요 = 'will probably watch it.'",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"것 같아요 is pronounced [걷 가타요] → naturally [걷까타요]: the final ㅅ becomes [t] and tenses the following ㄱ to [ㄲ].",drill:{instruction:"Make each statement softer with the correct form of -는 것 같아요.",model:"이 노래가 좋다 → 이 노래가 좋은 것 같아요.",items:["그 배우가 연기를 잘하다 → ___","방송이 벌써 끝나다 (past) → ___","내일 콘서트 표가 매진되다 (future guess) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이 드라마가 재미있은 것 같아요.",right:"이 드라마가 재미있는 것 같아요.",explanation:"재미있다 and 맛있다 look like adjectives but contain 있다, so they pattern as verbs here: use -는 것 같아요, not the adjective -은 것 같아요."}},{title:"V/A-다고 했어요 — They said that… (reported speech)",func:"Reports what someone else said (or what you read or heard). It quotes a statement indirectly: 'He said that it's fun.' Used constantly for relaying news, comments, and messages.",formTable:[{when:"Verb, present",add:"+ -ㄴ/는다고 했어요",ex:"보다 → 본다고 했어요 / 먹다 → 먹는다고 했어요 (said they watch/eat)"},{when:"Adjective, present",add:"+ -다고 했어요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있다고 했어요 (said it's fun)"},{when:"Past (verb or adjective)",add:"+ -았/었다고 했어요",ex:"끝나다 → 끝났다고 했어요 (said it ended)"},{when:"Noun statement",add:"이다 → -(이)라고 했어요",ex:"가수 → 가수라고 했어요 (said they're a singer)"}],examples:[{ko:"친구가 그 영화 진짜 재미있다고 했어요.",romanization:"chinguga geu yeonghwa jinjja jaemiitdago haesseoyo.",en:"My friend said that movie is really fun.",note:"재미있다 is an adjective, so plain -다고 했어요 with no 는."},{ko:"동생이 새 게임을 한다고 했어요.",romanization:"dongsaengi sae geimeul handago haesseoyo.",en:"My younger sibling said they're going to play a new game.",note:"하다 is a verb ending in a vowel → -ㄴ다고 했어요 (한다고)."},{ko:"뉴스에서 그 가수가 컴백했다고 했어요.",romanization:"nyuseueseo geu gasuga keombaekhaetdago haesseoyo.",en:"The news said that singer made a comeback.",note:"Past tense 했 + -다고 했어요; the source (뉴스에서) is marked with 에서."},{ko:"그 사람이 인기 유튜버라고 했어요.",romanization:"geu sarami ingi yutyubeorago haesseoyo.",en:"They said that person is a popular YouTuber.",note:"유튜버 is a noun → -(이)라고 했어요; ends in a vowel so 라고, not 이라고."}],keyPoint:{label:"Verb -ㄴ/는다고 vs. adjective -다고",body:"The trap is the present tense. Verbs insert 는/ㄴ: 먹는다고, 본다고. Adjectives do NOT: 좋다고, 재미있다고. Past tense erases the difference — both verbs and adjectives use -았/었다고 (봤다고, 좋았다고).",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"-다고 했어요: 했어요 is said [해써요], and the whole reporting tail comes out quickly and flatly, lower in pitch than the quoted part.",drill:{instruction:"Report each sentence with -다고 했어요.",model:'"콘서트가 멋있어요" → 콘서트가 멋있다고 했어요.',items:['"저는 매일 웹툰을 봐요" → ___ (said they watch every day)','"그 앨범이 좋았어요" → ___ (said it was good)','"제 친구는 게이머예요" → ___ (said their friend is a gamer)']},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구가 영화를 본다고 말했어요 라고 했어요.",right:"친구가 영화를 본다고 했어요.",explanation:"English 'said that' makes learners reach for an extra verb. In Korean, -다고 했어요 already contains 'said.' Don't double it up; just attach -다고 to the quoted clause and end with 했어요."}},{title:"A/V-지만 — but / although (clean contrast)",func:"Joins two clauses in a straightforward 'A, but B' contrast. Neutral and clear, it works in both speech and writing, much like English 'but' or 'although.'",formTable:[{when:"Verb or adjective stem",add:"+ -지만",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있지만 (is fun, but)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었 + -지만",ex:"봤다 → 봤지만 (watched it, but)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"+ -(이)지만",ex:"가수 → 가수지만 (is a singer, but)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 드라마는 길지만 정말 재미있어요.",romanization:"i deuramaneun giljiman jeongmal jaemiisseoyo.",en:"This drama is long, but it's really fun.",note:"길다 + 지만; clean contrast between 'long' and 'fun.'"},{ko:"그 게임을 좋아하지만 시간이 없어요.",romanization:"geu geimeul joahajiman sigani eopseoyo.",en:"I like that game, but I don't have time.",note:"좋아하다 (verb) + 지만; one clause for liking, one for the obstacle."},{ko:"표를 예매했지만 못 갔어요.",romanization:"pyoreul yemaehaetjiman mot gasseoyo.",en:"I booked the ticket, but I couldn't go.",note:"Past -했 + 지만 contrasts a completed action with the result."},{ko:"그 사람은 유명한 가수지만 저는 노래를 몰라요.",romanization:"geu sarameun yumyeonghan gasujiman jeoneun noraereul mollayo.",en:"That person is a famous singer, but I don't know their songs.",note:"Noun 가수 + 지만; contrasts fame with the speaker's unfamiliarity."}],keyPoint:{label:"-지만 (one sentence) vs. 하지만 (sentence-starter)",body:"-지만 is a connector glued onto a verb or adjective inside one sentence: 좋지만 비싸요. 하지만 is a standalone conjunction that starts a new sentence: 좋아요. 하지만 비싸요. Same meaning 'but,' different position — don't put -지만 at the start of a sentence or 하지만 in the middle of one.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"-지만 is said evenly as [지만]; in 봤지만 the ㅅ tenses the ㅈ, giving [봗찌만].",drill:{instruction:"Join the two ideas into one sentence with -지만.",model:"이 영화 / 무섭다 + 재미있다 → 이 영화는 무섭지만 재미있어요.",items:["이 게임 / 어렵다 + 재미있다 → ___","표를 사다(past) + 시간이 없어서 못 가다 → ___","그 배우 / 유명한 사람이다 + 저는 잘 모르다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이 드라마는 길어요지만 재미있어요.",right:"이 드라마는 길지만 재미있어요.",explanation:"Learners attach -지만 to the full polite form (길어요지만). -지만 goes on the plain stem instead: 길다 → 길지만. For past, use 봤지만, not 봤어요지만."}},{title:"V-(으)ㄹ게요 — I'll do it (promise / casual intention)",func:"States what the speaker will do, often as a light promise or a response to the listener: 'I'll watch it,' 'I'll send it to you.' It is speaker-only (you can't use it for someone else) and signals you're deciding in reaction to the situation. Extremely common in texting and chatting.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -ㄹ게요",ex:"보다 → 볼게요 (I'll watch it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -을게요",ex:"찾다 → 찾을게요 (I'll look it up)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop nothing, add 게요)",add:"+ -게요",ex:"만들다 → 만들게요 (I'll make it)"}],examples:[{ko:"그 드라마 오늘 밤에 볼게요.",romanization:"geu deurama oneul bame bolgeyo.",en:"I'll watch that drama tonight.",note:"보다 ends in a vowel → 볼게요; a personal decision about what I'll do."},{ko:"링크 보내 줄게요.",romanization:"lingkeu bonae julgeyo.",en:"I'll send you the link.",note:"Very common in chat; 보내 주다 → 보내 줄게요, promising to do it for the listener."},{ko:"제가 영상 편집할게요.",romanization:"jega yeongsang pyeonjipalgeyo.",en:"I'll edit the video.",note:"하다 → 할게요; offering to take on the task yourself."},{ko:"댓글 확인하고 답장할게요.",romanization:"daetgeul hwaginhago datjanghalgeyo.",en:"I'll check the comments and reply.",note:"Two actions chained with -고, then -ㄹ게요 for the promise to reply."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ게요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요",body:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is a promise made to the listener, decided on the spot ('okay, I'll do it'). -(으)ㄹ 거예요 is a plain statement of plan or prediction and can describe anyone ('I/he will probably do it'). Use 볼게요 when responding to someone; 볼 거예요 when just stating your plan.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"-ㄹ게요 is pronounced [ㄹ께요]: the ㄱ is tensed to [ㄲ], so 볼게요 sounds like [볼께요].",drill:{instruction:"Answer each situation with a -(으)ㄹ게요 promise using the given verb.",model:"친구가 영화를 추천했어요. (보다) → 그럼 제가 볼게요.",items:["친구가 노래를 추천했어요. (듣다) → ___","사진이 필요해요. (보내다) → ___","게임 같이 하고 싶어요. (만들다 / 방을 만들다) → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구가 그 드라마를 볼게요.",right:"친구가 그 드라마를 볼 거예요.",explanation:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is only for the speaker's own promise. To talk about what someone else will do, switch to -(으)ㄹ 거예요. You can't promise on another person's behalf with -ㄹ게요."}},{title:"V-아/어 주세요 — Please do it (for me)",func:"Asks someone to do something for you, politely: 'please send it,' 'please recommend one.' The 주다 ('give') part adds the 'for me / for my benefit' nuance, making it warmer than a bare command. The everyday way to make digital-life requests.",formTable:[{when:"Stem vowel ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"+ -아 주세요",ex:"찾다 → 찾아 주세요 (please find it)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"+ -어 주세요",ex:"보내다 → 보내 주세요 (please send it)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 → 해 주세요",ex:"추천하다 → 추천해 주세요 (please recommend one)"}],examples:[{ko:"재미있는 드라마 좀 추천해 주세요.",romanization:"jaemiinneun deurama jom chucheonhae juseyo.",en:"Please recommend a fun drama.",note:"추천하다 → 추천해 주세요; 좀 softens the request even more."},{ko:"그 영상 링크 보내 주세요.",romanization:"geu yeongsang lingkeu bonae juseyo.",en:"Please send me that video link.",note:"보내다 → 보내 주세요; a typical chat request to do something for you."},{ko:"비밀번호 좀 찾아 주세요.",romanization:"bimilbeonho jom chaja juseyo.",en:"Please find the password for me.",note:"찾다 has stem vowel ㅏ → 찾아 주세요."},{ko:"잠깐만 음악 좀 꺼 주세요.",romanization:"jamkkanman eumak jom kkeo juseyo.",en:"Please turn the music off for a moment.",note:"끄다 → 꺼 주세요 (ㅡ drops before 어); asking someone to do it for your sake."}],keyPoint:{label:"-아/어 주세요 (do it for me) vs. -(으)세요 (plain polite command)",body:"-(으)세요 is a polite instruction with no 'for me' feeling: 여기 앉으세요 ('please sit here'). Adding 주다 → -아/어 주세요 frames it as a favor done for the speaker: 사진 보내 주세요 ('send me the photo, please'). For requests where someone helps you, use -아/어 주세요.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"주세요 is said smoothly as [주세요]; keep the 아/어 and 주 as two clear beats (보내 주세요 = bo-nae ju-se-yo), not crushed together.",drill:{instruction:"Turn each verb into a polite '(do it) for me' request with -아/어 주세요.",model:"링크를 보내다 → 링크를 보내 주세요.",items:["좋은 노래를 추천하다 → ___","이 사진을 찾다 → ___","소리를 조금 줄이다 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"드라마 추천 주세요.",right:"드라마 추천해 주세요.",explanation:"Learners drop the verb and attach 주세요 to a noun. 주세요 needs an -아/어 verb form in front: 추천하다 → 추천해 주세요. (주세요 by itself only works with a noun meaning 'please give me ~,' e.g. 물 주세요.)"}},{title:"V/A-거든요 — you see, … (a reason or background)",func:`-거든요 ends a sentence to offer a reason or new background the listener doesn't know yet — like "you see" or "because…". It often answers a real or implied "why?"`,formTable:[{when:"Verb/adjective stem (present)",add:"-거든요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있거든요 · 좋아하다 → 좋아하거든요"},{when:"Past",add:"-았/었거든요",ex:"보다 → 봤거든요 (I watched it, you see)"},{when:"Noun + (이)거든요",add:"(이)거든요",ex:"팬이다 → 팬이거든요 (I'm a fan, you see)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 드라마 꼭 보세요. 정말 재미있거든요.",romanization:"i deurama kkok boseyo. jeongmal jaemiitgeodeunyo.",en:"You should definitely watch this drama — it's really fun, you see.",note:"거든요 gives the reason for the recommendation."},{ko:"그 노래 알아요. 제가 그 가수 팬이거든요.",romanization:"geu norae arayo. jega geu gasu paenigeodeunyo.",en:"I know that song — I'm a fan of that singer, you see.",note:"Noun + 이거든요 supplies background."},{ko:"어제 그 영화 봤거든요. 진짜 슬펐어요.",romanization:"eoje geu yeonghwa bwatgeodeunyo. jinjja seulpeosseoyo.",en:"I watched that movie yesterday, you see — it was really sad.",note:"Past 봤거든요 sets up the next comment."},{ko:"지금 통화 못 해요. 영화 보는 중이거든요.",romanization:"jigeum tonghwa mot haeyo. yeonghwa boneun jungigeodeunyo.",en:"I can't talk right now — I'm in the middle of a movie, you see.",note:"거든요 explains the refusal."}],keyPoint:{label:"거든요 (new info) vs 잖아요 (shared info)",body:"Both give a reason, but 거든요 tells the listener something NEW they didn't know (재미있거든요), while 잖아요 appeals to something you BOTH already know (재미있잖아요). Use 거든요 to introduce a reason, 잖아요 to remind."},pronunciation:"재미있거든요: the ㅆ+ㄱ tightens and the ending links — roughly [재미읻꺼든뇨].",drill:{instruction:"Add a reason with -거든요.",model:"(이 드라마 재미있다) → 이 드라마 재미있거든요.",items:["(저 그 가수 좋아하다) → ______","(어제 그 영화 보다 — past) → ______","(지금 바쁘다) → ______"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"재미있거든요? (rising, like a question)",right:"재미있거든요. (falling, a statement)",explanation:"거든요 is not a question. Say it with a falling, confiding tone — it sets up a reason or a follow-up, not a yes/no question."}}],g=[{title:"V-아/어야 해요 — have to / must",func:"Expresses obligation or necessity: something you have to do. In school life it covers class rules, assignments, deadlines, and things teachers require of students.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"+ -아야 해요",ex:"가다 → 가야 해요 (have to go)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in any other vowel",add:"+ -어야 해요",ex:"먹다 → 먹어야 해요 (have to eat)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 → 해야 해요",ex:"공부하다 → 공부해야 해요 (have to study)"},{when:"Spoken variant, same meaning",add:"+ -아/어야 돼요",ex:"내다 → 내야 돼요 (have to hand it in)"}],examples:[{ko:"내일까지 숙제를 내야 해요.",romanization:"naeilkkaji sukjereul naeya haeyo.",en:"I have to hand in the homework by tomorrow.",note:"내다 (to hand in) is the core homework verb; -까지 marks the deadline."},{ko:"수업 시간에는 휴대폰을 꺼야 해요.",romanization:"sueop siganeneun hyudaeponeul kkeoya haeyo.",en:"During class you have to turn off your phone.",note:"끄다 has a ㅡ stem; ㅡ drops before -어야, giving 꺼야 — a common spelling to notice."},{ko:"시험이 있어서 오늘 도서관에 가야 해요.",romanization:"siheomi isseoseo oneul doseogwane gaya haeyo.",en:"I have a test, so I have to go to the library today.",note:"가다 ends in ㅏ, so it takes -아야; 도서관 keeps it in the school setting."},{ko:"교실에서는 한국어로 말해야 해요.",romanization:"gyosireseoneun hangugeoro malhaeya haeyo.",en:"In the classroom you have to speak in Korean.",note:"말하다 is a 하다 verb → 해야; a typical classroom rule."}],keyPoint:{label:"-아/어야 해요 vs. -아/어야 돼요",body:"Both mean 'have to,' and in everyday speech they are interchangeable. -아/어야 돼요 sounds a touch more colloquial and is very common when talking; -아/어야 해요 is a little more neutral and shows up more in writing. Pick whichever you hear most — the meaning does not change."},pronunciation:"말해야 is pronounced [마래야]: the ㅎ weakens between vowels. 해야 해요 keeps two clear 해 syllables — don't blur them into one.",drill:{instruction:"Change each verb to the -아/어야 해요 form to state a school obligation.",model:"공부하다 → 공부해야 해요.",items:["책을 읽다 → 책을 ___ (have to read the book)","교복을 입다 → 교복을 ___ (have to wear the uniform)","일찍 오다 → 일찍 ___ (have to come early)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"숙제를 내야 하는 게 필요해요.",right:"숙제를 내야 해요.",explanation:"English 'I need to / it's necessary' tempts learners to add 필요해요. Korean already builds the necessity into -아/어야 해요, so adding 필요하다 on top is redundant and unnatural."}},{title:"V-기로 했어요 — decided to V",func:"States a decision, plan, or promise that you (or a group) have already made. Common when classmates agree on a study plan or you resolve to do something for school.",formTable:[{when:"Any verb, attach to the plain stem",add:"+ -기로 했어요",ex:"공부하다 → 공부하기로 했어요 (decided to study)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem (no change)",add:"+ -기로 했어요",ex:"읽다 → 읽기로 했어요 (decided to read)"},{when:"Negative decision",add:"안 V / V-지 않 + -기로 했어요",ex:"지각 안 하기로 했어요 (decided not to be late)"},{when:"A standing rule (let's make it a habit)",add:"+ -기로 해요",ex:"매일 복습하기로 해요 (let's review every day)"}],examples:[{ko:"친구들하고 같이 도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요.",romanization:"chingudeulhago gachi doseogwaneseo gongbuhagiro haesseoyo.",en:"I decided to study with my friends at the library.",note:"A shared decision; 하고 같이 marks who you made the plan with."},{ko:"이번 학기에는 결석하지 않기로 했어요.",romanization:"ibeon hakgieneun gyeolseokhaji anki-ro haesseoyo.",en:"This semester I decided not to be absent.",note:"Negative form uses -지 않기로; 결석하다 (to be absent) ties it to attendance."},{ko:"주말에 같이 시험공부 하기로 했어요.",romanization:"jumare gachi siheomgongbu hagiro haesseoyo.",en:"We decided to study for the test together on the weekend.",note:"A mutual promise between classmates — a very typical use."},{ko:"선생님이 다음 주에 시험을 보기로 했어요.",romanization:"seonsaengnimi daeum jue siheomeul bogiro haesseoyo.",en:"The teacher decided to give a test next week.",note:"The subject doesn't have to be 'I'; here the teacher made the decision."}],keyPoint:{label:"-기로 했어요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요",body:"-기로 했어요 highlights that a decision was made (often jointly or as a resolution). -(으)ㄹ 거예요 just states a future plan or prediction. Use -기로 했어요 when the point is 'we agreed/decided,' not merely 'I will.'"},pronunciation:"했어요 is pronounced [해써요] with a tense ㅆ. 않기로 is read [안키로]: the ㅎ of 않 fuses with the following ㄱ into an aspirated [키].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each plan into a decision with -기로 했어요.",model:"매일 단어를 외우다 → 매일 단어를 외우기로 했어요.",items:["동아리에 들다 → 동아리에 ___ (decided to join the club)","수업을 빠지지 않다 → 수업을 ___ (decided not to skip class)","도서관에서 만나다 → 도서관에서 ___ (decided to meet at the library)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"공부하는 것을 결정했어요.",right:"공부하기로 했어요.",explanation:"Learners translate 'decided to study' literally with 결정하다 + a nominalized clause. Native speakers package the decision into -기로 하다 instead. Use the idiomatic pattern, not a word-for-word 'decide that.'"}},{title:"V-기 시작하다 — start doing",func:"Says that an action begins or has begun. The verb is nominalized with -기 and followed by 시작하다 (to start). Useful for saying when you started studying, learning, or a new routine.",formTable:[{when:"Any verb stem + 시작하다",add:"+ -기 시작하다",ex:"공부하다 → 공부하기 시작하다 (start studying)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem (no change)",add:"+ -기 시작하다",ex:"읽다 → 읽기 시작하다 (start reading)"},{when:"Past (started, and it continues)",add:"+ -기 시작했어요",ex:"배우다 → 배우기 시작했어요 (started learning)"},{when:"Present / general statement",add:"+ -기 시작해요",ex:"듣다 → 듣기 시작해요 (start listening)"}],examples:[{ko:"작년부터 한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",romanization:"jangnyeonbuteo hangugeoreul baeugi sijakaesseoyo.",en:"I started learning Korean last year.",note:"-부터 marks the starting point in time, pairing naturally with -기 시작하다."},{ko:"수업이 끝나고 숙제를 하기 시작했어요.",romanization:"sueobi kkeunnago sukjereul hagi sijakaesseoyo.",en:"After class ended, I started doing my homework.",note:"Marks a clear 'began the action' moment right after a school event."},{ko:"요즘 시험 때문에 도서관에 다니기 시작했어요.",romanization:"yojeum siheom ttaemune doseogwane danigi sijakaesseoyo.",en:"These days, because of exams, I've started going to the library.",note:"다니다 (to go regularly) + 시작하다 = the start of a new routine."},{ko:"선생님이 설명하기 시작했어요.",romanization:"seonsaengnimi seolmyeonghagi sijakaesseoyo.",en:"The teacher started explaining.",note:"Any subject works; here the teacher is the one beginning the action."}],keyPoint:{label:"V-기 시작하다 vs. N을/를 시작하다",body:"Use V-기 시작하다 to start an action (a verb): 공부하기 시작했어요. To start a thing (a noun), use N을/를 시작하다: 수업을 시작해요 (start the class). Don't say 수업하기 시작하다 when you simply mean 수업을 시작하다."},pronunciation:"시작했어요 is read [시자캐써요]: the ㄱ of 작 + the ㅎ of 했 fuse into an aspirated [ㅋ], and 했어요 becomes [캐써요].",drill:{instruction:"Combine into one sentence using -기 시작했어요.",model:"한국어 / 배우다 → 한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",items:["단어 / 외우다 → 단어를 ___ (started memorizing words)","아침에 운동 / 하다 → 아침에 운동을 ___ (started exercising in the morning)","책 / 읽다 → 책을 ___ (started reading the book)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"한국어를 배우는 것을 시작했어요.",right:"한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",explanation:"English speakers reach for the heavier -는 것 nominalizer for 'started to learn.' With 시작하다, Korean prefers the lighter -기. -는 것을 시작하다 sounds clumsy and over-literal."}},{title:"N 동안 — for / during (a period)",func:"Marks a span of time over which something happens: 'for' a duration or 'during' a named period. Essential for saying how long you studied, or that something happened over vacation or a semester.",formTable:[{when:"After a duration (length of time)",add:"N 동안 = 'for N'",ex:"한 시간 동안 (for one hour)"},{when:"After a named period",add:"N 동안 = 'during N'",ex:"방학 동안 (during the vacation)"},{when:"With a verb ('while V-ing')",add:"V-는 동안",ex:"공부하는 동안 (while studying)"},{when:"No particle before 동안",add:"(do NOT add 에/을)",ex:"삼 년 동안 (for three years), not 삼 년에 동안"}],examples:[{ko:"두 시간 동안 도서관에서 공부했어요.",romanization:"du sigan dongan doseogwaneseo gongbuhaesseoyo.",en:"I studied at the library for two hours.",note:"두 시간 is a duration, so 동안 means 'for'; no particle between the number phrase and 동안."},{ko:"방학 동안 한국어 학원에 다녔어요.",romanization:"banghak dongan hangugeo hagwone danyeosseoyo.",en:"During vacation I went to a Korean language academy.",note:"방학 (school vacation) is a named period, so 동안 means 'during.'"},{ko:"시험을 보는 동안 휴대폰을 쓸 수 없어요.",romanization:"siheomeul boneun dongan hyudaeponeul sseul su eopseoyo.",en:"While taking the test, you can't use your phone.",note:"With a verb, use -는 동안 for 'while'; 시험을 보다 (to take a test) sets the school scene."},{ko:"삼 년 동안 한국어를 공부했어요.",romanization:"sam nyeon dongan hangugeoreul gongbuhaesseoyo.",en:"I studied Korean for three years.",note:"삼 년 (three years) is a duration → 'for'; again no particle before 동안."}],keyPoint:{label:"N 동안 (a stretch) vs. N에 (a point)",body:"Use 동안 for a span of time you do something over: 세 시간 동안 (for three hours). Use 에 for a single point in time: 세 시에 (at three o'clock). Don't mix them — 세 시간에 or 세 시간에 동안 are both wrong."},pronunciation:"동안 is two even syllables [동안]. 공부했어요 is [공부해써요], with the tense ㅆ in 했.",drill:{instruction:"Add 동안 (or -는 동안) to express the time span.",model:"한 시간 / 공부하다 → 한 시간 동안 공부했어요.",items:["방학 / 학원에 다니다 → 방학 ___ 학원에 다녔어요","두 시간 / 숙제를 하다 → 두 시간 ___ 숙제를 했어요","수업을 듣다 / 졸다 → 수업을 ___ 졸았어요 (dozed off while in class)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"세 시간에 동안 공부했어요.",right:"세 시간 동안 공부했어요.",explanation:"English 'for three hours' makes learners insert a particle, but 동안 attaches straight to the time noun with nothing in between. No 에, no 을 before 동안."}},{title:"V/A-(으)니까 — because / so (reason before a plan)",func:"Gives a reason that leads into a request, suggestion, or plan: 'since A, (let's) B.' In school talk it links why something matters to what you should do next.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -니까",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘니까 (since I'm busy)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -으니까",ex:"많다 → 많으니까 (since there are many)"},{when:"하다 verbs/adjectives",add:"하 + -니까",ex:"중요하다 → 중요하니까 (since it's important)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었 + -으니까",ex:"끝났다 → 끝났으니까 (since it ended)"}],examples:[{ko:"성적이 중요하니까 미리 준비해요.",romanization:"seongjeogi jungyohanikka miri junbihaeyo.",en:"Grades are important, so let's prepare in advance.",note:"Reason (중요하니까) leads into the suggestion."},{ko:"시험이 가까우니까 도서관에서 공부해요.",romanization:"siheomi gakkaunikka doseogwaneseo gongbuhaeyo.",en:"The exam is near, so I study at the library.",note:"가깝다 is ㅂ-irregular: 가까우니까."},{ko:"숙제가 많으니까 오늘 시작해요.",romanization:"sukjega maneunikka oneul sijakaeyo.",en:"There's a lot of homework, so let's start today.",note:"Consonant stem 많다 → 많으니까."},{ko:"수업이 끝났으니까 같이 밥 먹으러 가요.",romanization:"sueobi kkeunnasseunikka gachi bap meogeureo gayo.",en:"Class is over, so let's go eat together.",note:"Past 끝났 + -으니까; reason plus an invitation."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)니까 vs. -아/어서 for 'because'",body:"Both give reasons, but only -(으)니까 can lead into a command, suggestion, or 'let's' (시험이 가까우니까 공부해요). -아/어서 cannot: you can't say 시험이 가까워서 공부합시다. Use -(으)니까 when the next clause is a request or proposal."},pronunciation:"많으니까 is read [마느니까]: the ㅎ in 많 is silent before a vowel. 끝났으니까 is [끈나쓰니까].",drill:{instruction:"Join the reason and the plan with -(으)니까.",model:"시험이 가깝다 + 도서관에 가요 → 시험이 가까우니까 도서관에 가요.",items:["숙제가 많다 + 오늘 시작해요 → ___","성적이 중요하다 + 열심히 공부해요 → ___","수업이 끝났다 + 같이 가요 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"비가 와서 우산을 가져가세요.",right:"비가 오니까 우산을 가져가세요.",explanation:"When the second clause is a command or suggestion ('take an umbrella'), Korean needs -(으)니까, not -아/어서. -아/어서 only works before a plain statement, not before an order or 'let's.'"}},{title:"V-기 위해서 — in order to (purpose)",func:"Marks a deliberate goal: you do B in order to achieve A. Good for academic goals like passing, getting a scholarship, raising your grade, or getting into a program. Often shortened to -기 위해.",formTable:[{when:"Any verb, attach to the stem",add:"+ -기 위해서",ex:"합격하다 → 합격하기 위해서 (in order to pass)"},{when:"Common short form",add:"+ -기 위해",ex:"받다 → 받기 위해 (in order to get)"},{when:"With a noun goal",add:"N을/를 위해서",ex:"시험을 위해서 (for the exam)"},{when:"Negative goal (use 지 않기 위해서)",add:"V-지 않기 위해서",ex:"늦지 않기 위해서 (in order not to be late)"}],examples:[{ko:"좋은 성적을 받기 위해서 매일 공부해요.",romanization:"joeun seongjeogeul batgi wihaeseo maeil gongbuhaeyo.",en:"I study every day in order to get good grades.",note:"받다 → 받기 위해서; purpose plus the daily habit."},{ko:"장학금을 받기 위해 열심히 공부했어요.",romanization:"janghakgeumeul batgi wihae yeolsimhi gongbuhaesseoyo.",en:"I studied hard in order to get a scholarship.",note:"Short form -기 위해; a very common academic goal."},{ko:"시험에 합격하기 위해서 학원에 다녀요.",romanization:"siheome hapgyeokhagi wihaeseo hagwone danyeoyo.",en:"I go to an academy in order to pass the exam.",note:"합격하다 (to pass) + -기 위해서; 학원 is a private academy."},{ko:"지각하지 않기 위해서 일찍 일어나요.",romanization:"jigakhaji anki wihaeseo iljjik ireonayo.",en:"I get up early so as not to be late.",note:"Negative goal uses -지 않기 위해서; 지각하다 = to be late to school."}],keyPoint:{label:"V-기 위해서 vs. V-(으)러",body:"Both express purpose, but -(으)러 only attaches to motion verbs like 가다/오다 (공부하러 가요 = go to study). -기 위해서 has no such limit and works with any main verb (장학금을 받기 위해서 공부해요). For a goal that isn't 'going somewhere to do it,' use -기 위해서."},pronunciation:"받기 위해서 is read [받끼 위해서]: the ㄷ tenses the following ㄱ to [ㄲ]. 합격하기 is [합껴카기].",drill:{instruction:"State the goal with -기 위해서.",model:"좋은 성적을 받다 / 매일 공부해요 → 좋은 성적을 받기 위해서 매일 공부해요.",items:["장학금을 받다 / 열심히 공부해요 → ___","시험에 합격하다 / 학원에 다녀요 → ___","지각하지 않다 / 일찍 일어나요 → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"장학금을 받으려고 위해서 공부해요.",right:"장학금을 받기 위해서 공부해요.",explanation:"Learners mix two purpose patterns. Either use -(으)려고 (받으려고 공부해요) OR -기 위해서 (받기 위해서 공부해요), never both together. Don't stack 위해서 after a -(으)려고 ending."}}],y=[{title:"N(으)로 — by (a means of transport) / via (a route)",func:"Attach (으)로 to a noun to say what you travel by (버스로 = by bus) or which way you go (이 길로 = this way). In transportation talk it is the everyday particle for naming the vehicle, the subway line, or the road you take.",formTable:[{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"-로",ex:"택시 → 택시로 (by taxi)"},{when:"Noun ends in ㄹ",add:"-로",ex:"지하철 → 지하철로 (by subway)"},{when:"Noun ends in any other consonant",add:"-으로",ex:"오른쪽 → 오른쪽으로 (to the right)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 버스로 학교에 가요.",romanization:"jeoneun beoseuro hakgyoe gayo.",en:"I go to school by bus.",note:"버스 ends in a vowel, so plain -로 marks the means of transport."},{ko:"지하철로 가면 더 빨라요.",romanization:"jihacheollo gamyeon deo ppallayo.",en:"It's faster if you go by subway.",note:"지하철 ends in ㄹ, so it takes -로 (never -으로); the ㄹㄹ links smoothly as 'cheollo'."},{ko:"이 길로 가세요.",romanization:"i gillo gaseyo.",en:"Go this way (via this road).",note:"Here (으)로 marks a route, not a vehicle. 길 ends in ㄹ, so -로."},{ko:"공항까지 택시로 갈까요?",romanization:"gonghang-kkaji taeksiro galkkayo?",en:"Shall we go to the airport by taxi?",note:"택시 → -로 names the vehicle; 까지 marks the destination 'all the way to'."}],keyPoint:{label:"(으)로 가다 vs 을/를 타고 가다",body:"버스로 가요 and 버스를 타고 가요 both mean 'go by bus.' (으)로 simply labels the means in one word; 을/를 타고 literally says 'ride and go,' picturing you boarding. Use (으)로 for the quick, neutral 'by ___,' and 을/를 타고 when you want to stress actually getting on."},pronunciation:"After ㄹ the two ㄹ sounds link: 지하철로 is said [지하철로] as 'jihacheollo,' not 'jihacheoro.' Same for 이 길로 → 'gillo.'",drill:{instruction:"Add (으)로 to each word to mark the means or route of travel.",model:"기차 → 기차로 가요. (I go by train.)",items:["택시 → ___ 가요. (by taxi)","지하철 → ___ 가요. (by subway)","이 길 → ___ 가세요. (via this road)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 버스에 가요.",right:"저는 버스로 가요.",explanation:"English 'by bus' tempts learners to grab 에. But the means of transport always takes (으)로, never 에. 버스에 would point to the bus as a destination, like 'into the bus.'"}},{title:"V-(으)러 가요 — go (somewhere) in order to do something",func:"Attach -(으)러 to a verb stem before a movement verb (가다 / 오다 / 다니다) to give the purpose of the trip: you go or come somewhere in order to do that action. Very common for travel errands like buying tickets or topping up a card.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으러",ex:"표를 받다 → 표를 받으러 가요 (go to pick up the ticket)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-러",ex:"타다 → 타러 가요 (go to catch/board it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-러 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"(표를) 팔다 → 팔러 가요 (go to sell the ticket)"}],examples:[{ko:"기차표를 사러 역에 가요.",romanization:"gichapyoreul sareo yeoge gayo.",en:"I'm going to the station to buy a train ticket.",note:"사다 ends in a vowel → -러; the purpose (buying a ticket) comes before the movement verb 가요."},{ko:"친구를 만나러 부산에 가요.",romanization:"chingureul mannareo busane gayo.",en:"I'm going to Busan to meet a friend.",note:"만나다 → 만나러; the whole trip is for meeting the friend."},{ko:"버스를 타러 정류장에 뛰어갔어요.",romanization:"beoseureul tareo jeongnyujange ttwieogasseoyo.",en:"I ran to the bus stop to catch the bus.",note:"타다 → 타러; the destination keeps 에, the purpose clause stays separate."},{ko:"교통카드를 충전하러 편의점에 가요.",romanization:"gyotongkadeureul chungjeonhareo pyeonuijeome gayo.",en:"I'm going to the convenience store to top up my transit card.",note:"충전하다 → 충전하러; a real pre-ride errand."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)러 needs a 'go/come' verb",body:"-(으)러 must be followed by a movement verb (가다, 오다, 다니다). You cannot say 표를 사러 공부해요 — the main verb has to involve going or coming. If your main verb is not movement, use -(으)려고 instead (Note 3)."},pronunciation:"받으러 links the final ㄷ into the 으: say [바드러] 'badeureo,' not [받으러] with a hard stop.",drill:{instruction:"Join the task verb to -(으)러 and finish with 가요.",model:"표를 사다 → 표를 사러 가요.",items:["기차를 타다 → ___ 가요. (go to catch the train)","카드를 충전하다 → ___ 가요. (go to top up the card)","표를 받다 → ___ 가요. (go to pick up the ticket)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"표를 사기 위해 가요.",right:"표를 사러 가요.",explanation:"Learners reach for -기 위해 because it maps onto English 'in order to.' But for a physical errand with go/come, -(으)러 is far more natural and shorter. Save -기 위해 for abstract or formal goals."}},{title:"V-(으)려고 해요 — be planning / intending to do something",func:"Attach -(으)려고 하다 to a verb stem to say what you intend or plan to do. With travel it means 'I'm going to / I'm trying to' make the trip or booking. It states a decided intention, softer than the firm future -(으)ㄹ 거예요.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으려고 해요",ex:"표를 찾다 → 찾으려고 해요 (I plan to pick up the ticket)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-려고 해요",ex:"타다 → 타려고 해요 (I intend to take it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-려고 해요 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"(표를) 팔다 → 팔려고 해요 (I plan to sell the ticket)"}],examples:[{ko:"이번 주말에는 기차로 부산에 가려고 해요.",romanization:"ibeon jumareneun gicharo busane garyeogo haeyo.",en:"This weekend I'm planning to go to Busan by train.",note:"가다 → 가려고 해요; a decided personal plan, softer than 갈 거예요."},{ko:"차가 막혀서 지하철을 타려고 해요.",romanization:"chaga makhyeoseo jihacheoreul taryeogo haeyo.",en:"Traffic is bad, so I'm going to take the subway.",note:"타다 → 타려고 해요; an intention formed in reaction to the situation."},{ko:"부산 가는 KTX 표를 예매하려고 해요.",romanization:"busan ganeun KTX pyoreul yemaeharyeogo haeyo.",en:"I'm trying to book a KTX ticket to Busan.",note:"예매하다 → 예매하려고 해요; the main verb is not a movement verb, which is fine for -(으)려고."},{ko:"막차 시간을 확인하려고 했어요.",romanization:"makcha siganeul hwaginharyeogo haesseoyo.",en:"I was going to check the last-train time.",note:"Past 했어요 signals an intention that may not have happened — a planned-but-changed nuance."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)려고 vs -(으)러",body:"-(으)러 needs a movement main verb and means 'go/come TO DO something' (표를 사러 가요). -(으)려고 (해요) states the intention itself and works with ANY main verb (표를 사려고 해요 = 'I plan to buy a ticket'). If you can drop 가요/와요 and still have a full sentence, you want -(으)려고 해요."},pronunciation:"In casual speech people sometimes say 갈려고 with an extra ㄹ, but the standard form keeps one ㄹ: 가려고 'garyeogo,' not 갈려고.",drill:{instruction:"Turn each plan into a -(으)려고 해요 statement of intention.",model:"기차를 타다 → 기차를 타려고 해요.",items:["표를 예매하다 → ___. (plan to book the ticket)","지하철로 가다 → ___. (plan to go by subway)","표를 찾다 → ___. (plan to pick up the ticket)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"표를 사려고 가요. (meaning 'I plan to buy a ticket')",right:"표를 사려고 해요.",explanation:"To STATE the plan you need -(으)려고 해요 with 하다 at the end. 표를 사려고 가요 only works as 'I go in order to buy a ticket' (a purpose for going), overlapping with -(으)러. Decide: stating a plan → 해요; purpose for going → 가요/러."}},{title:"A/V-(으)면 — if / when (a travel condition happens)",func:"Attach -(으)면 to a verb or adjective stem to set up a condition: 'if/when ___, then ___.' In transportation it covers fallback plans: if the bus is delayed, if you miss the last train, if tickets are sold out.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-면",ex:"막히다 → 막히면 (if it's jammed)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으면",ex:"놓치다 is a vowel stem; consonant example: 없다 → 없으면 (if there isn't)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-면 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"길다 → 길면 (if it's long)"}],examples:[{ko:"길이 막히면 지하철을 타세요.",romanization:"giri makhimyeon jihacheoreul taseyo.",en:"If the road is jammed, take the subway.",note:"막히다 (vowel stem) → 막히면; the condition comes first, the advice second."},{ko:"버스를 놓치면 다음 버스를 기다려야 해요.",romanization:"beoseureul notchimyeon daeum beoseureul gidaryeoya haeyo.",en:"If you miss the bus, you have to wait for the next one.",note:"놓치다 → 놓치면; a real cause-and-effect."},{ko:"표가 매진이면 다음 기차를 확인하세요.",romanization:"pyoga maejinimyeon daeum gichareul hwaginhaseyo.",en:"If the tickets are sold out, check the next train.",note:"Noun state 매진 + 이면; for nouns use 이면 after a consonant."},{ko:"배차 간격이 길면 택시가 나아요.",romanization:"baecha gangyeogi gilmyeon taeksiga naayo.",en:"If the service interval is long, a taxi is better.",note:"Adjective stem 길- (ends in ㄹ) → 길면, ㄹ stays."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 vs -(으)니까",body:"-(으)면 is hypothetical 'if' (막히면 = if it gets jammed — may or may not happen). -(으)니까 gives a real, already-true reason 'since/because' (막히니까 = since it's jammed right now). Don't use -(으)면 for a present fact you can already see."},pronunciation:"없으면 links the ㅄ cluster: say [업쓰면] 'eopsseumyeon.' After a consonant stem, the buffer 으 is heard clearly: 먹으면 [머그면]. 놓치다 ends in a vowel, so it simply becomes 놓치면.",drill:{instruction:"Build an 'if' clause with -(으)면, then keep the given result.",model:"길이 막히다 → 길이 막히면 지하철을 타세요.",items:["버스를 놓치다 → ___ 다음 버스를 기다려야 해요.","표가 없다 → ___ 입석으로 갈 수 있어요.","배차 간격이 길다 → ___ 택시가 나아요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"지금 막히면 지하철을 타요. (meaning 'since it's jammed now')",right:"지금 막히니까 지하철을 타요.",explanation:"When the situation is already true right now, Korean uses -(으)니까 (since/because), not -(으)면. -(으)면 is for hypothetical or repeated conditions, not a fact you're looking at this moment."}},{title:"N 때문에 — because of (a transport cause)",func:"Use N 때문에 to name the noun reason that something happened or that a plan changed. In transportation it explains delays and disruptions: 지연 때문에 (because of a delay), 사고 때문에 (because of an accident).",formTable:[{when:"Noun cause (any ending)",add:"N 때문에",ex:"지연 → 지연 때문에 (because of a delay)"},{when:"Do NOT add 이/가 or 을/를",add:"(no particle before 때문에)",ex:"사고 때문에, not 사고가 때문에"},{when:"For a verb/adjective reason instead",add:"use -아/어서 or -(으)니까",ex:"막혀서 늦었어요 (was late because it was jammed)"}],examples:[{ko:"버스 지연 때문에 약속에 늦었어요.",romanization:"beoseu jiyeon ttaemune yaksoge neujeosseoyo.",en:"I was late for the appointment because of a bus delay.",note:"지연 is the noun cause; nothing goes between it and 때문에."},{ko:"사고 때문에 길이 많이 막혀요.",romanization:"sago ttaemune giri mani makhyeoyo.",en:"The road is very jammed because of an accident.",note:"사고 (accident) is a very common everyday traffic cause."},{ko:"비 때문에 택시가 안 잡혀요.",romanization:"bi ttaemune taeksiga an japhyeoyo.",en:"Because of the rain, I can't catch a taxi.",note:"비 (rain) + 때문에; a typical transport complaint."},{ko:"막차 때문에 지금 출발해야 해요.",romanization:"makcha ttaemune jigeum chulbalhaeya haeyo.",en:"Because of the last train, we have to leave now.",note:"The last-train schedule is the reason for hurrying."}],keyPoint:{label:"N 때문에 vs V/A-아서",body:"Use N 때문에 when the reason is a NOUN (지연 때문에). When the reason is a verb or adjective, switch to -아/어서: 지하철이 늦어서 (because the subway was late). You cannot put a full verb directly before 때문에 — you'd need to nominalize it (늦었기 때문에)."},pronunciation:"때문에 links the ㄴ into 에: say [때무네] 'ttaemune,' as one smooth phrase, not 'ttaemun-e' with a pause.",drill:{instruction:"Put the noun cause before 때문에 (no extra particle) and keep the result.",model:"지연 → 지연 때문에 늦었어요.",items:["사고 → ___ 길이 막혀요.","비 → ___ 택시가 안 잡혀요.","막차 → ___ 지금 출발해요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"지연이 때문에 늦었어요.",right:"지연 때문에 늦었어요.",explanation:"English 'because of the delay' has 'the,' which tempts learners to add a subject particle 이/가. But 때문에 attaches straight to the bare noun — no 이/가 and no 을/를 in front of it."}},{title:"V-(으)ㄹ 때 — when (the moment you do a travel action)",func:"Attach -(으)ㄹ 때 to a verb stem to name the moment an action happens: 'when transferring,' 'when booking,' 'when you ride.' It is the everyday way to give travel tips and warnings tied to a specific moment.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ 때",ex:"타다 → 탈 때 (when you ride)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"-을 때",ex:"갈아타다 → 갈아탈 때 (when you transfer); 받다 → 받을 때 (when you receive)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-ㄹ 때 (one ㄹ, the stem ㄹ drops into it)",ex:"놀다 → 놀 때; here use 만들다 → 만들 때"}],examples:[{ko:"환승할 때 출구 번호도 확인하세요.",romanization:"hwanseunghal ttae chulgu beonhodo hwaginhaseyo.",en:"When you transfer, check the exit number too.",note:"환승하다 → 환승할 때; a real subway-navigation tip."},{ko:"표를 예매할 때 편도인지 왕복인지 확인하세요.",romanization:"pyoreul yemaehal ttae pyeondoinji wangboginji hwaginhaseyo.",en:"When booking a ticket, check whether it's one-way or round-trip.",note:"예매하다 → 예매할 때; a useful ticket-buying check."},{ko:"버스를 탈 때 교통카드를 찍으세요.",romanization:"beoseureul tal ttae gyotongkadeureul jjigeuseyo.",en:"When you get on the bus, tap your transit card.",note:"타다 → 탈 때; an everyday boarding habit."},{ko:"막차를 탈 때는 시간을 꼭 확인해야 해요.",romanization:"makchareul tal ttaeneun siganeul kkok hwaginhaeya haeyo.",en:"When taking the last train, you really have to check the time.",note:"때 can take 는 (탈 때는) for topic contrast."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 때 (present/general) vs -았/었을 때 (completed)",body:"Plain -(으)ㄹ 때 names the moment of an ongoing or general action: 탈 때 = 'when (in the act of) riding.' Add past -았/었을 때 for a moment after something finished: 내렸을 때 = 'when I had gotten off.' For habits and instructions you almost always want plain -(으)ㄹ 때."},pronunciation:"The 때 after -ㄹ tenses to a hard sound: 탈 때 is said [탈 떼] 'tal ttae,' and 할 때 is 'hal ttae,' with a tightened ㄸ.",drill:{instruction:"Make a -(으)ㄹ 때 clause and keep the given advice.",model:"환승하다 → 환승할 때 출구 번호도 확인하세요.",items:["예매하다 → ___ 편도인지 왕복인지 확인하세요.","타다 → 버스를 ___ 교통카드를 찍으세요.","내리다 → 지하철에서 ___ 가방을 챙기세요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"환승하는 때 출구 번호를 확인하세요.",right:"환승할 때 출구 번호를 확인하세요.",explanation:"Learners often attach the present modifier -는 to 때, copying English 'the time when you transfer.' But 때 takes the -(으)ㄹ modifier, not -는: 환승할 때, never 환승하는 때."}}],p=[{title:'V/A-잖아요 — "you know (we both know this)"',func:"Reminds the listener of something you assume you both already know, or gently corrects them with a fact they should remember. It softens a reminder by framing it as shared knowledge rather than news — very common when explaining everyday manners and customs.",formTable:[{when:"Verb or Adjective stem (present)",add:"-잖아요",ex:"한국에서는 신발을 벗잖아요 (You take your shoes off in Korea, you know)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었잖아요",ex:"지난번에 제가 말했잖아요 (I told you last time, remember)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)잖아요",ex:"오늘 어버이날이잖아요 (It's Parents' Day today, you know)"}],examples:[{ko:"윗사람한테는 두 손으로 드리잖아요.",romanization:"witsaram-hante-neun du soneuro deurijanhayo.",en:"You hand things to elders with both hands, you know.",note:"Frames a manners rule as shared knowledge, so it sounds like a friendly reminder, not a lecture."},{ko:"집들이에는 보통 휴지나 세제를 사 가잖아요.",romanization:"jipdeurie-neun botong hyujina sejereul sa gajanhayo.",en:"You usually bring toilet paper or detergent to a housewarming, you know.",note:"Explains a custom the speaker assumes is familiar to both people."},{ko:"어제 같이 봤잖아요, 기억 안 나요?",romanization:"eoje gachi bwatjanhayo, gieok an nayo?",en:"We saw it together yesterday, remember? Don't you recall?",note:"Past form gently corrects the listener about a shared experience."},{ko:"지하철에서는 노약자석을 비워 두잖아요.",romanization:"jihacheoreseo-neun noyakjaseogeul biwo dujanhayo.",en:"You leave the priority seats empty on the subway, you know.",note:"Appeals to a publicly known norm to back up the speaker's point."}],keyPoint:{label:"-잖아요 vs. -거든요",body:"Use -잖아요 when you think the listener ALREADY knows it ('as you know'). Use -거든요 when it's NEW background the listener does not know yet ('the thing is…'). Same slot, opposite assumption about the listener."},pronunciation:"-잖아요 is pronounced [자나요]. The spelling keeps ㄶ, but the ㅎ is not heard before ㄴ; keep the ㄴ sound and use three clear syllables: 자-나-요.",drill:{instruction:"Complete each sentence with -잖아요 to remind the listener of a shared fact.",model:"한국에서는 어른이 먼저 드시잖아요. (Elders eat first in Korea, you know.)",items:["설날에는 어른들께 세배를 ___ (드리다)","어제 제가 이미 ___ (말하다, past)","그 식당은 일요일에 문을 ___ (닫다)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"한국에서는 신발을 벗어요, 잖아요.",right:"한국에서는 신발을 벗잖아요.",explanation:"-잖아요 is a sentence ending attached straight to the stem (벗 + 잖아요). It is not a tag word you stick after a finished -아요/-어요 sentence the way English adds 'right?' at the end."}},{title:`V/A-거든요 — "the thing is… (here's the background)"`,func:"Gives a reason or piece of background the listener does not yet know, often answering a real or expected 'why?'. It signals 'the thing is…' and is a soft, polite way to explain yourself in conversation.",formTable:[{when:"Verb or Adjective stem (present)",add:"-거든요",ex:"제가 매운 걸 못 먹거든요 (The thing is, I can't eat spicy food)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었거든요",ex:"어제 늦게 잤거든요 (I went to bed late yesterday, you see)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)거든요",ex:"오늘이 제 생일이거든요 (Today's my birthday, you see)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘은 일찍 가 볼게요. 시댁에 가야 하거든요.",romanization:"oneureun iljjik ga bolgeyo. sidaege gaya hageodeunyo.",en:"I'll head off early today. The thing is, I have to visit my in-laws.",note:"Gives an unknown reason for leaving early, softening the abruptness."},{ko:"꽃을 사 왔어요. 오늘 처음 인사 드리는 거거든요.",romanization:"kkocheul sa wasseoyo. oneul cheoeum insa deurineun geogeodeunyo.",en:"I brought flowers. It's the first time I'm greeting you properly, you see.",note:"Explains the gift by giving social context the host didn't know."},{ko:"술은 안 마실게요. 제가 운전해야 하거든요.",romanization:"sureun an masilgeyo. jega unjeonhaeya hageodeunyo.",en:"I won't drink. The thing is, I have to drive.",note:"Declines politely by offering a reason, which is socially expected."},{ko:"연락 못 드려서 죄송해요. 핸드폰이 고장 났거든요.",romanization:"yeollak mot deuryeoseo joesonghaeyo. haendeuponi gojang natgeodeunyo.",en:"Sorry I couldn't reach you. My phone broke, you see.",note:"Past form gives the unknown cause behind an apology."}],keyPoint:{label:"-거든요 vs. -아서/어서",body:"Both give reasons. -아서/어서 joins the reason to a clause in the SAME sentence ('Because A, B'). -거든요 ends its own sentence and offers the reason as new information the listener didn't have, so it usually stands alone as a reply."},pronunciation:"-거든요 is [거드뇨] in quick speech, but the careful form is [거든뇨]→[거든요]. Keep the 든 clear.",drill:{instruction:"Finish each reply with -거든요 to give the listener new background.",model:"저는 안 갈래요. 그날 약속이 있거든요. (I won't go — I have plans that day, you see.)",items:["선물을 준비했어요. 오늘이 집들이 ___ (이다)","고기를 안 먹어요. 제가 채식을 ___ (하다)","조금 늦었어요. 길이 많이 ___ (막히다, past)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"A stand-alone -아서요 reason is unnatural or incomplete.",right:"왜 안 가요? — 약속이 있어서요. / 약속이 있거든요.",explanation:"Both replies are natural. -아서요 gives a direct, polite reason and is extremely common in everyday answers. -거든요 presents the reason as background the listener may not know and can sound a little more explanatory. Choose the nuance instead of treating -아서요 as an error."}},{title:'V/A-는데요 — "soften a careful statement or question"',func:"Ends a turn on an open, non-final note that leaves room for the listener to react. It softens statements, eases into careful questions, and sets up background before a request — essential for polite, indirect social talk.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem (present)",add:"-는데요",ex:"지금 가는데요 (Well, I'm going now…)"},{when:"Adjective stem (present)",add:"-(으)ㄴ데요",ex:"좀 비싼데요 (It's a bit pricey, though…)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었는데요",ex:"벌써 끝났는데요 (It's already finished, though…)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"인데요",ex:"저는 처음인데요 (It's my first time, you see…)"}],examples:[{ko:"이거 집들이 선물로 드리려고 하는데요, 괜찮을까요?",romanization:"igeo jipdeuri seonmullo deuriryeogo haneundeyo, gwaenchaneulkkayo?",en:"I was thinking of giving this as a housewarming gift… would that be okay?",note:"Sets up the situation softly before a careful yes/no question."},{ko:"옷차림이 좀 캐주얼한데요, 그래도 괜찮아요?",romanization:"otcharimi jom kejueolhandeyo, geuraedo gwaenchanayo?",en:"My outfit is a bit casual, though… is that still all right?",note:"Adjective form softens a worry instead of stating it bluntly."},{ko:"호칭을 잘 모르는데요, 뭐라고 부르면 될까요?",romanization:"hochingeul jal moreuneundeyo, mworago bureumyeon doelkkayo?",en:"I'm not really sure what to call you… what should I say?",note:"Trails off so the request for help feels hesitant, not pushy."},{ko:"예약은 했는데요, 시간이 좀 늦을 것 같아요.",romanization:"yeyageun haetneundeyo, sigani jom neujeul geot gatayo.",en:"I did make a reservation, but… we might be a little late.",note:"Past -는데요 concedes one point before adding a softer caveat."}],keyPoint:{label:"Adjective -(으)ㄴ데요 vs. Verb -는데요",body:"Verbs always take -는데요 (가는데요, 먹는데요). Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ데요 (비싼데요, 좋은데요). 있다/없다 act like verbs: 있는데요, 없는데요. Sorting verb from adjective is the part learners trip on most."},pronunciation:"-는데요 is [는데요]. After ㄹ-final verb stems the ㄹ drops: 살다 → 사는데요 (not 살는데요).",drill:{instruction:"Add -는데요 / -(으)ㄴ데요 to soften each opener, then trail off.",model:"이 자리 제 자린데요… (Um, this is my seat, though…)",items:["지금 좀 ___, 나중에 전화드릴게요. (바쁘다, adjective)","예약을 ___, 확인 좀 부탁드려요. (하다, past verb)","여기 사진 ___, 한 장만 부탁드려도 될까요? (찍다, verb)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"좀 비싸는데요.",right:"좀 비싼데요.",explanation:"Learners over-apply the verb pattern -는데요 to adjectives. Adjectives (비싸다, 좋다, 크다) must use -(으)ㄴ데요, so it's 비싼데요, not 비싸는데요."}},{title:`V/A-(으)ㄴ/는 편이다 — "I tend to / I'm rather …"`,func:"Describes a general tendency rather than a hard fact — 'on the … side', 'tends to be …'. It hedges a judgment, which sounds modest and natural when you comment on people, habits, or customs without being too absolute.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem (present habit)",add:"-는 편이다",ex:"저는 일찍 가는 편이에요 (I tend to arrive early)"},{when:"Adjective stem (present)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 편이다",ex:"한국 사람들은 정이 많은 편이에요 (Koreans tend to be warm-hearted)"},{when:"Past verb (one-time result)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 편이다",ex:"선물을 많이 받은 편이에요 (I got rather a lot of gifts)"},{when:"Negative (verb)",add:"안/잘 안 …-는 편이다",ex:"술을 잘 안 마시는 편이에요 (I tend not to drink much)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 처음엔 호칭을 조심하는 편이에요.",romanization:"jeoneun cheoeumen hochingeul josimhaneun pyeonieyo.",en:"At first I tend to be careful with how I address people.",note:"Hedges a self-description so it sounds modest, not absolute."},{ko:"요즘 젊은 사람들은 명절에 한복을 잘 안 입는 편이에요.",romanization:"yojeum jeolmeun saramdeureun myeongjeore hanbogeul jal an imneun pyeonieyo.",en:"These days young people tend not to wear hanbok on the holidays.",note:"A soft generalization about a changing custom, leaving room for exceptions."},{ko:"그 선배님은 후배들을 잘 챙기는 편이에요.",romanization:"geu seonbaenimeun hubaedeureul jal chaenggineun pyeonieyo.",en:"That senior tends to look out for the juniors.",note:"Comments on someone's habit without stating it as a fixed rule."},{ko:"저는 분위기가 어색하면 먼저 말을 거는 편이에요.",romanization:"jeoneun bunwigiga eosaekamyeon meonjeo mareul geoneun pyeonieyo.",en:"When the mood is awkward, I tend to be the one who speaks up first.",note:"Describes a personal habit in a social setting, softly."}],keyPoint:{label:"Verb 는 편 vs. Adjective (으)ㄴ 편",body:"With a verb, use -는 편이다 for a habit (가는 편, 챙기는 편). With an adjective, use -(으)ㄴ 편이다 for a quality (많은 편, 비싼 편). 편이다 means 'leans that way', so never use it for a one-time event like '오늘 갔어요'."},pronunciation:"편이에요 is [펴니에요]; the ㄴ links into the next syllable. Keep 편 short, not 'pyeon-i'.",drill:{instruction:"Turn each fact into a softer tendency with -는/-(으)ㄴ 편이에요.",model:"저는 / 일찍 자다 → 저는 일찍 자는 편이에요. (I tend to sleep early.)",items:["저는 / 선물을 미리 챙기다 (verb habit)","우리 가족은 / 정이 많다 (adjective)","저는 / 술을 잘 안 마시다 (negative verb)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"한국 사람들은 정이 많는 편이에요.",right:"한국 사람들은 정이 많은 편이에요.",explanation:"많다 is an adjective, so it takes -(으)ㄴ 편이다 → 많은 편이에요, not the verb ending -는 편. Check whether the word is a verb or an adjective before you pick 는 or (으)ㄴ."}},{title:`V/A-(으)니까 — "since (so here's what to do)"`,func:"Gives a reason that leads into advice, a request, or a decision: 'since X, (let's / please / I'll) …'. Unlike -아서/어서, it's the natural choice when the second clause is a suggestion or command — very common when explaining the reason behind a social custom.",formTable:[{when:"Verb/Adjective stem ending in a vowel",add:"-니까",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘니까 미리 챙기세요 (Since you're busy, prepare it in advance)"},{when:"Verb/Adjective stem ending in a consonant",add:"-으니까",ex:"많다 → 사람이 많으니까 조심하세요 (Since there are many people, be careful)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었으니까",ex:"덕분에 잘 됐으니까 감사 인사를 하세요 (Since it went well thanks to them, say thank you)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)니까",ex:"오늘이 첫날이니까 일찍 가요 (Since today's the first day, let's go early)"}],examples:[{ko:"집들이니까 작은 선물이라도 챙겨 가세요.",romanization:"jipdeurinikka jageun seonmurirado chaenggyeo gaseyo.",en:"Since it's a housewarming, take at least a small gift.",note:"Reason leading straight into advice — the classic -(으)니까 use."},{ko:"어르신도 계시니까 말투를 조심해요.",romanization:"eoreusindo gyesinikka maltureul josimhaeyo.",en:"Since there are elders there too, be careful with your tone.",note:"계시다 keeps it respectful; -(으)니까 ties the reason to the action."},{ko:"아직 잘 모르니까 호칭을 먼저 여쭤볼게요.",romanization:"ajik jal moreunikka hochingeul meonjeo yeojjwobolgeyo.",en:"Since I'm not sure yet, I'll ask first how to address them.",note:"Reason + the speaker's own decision (-ㄹ게요)."},{ko:"분위기가 좀 어색하니까 제가 먼저 말을 걸어 볼게요.",romanization:"bunwigiga jom eosaekanikka jega meonjeo mareul georeo bolgeyo.",en:"Since the mood is a bit awkward, I'll be the one to break the ice.",note:"-(으)니까 supports a decision to act."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)니까 vs. -아서/어서",body:"Both mean 'because', but only -(으)니까 can lead into a command or suggestion: 늦었으니까 빨리 가세요 (O), 늦어서 빨리 가세요 (X). Use -아서/어서 for plain cause-and-result, -(으)니까 when you're telling someone what to do because of the reason."},pronunciation:"-(으)니까 is [(으)니까] with a tense 까. Don't soften it to '니가'; the double ㄲ stays strong.",drill:{instruction:"Join the reason and the advice with -(으)니까.",model:"집들이다 + 선물을 챙기세요 → 집들이니까 선물을 챙기세요.",items:["어르신도 계시다 + 호칭을 조심하세요","처음 만나다 + 존댓말을 쓰세요","길이 막히다 (past) + 일찍 출발해요"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"늦어서 빨리 가세요.",right:"늦었으니까 빨리 가세요.",explanation:"When the second clause is a command or suggestion (가세요, 합시다, -ㄹ게요), Korean needs -(으)니까, not -아서/어서. -아서/어서 only works before a plain statement, so 'Since we're late, please hurry' must use 늦었으니까."}},{title:`A/V-지만 — "… but (I'll still do the polite thing)"`,func:"Admits one fact, then turns to a contrasting choice: 'A, but B'. In social situations the second clause usually shows the careful or considerate option, so it's handy for explaining why you act politely even when you don't strictly have to.",formTable:[{when:"Verb or Adjective stem",add:"-지만",ex:"바쁘지만 갈게요 (I'm busy, but I'll go)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었지만",ex:"늦었지만 인사는 드렸어요 (I was late, but I did greet them)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)지만",ex:"동갑이지만 존댓말을 써요 (We're the same age, but I use polite speech)"}],examples:[{ko:"동갑이지만 처음엔 존댓말을 써요.",romanization:"donggabijiman cheoeumen jondaenmareul sseoyo.",en:"We're the same age, but at first I use polite speech.",note:"Being the same age doesn't automatically mean casual speech."},{ko:"조금 어색하지만 제가 먼저 인사할게요.",romanization:"jogeum eosaekajiman jega meonjeo insahalgeyo.",en:"It's a little awkward, but I'll greet them first.",note:"Discomfort first, then the considerate action."},{ko:"바쁘지만 집들이 선물은 미리 챙겼어요.",romanization:"bappeujiman jipdeuri seonmureun miri chaenggyeosseoyo.",en:"I'm busy, but I prepared the housewarming gift ahead of time.",note:"Effort despite being busy shows consideration."},{ko:"친하지 않지만 선배님이라고 부르는 게 안전해요.",romanization:"chinhaji anchiman seonbaenimirago bureuneun ge anjeonhaeyo.",en:"We're not close, but calling them seonbaenim is the safe choice.",note:"Title before closeness — the careful option."}],keyPoint:{label:"-지만 vs. 그렇지만 / 그런데",body:"-지만 joins the two clauses inside one sentence (바쁘지만 갈게요). 그렇지만 / 그런데 do the same job but start a new sentence: 바빠요. 그렇지만 갈게요. Use -지만 to keep it as one smooth sentence."},pronunciation:"-지만 itself is [지만], but a preceding final obstruent can tense its ㅈ: 봤지만 is pronounced [봗찌만]. With 어렵지만, the ㅂ stays because -지만 begins with a consonant.",drill:{instruction:"Connect the two ideas with -지만, putting the polite choice second.",model:"동갑이다 + 존댓말을 써요 → 동갑이지만 존댓말을 써요.",items:["바쁘다 + 집들이에 갔어요","어색하다 + 먼저 도와드렸어요 (past)","친하지 않다 + 선배님이라고 불러요"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"동갑이에요지만 존댓말을 써요.",right:"동갑이지만 존댓말을 써요.",explanation:"-지만 attaches to the plain stem, not to a finished 해요/예요 form. With a noun it's 동갑 + 이지만 → 동갑이지만, never 동갑이에요 + 지만."}}],w=[{title:"Lexical causatives: V + -이/히/리/기/우/구/추-",func:"A fixed, closed set of verbs that build a causative meaning ('make/get X to happen', or 'do the action to X') right into the verb stem by inserting -이-, -히-, -리-, -기-, -우-, -구-, or -추-. The doer of the plain verb becomes the object (usually -을/를), and a new causer becomes the subject. Because the set is fixed, you can't apply it to just any verb — each causative pair is learned as its own vocabulary item.",formTable:[{when:"-이- (e.g. after ㄱ, ㅁ, vowel stems)",add:"먹다 → 먹이다 (feed), 죽다 → 죽이다 (kill), 끓다 → 끓이다 (boil sth)",ex:"엄마가 아기에게 밥을 먹여요. (Mom feeds the baby.)"},{when:"-히- (often after ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ)",add:"입다 → 입히다 (dress sb), 앉다 → 앉히다 (seat sb), 눕다 → 눕히다 (lay sb down)",ex:"아이에게 옷을 입혔어요. (I dressed the child.)"},{when:"-리- (often after ㄹ)",add:"살다 → 살리다 (save), 울다 → 울리다 (make cry), 알다 → 알리다 (let know)",ex:"동생이 형을 울렸어요. (The younger one made the older one cry.)"},{when:"-기-",add:"웃다 → 웃기다 (make laugh), 벗다 → 벗기다 (take sth off sb), 남다 → 남기다 (leave sth)",ex:"그 영화가 저를 웃겼어요. (That movie made me laugh.)"},{when:"-우- (note the stem often changes shape)",add:"자다 → 재우다 (put to sleep), 깨다 → 깨우다 (wake sb), 타다 → 태우다 (give a ride)",ex:"아빠가 아기를 재웠어요. (Dad put the baby to sleep.)"},{when:"-구-/-추- (a very small set)",add:"달다 → 달구다 (heat sth up), 낮다 → 낮추다 (lower), 맞다 → 맞추다 (set/adjust)",ex:"소리를 좀 낮춰 주세요. (Please turn the volume down.)"}],examples:[{ko:"제가 강아지한테 밥을 먹였어요.",romanization:"Jega gangaji-hante bab-eul meogyeosseoyo.",en:"I fed the puppy.",note:"먹다 (eat) → 먹이다 (feed): the eater (강아지) takes -한테/에게, and the new causer (저) becomes the subject."},{ko:"엄마가 아이를 일찍 재웠어요.",romanization:"Eomma-ga ai-reul iljjik jaewosseoyo.",en:"Mom put the child to bed early.",note:"자다 (sleep) → 재우다: the stem changes shape (자→재우), so memorize this pair — you can't just add a suffix."},{ko:"간호사가 환자를 침대에 눕혔어요.",romanization:"Ganhosa-ga hwanja-reul chimdae-e nupyeosseoyo.",en:"The nurse laid the patient down on the bed.",note:"눕다 (lie down) → 눕히다: the causee (환자) takes -을/를 because the nurse is physically doing it to them."},{ko:"제 친구는 항상 저를 웃겨요.",romanization:"Je chingu-neun hangsang jeo-reul utkyeoyo.",en:"My friend always makes me laugh.",note:"웃다 (laugh) → 웃기다: the causer doesn't have to be a person — a joke or a movie can be the subject too."}],keyPoint:{label:"Same suffix, opposite job: causative vs. passive",body:"The suffixes -이/히/리/기- also build passives, so one word can be either. 보이다 = 'be seen' (passive) or 'show' (causative); 잡히다 = 'be caught' (passive) or 'get sb to hold' (causative). Tell them apart by the arguments: a causative ADDS a causer-subject and keeps an object (친구가 저를 웃겨요 'my friend makes me laugh'), while a passive DROPS the agent and the old object becomes the subject (도둑이 잡혔어요 'the thief got caught'). If there's still a direct object being acted on, it's causative."},pronunciation:"Listen for the linking and aspiration the inserted consonant creates: 먹이다 is [머기다] (ㄱ links across), 입히다 is [이피다] (ㅂ+ㅎ → aspirated ㅍ), and 앉히다 is [안치다] (ㅈ+ㅎ → ㅊ). The suffix tends to fuse with the stem-final sound rather than staying separate.",drill:{instruction:"Turn the plain verb into its lexical causative and finish the sentence.",model:"아기가 자다 → 엄마가 아기를 ____. → 엄마가 아기를 재워요. (Mom puts the baby to sleep.)",items:["아이가 옷을 입다 → 제가 아이에게 옷을 ____. (→ 입혀요)","모기가 죽다 → 이 약이 모기를 ____. (→ 죽여요)","동생이 울다 → 형이 동생을 ____. (→ 울려요)","아기가 깨다 → 시끄러운 소리가 아기를 ____. (→ 깨워요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"엄마가 아기를 먹게 했어요. (to mean 'Mom fed the baby')",right:"엄마가 아기에게 밥을 먹였어요.",explanation:"English has one verb 'feed', so learners grab the all-purpose V-게 하다 and say 먹게 했어요 — but that means 'made/let the baby eat (on its own)', not 'spoon-fed it'. When Korean has a dedicated lexical causative (먹이다, 재우다, 입히다), native speakers use it for the hands-on action. Save V-게 하다 for getting someone to do it themselves."}},{title:"V-게 하다 — make / have / let someone do something",func:"The productive, all-purpose causative: add -게 하다 to almost any verb or adjective stem to mean 'make/have/let someone do or become something.' Unlike the lexical causatives, this works with every verb, so it's the go-to whenever no special causative form exists. The causee is marked with -이/가, -을/를, or -에게/한테 depending on the nuance (letting vs. making vs. getting someone to do it).",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem + -게 하다",add:"가다 → 가게 하다, 먹다 → 먹게 하다, 공부하다 → 공부하게 하다",ex:"선생님이 학생들을 일찍 가게 했어요. (The teacher made the students leave early.)"},{when:"Adjective stem + -게 하다 ('make become ~')",add:"행복하다 → 행복하게 하다, 깨끗하다 → 깨끗하게 하다",ex:"그 소식이 저를 행복하게 했어요. (That news made me happy.)"},{when:"Causee marker sets the nuance",add:"-이/가 (let/allow, softer), -을/를 (make, direct), -에게/한테 (get sb to do)",ex:"아이에게 채소를 먹게 했어요. (I got the child to eat vegetables.)"},{when:"Past / negative command",add:"-게 했어요, -게 하지 마(세요)",ex:"동생을 울게 하지 마. (Don't make your little sibling cry.)"}],examples:[{ko:"엄마가 저를 매일 운동하게 해요.",romanization:"Eomma-ga jeo-reul maeil undonghage haeyo.",en:"My mom makes me exercise every day.",note:"운동하게 하다 is a productive causative meaning 'make/have someone exercise.' 운동시키다 is also common and often sounds more direct or institutional, as in 코치가 선수들을 운동시켜요."},{ko:"선생님이 학생들에게 책을 읽게 했어요.",romanization:"Seonsaengnim-i haksaengdeul-ege chaek-eul ilkge haesseoyo.",en:"The teacher had the students read the book.",note:"-에게 marks the causee when you get someone to perform the action themselves."},{ko:"아이가 자고 싶어 해서 자게 했어요.",romanization:"Ai-ga jago sipeo haeseo jage haesseoyo.",en:"The child wanted to sleep, so I let him.",note:"With -이/가 and a permissive context, -게 하다 reads as 'let/allow', not 'force'."},{ko:"이 노래가 사람들을 춤추게 해요.",romanization:"I norae-ga saramdeul-eul chumchuge haeyo.",en:"This song makes people dance.",note:"The causer can be an inanimate thing (노래); -게 하다 freely takes non-human subjects."}],keyPoint:{label:"Lexical causative vs. V-게 하다 — how hands-on is the causer?",body:"When both exist, they differ in directness. 재우다 / 먹이다 mean the causer DOES the action to the causee (physically putting to sleep, spoon-feeding). 자게 하다 / 먹게 하다 mean the causer gets the causee to do it THEMSELVES. So 아기를 재웠어요 = 'I put the baby to sleep,' but 아기를 자게 했어요 = 'I made the baby sleep (e.g., by turning off the lights).' Use -게 하다 whenever there's no lexical form, or when you specifically mean indirect causing / letting."},pronunciation:"-게 하다 is said as written, [게 하다]; in fast speech 하다 can weaken, but unlike some patterns it does NOT contract into one word — keep 게 and 하다 separate. Don't confuse the causative with plain adverbial -게 (e.g. 크게 'loudly'); the causative needs the following 하다.",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite using V-게 하다 to express 'make / have someone do it.'",model:"학생들이 책을 읽어요. (causer: 선생님) → 선생님이 학생들에게 책을 읽게 해요.",items:["아이가 일찍 자요. (causer: 엄마) → 엄마가 아이를 일찍 ____. (→ 자게 해요)","동생이 방을 청소해요. (causer: 형) → 형이 동생에게 방을 ____. (→ 청소하게 해요)","학생들이 숙제를 내요. (causer: 선생님) → 선생님이 학생들에게 숙제를 ____. (→ 내게 해요)","아이가 채소를 먹어요. (causer: 아빠) → 아빠가 아이에게 채소를 ____. (→ 먹게 해요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"선생님이 저를 공부하라고 했어요. (used loosely for 'made me study')",right:"선생님이 저를 공부하게 했어요.",explanation:"English 'make me do' covers everything, so learners reach for -라고 하다, which actually means 'told me to' (a reported spoken order), not 'made me'. For the neutral, everyday 'make/have someone do X,' the default is -게 하다. Use -라고 하다 only when there was a real spoken command, and save -게 만들다 (next note) for forceful or emotional results."}},{title:"V-게 만들다 — bring about a result (often emotional or forceful)",func:"Structurally just like V-게 하다 (add -게 만들다 to a verb or adjective stem), but with a stronger 'bring about / cause to become' flavor. Since 만들다 means 'make/create,' V-게 만들다 stresses that the causer actively PRODUCES the resulting state or behavior. It's especially common when the result is emotional (made me cry / angry / happy) or when the causing feels forceful, unintended, or hard to resist. The causer is very often an inanimate thing or a situation.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem + -게 만들다",add:"울다 → 울게 만들다, 떠나다 → 떠나게 만들다, 포기하다 → 포기하게 만들다",ex:"그 영화가 저를 울게 만들었어요. (That movie made me cry.)"},{when:"Adjective stem + -게 만들다 ('cause to become ~')",add:"화나다 → 화나게 만들다, 행복하다 → 행복하게 만들다",ex:"그 말이 저를 화나게 만들었어요. (Those words made me angry.)"},{when:"Inanimate / situational causer (very common)",add:"스트레스가 / 그 일이 / 날씨가 ... + -게 만들다",ex:"스트레스가 저를 아프게 만들었어요. (Stress made me sick.)"},{when:"Past / negative command",add:"-게 만들었어요, -게 만들지 마(세요)",ex:"분위기를 어색하게 만들지 마세요. (Don't make things awkward.)"}],examples:[{ko:"그 드라마가 모두를 울게 만들었어요.",romanization:"Geu deurama-ga modu-reul ulge mandeureosseoyo.",en:"That drama made everyone cry.",note:"Emotional result + inanimate causer (드라마): the most typical use of -게 만들다."},{ko:"네 말이 나를 화나게 만들었어.",romanization:"Ne mal-i na-reul hwanage mandeureosseo.",en:"What you said made me angry.",note:"-게 만들다 fits strong feelings; here it sounds more loaded than the neutral 화나게 했어."},{ko:"더운 날씨가 저를 지치게 만들어요.",romanization:"Deoun nalssi-ga jeo-reul jichige mandeureoyo.",en:"The hot weather wears me out.",note:"A situation (날씨) as the causer is very natural with -게 만들다, often for an unwanted result."},{ko:"그 사고가 사람들을 불안하게 만들었어요.",romanization:"Geu sago-ga saramdeul-eul buranhage mandeureosseoyo.",en:"That accident made people anxious.",note:"With an adjective (불안하다), -게 만들다 means 'cause people to become anxious.'"}],keyPoint:{label:"-게 하다 vs. -게 만들다 — neutral instruction vs. strong/emotional result",body:"-게 하다 is the neutral default: a person tells or gets someone to do something (엄마가 저를 운동하게 해요 'Mom makes me exercise'). -게 만들다 highlights a produced result, usually emotional or forceful, and very often with a thing/situation as the cause (그 영화가 저를 울게 만들었어요 'That movie made me cry'). You usually wouldn't say 엄마가 저를 운동하게 만들어요 for routine instructing — that sounds like she forced an unwilling you into it. Rule of thumb: choosing/instructing → -게 하다; an event or feeling that takes you over → -게 만들다."},pronunciation:"만들다 is [만들다]; in the past tense the ㄹ stays and you get 만들었어요 [만드러써요] — don't drop the ㄹ. -게 is light and unstressed, so the phrase often runs together as one breath group: 울게 만들었어요 → [울게 만드러써요].",drill:{instruction:"Use V-게 만들다 to say what the thing or situation caused (pick an emotion/result that fits).",model:"그 영화 / 나 / 울다 → 그 영화가 나를 울게 만들었어. (That movie made me cry.)",items:["그 노래 / 사람들 / 춤추다 → ____. (→ 그 노래가 사람들을 춤추게 만들었어요)","네 말 / 나 / 화나다 → ____. (→ 네 말이 나를 화나게 만들었어)","시험 / 학생들 / 긴장하다 → ____. (→ 시험이 학생들을 긴장하게 만들었어요)","그 소식 / 우리 / 행복하다 → ____. (→ 그 소식이 우리를 행복하게 만들었어요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"엄마가 저를 매일 청소하게 만들어요. (for routine 'Mom makes me clean every day')",right:"엄마가 저를 매일 청소하게 해요.",explanation:"English 'make me clean' is neutral, but -게 만들다 adds a forceful, almost resentful 'forced me into it' coloring, so it sounds off for an everyday instruction. For routine 'has me / makes me do X,' use -게 하다. Reserve -게 만들다 for genuinely strong or emotional results, and especially when a thing or situation is the cause (그 영화가 저를 울게 만들었어요)."}}],f=[{title:"V-겠어요 / V-겠습니다 — stating your own intention",func:"Attach 겠 to a verb stem to say what YOU are going to do, decided right now, as a will or promise. Because it carries the speaker's own will, it almost always has a first-person subject (저/제가). 겠어요 is polite-conversational; 겠습니다 is formal and sounds like a firm, clean promise — the kind you make to a boss, a teacher, or a customer.",formTable:[{when:"Polite spoken intention",add:"verb stem + 겠어요",ex:"먹다 → 먹겠어요 (I'll eat / I'll have it)"},{when:"Formal commitment / announcement",add:"verb stem + 겠습니다",ex:"하다 → 하겠습니다 (I will do it)"},{when:"Set greeting phrases",add:"frozen 겠 forms",ex:"잘 먹겠습니다 / 처음 뵙겠습니다"}],examples:[{ko:"제가 먼저 할게요. 제가 하겠습니다.",romanization:"je-ga meon-jeo hal-ge-yo. je-ga ha-get-sseum-ni-da.",en:"Let me do it first. I'll do it.",note:"First-person + 하겠습니다 = a firm offer to take something on; very common when volunteering."},{ko:"오늘은 제가 살게요. 제가 사겠습니다.",romanization:"o-neu-reun je-ga sal-ge-yo. je-ga sa-get-sseum-ni-da.",en:"Today it's on me — I'll pay.",note:"겠 here is a decision made on the spot, not a long-standing plan."},{ko:"잘 먹겠습니다.",romanization:"jal meok-kket-sseum-ni-da.",en:"Thank you for the food (lit. I'll eat well).",note:"A fixed mealtime phrase — 겠 signals you're about to start eating now."},{ko:"내일까지 끝내겠습니다.",romanization:"nae-il-kka-ji kkeun-nae-get-sseum-ni-da.",en:"I'll get it done by tomorrow.",note:"A promise to someone you answer to; 겠습니다 sounds more responsible than a plain future."}],keyPoint:{label:"겠어요 vs 겠습니다",body:"Same intention meaning, different register. 겠어요 is everyday polite ('제가 할게요'/'먹겠어요'); 겠습니다 is formal and weightier, so it's the default for promises, service speech, and announcements. Don't use either for someone ELSE's plan — for that you need -(으)ㄹ 거예요 (친구가 올 거예요)."},pronunciation:"겠 ends in a [ㄷ] sound, so before 습니다 the ㅅ tenses and the batchim drops in: 겠습니다 → [게씀니다]. 겠어요 links smoothly as [게써요].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each verb into a first-person intention using -겠습니다.",model:"돕다 → 제가 돕겠습니다.",items:["하다 → 제가 ___. (하겠습니다)","먼저 가다 → 제가 먼저 ___. (가겠습니다)","다시 연락하다 → 제가 다시 ___. (연락하겠습니다)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구가 내일 오겠어요 always reports the friend's plan.",right:"친구가 내일 올 거예요. (plan/prediction) / 이 시간이면 친구도 곧 오겠어요. (speaker's inference)",explanation:"Volitional 겠 normally expresses the speaker's own intention, so use -(으)ㄹ 거예요 to report another person's plan. But third-person 겠 is possible when it expresses the speaker's inference rather than that person's will."}},{title:"A/V-겠네요 / A/V-겠군요 — 'that must be…' (inference from evidence)",func:"This is the other big job of 겠: drawing a confident conclusion from what you can see, hear, or have just been told. Add 겠 to a verb or adjective stem, then 네요/군요 to react out loud. It means 'must be / I bet / I can tell.' 겠네요 is the everyday reaction; 겠군요 sounds a touch more reflective ('Ah, so it must be…').",formTable:[{when:"Reacting to fresh evidence (spoken)",add:"stem + 겠네요",ex:"춥다 → 춥겠네요 (It must be cold!)"},{when:"Realization / 'I see, so…'",add:"stem + 겠군요",ex:"맛있다 → 맛있겠군요 (That must be tasty)"},{when:"Inferring about the past",add:"-았/었 + 겠네요",ex:"힘들었겠네요 (That must have been hard)"}],examples:[{ko:"밖에 눈이 와요? 길이 막히겠네요.",romanization:"ba-kke nu-ni wa-yo? gi-ri ma-ki-get-ne-yo.",en:"It's snowing outside? Then the roads must be jammed.",note:"Conclusion drawn from the snow you just heard about — classic 겠네요."},{ko:"이 집 줄이 기네요. 진짜 맛있겠어요.",romanization:"i jip ju-ri gi-ne-yo. jin-jja ma-sit-kket-sseo-yo.",en:"The line at this place is long. It must be really good.",note:"The long line is the evidence; 겠 turns it into a guess about the food."},{ko:"하루 종일 일했어요? 정말 피곤하겠네요.",romanization:"ha-ru jong-il il-hae-sseo-yo? jeong-mal pi-gon-ha-get-ne-yo.",en:"You worked all day? You must be really tired.",note:"Empathetic inference — Koreans use 겠네요 a lot to show they get how you feel."},{ko:"시험에 합격했어요? 부모님이 좋아하셨겠어요.",romanization:"si-heo-me hap-kkyeo-kae-sseo-yo? bu-mo-ni-mi jo-a-ha-syeot-kket-sseo-yo.",en:"You passed the exam? Your parents must have been so happy.",note:"-셨겠어요 = past inference + honorific; 겠 works on past events too."}],keyPoint:{label:"The two faces of 겠: will vs guess",body:"With a first-person action verb, 겠 = intention (먹겠어요 = I'll eat). With an adjective, or a third-person situation plus 네요/군요, 겠 = inference (맛있겠네요 = that must be tasty). Adjectives can't 'intend' anything, so 춥겠어요 can only mean 'it must be cold,' never 'I will be cold.'"},pronunciation:"겠네요 → [겐네요] (the [ㄷ] of 겠 becomes ㄴ before ㄴ, by nasalization). 겠어요 links as [게써요], and 겠군요 → [겓꾼뇨] with the ㄱ tensing.",drill:{instruction:"React to each situation with an inference using -겠네요.",model:"하루 종일 굶었어요. → 배고프겠네요.",items:["다음 주에 시험이 세 개 있어요. → ___. (바쁘겠네요/힘들겠네요)","어제 다섯 시간밖에 못 잤어요. → ___. (피곤하겠네요)","열 명 요리를 혼자 했어요. → ___. (정말 힘들었겠네요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"맛있을 거예요. (reacting to food right in front of you)",right:"맛있겠어요.",explanation:"English 'it'll be delicious' and 'it must be delicious' look similar, but Korean splits them: -(으)ㄹ 거예요 is a neutral prediction, while 겠 is the on-the-spot reaction to evidence in front of you. Looking at a steaming dish, say 맛있겠어요, not 맛있을 거예요."}},{title:"V-(으)ㄹ 거예요 — talking about a future plan",func:"Use -(으)ㄹ 거예요 to state a plan or prediction about the future — yours OR someone else's. Unlike volitional 겠, it doesn't sound like an on-the-spot vow; it just reports what is going to happen. It's the everyday way to say 'I'm going to…' / 'X is going to…'.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄹ 거예요",ex:"가다 → 갈 거예요 (I'm going to go)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ 을 거예요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을 거예요 (I'm going to eat)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem",add:"+ 거예요 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"만들다 → 만들 거예요 (I'll make it)"}],examples:[{ko:"이번 주말에 부산에 갈 거예요.",romanization:"i-beon ju-ma-re bu-sa-ne gal kkeo-ye-yo.",en:"I'm going to Busan this weekend.",note:"A settled plan, not a spur-of-the-moment decision — that's the difference from 겠."},{ko:"민수 씨는 안 올 거예요. 바쁘대요.",romanization:"min-su ssi-neun an ol kkeo-ye-yo. ba-ppeu-dae-yo.",en:"Minsu isn't going to come. He says he's busy.",note:"Third-person plan — perfectly fine here, unlike volitional 겠."},{ko:"저녁은 집에서 먹을 거예요.",romanization:"jeo-nyeo-geun ji-be-seo meo-geul kkeo-ye-yo.",en:"I'm going to eat dinner at home.",note:"Stem 먹- ends in a consonant, so it takes 을 거예요."},{ko:"내일은 날씨가 맑을 거예요.",romanization:"nae-i-reun nal-ssi-ga mal-geul kkeo-ye-yo.",en:"The weather will be clear tomorrow.",note:"A neutral forecast — the typical 'prediction' use, where 겠 would sound oddly personal."}],keyPoint:{label:"거예요 vs 겠",body:"Both reach into the future, but the feel differs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요 = a settled plan or neutral prediction, and works for anyone (저도, 친구도). 겠 = a will/promise made right now, almost always first-person (제가 하겠습니다). So a server taking your order says 주문하시겠어요? (your intention), while you describe your trip with 갈 거예요 (it's planned)."},pronunciation:"Standard spelling is 거예요 (you'll also see 거에요, but 거예요 is correct). The ㄹ of the -(으)ㄹ form tenses the next sound: 갈 거예요 → [갈 꺼예요], 먹을 거예요 → [머글 꺼예요].",drill:{instruction:"Make a future-plan sentence with -(으)ㄹ 거예요.",model:"주말 / 쉬다 → 주말에 쉴 거예요.",items:["내년 / 한국어를 배우다 → ___. (내년에 한국어를 배울 거예요)","오후 / 친구를 만나다 → ___. (오후에 친구를 만날 거예요)","점심 / 김밥을 먹다 → ___. (점심에 김밥을 먹을 거예요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"제가 도와줄 거예요 is always wrong as an offer.",right:"제가 도와드릴게요. / 제가 도와드리겠습니다. / 제가 도와줄 거예요.",explanation:"All three can work, with different tones. 도와드릴게요 is the natural everyday offer; 도와드리겠습니다 is formal and firm. 도와줄 거예요 can reassure or emphasize a future plan to help, although it is less like a spontaneous service-style offer."}},{title:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 — a soft, tentative guess",func:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 means 'I think / it seems / probably.' It softens a statement into a guess about the future or about something uncertain, so it sounds modest and polite. It pairs with this 겠 chapter as the gentler cousin of inference: where 겠네요 is a confident 'must be,' -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 is a hedged 'I think / probably.'",formTable:[{when:"Verb, future guess",add:"+ -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"오다 → 올 것 같아요 (I think it'll come/rain)"},{when:"Adjective, present-state guess",add:"+ -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",ex:"비싸다 → 비싼 것 같아요 (It seems expensive)"},{when:"Adjective, future or tentative expectation",add:"+ -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"비싸다 → 비쌀 것 같아요 (I think it will be expensive)"},{when:"Past guess (already happened)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",ex:"가다 → 간 것 같아요 (Seems they left)"}],examples:[{ko:"오늘 비가 올 것 같아요.",romanization:"o-neul bi-ga ol geot ga-ta-yo.",en:"I think it's going to rain today.",note:"A hedged forecast — softer and more natural in conversation than 비가 오겠어요."},{ko:"이 옷은 저한테 좀 클 것 같아요.",romanization:"i o-seun jeo-han-te jom keul geot ga-ta-yo.",en:"I think this shirt will be a bit big on me.",note:"Adjective 크다 + ㄹ 것 같아요; 좀 makes the guess even gentler."},{ko:"약속에 조금 늦을 것 같아요. 미안해요.",romanization:"yak-so-ge jo-geum neu-jeul geot ga-ta-yo. mi-an-hae-yo.",en:"I think I'll be a little late. Sorry.",note:"Used to soften bad news about yourself — much politer than a flat 늦어요."},{ko:"저 사람 한국 사람인 것 같아요.",romanization:"jeo sa-ram han-guk sa-ra-min geot ga-ta-yo.",en:"That person seems to be Korean.",note:"A guess about a present fact — gentler and less pushy than asserting it directly."}],keyPoint:{label:"겠네요 vs (으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"Both are guesses, but the confidence differs. 겠네요 = a confident 'it must be…,' usually a quick reaction to evidence right there (춥겠네요). -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 = a softer 'I think / probably' that leaves room for doubt (추울 것 같아요). When you want to sound modest or unsure, reach for -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요."},pronunciation:"것 같아요 → [걷 까타요]: the ㄱ of 같- tenses to [까] after the [ㄷ] of 것, and 같아요 links as [가타요]. The ㄹ form tenses too: 올 것 → [올 껃].",drill:{instruction:"Soften each guess with -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요.",model:"비가 와요. → 비가 올 것 같아요.",items:["이 가방은 비싸요. → ___. (비쌀 것 같아요)","오늘은 좀 늦어요. → ___. (늦을 것 같아요)","저 영화는 재미있어요. → ___. (재미있을 것 같아요)"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"맛있을 것 같아요. (reacting to a dish steaming in front of you)",right:"맛있겠어요.",explanation:"When the evidence is right there and you're confident, -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 sounds oddly hesitant — like you're unsure about food you can clearly see is good. Use the confident 겠어요/겠네요 for that on-the-spot reaction, and save -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 for genuinely uncertain guesses."}}],b=[{title:'V-았/었으면 좋겠어요 — a wish ("I wish / I hope")',func:'Expresses a wish that the present situation were different, or a hope for something to come true. The past-shaped -았/었- here is NOT real past tense; it sets the wish against current reality. English renders it as "I wish..." (reality is the opposite) or "I hope..." (still possible).',formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-았으면 좋겠어요",ex:"오다 → 봄이 왔으면 좋겠어요 (I wish spring would come)"},{when:"Stem ends in any other vowel",add:"-었으면 좋겠어요",ex:"있다 → 시간이 있었으면 좋겠어요 (I wish I had time)"},{when:"하다 verbs/adjectives",add:"-했으면 좋겠어요",ex:"건강하다 → 건강했으면 좋겠어요 (I hope you stay healthy)"},{when:"Casual register (반말)",add:"-(았/었)으면 좋겠다",ex:"비가 그쳤으면 좋겠다 (I wish the rain would stop)"}],examples:[{ko:"주말에 날씨가 좋았으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"jumare nalssiga joasseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I hope the weather is nice this weekend.",note:"A future hope that is still possible; -았- does not mean past here."},{ko:"시험이 너무 어렵지 않았으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"siheomi neomu eoryeopji anasseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I hope the exam isn't too hard.",note:"Negative wish: 어렵지 않다 takes -았으면 just like any other stem."},{ko:"지금 친구가 옆에 있었으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"jigeum chinguga yeope isseosseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I wish a friend were here beside me right now.",note:"지금 makes it clearly contrary to present fact — a true 'I wish' reading."},{ko:"내일 일찍 일어났으면 좋겠어요.",romanization:"naeil iljjik ireonasseumyeon jokesseoyo.",en:"I hope I get up early tomorrow.",note:"The speaker's own hope/resolve; the wish points forward in time."}],keyPoint:{label:"-았/었으면 좋겠어요 vs -(으)면 좋겠어요",body:"Both mean 'I wish/hope.' The -았/었- version is by far the more common, natural choice in everyday speech and leans toward wishing reality were currently otherwise. The plain -(으)면 좋겠어요 sounds a bit more like a neutral 'it would be good if...'. When in doubt, use -았/었으면 좋겠어요."},pronunciation:"좋겠어요 is pronounced [조케써요]: the ㅎ of 좋 and the ㄱ of 겠 fuse into aspirated [ㅋ], and 겠어요 links smoothly as [게써요].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each plain wish into a full -았/었으면 좋겠어요 sentence.",model:"비가 오다 → 비가 왔으면 좋겠어요.",items:["주말이 길다 → 주말이 _______ 좋겠어요.","방이 조금 더 따뜻하다 → 방이 조금 더 _______ 좋겠어요.","내일 안 춥다 → 내일 _______ 좋겠어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"시간이 있으면 원해요.",right:"시간이 있었으면 좋겠어요.",explanation:"English 'I wish' tempts learners to reach for 원하다 ('want'). Korean does not use 원하다 for wishing a situation were different. The fixed frame is -았/었으면 좋겠어요, literally 'it would be good if it had been (so).' Don't translate 'wish' as 원하다."}},{title:"V-았/었으면 … -았/었을 텐데요 — a past counterfactual with regret",func:"States a counterfactual about the past: a condition that did NOT happen, and the result that therefore did NOT happen. -았/었으면 marks the unreal past condition; the result clause stays hypothetical with -았/었을 텐데요 ('would have...'). The 텐데 ending softens it into a wistful 'but it wasn't so.'",formTable:[{when:"Condition: stem + 았/었 + 으면",add:"-았/었으면",ex:"알다 → 알았으면 (if I had known)"},{when:"Result: stem + 았/었 + 을 텐데요",add:"-았/었을 텐데요",ex:"가다 → 갔을 텐데요 (I would have gone)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"-했으면 / -했을 텐데요",ex:"공부했으면 합격했을 텐데요 (if I'd studied, I'd have passed)"},{when:"Whole frame",add:"A-았/었으면 B-았/었을 텐데요",ex:"일찍 출발했으면 안 늦었을 텐데요 (if I'd left early, I wouldn't have been late)"}],examples:[{ko:"미리 공지를 알았으면 시험 준비를 더 했을 텐데요.",romanization:"miri gongjireul arasseumyeon siheom junbireul deo haesseul tendeyo.",en:"If I had known about the notice in advance, I would have prepared more for the exam.",note:"미리 알다 from the chapter's regret vocabulary; the result also stays unreal with 했을 텐데요."},{ko:"조금만 더 일찍 일어났으면 버스를 탔을 텐데요.",romanization:"jogeumman deo iljjik ireonasseumyeon beoseureul tasseul tendeyo.",en:"If I had gotten up just a little earlier, I would have caught the bus.",note:"A typical regret scene: a small unmet condition with a clear missed result."},{ko:"우산을 가져갔으면 비를 안 맞았을 텐데요.",romanization:"usaneul gajyeogasseumyeon bireul an majasseul tendeyo.",en:"If I had taken an umbrella, I wouldn't have gotten rained on.",note:"가져갔으면 strongly implies the speaker did NOT take one."},{ko:"그때 바로 사과했으면 사이가 안 나빠졌을 텐데요.",romanization:"geuttae baro sagwahaesseumyeon saiga an nappajyeosseul tendeyo.",en:"If I had apologized right then, the relationship wouldn't have gone bad.",note:"사과하다 fits the regret vocabulary of the chapter; 달라지다/나빠지다 carry the changed outcome."}],keyPoint:{label:"Keep the result clause hypothetical",body:"Whichever condition you use, the result must also be marked unreal: -았/었을 텐데요, -았/었을 거예요, or -았/었을지도 몰라요 ('might have'). Never finish a past counterfactual with a plain past or present verb — that cancels the 'would have' meaning. 텐데요 adds the softest, most wistful tone of the three."},pronunciation:"텐데요 is [텐데요], no special change. Note 갔을 텐데요 → [가쓸 텐데요]: 갔 closes as [갇] and links into 을 as [가쓸].",drill:{instruction:"Complete each counterfactual so both the condition and the result stay past-hypothetical.",model:"약을 먹다 / 안 아프다 → 약을 먹었으면 안 아팠을 텐데요.",items:["전화하다 / 만나다 → 전화______ 우리가 만났을 텐데요.","표를 예매하다 / 자리가 있다 → 표를 예매______ 자리가 있었을 텐데요.","조심하다 / 안 다치다 → 조심______ 안 ______ 텐데요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"알았으면 전화해요.",right:"알았으면 전화했을 텐데요.",explanation:"Learners set up the 'if had' clause correctly but then drop into a plain present/future result ('I call' / 'I will call'). In Korean the result of a past counterfactual must itself be marked unreal: -았/었을 텐데요. A plain result clause cancels the 'would have' meaning."}},{title:"V-았/었더라면 … -았/었을 거예요 — a stronger, more reflective counterfactual",func:"Replays a missed past from a reflective distance: 'if (only) it had happened, the result would have...'. -았/었더라면 marks the unreal past condition with a heavier, more regretful tone than -았/었으면; the result stays hypothetical with -았/었을 거예요 ('would have').",formTable:[{when:"Condition: stem + 았/었 + 더라면",add:"-았/었더라면",ex:"도착하다 → 도착했더라면 (if I had arrived)"},{when:"Result: stem + 았/었 + 을 거예요",add:"-았/었을 거예요",ex:"보다 → 봤을 거예요 (I would have seen it)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"-했더라면",ex:"공부했더라면 (if I had studied)"},{when:"Whole frame",add:"A-았/었더라면 B-았/었을 거예요",ex:"조금만 일찍 도착했더라면 시험을 봤을 거예요 (if I'd arrived a bit earlier, I'd have taken the exam)"}],examples:[{ko:"조금만 일찍 도착했더라면 시험을 볼 수 있었을 거예요.",romanization:"jogeumman iljjik dochakaetdeoramyeon siheomeul bol su isseosseul geoyeyo.",en:"If I had arrived just a little earlier, I could have taken the exam.",note:"Arriving early didn't happen, so the result stays hypothetical too."},{ko:"지도를 미리 봤더라면 길을 안 잃었을 거예요.",romanization:"jidoreul miri bwatdeoramyeon gireul an ireosseul geoyeyo.",en:"If I had looked at the map in advance, I wouldn't have gotten lost.",note:"봤더라면 = the look-up that never happened; 안 잃었을 거예요 = the avoided result."},{ko:"그 소식을 들었더라면 바로 전화했을 거예요.",romanization:"geu sosigeul deureotdeoramyeon baro jeonhwahaesseul geoyeyo.",en:"If I had heard the news, I would have called right away.",note:"Both clauses carry past 았/었; the whole sentence is firmly counterfactual."},{ko:"그때 사과했더라면 결과가 달라졌을 거예요.",romanization:"geuttae sagwahaetdeoramyeon gyeolgwaga dallajyeosseul geoyeyo.",en:"If I had apologized then, the outcome would have been different.",note:"사과하다, 결과, 달라지다 are all the chapter's target regret words."}],keyPoint:{label:"-았/었더라면 vs -았/었으면",body:"Both open a past counterfactual, but -았/었더라면 is heavier and more clearly regretful — it almost always implies 'and that's a shame, because it didn't happen.' -았/었으면 is more neutral and far more common in casual speech. In friendly chat 더라면 can sound dramatic, so friends often just use -았/었으면. The result clause stays hypothetical either way."},pronunciation:"봤더라면 → [봗떠라면]: the ㅆ of 봤 closes to [ㄷ] before a consonant, and ㄷ of 더 tenses to [떠]. 들었더라면 → [드럳떠라면] follows the same tensing.",drill:{instruction:"Complete each -았/었더라면 … -았/었을 거예요 counterfactual.",model:"약을 먹다 / 안 아프다 → 약을 먹었더라면 안 아팠을 거예요.",items:["전화하다 / 만나다 → 전화______ 우리가 만났을 거예요.","표를 예매하다 / 자리가 있다 → 표를 예매______ 자리가 있었을 거예요.","조심하다 / 안 다치다 → 조심______ 안 ______ 거예요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"알았으면더라면 전화했을 거예요.",right:"알았더라면 전화했을 거예요.",explanation:"Learners sometimes try to stack -으면 and -더라면 together. They are two different conditional endings and cannot combine. Use one or the other directly on the past stem: 알았으면 OR 알았더라면, never 알았으면더라면."}},{title:`V-지 않았더라면 — a negative counterfactual ("if … hadn't …")`,func:"Forms the negative side of a past counterfactual: 'if (something) had NOT happened.' Because the event DID happen, a certain (often bad) result followed — and you imagine the opposite past. It pairs with a hypothetical result clause.",formTable:[{when:"Long negation (standard)",add:"V-지 않았더라면",ex:"놓치다 → 놓치지 않았더라면 (if I hadn't missed it)"},{when:"Short negation 안",add:"안 + V-았/었더라면",ex:"안 갔더라면 (if I hadn't gone)"},{when:"Inability 못",add:"못 + V-았/었더라면",ex:"못 봤더라면 (if I hadn't been able to see it)"},{when:"Result clause",add:"-았/었을 거예요 / -았/었을 텐데요",ex:"포기하지 않았더라면 합격했을지도 몰라요 (if I hadn't given up, I might have passed)"}],examples:[{ko:"버스를 놓치지 않았더라면 면접에 늦지 않았을 텐데요.",romanization:"beoseureul nochiji anatdeoramyeon myeonjeobe neutji anasseul tendeyo.",en:"If I hadn't missed the bus, I wouldn't have been late for the interview.",note:"놓치다 (to miss) is the chapter's regret vocabulary; 놓치지 않았더라면 keeps the negative inside the condition."},{ko:"포기하지 않았더라면 합격했을지도 몰라요.",romanization:"pogihaji anatdeoramyeon hapgyeokaesseuljido mollayo.",en:"If I hadn't given up, I might have passed.",note:"-았/었을지도 몰라요 keeps the outcome uncertain but still unreal."},{ko:"그때 화를 내지 않았더라면 지금 후회하지 않았을 텐데요.",romanization:"geuttae hwareul naeji anatdeoramyeon jigeum huhoehaji anasseul tendeyo.",en:"If I hadn't gotten angry then, I wouldn't be regretting it now.",note:"후회하다 is a target word; the result uses 텐데요 for present regret."},{ko:"네가 도와주지 않았더라면 저는 실패했을 거예요.",romanization:"nega dowajuji anatdeoramyeon jeoneun silpaehaesseul geoyeyo.",en:"If you hadn't helped me, I would have failed.",note:"A common gratitude use: the negative counterfactual highlights how crucial the real help was."}],keyPoint:{label:"-지 않았더라면 vs -았/었더라면",body:"Use affirmative -았/었더라면 to imagine an action you wish HAD happened ('if I had studied'). Use -지 않았더라면 to imagine an action you wish had NOT happened ('if I hadn't missed it'). The result clause works identically for both; only the polarity of the condition changes."},pronunciation:"않았더라면 → [아낟떠라면]: the ㅎ of 않 is silent so 않았 reads [아낟] (the ㅆ closing to [ㄷ] before 더), and 더 tenses to [떠].",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each affirmative idea as a negative counterfactual ('if … hadn't …').",model:"늦게 자다 → 늦게 자지 않았더라면",items:["버스를 놓치다 → 버스를 _______ 않았더라면","길을 잃다 → 길을 _______ 않았더라면","화를 내다 → 화를 _______ 않았더라면"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"안 비가 왔더라면 소풍을 갔을 거예요.",right:"비가 오지 않았더라면 소풍을 갔을 거예요.",explanation:"Learners often put 안 before the noun (안 비가). With this frame, the clean choice is long negation V-지 않았더라면. If you do use short 안, it goes directly before the verb (비가 안 왔더라면), never before the subject noun."}},{title:"-(으)면 vs -았/었으면 — open condition vs missed condition",func:"Distinguishes a real, still-open condition from an unreal, already-missed one. Plain V-(으)면 leaves the door open ('if/when you have time'). Past-shaped V-았/었으면 usually looks backward at a condition that did NOT happen, setting up a counterfactual result.",formTable:[{when:"Open if/when condition",add:"V-(으)면",ex:"시간이 있으면 같이 확인해요 (if you have time, let's check together)"},{when:"Missed/unreal past condition",add:"V-았/었으면",ex:"시간이 있었으면 같이 확인했을 텐데요 (if there had been time, we'd have checked)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"-하면 / -했으면",ex:"준비하면 덜 불안해요 / 준비했으면 안 늦었을 텐데요"},{when:"Reusable rule (always V-(으)면)",add:"V-(으)면",ex:"미리 준비하면 덜 불안해요 (if you prepare ahead, you feel less anxious)"}],examples:[{ko:"시간이 있으면 같이 확인해요.",romanization:"sigani isseumyeon gachi hwaginhaeyo.",en:"If you have time, let's check it together.",note:"An open condition — time may still be available, so the result is a real plan."},{ko:"시간이 있었으면 같이 확인했을 텐데요.",romanization:"sigani isseosseumyeon gachi hwaginhaesseul tendeyo.",en:"If there had been time, we would have checked it together.",note:"The past form turns the same idea into hindsight about a missed chance."},{ko:"미리 준비하면 덜 불안해요.",romanization:"miri junbihamyeon deol buranhaeyo.",en:"If you prepare in advance, you feel less anxious.",note:"A reusable rule, so plain V-(으)면 is correct — not counterfactual."},{ko:"그때 미리 알았으면 놓치지 않았을 거예요.",romanization:"geuttae miri arasseumyeon nochiji anasseul geoyeyo.",en:"If I had known in advance then, I wouldn't have missed it.",note:"그때 plus the unreal result shows this is a missed past, not an open rule."}],keyPoint:{label:"Let context and the result decide the reading",body:"The past form alone doesn't always mean counterfactual — context and the result clause decide. A simple test: can the condition still happen? If yes, plain V-(으)면 with a real result is usually enough. If it's already too late, use V-았/었으면 and keep the result unreal (-았/었을 텐데요 / -았/었을 거예요)."},pronunciation:"있었으면 → [이써쓰면]: 있 closes as [읻] then links into 었 as [이써], and 었으면 connects as [쓰면]. 있으면 (open) stays lighter as [이쓰면].",drill:{instruction:"Choose -(으)면 for an open condition or -았/었으면 for a missed one, then complete the sentence.",model:"시간이 있다 (open) → 시간이 있으면 같이 확인해요.",items:["내일 비가 안 오다 (open hope) → 내일 비가 안 ____ 좋겠어요.","그때 일찍 출발하다 (missed) → 그때 일찍 ______ 안 늦었을 텐데요.","주말에 날씨가 좋다 (still possible) → 주말에 날씨가 ____ 소풍을 가요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"어제 시간이 있으면 같이 봤을 거예요.",right:"어제 시간이 있었으면 같이 봤을 거예요.",explanation:"For a clearly past, missed condition (어제 'yesterday'), the plain present -(으)면 clashes with the past result 봤을 거예요. Match the condition to the timeframe: a missed past needs -았/었으면, not plain -(으)면."}}],k=[{title:"V-기 — turning a verb into a noun",func:"Add 기 to a verb stem to name the action as a thing. The 기-noun can be a task, a goal, or the object of verbs like 좋아하다, 싫어하다, 시작하다, and it is the natural subject of difficulty/ease adjectives like 어렵다, 쉽다, 힘들다.",formTable:[{when:"Any verb stem (vowel or consonant)",add:"stem + 기",ex:"먹다 → 먹기, 읽다 → 읽기"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ (ㄹ stays before 기)",add:"stem + 기",ex:"살다 → 살기, 만들다 → 만들기"},{when:"As the object of 좋아하다/싫어하다 etc.",add:"V-기 + 를/을",ex:"운동하기를 좋아해요"},{when:"As the subject of 어렵다/쉽다/힘들다",add:"V-기 + 가",ex:"말하기가 어려워요"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 책 읽기를 좋아해요.",romanization:"jeo-neun chaek ilg-gi-reul jo-a-hae-yo.",en:"I like reading books.",note:"읽기 names the action so it can be the object of 좋아하다."},{ko:"오늘 숙제는 일기 쓰기예요.",romanization:"o-neul suk-je-neun il-gi sseu-gi-ye-yo.",en:"Today's homework is writing a diary.",note:"쓰기 is a plain noun (a task/title) here, right after 은."},{ko:"한국어 말하기가 제일 어려워요.",romanization:"han-gug-eo mal-ha-gi-ga je-il eo-ryeo-wo-yo.",en:"Speaking Korean is the hardest.",note:"말하기 is the subject (with 가) of 어렵다 — a very common skill-label use."},{ko:"저는 일찍 일어나기가 힘들어요.",romanization:"jeo-neun il-jjik il-eo-na-gi-ga him-deul-eo-yo.",en:"Getting up early is hard for me.",note:"Adjectives of difficulty/ease (힘들다, 쉽다, 좋다) naturally take a V-기 subject."}],keyPoint:{label:"When to reach for V-기",body:"Use V-기 to label an action as a thing — especially as the subject of 어렵다/쉽다/좋다/힘들다, or the object of 좋아하다/싫어하다/시작하다. It sounds abstract and title-like, which is exactly why textbook section headings are 듣기, 읽기, 쓰기, 말하기."},pronunciation:"After a stem ending in a plain consonant, the 기 tenses: 읽기 [일끼], 먹기 [먹끼], 닦기 [닥끼]. Vowel stems stay soft: 가기 [가기], 자기 [자기].",drill:{instruction:"Add 기 to the bracketed verb and choose 를 or 가 so the sentence works.",model:"저는 [수영하다] 좋아해요. → 저는 수영하기를 좋아해요.",items:["[운동하다] 건강에 좋아요.","저는 매운 음식 [먹다] 좋아해요.","[일찍 자다] 쉽지 않아요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 읽다를 좋아해요.",right:"저는 읽기를 좋아해요.",explanation:"English 'reading' can be a noun on its own, but Korean cannot use the dictionary form 읽다 as a noun. Nominalise the stem first: 읽 + 기 → 읽기, then add the particle."}},{title:"V-는 것/거 — an action or fact as a noun",func:"Add 는 것 (casual 는 거) to a verb to name an action, a habit, or something you saw or know — as a noun. It points at the real, ongoing act, and it is the everyday default after 좋아하다 and as a sentence subject.",formTable:[{when:"Verb (present action/habit)",add:"stem + 는 것/거",ex:"먹다 → 먹는 것, 보다 → 보는 것"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ (ㄹ drops before 는)",add:"stem(-ㄹ) + 는 것",ex:"살다 → 사는 것, 만들다 → 만드는 것"},{when:"Subject, casual speech (것이 → 게)",add:"는 게",ex:"보는 게 좋아요"},{when:"Object, casual speech (것을 → 걸)",add:"는 걸",ex:"보는 걸 좋아해요"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 영화 보는 것을 좋아해요.",romanization:"jeo-neun yeong-hwa bo-neun geos-eul jo-a-hae-yo.",en:"I like watching movies.",note:"보는 것 names the activity of watching — sounds more everyday than 보기."},{ko:"친구를 기다리는 게 지루해요.",romanization:"chin-gu-reul gi-da-ri-neun ge ji-ru-hae-yo.",en:"Waiting for a friend is boring.",note:"것이 contracts to 게 in speech; here it is the subject of 지루하다."},{ko:"매일 운동하는 것이 중요해요.",romanization:"mae-il un-dong-ha-neun geos-i jung-yo-hae-yo.",en:"Exercising every day is important.",note:"는 것 carries the habit (every day), which 기 cannot do as smoothly."},{ko:"동생이 몰래 과자 먹는 걸 봤어요.",romanization:"dong-saeng-i mol-lae gwa-ja meog-neun geol bwass-eo-yo.",en:"I saw my younger sibling secretly eating snacks.",note:"는 것 names an action you witnessed; 걸 = 것을 in speech."}],keyPoint:{label:"기 vs 는 것",body:"Both make a verb into a noun. V-기 is abstract and title-like — good for skills, tasks, and subjects of 어렵다/쉽다 (말하기가 어려워요). V-는 것 points at the real act or a habit, and is the default after 좋아하다 and as a subject (영화 보는 게 좋아요). When you are talking and unsure, 는 거 is the safer choice."},pronunciation:"것 links its final ㅅ into a following vowel: 것을 [거슬], 것이 [거시]. In casual speech learn the contractions early — 것이 → 게 [게], 것을 → 걸 [걸], 것은 → 건 [건] — they are everywhere.",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite with V-는 거 and the right contracted particle (게 for subject, 걸 for object).",model:"[요리하다] 재미있어요. → 요리하는 게 재미있어요.",items:["저는 [노래 부르다] 좋아해요.","[아침에 일찍 일어나다] 힘들어요.","동생이 [몰래 과자 먹다] 봤어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 영화 보것을 좋아해요.",right:"저는 영화 보는 것을 좋아해요.",explanation:"Learners often drop 는 and glue 것 straight onto the stem. The verb must take its present modifier ending -는 before 것: 보 + 는 + 것. Without 는, the phrase is ungrammatical."}},{title:"V-기로 했어요 — decided to do",func:"Use V-기로 하다 for a decision, plan, or agreement to do something. The 기-noun takes 로 ('settle on') plus 하다. It is almost always said in the past — 했어요 — because the decision is already made by the time you mention it.",formTable:[{when:"State a decision (most common)",add:"stem + 기로 했어요",ex:"운동하다 → 운동하기로 했어요"},{when:"Deciding right now / promise",add:"stem + 기로 해요 / 할게요",ex:"담배를 끊기로 할게요"},{when:"Mutual agreement (we agreed)",add:"stem + 기로 했어요 (with 같이/우리)",ex:"같이 가기로 했어요"},{when:"Decide NOT to do",add:"안 + V / V-지 않 + 기로 했어요",ex:"야식을 안 먹기로 했어요"}],examples:[{ko:"내일부터 일찍 일어나기로 했어요.",romanization:"nae-il-bu-teo il-jjik il-eo-na-gi-ro haess-eo-yo.",en:"I've decided to get up early from tomorrow.",note:"Personal resolution; past 했어요 even though the action is in the future."},{ko:"우리 주말에 같이 등산하기로 했어요.",romanization:"u-ri ju-mal-e ga-chi deung-san-ha-gi-ro haess-eo-yo.",en:"We decided to go hiking together on the weekend.",note:"우리 + 같이 makes it a mutual agreement, not a solo decision."},{ko:"밤에는 커피를 안 마시기로 했어요.",romanization:"bam-e-neun keo-pi-reul an ma-si-gi-ro haess-eo-yo.",en:"I've decided not to drink coffee at night.",note:"The negative 안 goes before the verb, not after 기로."},{ko:"오늘은 집에서 쉬기로 했어요.",romanization:"o-neul-eun jib-e-seo swi-gi-ro haess-eo-yo.",en:"I've decided to rest at home today.",note:"쉬기 nominalised, then 로 했어요 for the decision."}],keyPoint:{label:"했어요 vs 해요",body:"The decision is usually reported in the past: V-기로 했어요 ('I (have) decided'), because the decision already exists by the time you say it. Use the present V-기로 해요 / 할게요 only when you are making the resolution at the very moment of speaking ('okay, I'll do X from now on')."},pronunciation:"기로 is read smoothly [기로], with no special change. A tensing stem still tenses the 기: 먹기로 [먹끼로]; and ㅎ + ㄱ aspirates: 끊기로 [끈키로].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each plan into a decision with V-기로 했어요.",model:"다이어트를 하다 → 다이어트를 하기로 했어요.",items:["매일 한국어를 공부하다","주말에 친구를 만나다","이제부터 야식을 안 먹다"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저는 운동하기를 했어요.",right:"저는 운동하기로 했어요.",explanation:"The pattern needs the particle 로 ('settle on'), not the object particle 를. 기 + 로 + 하다 is a fixed chunk meaning 'decide to'; 기를 하다 is not a decision and sounds wrong."}},{title:"V-기 위해(서) — in order to do",func:"Use V-기 위해(서) to give the purpose of an action: 'in order to V'. The verb is nominalised with 기, then 위해(서) ('for the sake of') marks it as the goal. For a noun goal, use N을/를 위해(서) with no 기.",formTable:[{when:"Verb purpose",add:"stem + 기 위해(서)",ex:"합격하다 → 합격하기 위해서"},{when:"Noun purpose",add:"N + 을/를 위해(서)",ex:"건강을 위해서"},{when:"Drop 서 (a touch more formal)",add:"stem + 기 위해",ex:"살을 빼기 위해 운동해요"},{when:"Written/formal style",add:"stem + 기 위하여",ex:"성공하기 위하여 노력합니다"}],examples:[{ko:"건강해지기 위해서 매일 운동해요.",romanization:"geon-gang-hae-ji-gi wi-hae-seo mae-il un-dong-hae-yo.",en:"I exercise every day in order to get healthy.",note:"Purpose comes first; the main action (운동해요) follows."},{ko:"시험에 합격하기 위해서 열심히 공부해요.",romanization:"si-heom-e hap-gyeok-ha-gi wi-hae-seo yeol-sim-hi gong-bu-hae-yo.",en:"I study hard in order to pass the exam.",note:"The classic goal-then-effort pattern."},{ko:"한국에서 일하기 위해 한국어를 배워요.",romanization:"han-gug-e-seo il-ha-gi wi-hae han-gug-eo-reul bae-wo-yo.",en:"I'm learning Korean in order to work in Korea.",note:"서 dropped here; the meaning is the same, just a little more formal."},{ko:"가족을 위해서 열심히 일해요.",romanization:"ga-jog-eul wi-hae-seo yeol-sim-hi il-hae-yo.",en:"I work hard for my family.",note:"Noun version: N + 을 위해서, with no 기 because the goal is already a noun."}],keyPoint:{label:"V-기 위해서 vs V-(으)려고",body:"Both mean 'in order to'. 기 위해서 states a deliberate, often weighty goal and works in writing and speech (시험에 합격하기 위해서 공부해요). V-(으)려고 is lighter and conversational. For a noun goal there is no 기 — you must use N을/를 위해서 (건강을 위해서)."},pronunciation:"위해서 [위해서]; the formal 위하여 [위하여]. The 기 still tenses after a plain-consonant stem: 먹기 위해 [먹끼 위해].",drill:{instruction:"Join the goal and the action with V-기 위해서 (or N을/를 위해서 for a noun goal).",model:"[살을 빼다] / 매일 운동해요 → 살을 빼기 위해서 매일 운동해요.",items:["[한국 친구를 사귀다] / 한국어를 배워요","[건강] / 채소를 많이 먹어요","[시험에 합격하다] / 매일 단어를 외워요"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"한국어를 배우다 위해서 학원에 다녀요.",right:"한국어를 배우기 위해서 학원에 다녀요.",explanation:"A verb before 위해서 must be nominalised: 배우 + 기 → 배우기 위해서. You cannot put the dictionary form 배우다 straight in front of 위해서."}},{title:"V-기 전에 — before doing",func:"Use V-기 전에 to say one action happens before another: 'before V-ing'. The verb takes 기, then 전에 ('before') follows. The tense lives only in the main clause, never on the verb before 전에.",formTable:[{when:"Verb (before doing)",add:"stem + 기 전에",ex:"자다 → 자기 전에, 먹다 → 먹기 전에"},{when:"Noun (before a thing/event)",add:"N + 전에 (no 기)",ex:"수업 전에, 점심 전에"},{when:"Tense stays on the main verb",add:"...기 전에 + past/present/future main verb",ex:"먹기 전에 손을 씻었어요"}],examples:[{ko:"자기 전에 휴대폰 보는 것을 줄이기로 했어요.",romanization:"ja-gi jeon-e hyu-dae-pon bo-neun geos-eul ju-ri-gi-ro haess-eo-yo.",en:"I decided to cut down on looking at my phone before sleeping.",note:"자기 전에 is fixed with 기; 보는 것 is the habit being reduced."},{ko:"밥을 먹기 전에 손을 씻어요.",romanization:"bab-eul meog-gi jeon-e son-eul ssis-eo-yo.",en:"I wash my hands before eating.",note:"An everyday routine sentence; tense is on the main verb 씻어요."},{ko:"단어를 외우기 전에 예문을 먼저 들어요.",romanization:"dan-eo-reul oe-u-gi jeon-e ye-mun-eul meon-jeo deul-eo-yo.",en:"Before memorizing words, I listen to example sentences first.",note:"외우기 전에 is far more natural than trying to use 외우는 것 here."},{ko:"말하기 전에 한 번 더 생각해요.",romanization:"mal-ha-gi jeon-e han beon deo saeng-gak-hae-yo.",en:"Before speaking, I think one more time.",note:"A handy social-habit sentence."}],keyPoint:{label:"기 전에 vs N 전에",body:"Before a verb, you need 기: 먹기 전에, 자기 전에. Before a plain noun, there is no 기 — just add 전에: 수업 전에, 점심 전에, 운동 전에. And the opposite of 전에 is 후에 / -(으)ㄴ 후에 (after doing): 먹은 후에."},pronunciation:"전에 [저네] in fast speech, as the ㄴ links to the next vowel. The 기 still tenses after a plain-consonant stem: 먹기 전에 [먹끼 저네].",drill:{instruction:"Complete each sentence with V-기 전에 using the bracketed verb.",model:"[자다] 이를 닦아요. → 자기 전에 이를 닦아요.",items:["[밥을 먹다] 손을 씻어요.","[나가다] 날씨를 확인해요.","[공부하다] 목표를 먼저 정해요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"밥을 먹었기 전에 손을 씻었어요.",right:"밥을 먹기 전에 손을 씻었어요.",explanation:"Do not put past tense on the verb before 전에. The verb before 전에 stays as plain 기 (먹기 전에); the past meaning goes on the main verb (씻었어요)."}},{title:"V-기 시작하다 — start doing",func:"Use V-기 시작하다 to say you start an action. 시작하다 ('start') can take a plain noun object (운동을 시작하다), but when the thing you start is a verb, you nominalise it with 기 first. The tense goes on 시작하다.",formTable:[{when:"Start a verb action",add:"stem + 기 시작하다",ex:"듣다 → 듣기 시작하다, 읽다 → 읽기 시작하다"},{when:"Tense lives on 시작하다",add:"...기 시작했어요 / 시작해요 / 시작할 거예요",ex:"걷기 시작했어요"},{when:"Start a noun (no 기)",add:"N을/를 시작하다",ex:"운동을 시작했어요"}],examples:[{ko:"한국어 뉴스를 조금씩 듣기 시작했어요.",romanization:"han-gug-eo nyu-seu-reul jo-geum-ssik deud-gi si-jak-haess-eo-yo.",en:"I started listening to Korean news little by little.",note:"듣다 becomes 듣기 before 시작하다; the past tense is on 시작했어요."},{ko:"아침에 걷기 시작하니까 잠이 덜 와요.",romanization:"a-chim-e geod-gi si-jak-ha-ni-kka jam-i deol wa-yo.",en:"Since I started walking in the morning, I feel less sleepy.",note:"The whole started action can become a reason with -니까."},{ko:"책을 읽기 시작하면 휴대폰을 덜 보게 돼요.",romanization:"chaeg-eul ilg-gi si-jak-ha-myeon hyu-dae-pon-eul deol bo-ge dwae-yo.",en:"Once I start reading a book, I end up looking at my phone less.",note:"시작하면 turns the start into a condition."},{ko:"운동을 시작했어요.",romanization:"un-dong-eul si-jak-haess-eo-yo.",en:"I started exercising.",note:"운동 is already a noun, so it takes 을 directly — no 기 needed."}],keyPoint:{label:"기 시작하다 vs N 시작하다",body:"If you start a verb action, nominalise it: 걷기 시작하다, 듣기 시작하다, 읽기 시작하다. If you start a thing that is already a noun, just add 을/를: 운동을 시작하다, 공부를 시작하다. So '운동하다' becomes 운동하기 시작하다 OR 운동을 시작하다 — but never 운동기 시작하다."},pronunciation:"The 기 tenses after a plain-consonant stem: 듣기 [듣끼], 읽기 [일끼], 걷기 [걷끼]. 시작하다 is [시자카다], with ㄱ + ㅎ blending into an aspirated sound.",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each into 'started doing' with V-기 시작했어요.",model:"한국 드라마를 보다 → 한국 드라마를 보기 시작했어요.",items:["아침마다 걷다","한국어 뉴스를 듣다","매일 책을 읽다"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"운동기 시작했어요.",right:"운동을 시작했어요. / 운동하기 시작했어요.",explanation:"Do not attach 기 to a noun. 운동 is a noun, so use 운동을 시작하다; only the verb 운동하다 can take 기 → 운동하기 시작하다."}}],v=[{title:"A/V-네요: realizing or noticing something right now",func:`-네요 is the on-the-spot reaction ending. You attach it when you discover, see, hear, taste, or otherwise newly notice something at the moment of speaking. It carries a light feeling of mild surprise or fresh awareness: "Oh, it's ...!" Use it when the information just hit you, not when you are reporting a fact you already knew.`,formTable:[{when:"Verb stem (present)",add:"-네요",ex:"오다 -> 오네요 (Oh, it's coming/raining)"},{when:"Adjective stem",add:"-네요",ex:"맛있다 -> 맛있네요 (Wow, this is tasty)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었네요",ex:"끝나다 -> 끝났네요 (Oh, it's already over)"},{when:"ㄹ-stem (drop ㄹ)",add:"-네요",ex:"멀다 -> 머네요 (It's far, isn't it)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)네요",ex:"학생이네요 / 가수네요 (Oh, you're a student/singer)"}],examples:[{ko:"밖에 비가 오네요.",romanization:"bakke biga oneyo.",en:"Oh, it's raining outside.",note:"You just looked out and noticed the rain now -- that fresh discovery is exactly what -네요 marks."},{ko:"이 김치찌개 정말 맛있네요!",romanization:"i gimchijjigae jeongmal masinneyo!",en:"Wow, this kimchi stew is really delicious!",note:"Said after the first bite -- a reaction to something you are experiencing at this moment."},{ko:"벌써 6시네요. 시간 빠르네요.",romanization:"beolsseo yeoseot sineyo. sigan ppareuneyo.",en:"It's already 6. Time flies.",note:"Noticing the clock and the speed of time on the spot; 빠르네요 reacts to a realization, not a stored fact."},{ko:"한국어를 정말 잘하시네요.",romanization:"hangugeoreul jeongmal jalhasineyo.",en:"You really speak Korean well!",note:"A natural compliment -- you just heard them speak and are reacting, which is why -네요 sounds warm here."}],keyPoint:{label:"-네요 = reacting to NEW information",body:"The core trigger is newness to the speaker. If you already knew the fact and are just stating it, use plain -아/어요. The moment of fresh noticing is what makes -네요 the right choice."},pronunciation:"Past tense -았네요/-었네요 has the ㅅ assimilate to ㄴ: 끝났네요 sounds like [끈난네요], 갔네요 like [간네요]. Learners who pronounce a clear [ㅅ] here sound non-native.",drill:{instruction:"React in the moment using A/V-네요. Fill in the blank with the verb/adjective in parentheses.",model:"(예쁘다) -> 꽃이 예쁘네요!",items:["날씨가 정말 (춥다) -> 날씨가 정말 ____.","벌써 다 (먹다, past) -> 벌써 다 ____.","여기 사람이 많이 (있다) -> 여기 사람이 많이 ____."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"어제 비가 왔네요. (to report yesterday's weather you already knew)",right:"어제 비가 왔어요. / (noticing puddles now) 비가 왔네요.",explanation:"English speakers reach for -네요 whenever something feels noteworthy, but it must mark information that is new to you at the moment of speaking. Reporting a fact you already knew takes plain -아/어요; -네요 only fits when you are noticing it fresh."}},{title:`A/V-군요 / -구나: "I see, so that's how it is"`,func:`-군요 (polite) and -구나 (casual) express the moment of understanding: you take in new information and acknowledge it, often realizing the reason or background behind something. Where -네요 is a gut reaction to what you perceive, -군요/-구나 is the click of comprehension -- "Ah, so THAT'S why / so it's like that."`,formTable:[{when:"Adjective stem",add:"-군요 / -구나",ex:"춥다 -> 춥군요 / 춥구나 (Ah, it's cold)"},{when:"Verb stem (present)",add:"-는군요 / -는구나",ex:"가다 -> 가는군요 / 가는구나 (I see, you're going)"},{when:"Past tense (verb & adj)",add:"-았/었군요 / -았/었구나",ex:"아프다 -> 아팠군요 / 아팠구나 (Ah, you were sick)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)군요 / (이)구나",ex:"동생이군요 / 동생이구나 (Oh, it's your younger sibling)"}],examples:[{ko:"아, 그래서 어제 못 왔군요.",romanization:"a, geuraeseo eoje mot watgunyo.",en:"Ah, so that's why you couldn't come yesterday.",note:"그래서 + -군요 is the classic pairing: you just grasped the reason behind something."},{ko:"이게 그 유명한 식당이군요.",romanization:"ige geu yumyeonghan sikdangigunyo.",en:"So THIS is that famous restaurant.",note:"You connect what you heard before with what you now see -- a moment of recognition, not just surprise."},{ko:"민수 씨가 요리를 잘하는군요.",romanization:"minsu ssiga yorireul jalhaneun-gunyo.",en:"I see, so Minsu is good at cooking.",note:"Verb 잘하다 takes -는군요 in the present; you are registering a newly learned fact about Minsu."},{ko:"벌써 졸업했구나. 시간 진짜 빠르다.",romanization:"beolsseo joreophaetguna. sigan jinjja ppareuda.",en:"Oh, you already graduated. Time really flies.",note:"Casual -구나 to a close friend; it shows you absorbing news and reflecting on it."}],keyPoint:{label:"Verbs need -는군요 in the present",body:"Adjectives and nouns attach -군요 directly (춥군요, 학생이군요), but present-tense verbs insert -는-: 가는군요, 먹는군요. Past tense is uniform for both: -았/었군요. -구나 is simply the casual (반말) version."},pronunciation:'-는군요 links the final ㄴ into the 요 in fast speech, sounding like [는군뇨]. The casual -구나 is often drawn out for feeling: 그렇구나~, signaling a thoughtful "ahh, I see."',drill:{instruction:"Show that you just understood something using -군요 (polite) or -구나 (casual).",model:"(친구가 미국 사람이다) -> 미국 사람이군요!",items:["(그래서 전화를 안 받았다) -> 그래서 전화를 안 ____.","(이 노래를 직접 만들었다) -> 이 노래를 직접 ____.","(casual: 너도 이 영화 봤다) -> 너도 이 영화 ____."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"민수 씨가 요리를 잘하군요.",right:"민수 씨가 요리를 잘하는군요.",explanation:"English speakers attach -군요 straight to every stem because adjectives work that way (춥군요). But present-tense verbs require the inserted -는-: 잘하다 -> 잘하는군요, 먹다 -> 먹는군요. Forgetting -는- is the single most common mistake with this ending."}},{title:'V/A-(으)ㄹ걸요: "probably," a soft guess',func:`-(으)ㄹ걸요 offers a tentative guess based on your own reasoning or hunch, softened so it doesn't sound too sure. It means "I think probably ..." or "it's likely that ...," and is common when answering someone gently without claiming certainty. (Written the same, -(으)ㄹ걸 그랬다 with a sighing tone means regret, "I should have ...," but here we focus on the guessing use.)`,formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ending in vowel",add:"-ㄹ걸요",ex:"오다 -> 올걸요 (They'll probably come)"},{when:"Verb stem ending in consonant",add:"-을걸요",ex:"먹다 -> 먹을걸요 (They'll probably eat / they probably do eat)"},{when:"Past tense (guessing about the past)",add:"-았/었을걸요",ex:"가다 -> 갔을걸요 (They probably already left)"},{when:"Adjective stem",add:"-(으)ㄹ걸요",ex:"비싸다 -> 비쌀걸요 / 좋다 -> 좋을걸요 (probably expensive/good)"},{when:"ㄹ-stem (drop ㄹ, add -걸요)",add:"-ㄹ걸요",ex:"멀다 -> 멀걸요 (It's probably far)"}],examples:[{ko:"지금 가게 문 닫았을걸요.",romanization:"jigeum gage mun dadasseulgeoryo.",en:"The shop is probably closed now.",note:"A soft prediction about a present situation; -았을걸요 guesses the door is already in a closed state."},{ko:"민지 씨는 매운 거 잘 먹을걸요.",romanization:"minji ssineun maeun geo jal meogeulgeoryo.",en:"Minji can probably handle spicy food well.",note:"You're guessing from what you know of her -- not stating a fact, just a confident-but-soft hunch."},{ko:"그 영화 벌써 끝났을걸요.",romanization:"geu yeonghwa beolsseo kkeunnasseulgeoryo.",en:"That movie has probably already ended.",note:"Past-guess -았을걸요 estimates about something completed; very natural in casual conversation."},{ko:"주말이라서 길이 많이 막힐걸요.",romanization:"jumariraseo giri mani makilgeoryo.",en:"Since it's the weekend, the roads will probably be jammed.",note:"Reasoning from a known cause (주말이라서) to a likely outcome -- the textbook use of a soft guess."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ걸요 = your guess, not shared knowledge",body:`Use -(으)ㄹ걸요 when the conclusion comes from your own inference and you want to stay humble about it. Compare -(으)ㄹ 거예요, which sounds more confident/declarative. -걸요 always softens, as if to say "I'm fairly sure, but don't quote me."`},pronunciation:"The 걸 is tensed to [껄]: 올걸요 sounds like [올껄요], 먹을걸요 like [머글껄요]. The sentence also rises slightly at the end, giving it its tentative, musing feel.",drill:{instruction:"Answer with a soft guess using V/A-(으)ㄹ걸요.",model:"(은행이 지금 열었을까?) -> 지금 문 열었을걸요.",items:["(이 책 재미있다?) -> 그 책 아마 ____.","(민수가 집에 있다?) -> 지금 집에 ____.","(표가 비싸다?) -> 주말이라서 표가 ____."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"내일 비가 올 거예요. (when you only have a vague hunch, not real evidence)",right:"내일 비가 올걸요. (a soft, unsure guess) -- use 올 거예요 only when you're genuinely confident.",explanation:'English "probably" and "will" feel interchangeable, so learners default to -(으)ㄹ 거예요 even for hunches. But -(으)ㄹ 거예요 sounds like a confident claim, while -(으)ㄹ걸요 keeps it hedged. Match the ending to how sure you actually are. Also keep -(으)ㄹ걸요 (guess) separate from -(으)ㄹ걸 그랬다 (regret, "I should have").'}},{title:'V-았/었을 텐데(요): "it would have ..., but"',func:`-았/었을 텐데(요) expresses a confident guess about an unrealized past -- what surely would have happened -- usually paired with a contrasting reality, regret, or concern. It looks back and supposes: "It would have been ... (but it wasn't / but something got in the way)." The 텐데 leaves the thought hanging, inviting sympathy or a soft follow-up rather than a blunt complaint.`,formTable:[{when:"Verb, past supposition",add:"-았/었을 텐데(요)",ex:"오다 -> 왔을 텐데요 (They would have come)"},{when:"Often after an unreal condition -았/었으면",add:"-았/었으면 ... -았/었을 텐데요",ex:"시간이 있었으면 갔을 텐데요 (If I'd had time, I'd have gone)"},{when:"Adjective, past supposition",add:"-았/었을 텐데(요)",ex:"쉽다 -> 쉬웠을 텐데요 (It would have been easy)"},{when:"Present/future concern (contrast)",add:"-(으)ㄹ 텐데(요)",ex:"바쁘다 -> 바쁠 텐데요 (They must be busy, though)"}],examples:[{ko:"어제 시간이 있었으면 같이 갔을 텐데요.",romanization:"eoje sigani isseosseumyeon gachi gasseul tendeyo.",en:"If I'd had time yesterday, I would have gone with you.",note:`The trip didn't happen; -았/었을 텐데요 keeps the regret soft instead of stating a flat "I couldn't go."`},{ko:"미리 알았으면 도와드렸을 텐데요.",romanization:"miri arasseumyeon dowadeuryeosseul tendeyo.",en:"If I'd known in advance, I would have helped.",note:"Polite regret after missing a chance to help -- 미리 알았으면 sets the unreal condition."},{ko:"조금만 일찍 왔으면 표를 살 수 있었을 텐데요.",romanization:"jogeumman iljjik wasseumyeon pyoreul sal su isseosseul tendeyo.",en:"If you'd come just a bit earlier, you could have bought a ticket.",note:"Gentle 'too bad' about a missed chance; the 텐데 trails off, softening it."},{ko:"버스를 탔으면 안 늦었을 텐데요.",romanization:"beoseureul tasseumyeon an neujeosseul tendeyo.",en:"If you'd taken the bus, you wouldn't have been late.",note:"Looking back at what would have avoided the problem -- sympathetic, not accusatory."}],keyPoint:{label:"Past -았/었을 텐데 = unreal past; present -(으)ㄹ 텐데 = current concern",body:`Add the past -았/었- when you're supposing about something that did NOT happen (regret): 갔을 텐데요. Drop it for a present/future worry or contrast: 지금 바쁠 텐데요 ("they must be busy right now, though"). Mixing these changes the meaning from regret to ongoing concern.`},pronunciation:"텐데 is [텐데] with a clear, even tone, but the sentence-final 요 trails off and drops in pitch -- that falling, unfinished melody is what signals the lingering 'but...' feeling. Don't end it sharply.",drill:{instruction:"Express soft regret about an unreal past using -았/었으면 ... -았/었을 텐데요.",model:"(시간 / 같이 가다) -> 시간이 있었으면 같이 갔을 텐데요.",items:["(미리 알다 / 도와주다) -> 미리 ____ 도와드렸을 텐데요.","(일찍 출발하다 / 안 늦다) -> 일찍 출발했으면 ____.","(연습 많이 하다 / 쉽다) -> 연습을 많이 했으면 훨씬 ____."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"시간이 있으면 같이 갔을 텐데요. (present 있으면 with a past-regret result)",right:"시간이 있었으면 같이 갔을 텐데요.",explanation:'For regret about the past, the IF-clause must also be past-unreal: 있었으면, not 있으면. English "if I had time" hides the tense, so learners use present 있으면 and clash with the past 갔을 텐데요. Match both halves to the unreal past: 있었으면 ... 갔을 텐데요.'}},{title:"-군요 vs -네요: both react, but not the same way",func:`-네요 and -군요 both respond to new information, but with a different feel. -네요 is an immediate, conversational gut reaction to what you directly perceive right now ("oh, look at that"). -군요 is a slightly more composed acknowledgement -- you take in a fact or explanation and register it ("I see, so that's the case"). Knowing which one to reach for keeps your reactions sounding natural.`,formTable:[{when:"Fresh, direct noticing (see/hear/feel now)",add:"-네요",ex:"비가 오네요 (Oh, it's raining)"},{when:"Acknowledging a fact/explanation you just learned",add:"-군요 / -구나",ex:"아, 그렇군요 (Ah, I see)"},{when:"Verb present: -네요 (no 는) vs -는군요 (needs 는)",add:"-네요 / -는군요",ex:"가네요 / 가는군요",ex2:""},{when:"Reacting to a reason (그래서 ...)",add:"often -군요",ex:"그래서 못 왔군요 (So that's why you couldn't come)"}],examples:[{ko:"어, 눈이 오네요!",romanization:"eo, nuni oneyo!",en:"Oh, it's snowing!",note:"-네요: a spontaneous reaction to what you see out the window this second."},{ko:"아, 그래서 늦으셨군요.",romanization:"a, geuraeseo neujeusyeotgunyo.",en:"Ah, so that's why you were late.",note:"-군요: you take in the explanation and acknowledge the reason -- calmer than a gut reaction."},{ko:"그렇군요. 제가 잘못 알고 있었네요.",romanization:"geureokunyo. jega jalmot algo isseonneyo.",en:"I see. I had it wrong.",note:"Both in one breath: 군요 accepts the new fact, 네요 marks your own fresh realization about yourself."},{ko:"이 집 커피가 진짜 맛있네요.",romanization:"i jip keopiga jinjja masinneyo.",en:"The coffee here is really good.",note:"-네요: a direct, in-the-moment reaction to taste -- 맛있군요 would sound oddly stiff here."}],keyPoint:{label:"Perceiving now -> -네요; registering a fact -> -군요",body:"If you're reacting to something you directly sense at this moment (taste, weather, someone's arrival), -네요 sounds warm and natural. If you're acknowledging information or a reason that just clicked, -군요 fits. -군요 can sound stiff for simple sensory reactions, and -네요 can sound under-reactive for absorbing a full explanation."},pronunciation:"그렇군요 is pronounced [그러쿠뇨] (ㅎ + ㄱ tenses/aspirates to ㅋ), and 그렇네요 is [그런네요] (ㅎ assimilates to ㄴ before ㄴ). Both are extremely common backchannels -- saying them clearly helps you sound like an engaged listener.",drill:{instruction:"Choose -네요 (direct noticing) or -군요 (acknowledging a fact/reason), then fill the blank.",model:"(친구 설명을 듣고) 아, 그래서 그랬____. -> 그랬군요.",items:["(창밖을 보며) 어, 비가 ____. [오다]","(이유를 듣고) 아, 그래서 못 ____. [오다, past]","(처음 먹어 보고) 이 떡볶이 진짜 ____! [맛있다]"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"(처음 맛보고) 와, 이거 맛있군요!",right:"(처음 맛보고) 와, 이거 맛있네요!",explanation:`For a direct, in-the-moment sensory reaction (taste, weather, a view), Korean prefers -네요; -군요 sounds too composed or bookish there. Learners over-use -군요 because it's taught as "I see," but reserve it for acknowledging facts or reasons, not for spontaneous "wow, this is good!" reactions.`}}],_=[{title:"이/가 — a new or focused subject (new information)",func:"The subject marker 이/가 spotlights WHO or WHAT does or is something. Use it when the subject is new to the conversation, when it answers a 'who?'/'what?' question, or when you want the listener's attention on that exact element. It introduces; it does not assume.",formTable:[{when:"subject noun ends in a consonant",add:"+ 이",ex:"선생님 → 선생님이"},{when:"subject noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가",ex:"친구 → 친구가"},{when:"answering 누가/뭐가 (who/what)",add:"marked noun = the answer",ex:"누가 왔어요? → 동생이 왔어요."}],examples:[{ko:"비가 와요.",romanization:"biga wayo.",en:"It's raining.",note:"비 is brand-new on the scene, so 가 (not 는) presents it as fresh news."},{ko:"누가 한국어를 가르쳐요?",romanization:"nuga hangugeoreul gareuchyeoyo?",en:"Who teaches Korean?",note:"The question word 누가 is the focus, so the subject naturally takes 가."},{ko:"제 친구가 부산에 살아요.",romanization:"je chinguga busane sarayo.",en:"My friend lives in Busan.",note:"Introducing the friend for the first time, so 가 presents the new subject."},{ko:"문제가 있어요.",romanization:"munjega isseoyo.",en:"There's a problem.",note:"Existence sentences with 있다/없다 present new info, so the thing takes 이/가."}],keyPoint:{label:"이/가 = new and in focus",body:"Compare 누가 왔어요? — 친구가 왔어요 (NEW: 가) with 친구는 언제 와요? (already-known friend: 는). 이/가 answers 'who/what'; 은/는 talks ABOUT a known thing."},pronunciation:"이 after a consonant links smoothly: 선생님이 sounds like 선생니미; 책이 like 채기. The final consonant slides onto 이.",drill:{instruction:"Fill the blank with 이 or 가 to mark the new/focused subject.",model:"누가 왔어요? → 동생___ 왔어요. → 동생이",items:["눈___ 와요. (눈 = snow) → 눈이","뭐가 맛있어요? → 김치___ 맛있어요. → 김치가","선생님___ 교실에 계세요. → 선생님이"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"비는 와요.",right:"비가 와요.",explanation:"English has no topic/subject split, so learners default to one marker for everything. Brand-new, on-the-scene information (weather, sudden events, answers to 'who/what') takes 이/가, not 은/는."}},{title:"은/는 — topic continuity (an already-known topic)",func:"The topic marker 은/는 sets up what the sentence is ABOUT. Use it for something already known, already mentioned, or generally assumed — the shared starting point. It says 'as for X…' and then comments on it. It carries old information, not new.",formTable:[{when:"topic noun ends in a consonant",add:"+ 은",ex:"선생님 → 선생님은"},{when:"topic noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 는",ex:"저 → 저는"},{when:"stating a general fact",add:"topic + comment",ex:"강아지는 귀여워요. (Puppies are cute.)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 학생이에요.",romanization:"jeoneun haksaengieyo.",en:"I'm a student.",note:"'I' is the obvious shared topic of self-introduction, so 는 frames it and the comment follows."},{ko:"그 영화는 정말 재미있었어요.",romanization:"geu yeonghwaneun jeongmal jaemiisseosseoyo.",en:"That movie was really fun.",note:"그 영화 is already known (we both know which movie), so 는 continues it as the topic."},{ko:"한국어는 발음이 어려워요.",romanization:"hangugeoneun bareumi eoryeowoyo.",en:"As for Korean, the pronunciation is hard.",note:"한국어 is the established topic; the new comment is about its 발음 (which itself takes 이)."},{ko:"오늘은 날씨가 좋아요.",romanization:"oneureun nalssiga joayo.",en:"The weather is nice today.",note:"오늘 is the assumed frame ('as for today'), so 는 sets the scene and 날씨가 carries the new subject."}],keyPoint:{label:"은/는 = old, 이/가 = new",body:"First mention introduces with 이/가; once it's on the table, you keep talking about it with 은/는. 옛날에 한 남자가 살았어요. 그 남자는… (A man lived… The man…)."},pronunciation:"은 after a consonant resyllabifies: 선생님은 → 선생니믄; 책은 → 채근. Keep 는 as a clear 'neun' with no vowel reduction.",drill:{instruction:"Add 은 or 는 to set the already-known topic.",model:"저___ 한국 사람이에요. → 저는",items:["그 책___ 재미있어요. (그 책 = the book we mentioned) → 그 책은","선생님___ 친절하세요. → 선생님은","오늘___ 좀 피곤해요. → 오늘은"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"저가 학생이에요.",right:"저는 학생이에요. (or focused: 제가 했어요.)",explanation:"For self-introduction the speaker is the assumed topic, so use 저는, not 저가. Also note 저 + 가 irregularly becomes 제가 when you DO need focus ('I' as the one who did it) — 저가 is never correct."}},{title:"은/는 — contrast and limited scope",func:"Beyond topic-setting, 은/는 marks CONTRAST: 'X (but as for something else, it's different).' It quietly implies a comparison, even when the other side is unspoken. Use it to set one item against another, or to limit a statement to just that item.",formTable:[{when:"contrasting two nouns",add:"both take 은/는",ex:"커피는 마셔요. 차는 안 마셔요."},{when:"attaching contrast to an object",add:"은/는 replaces 을/를",ex:"고기는 안 먹어요. (meat, at least, I don't eat)"},{when:"limiting / 'this one, at least' nuance",add:"noun + 는 with implied 'others differ'",ex:"이건 알아요. (this one I do know — others maybe not)"}],examples:[{ko:"형은 키가 커요. 저는 작아요.",romanization:"hyeongeun kiga keoyo. jeoneun jagayo.",en:"My older brother is tall. I'm short.",note:"Two 는 set the siblings in direct contrast — tall vs. short."},{ko:"커피는 좋아하는데 차는 안 좋아해요.",romanization:"keopineun joahaneunde chaneun an joahaeyo.",en:"I like coffee but not tea.",note:"는 on both objects (not 를) signals the explicit coffee-vs-tea contrast."},{ko:"한국어는 할 수 있어요.",romanization:"hangugeoneun hal su isseoyo.",en:"Korean, at least, I can speak.",note:"는 here limits the claim to Korean, implying 'other languages, maybe not.'"},{ko:"오늘은 안 바빠요.",romanization:"oneureun an bappayo.",en:"Today I'm not busy (other days, I am).",note:"는 on 오늘 contrasts today against the implied busy other days."}],keyPoint:{label:"는 over 가/를 = contrast is intended",body:"커피를 마셔요 = plain 'I drink coffee.' 커피는 마셔요 = 'Coffee I drink (but something else, no).' Swapping 를→는 (or 가→는) adds an unspoken 'unlike the rest.'"},pronunciation:"In the contrast reading, 는/은 often carries a slight emphatic stress: 저'는 작아요. That extra stress is what flags the contrast aloud.",drill:{instruction:"Replace the neutral marker with 은/는 to add a contrast nuance.",model:"고기를 안 먹어요. (contrast) → 고기는 안 먹어요.",items:["야채를 좋아해요. (contrast) → 야채___ 좋아해요. → 야채는","주말에 일해요. (but weekdays, no) → 주말___ 일해요. → 주말은","동생은 와요. (but I'm not coming) → 저___ 안 가요. → 저는"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"커피를 좋아하는데 차를 안 좋아해요.",right:"커피는 좋아하는데 차는 안 좋아해요.",explanation:"English shows contrast with 'but' and word order, so learners keep 을/를 on both objects. Korean signals the contrast on the noun itself by switching 을/를 to 는 — leaving 를 there flattens the intended comparison."}},{title:"이/가 — 'this exact one' (exclusive identification)",func:"When you single out ONE item as the answer — 'it is THIS one, not the others' — the identified subject takes 이/가. This exclusive-focus use is why 이/가 (not 은/는) appears in answers, in 'who/what' replies, and in 'it's X that…' sentences. It pins down the exact one.",formTable:[{when:"answering with the exact identity",add:"answer + 이/가",ex:"누가 반장이에요? → 민수가 반장이에요."},{when:"'it is X' exclusive focus",add:"X + 이/가 + verb",ex:"이 책이 제일 좋아요. (THIS book is the best.)"},{when:"picking one out of several",add:"chosen item + 이/가",ex:"이게 제 거예요. (THIS one is mine.)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 사람이 제 선생님이에요.",romanization:"i sarami je seonsaengnimieyo.",en:"THIS person is my teacher.",note:"이 사람 is singled out from others present, so 이 marks the exclusive identification."},{ko:"누가 했어요? — 제가 했어요.",romanization:"nuga haesseoyo? — jega haesseoyo.",en:"Who did it? — I did.",note:"Answering 'who' pins down the exact doer; 저+가 → 제가 carries that exclusive focus."},{ko:"어느 가방이 마음에 들어요?",romanization:"eoneu gabangi maeume deureoyo?",en:"Which bag do you like?",note:"어느 (which) forces a one-out-of-many choice, so the subject takes 이."},{ko:"바로 이게 정답이에요.",romanization:"baro ige jeongdabieyo.",en:"THIS is exactly the right answer.",note:"바로 + 이게 (이것이) drives home 'this exact one and no other' — classic 이/가 focus."}],keyPoint:{label:"이/가 identifies, 은/는 describes",body:"이 사람이 김 선생님이에요 = 'THIS (one) is Mr. Kim' (picks him out). 이 사람은 김 선생님이에요 = 'As for this person, he's Mr. Kim' (just tells you about him). Use 이/가 to single out the exact one."},pronunciation:"이것이 commonly contracts to 이게, 그것이 → 그게, 저것이 → 저게 in speech. Learn the contracted 이게/그게/저게 as the everyday spoken forms.",drill:{instruction:"Fill in 이 or 가 to single out the exact one being identified.",model:"누가 일등이에요? → 수진___ 일등이에요. → 수진이",items:["어느 게 더 싸요? → 이것___ 더 싸요. → 이것이 (이게)","누가 운전해요? → 제___ 운전해요. → 제가","이 가방___ 제일 예뻐요. → 이 가방이"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"누가 했어요? — 저는 했어요.",right:"누가 했어요? — 제가 했어요.",explanation:"Answering a 'who' question identifies the exact doer, which demands 이/가, but English speakers reach for the topic-like 저는. 저는 했어요 sounds like 'As for me, I did (but talk about others separately)' — not a clean answer to 'who.'"}},{title:"N은/는 N이/가 … — topic + inner subject in one sentence",func:"Korean often uses BOTH markers in a single sentence: 은/는 names the big-picture topic, then 이/가 names the specific thing the comment is really about. Pattern: '[Topic]은/는 [inner subject]이/가 [adjective/verb].' This is the natural way to describe a part, trait, or condition of something — 'As for the elephant, its nose is long.'",formTable:[{when:"describing a body part / trait",add:"Topic은/는 + part이/가 + adj",ex:"코끼리는 코가 길어요. (The elephant's nose is long.)"},{when:"saying what someone likes/has",add:"Person은/는 + thing이/가 + 좋다/있다",ex:"저는 강아지가 좋아요. (As for me, I like dogs.)"},{when:"stating an ability/condition",add:"Topic은/는 + thing이/가 + 어렵다/많다",ex:"한국어는 발음이 어려워요. (Korean — its pronunciation is hard.)"}],examples:[{ko:"코끼리는 코가 길어요.",romanization:"kokkiri-neun ko-ga gireoyo.",en:"An elephant's nose is long.",note:"코끼리 is the topic (은/는); 코 is the inner subject (이/가) that 길어요 actually describes."},{ko:"저는 시간이 없어요.",romanization:"jeoneun sigani eopseoyo.",en:"I don't have time.",note:"Literally 'As for me, time doesn't exist.' 저 = topic (는), 시간 = subject of 없다 (이)."},{ko:"우리 형은 키가 커요.",romanization:"uri hyeongeun kiga keoyo.",en:"My older brother is tall.",note:"형 is the topic; 키 (height) is the inner subject of 크다, so 키가 커요 states 'tall' unambiguously. 형은 커요 can also be grammatical, but without context it may mean that he is big rather than specifically tall."},{ko:"이 식당은 김치찌개가 맛있어요.",romanization:"i sikdangeun gimchijjigaega masisseoyo.",en:"This restaurant's kimchi stew is delicious.",note:"식당 = topic (은); 김치찌개 = inner subject (가). The comment is really about the dish."}],keyPoint:{label:"The clearest beginner frame: topic + inner subject",body:"For a neutral description, mark the big topic with 은/는 and the specific part or condition with 이/가: 저는 머리가 아파요. Korean can also use repeated 이/가 in double-subject constructions or repeated 은/는 for contrast, so this is a reliable default pattern rather than an absolute ban on other combinations."},pronunciation:"코가 = ko-ga (smooth, no stop); 키가 = ki-ga; 시간이 = sigani (final ㄴ links onto 이). Keep both syllables of 이/가 light — the stress sits on the predicate (길어요, 커요).",drill:{instruction:"Build the sentence with 은/는 on the topic and 이/가 on the inner subject.",model:"나 / 머리 / 아프다 → 저는 머리가 아파요.",items:["기린 / 목 / 길다 → 기린은 목이 길어요.","저 / 동생 / 있다 → 저는 동생이 있어요.","이 커피 / 맛 / 좋다 → 이 커피는 맛이 좋아요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"코끼리가 코가 길어요 and 저는 시간은 없어요 are always ungrammatical.",right:"코끼리는 코가 길어요. / 저는 시간이 없어요. (neutral defaults)",explanation:"The right-hand sentences are the safest neutral forms for beginners. 코끼리가 코가 길어요 can occur as a double-subject construction, and 저는 시간은 없어요 can contrast time with something else ('I don't have time, at least'). Do not mark those forms wrong without context; teach the difference in focus."}}],x=[{title:"V-아/어 주세요 / V-(으)면 연락 주세요 — delivery requests and updates",func:"Delivery messages usually contain one concrete request and one condition. V-아/어 주세요 asks someone to do an action, while V-(으)면 marks the condition that triggers the next action: 도착하면 연락 주세요 means 'Please contact me when you arrive.' Look for the action closest to 주세요 to find what the sender wants.",formTable:[{when:"A direct but polite delivery request",add:"verb stem + -아/어 주세요",ex:"놓다 → 문 앞에 놓아 주세요 (Please leave it at the door.)"},{when:"A condition followed by a request",add:"verb stem + -(으)면 + request",ex:"도착하다 → 도착하면 연락 주세요 (Please contact me when you arrive.)"},{when:"A more careful request to the driver",add:"V-아/어 주실 수 있을까요?",ex:"보내다 → 사진을 보내 주실 수 있을까요?"},{when:"A completed delivery update",add:"V-았/었습니다 / V-아/어 두었습니다",ex:"배송했습니다 / 문 앞에 두었습니다"}],examples:[{ko:"문 앞에 놓아 주세요.",romanization:"mun ape noa juseyo.",en:"Please leave it at the door.",note:"문 앞 is the location; 놓아 주세요 is the requested action. 놓아 is often shortened to 놔 in casual messages."},{ko:"도착하면 전화하지 말고 문자 주세요.",romanization:"dochakamyeon jeonhwahaji malgo munja juseyo.",en:"When you arrive, please text instead of calling.",note:"-지 말고 replaces one action with another: not a call, but a text."},{ko:"택배를 경비실에 맡겨 두었습니다.",romanization:"taekbaereul gyeongbisire matgyeo dueotseumnida.",en:"Your parcel has been left with the security office.",note:"맡겨 두었습니다 reports that the parcel was placed there and remains available for pickup."},{ko:"배송 완료 사진을 보내 주실 수 있을까요?",romanization:"baesong wallyo sajineul bonae jusil su isseulkkayo?",en:"Could you send me a delivery-completion photo?",note:"-주실 수 있을까요? is useful when asking a driver for an extra action without sounding demanding."}],keyPoint:{label:"Find location, trigger, and requested action",body:"In 문 앞에 놓아 주세요, the location is 문 앞 and the action is 놓다. In 도착하면 문자 주세요, the trigger is 도착하면 and the requested action is 문자 주세요. Reading these parts separately lets you understand the message even when particles are omitted."},pronunciation:"놓아 주세요 is carefully [노아 주세요] and is often contracted to 놔 주세요 [놔 주세요] in speech. 맡겨 is pronounced [맏껴], with the following ㄱ becoming tense.",drill:{instruction:"Read the message and identify the required action.",model:"도착하면 문자 주세요. → Action: send a text after arrival.",items:["문 앞에 놓아 주세요. → Where should the parcel go?","전화하지 말고 문자 주세요. → Which action should be avoided?","경비실에 맡겨 두었습니다. → Where is the parcel now?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"도착하면 연락해요. = a clear request to the driver",right:"도착하면 연락 주세요. / 도착하면 연락해 주세요.",explanation:"A plain -해요 sentence can sound like a statement about what normally happens. In a delivery instruction, 주세요 or 해 주세요 makes the requested action explicit."}},{title:"N도 있으세요? / V-(으)세요 — clinic questions and medicine instructions",func:"At a clinic or pharmacy, staff often ask whether an additional symptom is present with N도 있으세요? and ask about your condition with 어디가 불편하세요? Instructions usually end in -(으)세요: 식후에 드세요 means 'Take it after meals.' The ending is polite, but the message may be a question, an instruction, or a recommendation depending on intonation and context.",formTable:[{when:"Asking whether another symptom is present",add:"symptom + 도 있으세요?",ex:"열도 있으세요? (Do you also have a fever?)"},{when:"Asking which body part feels wrong",add:"어디가 + 불편하세요/아프세요?",ex:"어디가 제일 아프세요?"},{when:"Giving a medicine instruction",add:"verb stem + -(으)세요",ex:"먹다 → 식후에 드세요 (Take it after meals.)"},{when:"Giving frequency",add:"하루에 + number + 번",ex:"하루에 세 번 드세요 (Take it three times a day.)"}],examples:[{ko:"기침 말고 열도 있으세요?",romanization:"gichim malgo yeoldo isseuseyo?",en:"Besides the cough, do you also have a fever?",note:"말고 means 'apart from / instead of.' 도 asks about an additional symptom."},{ko:"어디가 제일 불편하세요?",romanization:"eodiga jeil bulpyeonhaseyo?",en:"Where are you feeling the most discomfort?",note:"불편하다 can cover pain, irritation, and general discomfort, so it is broader than 아프다."},{ko:"이 약은 하루에 세 번 식후에 드세요.",romanization:"i yageun harue se beon sikhue deuseyo.",en:"Take this medicine three times a day after meals.",note:"Read the frequency and timing separately: 하루에 세 번 + 식후에."},{ko:"졸릴 수 있으니까 운전하지 마세요.",romanization:"jollil su isseunikka unjeonhaji maseyo.",en:"It may make you drowsy, so do not drive.",note:"The first clause gives a possible side effect; -지 마세요 gives the restriction."}],keyPoint:{label:"Separate symptom, frequency, timing, and warning",body:"A medicine message can contain four different facts: what symptom it treats, how often to take it, when to take it, and what to avoid. In 하루에 세 번 식후에 드세요, 세 번 is frequency and 식후에 is timing. Never treat the whole line as one memorized block."},pronunciation:"식후 is pronounced [시쿠] because ㄱ and ㅎ combine into ㅋ. 드세요 is [드세요]; it is the honorific everyday form used instead of 먹으세요 for medicine and food.",drill:{instruction:"Extract the practical information from each clinic or pharmacy line.",model:"하루에 두 번 드세요. → Frequency: twice a day.",items:["식후 30분에 드세요. → When should it be taken?","열도 있으세요? → What additional symptom is being checked?","운전하지 마세요. → What must the patient avoid?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"열이 나요 is the only possible way to mention a fever.",right:"열이 나요. (reporting the symptom) / 열도 있으세요? (checking its presence)",explanation:"나다 is the most useful pairing when you report that a fever developed. 있다 is also natural when a medical worker checks whether the fever is present. The surrounding task changes the preferred verb."}},{title:"V-아/어야 해요 / V-지 마세요 — housing notices and building rules",func:"Apartment and management-office notices tell you what is required, prohibited, or temporarily unavailable. V-아/어야 해요 marks an obligation, V-지 마세요 marks a prohibition, and N 중 marks an activity currently in progress. First find the date and time, then find the action ending.",formTable:[{when:"A required resident action",add:"verb stem + -아/어야 해요",ex:"버리다 → 화요일에 버려야 해요"},{when:"A prohibited action",add:"verb stem + -지 마세요",ex:"이용하다 → 이용하지 마세요"},{when:"A facility is being checked or repaired",add:"noun + 중 / V-는 중",ex:"점검 중 / 수리 중"},{when:"A service stops temporarily",add:"N이/가 중단돼요 / 단수예요",ex:"오전 10시부터 수도 공급이 중단돼요"}],examples:[{ko:"내일 오전 10시부터 두 시간 동안 단수예요.",romanization:"naeil ojeon yeol sibuteo du sigan dongan dansuyeyo.",en:"The water will be shut off for two hours starting at 10 a.m. tomorrow.",note:"부터 gives the start; 두 시간 동안 gives the duration; 단수 gives the service interruption."},{ko:"점검 중에는 엘리베이터를 이용하지 마세요.",romanization:"jeomgeom jungeneun ellibeiteoreul iyonghaji maseyo.",en:"Do not use the elevator during the inspection.",note:"중에는 defines the restricted period; -지 마세요 states the prohibited action."},{ko:"재활용품은 화요일 저녁에 버려야 해요.",romanization:"jaehwaryongpumeun hwayoil jeonyeoge beoryeoya haeyo.",en:"Recyclables must be put out on Tuesday evening.",note:"The topic is 재활용품; the required time is 화요일 저녁."},{ko:"불편 사항은 관리실로 연락해 주세요.",romanization:"bulpyeon sahangeun gwallisillo yeollakhae juseyo.",en:"Please contact the management office about any problems.",note:"관리실로 marks the contact destination; 불편 사항 means an issue or inconvenience to report."}],keyPoint:{label:"Read notices in this order: when → what changes → what you must do",body:"내일 오전 10시부터 / 단수 / 물을 미리 받아 두세요 means: start time / service change / preparation. Korean notices often omit a personal subject, so focus on time words and the final action ending rather than looking for 'you.'"},pronunciation:"단수예요 is pronounced [단수예요]. 점검 is [점검], and 관리실로 links as [괄리실로] in standard pronunciation because ㄴ before ㄹ becomes ㄹ.",drill:{instruction:"Read each notice and state what the resident must do.",model:"점검 중에는 이용하지 마세요. → Do not use it during inspection.",items:["오전 10시부터 단수예요. → When does the water stop?","물을 미리 받아 두세요. → What preparation is requested?","화요일 저녁에 버려야 해요. → When should it be put out?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"단수예요 means the building has no water permanently.",right:"단수예요 means the water supply is temporarily shut off; check the stated start and duration.",explanation:"Housing notices normally pair 단수 with a time range. Missing 부터, 까지, or 동안 can make you misunderstand a temporary interruption as a permanent condition."}},{title:"N부터 N까지 / N 동안 — finding time ranges in notices",func:"Notices often place the time before the main event. 부터 marks the starting point, 까지 marks the endpoint, and 동안 marks the duration. First find these time markers, then read what service changes or what action is required.",formTable:[{when:"Starting point",add:"time + 부터",ex:"오전 10시부터"},{when:"Endpoint or deadline",add:"time + 까지",ex:"오후 1시까지"},{when:"Complete time range",add:"start + 부터 + end + 까지",ex:"오전 10시부터 오후 1시까지"},{when:"Length of time",add:"duration + 동안",ex:"세 시간 동안"}],examples:[{ko:"오전 10시부터 오후 1시까지 단수예요.",romanization:"ojeon yeol sibuteo ohu han sikkaji dansuyeyo.",en:"The water will be shut off from 10 a.m. to 1 p.m.",note:"The 부터…까지 frame gives the full service-interruption window."},{ko:"점검은 세 시간 동안 진행됩니다.",romanization:"jeomgeomeun se sigan dongan jinhaengdoemnida.",en:"The inspection will continue for three hours.",note:"동안 gives the duration, not the finishing clock time."},{ko:"오늘 오후 10시까지 택배를 찾아가세요.",romanization:"oneul ohu yeol sikkaji taekbaereul chajagaseyo.",en:"Please pick up the parcel by 10 p.m. today.",note:"With one time followed by 까지, the time is usually a deadline."},{ko:"월요일부터 금요일까지 정상 운행합니다.",romanization:"woryoilbuteo geumyoilkkaji jeongsang unhaenghamnida.",en:"Normal service operates from Monday through Friday.",note:"부터 and 까지 can mark days as well as clock times."}],keyPoint:{label:"Range and duration answer different questions",body:"오전 10시부터 오후 1시까지 answers 'from when until when?' 세 시간 동안 answers 'for how long?' A notice may show both, but either one can be enough to plan what to do."},pronunciation:"부터 is [부터]. 시간 동안 is pronounced as written, [시간 동안], with each word kept clear. 까지 is [까지] with a tense ㄲ.",drill:{instruction:"Decide whether the highlighted time marks a start, deadline, full range, or duration.",model:"오후 6시까지 → Deadline.",items:["내일부터 → Start.","오전 9시부터 11시까지 → Full range.","두 시간 동안 → Duration."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"두 시간까지 점검해요. = The inspection lasts for two hours.",right:"두 시간 동안 점검해요. = The inspection lasts for two hours.",explanation:"까지 marks an endpoint or limit. To state how long an event continues, use 동안. Use 까지 only when 'up to two hours' or a deadline is the intended meaning."}},{title:"N이/가 지연되다 / V-는 중이다 — transit delays and transfers",func:"Transit announcements describe current status before giving the next action. 지연되다 means 'be delayed,' 운행이 중단되다 means service has stopped, and 운행 중이다 means service is operating. Transfer instructions use N으로 갈아타세요 and exit instructions use N번 출구로 나가세요.",formTable:[{when:"A train or bus is delayed",add:"N이/가 지연되고 있습니다",ex:"2호선 열차가 지연되고 있습니다"},{when:"An operation is currently in progress",add:"N 중입니다 / V-는 중입니다",ex:"운행 중입니다 / 출발 준비 중입니다"},{when:"Changing to another line",add:"line + (으)로 갈아타세요",ex:"다음 역에서 3호선으로 갈아타세요"},{when:"Leaving through an exit",add:"number + 번 출구로 나가세요",ex:"5번 출구로 나가세요"}],examples:[{ko:"현재 2호선 열차가 지연되고 있습니다.",romanization:"hyeonjae ihoseon yeolchaga jiyeondoego itseumnida.",en:"Line 2 trains are currently delayed.",note:"현재 marks the current status; 지연되고 있습니다 is a formal announcement style."},{ko:"다음 역에서 3호선으로 갈아타세요.",romanization:"daeum yeogeseo samhoseoneuro garataseyo.",en:"Transfer to Line 3 at the next station.",note:"에서 marks the transfer location; 으로 marks the new line."},{ko:"사고로 인해 일부 구간의 운행이 중단됐습니다.",romanization:"sagoro inhae ilbu guganeui unhaengi jungdandwaetseumnida.",en:"Service has been suspended on part of the route due to an accident.",note:"일부 구간 means only part of the route, not the entire system."},{ko:"버스 운행은 곧 재개될 예정입니다.",romanization:"beoseu unhaengeun got jaegaedoel yejeongimnida.",en:"Bus service is expected to resume shortly.",note:"재개되다 means resume; -(으)ㄹ 예정입니다 reports the announced plan."}],keyPoint:{label:"Status first, action second",body:"현재 열차가 지연되고 있습니다 only reports status. 다른 교통편을 이용해 주세요 adds the action expected from passengers. Do not assume every delay announcement means service has stopped; distinguish 지연, 중단, and 재개."},pronunciation:"지연 is [지연]. 갈아타세요 is pronounced [가라타세요] as the final ㄹ of 갈 links to 아. 중단됐습니다 is commonly heard as [중단됟씀니다].",drill:{instruction:"Classify each announcement as delay, suspension, resumption, or transfer.",model:"열차가 지연되고 있습니다. → Delay.",items:["운행이 중단됐습니다. → Which status?","곧 재개될 예정입니다. → Which status?","3호선으로 갈아타세요. → What action is required?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"지연되다 and 중단되다 both mean the service has completely stopped.",right:"지연되다 = be delayed / 중단되다 = be suspended or stopped",explanation:"A delayed train may still arrive and operate. 중단 signals that service is not operating on the stated section. This distinction changes whether you wait or find another route."}}],j=[{title:"Chat abbreviations: ㅋㅋ, ㅠㅠ, ㅇㅇ, ㄹㅇ, ㅇㅈ",func:"These are consonant-only shorthand 'reaction' tokens Koreans type in chat (KakaoTalk, comments, DMs). They're not words you pronounce out loud; they're typed signals, almost like emoji. ㅋㅋ shows laughter, ㅠㅠ shows crying/sadness, and ㅇㅇ / ㄹㅇ / ㅇㅈ are clipped one-shot replies (yeah / for real / agreed). They make a message feel casual and friendly, which is exactly why you use them with friends and peers, never with a boss, teacher, or stranger.",formTable:[{when:"Something is funny; softening a message; showing you're amused",add:"ㅋㅋ (more ㅋ = harder laugh: ㅋㅋㅋㅋ)",ex:"아 진짜 웃겨 ㅋㅋㅋ (lol that's so funny)"},{when:"Crying, sad, disappointed, or begging cutely",add:"ㅠㅠ (also ㅜㅜ); pure emotion, no laugh",ex:"나 시험 망했어 ㅠㅠ (I bombed the test)"},{when:"A short 'yeah / yes / ok' reply between friends",add:"ㅇㅇ (= 응응, a casual yes)",ex:"ㅇㅇ 나도 갈게 (yeah, I'll go too)"},{when:"Stressing 'for real / seriously / no joke'",add:"ㄹㅇ (= 레알, from English 'real')",ex:"ㄹㅇ 그 카페 맛있어 (that cafe's legit good)"},{when:"Agreeing hard with what someone said",add:"ㅇㅈ (= 인정, 'I acknowledge it')",ex:"ㅇㅈ 그 영화 별로였음 (agreed, that movie was meh)"}],examples:[{ko:"ㅋㅋㅋ 너 진짜 웃긴다",romanization:"kkk neo jinjja utginda",en:"lololol you're so funny",note:"Repeating ㅋ = a bigger laugh; a single ㅋ can read as cold or sarcastic."},{ko:"비 와서 약속 취소됐어 ㅠㅠ",romanization:"bi waseo yaksok chwiso-dwaesseo",en:"It rained so the plan got cancelled",note:"ㅠㅠ carries the disappointment, so you don't need extra sad words."},{ko:"A: 내일 시간 돼? / B: ㅇㅇ",romanization:"naeil sigan dwae? / eung-eung",en:"A: free tomorrow? / B: yep",note:"ㅇㅇ is a full reply on its own; typing 네 here would feel oddly stiff with a close friend."},{ko:"ㄹㅇ ㅇㅈ 그 식당 줄 너무 길어",romanization:"ral injeong geu sikdang jul neomu gireo",en:"for real, agreed, that restaurant's line is way too long",note:"ㄹㅇ and ㅇㅈ often stack at the start of a message to pile on agreement."}],keyPoint:{label:"Typed sound vs spoken word",body:"ㅋㅋ and ㅠㅠ are typed, not spoken — you don't say 'kuh-kuh' out loud. ㅇㅇ / ㄹㅇ / ㅇㅈ are clipped spellings of 응응 / 레알 / 인정, so reading them aloud means saying the full word, not naming the letters."},drill:{instruction:"Pick the abbreviation that fits the reaction in brackets.",model:"[laughing] 사진 봤어? ___ → 사진 봤어? ㅋㅋㅋ",items:["[crying] 또 야근이야 ___","[casual yes] 우리 7시에 만나자 / ___","[strong agreement] 이 노래 진짜 좋다 / ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"ㅋㅋ 알겠습니다, 부장님.",right:"네, 알겠습니다, 부장님.",explanation:"English speakers treat 'haha/lol' as harmless filler, but dropping ㅋㅋ or ㅇㅇ into a message to a superior reads as flippant and too casual. Keep these for friends and peers only."}},{title:"Colloquial intensifiers: 완전 / 진짜 / 너무 / 대박 / 레전드",func:"These are casual booster words meaning roughly 'so / totally / really / insanely (good)'. Speakers drop them in front of an adjective or verb, or use them alone as an exclamation, to crank up the feeling. 진짜 and 너무 are everyday-neutral; 완전 sounds breezy and young; 대박 and 레전드 are slangy hype words for something amazing or unforgettable. They live in speech, chat, and reviews, and they carry the emotion that flat, polite sentences leave out.",formTable:[{when:"'so / totally' before an adjective; very common in casual talk",add:"완전 + adjective",ex:"이거 완전 맛있어 (this is so good)"},{when:"'really / seriously'; the most neutral, works almost anywhere",add:"진짜 + adjective/verb",ex:"진짜 피곤해 (I'm really tired)"},{when:"'so (much)'; in casual speech often just means 'very', not 'excessively'",add:"너무 + adjective",ex:"너무 좋아 (I love it / it's so nice)"},{when:"'awesome / no way!'; exclamation for shocking-good (or shocking-bad) news",add:"대박 (standalone or 대박이다)",ex:"헐 대박 (omg no way!)"},{when:"'legendary / epic'; calling something unforgettably great (or hilariously bad)",add:"레전드 (standalone or 레전드 + 이다)",ex:"어제 공연 레전드였어 (last night's show was legendary)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 카페 분위기 완전 좋아",romanization:"i kape bunwigi wanjeon joa",en:"This cafe's vibe is totally great",note:"완전 sounds young and upbeat; in formal writing you'd use 아주/매우 instead."},{ko:"나 진짜 배고파 죽겠어",romanization:"na jinjja baegopa jukgesseo",en:"I'm seriously starving",note:"진짜 is the safest intensifier — natural in chat and in spoken sentences alike."},{ko:"헐 대박, 그거 진짜야?",romanization:"heol daebak, geugeo jinjjaya?",en:"Whoa, no way — is that for real?",note:"대박 works as a one-word reaction to surprising news, good or bad."},{ko:"어제 경기 봤어? 완전 레전드였어 ㅋㅋ",romanization:"eoje gyeonggi bwasseo? wanjeon rejeondeu-yeosseo",en:"Did you watch the game? It was an absolute legend lol",note:"레전드 + 이다 turns the noun into 'was epic'; 완전 stacks on top for extra hype."}],keyPoint:{label:"너무: textbook 'too much' vs real-life 'very'",body:"Textbooks teach 너무 = 'too (excessively)', but in everyday speech 너무 좋아 just means 'I love it', not 'I like it too much'. Context, not the word itself, decides whether it's negative."},pronunciation:"대박 is often stretched as 대애박~ for emphasis, and 헐 frequently leads into it (헐 대박). 완전 is written 완전 but in fast speech can sound like a quick 완젼.",drill:{instruction:"Fill each blank with a natural intensifier (more than one answer can work).",model:"이 떡볶이 ___ 매워 → 이 떡볶이 진짜 매워",items:["와 ___! 너 1등이야?","어제 콘서트 ___였어, 잊지 못할 거야","오늘 날씨 ___ 추워서 안 나갈래"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"그 영화 매우 대박이었습니다.",right:"그 영화 완전 대박이었어. / 그 영화 정말 좋았습니다.",explanation:"Don't mix slang (대박, 완전) with formal endings (-습니다) or stiff adverbs (매우). Slang intensifiers belong with casual endings; when you need to sound polite or formal, switch to 정말/아주/매우 and drop the slang."}},{title:"Platform verbs: 댓글 달다, 좋아요 누르다, 공유하다, 구독하다",func:"Every social-media action in Korean has a fixed noun + verb pairing you have to memorize rather than translate from English. You 'hang' a comment (댓글을 달다), 'press' a like (좋아요를 누르다), 'share' (공유하다), and 'subscribe' (구독하다). The object particle 을/를 is often dropped in casual chat (댓글 달았어), but the verb pairing is fixed — pick the wrong verb and it sounds off even when every word is technically correct.",formTable:[{when:"Leaving / writing a comment on a post or video",add:"댓글(을) 달다 (lit. 'hang a comment')",ex:"영상에 댓글 달았어 (I left a comment on the video)"},{when:"Tapping the like button / heart",add:"좋아요(를) 누르다 (lit. 'press the like')",ex:"사진에 좋아요 눌러 줘 (hit like on the photo)"},{when:"Forwarding / reposting content to others",add:"공유하다 (share)",ex:"이 글 친구한테 공유했어 (I shared this post with a friend)"},{when:"Following a channel/creator to get new uploads",add:"구독하다 (subscribe)",ex:"그 채널 구독했어 (I subscribed to that channel)"}],examples:[{ko:"구독하고 좋아요 눌러 주세요!",romanization:"gudok-hago joayo nulleo juseyo!",en:"Please subscribe and hit like!",note:"The set phrase every Korean YouTuber says; note 좋아요 pairs with 누르다, never 하다."},{ko:"내 사진에 누가 댓글 달았어",romanization:"nae sajin-e nuga daetgeul darasseo",en:"Someone left a comment on my photo",note:"달다 conjugates to 달았어 (darasseo); the 을 is dropped, which is normal in casual speech."},{ko:"이 영상 진짜 웃겨, 단톡방에 공유할게",romanization:"i yeongsang jinjja utgyeo, dantokbang-e gongyu-halge",en:"This video is hilarious, I'll share it to the group chat",note:"공유하다 takes 에 for the destination (group chat) and 을/를 for the thing shared."},{ko:"좋아요 천 개 넘었어 대박",romanization:"joayo cheon gae neomeosseo daebak",en:"It passed a thousand likes, awesome",note:"Here 좋아요 is the noun 'like(s)' being counted with 개, not the verb action."}],keyPoint:{label:"좋아요 누르다, not 좋아요 하다",body:"English 'like' is one word, so learners reach for 좋아요 하다 — but Korean fixes each action to its own verb: a like is pressed (누르다), a comment is hung (달다). Memorize the pair instead of translating the verb."},drill:{instruction:"Complete each sentence with the right verb in the right form.",model:"그 채널 좋아서 ___ → 그 채널 좋아서 구독했어",items:["내 글이 웃겼나 봐, 사람들이 댓글 많이 ___","이 영상 좋으면 좋아요 ___ 주세요","친구가 보낸 영상을 단톡방에 ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"사진을 좋아요 했어요. / 댓글을 썼어요.",right:"사진에 좋아요 눌렀어요. / 댓글을 달았어요.",explanation:"Two traps: (1) 좋아요 takes 누르다, not 하다; (2) for posting a comment Koreans say 댓글을 달다, not the literal 댓글을 쓰다. 쓰다 is fine for 'writing' the text itself, but 달다 is the platform action of posting it."}},{title:"Register: ㅇㅇ vs 네 / 알겠어요",func:"The same meaning ('yes / got it / agreed') wears very different clothes depending on who you're talking to. ㅇㅇ, 응, ㅇㅈ, and 반말 belong to text chat with close friends and peers. 네 / 알겠어요 / 알겠습니다 are what you say or type to anyone you respect — a teacher, a customer, an older person, a boss. Picking the wrong register isn't a grammar mistake; it's a social one. So before you reply, read the relationship: is this someone you'd speak 반말 to, or someone you owe 존댓말?",formTable:[{when:"Close friend / peer, in chat — quick 'yeah'",add:"ㅇㅇ / 응",ex:"ㅇㅇ 거기서 보자 (yeah, see you there)"},{when:"Close friend / peer — strong 'agreed, true'",add:"ㅇㅈ / 인정",ex:"ㅇㅈ 그건 좀 너무했어 (agreed, that was a bit much)"},{when:"Anyone you respect — polite 'yes'",add:"네 (not ㅇㅇ)",ex:"네, 알겠어요 (yes, got it)"},{when:"Boss / teacher / customer — polite 'understood'",add:"알겠어요 / 알겠습니다",ex:"네, 알겠습니다 (yes, understood)"}],examples:[{ko:"A: 내일 회의 2시야 / B: ㅇㅇ 알았어",romanization:"naeil hoeui du-siya / eung-eung arasseo",en:"A: tomorrow's meeting is at 2 / B: yep, got it",note:"Friend-to-friend, so ㅇㅇ + 반말 (알았어) is natural and warm."},{ko:"네, 알겠습니다. 바로 처리할게요",romanization:"ne, algetseumnida. baro cheori-halgeyo",en:"Yes, understood. I'll take care of it right away",note:"To a boss or client; 네 + 알겠습니다 is the safe default at work."},{ko:"선생님, 네 알겠어요",romanization:"seonsaengnim, ne algesseoyo",en:"Yes, I understand, teacher",note:"Even with a teacher you're close to, keep 네/알겠어요 — never ㅇㅇ."},{ko:"엄마가 ㅇㅇ라고 보냈길래 좀 웃었어",romanization:"eommaga eung-eung-rago bonaetgillae jom useosseo",en:"Mom texted back 'ㅇㅇ' so I laughed a little",note:"Shows ㅇㅇ as the casual marker — funny precisely because you don't expect it from a parent."}],keyPoint:{label:"Register is a relationship choice, not a vocab choice",body:"ㅇㅇ and 네 mean the same thing; what differs is who you're allowed to say it to. When unsure, default to 네/알겠어요 — being slightly too polite is invisible, but being too casual is rude."},drill:{instruction:"Choose ㅇㅇ or 네/알겠어요 for each reply based on who's speaking.",model:"(친구에게) 우리 카페 갈까? → ㅇㅇ 가자",items:["(부장님께) 이거 오늘까지 부탁해요 → ___","(친한 친구에게) 나 5분 늦어 → ___","(손님께) 영수증 드릴까요? → ___"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"(부장님께) ㅇㅇ 알겠어요.",right:"네, 알겠습니다.",explanation:"Learners who finally feel comfortable with chat slang start sprinkling ㅇㅇ everywhere, including replies to a boss or teacher. ㅇㅇ is 반말-level casual; to a superior it reads as curt and disrespectful. Match the register to the person: 존댓말 gets 네, not ㅇㅇ."}}],I=[{title:"아직 있나요? / 거래 가능할까요? — checking a secondhand listing",func:"A secondhand-market chat usually begins by checking whether the item is still available, then asks about time, place, price, or condition. 아직 있나요? is direct and neutral. N 가능할까요? asks whether an arrangement is possible and sounds more considerate than a flat 가능해요? when contacting a stranger.",formTable:[{when:"Checking whether the item is still available",add:"아직 + 있나요? / 판매 중인가요?",ex:"이 책 아직 있나요?"},{when:"Checking whether an arrangement is possible",add:"noun/action + 가능할까요?",ex:"오늘 저녁에 직거래 가능할까요?"},{when:"Asking carefully about condition",add:"N은/는 어떤가요? / N이/가 있나요?",ex:"상태는 어떤가요? / 하자가 있나요?"},{when:"Asking whether price adjustment is possible",add:"가격 조정 + 가능할까요?",ex:"가격 조금 조정 가능할까요?"}],examples:[{ko:"안녕하세요. 이 의자 아직 있나요?",romanization:"annyeonghaseyo. i uija ajik innayo?",en:"Hello. Is this chair still available?",note:"A greeting plus 아직 있나요? is a safe first message to a stranger."},{ko:"오늘 저녁 7시에 직거래 가능할까요?",romanization:"oneul jeonyeok ilgopsie jikgeorae ganeunghalkkayo?",en:"Would an in-person exchange at 7 this evening be possible?",note:"The message proposes both time and method, so the seller can answer clearly."},{ko:"사용감은 있지만 큰 하자는 없어요.",romanization:"sayonggameun itjiman keun hajaneun eopseoyo.",en:"It shows signs of use, but there are no major defects.",note:"사용감 means visible wear from normal use; 하자 means a defect or problem."},{ko:"죄송하지만 지금은 예약 중이에요.",romanization:"joesonghajiman jigeumeun yeyak jungieyo.",en:"Sorry, but it is currently reserved.",note:"예약 중 means another buyer has arranged the transaction but it may not be completed yet."}],keyPoint:{label:"Availability, condition, and arrangement are separate questions",body:"아직 있나요? checks availability. 상태는 어떤가요? checks condition. 직거래 가능할까요? checks the arrangement. Asking one clear question at a time makes a short marketplace chat easier to understand and answer."},pronunciation:"있나요 is pronounced [인나요] because ㅆ before ㄴ becomes [ㄴ]. 가능할까요 has a tense ㄲ: [가능할까요].",drill:{instruction:"Identify what information the buyer is checking.",model:"아직 있나요? → Availability.",items:["하자가 있나요? → Which information?","오늘 직거래 가능할까요? → Which information?","가격 조정 가능할까요? → Which information?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"이거 있어요? 얼마?",right:"안녕하세요. 아직 판매 중인가요? 가격은 얼마인가요?",explanation:"The clipped version can sound abrupt when contacting a stranger. A short greeting and one complete availability question keep the message polite without making it formal or wordy."}},{title:"V-아/어 놓다 / V-아/어 두다 — parcel storage and pickup status",func:"Parcel messages often describe an action whose result remains: a package was placed in a locker, left at the door, or stored at a pickup point. V-아/어 놓다 highlights the completed result you can now see or use. V-아/어 두다 highlights leaving something prepared or stored for later. In many delivery messages they overlap, but the practical result is the same: the item is waiting at the stated location.",formTable:[{when:"A completed placement whose result remains",add:"verb stem + -아/어 놓다",ex:"찍다 → 사진을 찍어 놓다 / 넣다 → 택배함에 넣어 놓다"},{when:"Something is stored or prepared for later",add:"verb stem + -아/어 두다",ex:"보관하다 → 경비실에 보관해 두다"},{when:"Formal completed delivery update",add:"-아/어 놓았습니다 / -아/어 두었습니다",ex:"택배함에 넣어 두었습니다"},{when:"Current storage status",add:"N에 보관 중입니다",ex:"편의점에 보관 중입니다"}],examples:[{ko:"택배를 무인 택배함에 넣어 두었습니다.",romanization:"taekbaereul muin taekbaehame neoeo dueotseumnida.",en:"The parcel has been placed in the unmanned parcel locker.",note:"넣어 두었습니다 tells you the action is complete and the parcel remains there."},{ko:"부재중이라 경비실에 보관해 놓았습니다.",romanization:"bujaejungira gyeongbisire bogwanhae noasseumnida.",en:"Because you were away, it has been stored at the security office.",note:"부재중 gives the reason; 경비실 gives the pickup location."},{ko:"수령 가능 시간은 오후 2시부터 10시까지입니다.",romanization:"suryeong ganeung siganeun ohu du sibuteo yeol sikkajiimnida.",en:"Pickup is available from 2 p.m. to 10 p.m.",note:"수령 means receiving or picking up; 부터…까지 gives the full time window."},{ko:"주소 불명으로 배송하지 못했습니다.",romanization:"juso bulmyeongeuro baesonghaji motaetseumnida.",en:"Delivery could not be completed because the address was unclear.",note:"-지 못했습니다 reports a failed action; the noun before (으)로 gives the cause."}],keyPoint:{label:"Find the current location before worrying about the grammar label",body:"넣어 두었습니다, 보관해 놓았습니다, and 보관 중입니다 all tell you that the parcel is currently waiting somewhere. The location phrase marked by 에 is the most important information: 택배함에, 경비실에, 편의점에."},pronunciation:"In 놓았습니다, the ㅎ is not heard before the vowel-initial ending. 넣어 is [너어], and 수령 is pronounced [수령].",drill:{instruction:"Read the parcel message and identify location, status, or pickup time.",model:"경비실에 보관 중입니다. → Location: security office; status: stored there now.",items:["택배함에 넣어 두었습니다. → Where is it?","배송하지 못했습니다. → Was delivery completed?","오후 2시부터 수령 가능합니다. → When can pickup begin?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"보관 중입니다 means someone is putting the parcel away right now.",right:"보관 중입니다 usually means the parcel is currently being kept at that location.",explanation:"N 중 can describe an ongoing status, not only an action happening at this second. In a parcel notice, it usually tells you where the item remains available for pickup."}},{title:"혹시 / 좀 / 괜찮으면 — softening a request in chat",func:"Korean chat often softens a request before the main grammar appears. 혹시 signals that the speaker is checking a possibility, 좀 makes the request less blunt, and 괜찮으면 means 'if it is okay with you.' These words do not change the core action, but they show that the listener has room to say no.",formTable:[{when:"Checking a possibility carefully",add:"혹시 + question",ex:"혹시 오늘 시간 돼요?"},{when:"Making a request less blunt",add:"object/action + 좀 + request",ex:"사진 좀 보내 줄래요?"},{when:"Leaving the choice open",add:"괜찮으면 + suggestion/request",ex:"괜찮으면 같이 먹어요"},{when:"Combining softeners naturally",add:"혹시 + time/condition + 좀 + request",ex:"혹시 시간 되면 이것 좀 봐 줄래요?"}],examples:[{ko:"혹시 오늘 저녁에 시간 돼요?",romanization:"hoksi oneul jeonyeoge sigan dwaeyo?",en:"Do you happen to have time this evening?",note:"혹시 prepares the listener for a request without assuming availability."},{ko:"시간 되면 이 메시지 좀 봐 줄래요?",romanization:"sigan doemyeon i mesiji jom bwa jullaeyo?",en:"If you have time, could you take a look at this message?",note:"시간 되면 gives an exit; 좀 softens the requested action."},{ko:"괜찮으면 역 앞에서 만날까요?",romanization:"gwaenchanheumyeon yeok apeseo mannalkkayo?",en:"If that works for you, shall we meet in front of the station?",note:"괜찮으면 checks whether the proposed location suits the other person."},{ko:"혹시 택배 받으면 알려 줄 수 있어요?",romanization:"hoksi taekbae badeumyeon allyeo jul su isseoyo?",en:"Could you let me know if you receive the parcel?",note:"-아/어 줄 수 있어요? asks whether the listener can do the favor."}],keyPoint:{label:"Soften the assumption, not the important detail",body:"혹시 and 괜찮으면 leave room for refusal, while 좀 softens the size or force of the request. Keep the practical detail clear: when, where, and what action. A vague request is not automatically polite."},pronunciation:"괜찮으면 is pronounced [괜차느면]: ㅎ is not heard before the vowel-initial ending, and the final consonant links forward. 혹시 is [혹씨] with a tense ㅆ.",drill:{instruction:"Identify which word softens the possibility, the request, or the condition.",model:"혹시 오늘 시간 돼요? → 혹시 softens the possibility check.",items:["이것 좀 봐 줄래요? → Which word softens the request?","괜찮으면 내일 만나요. → What condition is offered?","혹시 시간 되면 연락 주세요. → Which two softening steps appear?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"혹시 means 'by any chance,' so it must appear in every polite request.",right:"Use 혹시 when you genuinely check a possibility; use 좀 or a polite ending when the possibility itself is not in question.",explanation:"혹시 is useful but not a universal politeness marker. Overusing it can make a simple message hesitant. Match the softener to what you are actually leaving open."}},{title:"V-기는 어려울 것 같아요 / V-기 힘들 것 같아요 — a soft refusal with an alternative",func:"Korean often turns down a plan by describing difficulty rather than saying a flat 싫어요 or 안 돼요. V-기는 어려울 것 같아요 means 'I don't think it will be easy/possible to V.' V-기 힘들 것 같아요 is slightly more conversational. A considerate refusal usually adds a short reason or a workable alternative.",formTable:[{when:"A careful, neutral refusal",add:"verb stem + -기는 어려울 것 같아요",ex:"만나다 → 오늘 만나기는 어려울 것 같아요"},{when:"A conversational difficulty statement",add:"verb stem + -기 힘들 것 같아요",ex:"가다 → 이번 주에는 가기 힘들 것 같아요"},{when:"Soft apology before refusal",add:"미안하지만 / 죄송하지만 + refusal",ex:"미안하지만 오늘은 못 갈 것 같아요"},{when:"Offering a practical alternative",add:"refusal + 대신 / 다음에 / N은 어때요?",ex:"오늘은 어렵고 내일 저녁은 어때요?"}],examples:[{ko:"미안하지만 오늘 만나기는 어려울 것 같아요.",romanization:"mianhajiman oneul mannagineun eoryeoul geot gatayo.",en:"I'm sorry, but I don't think I can meet today.",note:"-기는 highlights the proposed action; 어려울 것 같아요 softens the refusal."},{ko:"이번 주는 시간이 안 될 것 같아요. 다음 주는 어때요?",romanization:"ibeon juneun sigani an doel geot gatayo. daeum juneun eottaeyo?",en:"I don't think this week will work. How about next week?",note:"The alternative makes the answer useful instead of merely vague."},{ko:"그 가격에는 판매하기 어려울 것 같아요.",romanization:"geu gagyeogeneun panmaehagi eoryeoul geot gatayo.",en:"I don't think I can sell it at that price.",note:"A marketplace refusal can be polite and still state the non-negotiable point clearly."},{ko:"오늘은 못 가지만 주말에는 괜찮아요.",romanization:"oneureun mot gajiman jumareneun gwaenchanayo.",en:"I can't go today, but the weekend works for me.",note:"-지만 contrasts the unavailable time with a possible alternative."}],keyPoint:{label:"A soft refusal still needs a clear answer",body:"어려울 것 같아요 lowers the force, but the listener still needs to know whether the plan is possible. Add 오늘은, 그 가격에는, or 이번 주에는 to show exactly what is difficult. If you want the relationship to continue, add a real alternative rather than an empty 다음에 봐요."},pronunciation:"어려울 것 같아요 is commonly heard as [어려울 걷 까타요]. The final ㅅ of 것 becomes [ㄷ], and the following ㄱ becomes tense.",drill:{instruction:"Find the refusal and the alternative in each reply.",model:"오늘은 어렵고 내일은 어때요? → Refusal: today; alternative: tomorrow.",items:["이번 주에는 가기 힘들 것 같아요. 다음 주는 괜찮아요. → What is the alternative?","그 가격에는 판매하기 어려워요. → What condition is refused?","오늘은 못 가지만 주말에는 갈 수 있어요. → What remains possible?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"다음에 봐요 always promises a specific future meeting.",right:"다음 주 토요일은 어때요? gives a real alternative; 다음에 봐요 may simply close the conversation politely.",explanation:"A vague 다음에 can be sincere or can be a soft social closing. If you need an actual plan, look for or propose a concrete day, time, or place."}}],T=[{title:"V-이/히/리/기- — the built-in passive (it happened, nobody to blame)",func:"A set of transitive verbs takes an inner suffix -이-, -히-, -리-, or -기- and becomes intransitive. The thing that was acted on moves into the 이/가 subject slot, the object particle 을/를 disappears, and the doer usually drops out of the sentence completely. This is the passive Koreans actually use in daily speech — one word, not a construction.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel or ㅎ",add:"-이-",ex:"보다 → 보이다 (to be visible), 놓다 → 놓이다 (to be placed)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ",add:"-히-",ex:"막다 → 막히다 (to get blocked), 잡다 → 잡히다 (to get caught)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ (and ㄷ-irregular 듣다)",add:"-리-",ex:"팔다 → 팔리다 (to be sold), 듣다 → 들리다 (to be heard)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄴ, ㅁ, ㅅ, ㅊ",add:"-기-",ex:"끊다 → 끊기다 (to get cut off), 쫓다 → 쫓기다 (to be chased)"},{when:"Rewriting an active sentence",add:"N을/를 V → N이/가 V-피동",ex:"직원이 문을 닫았어요 → 문이 닫혔어요"}],examples:[{ko:"지금 출근 시간이라 도로가 꽉 막혔어요.",romanization:"jigeum chulgeun siganira doroga kkwak makyeosseoyo.",en:"It's rush hour, so the road is completely jammed right now.",note:"막다 (to block) → 막히다. Nobody blocked the road on purpose, and 막히다 is simply the word Koreans use for traffic."},{ko:"지하철에서 통화하다가 갑자기 전화가 끊겼어요.",romanization:"jihacheoreseo tonghwahadaga gapjagi jeonhwaga kkeunkyeosseoyo.",en:"I was on a call on the subway and it suddenly got cut off.",note:"끊다 (to cut) → 끊기다. The phone is the subject; no one is accused of hanging up."},{ko:"위층에서 발소리가 계속 들려요.",romanization:"wicheungeseo balsoriga gyesok deullyeoyo.",en:"I keep hearing footsteps from upstairs.",note:"듣다 is a ㄷ-irregular verb, so the passive is 들리다, never 듣히다."},{ko:"표가 예매 시작 십 분 만에 다 팔렸어요.",romanization:"pyoga yemae sijak sip bun mane da pallyeosseoyo.",en:"The tickets were all gone ten minutes after booking opened.",note:'English says "sold out" with no subject; Korean makes the tickets the subject — 표가 팔렸어요.'}],keyPoint:{label:"The object particle has to go",body:"When a verb turns passive, 을/를 disappears with it. 문을 닫았어요 (someone shut the door) becomes 문이 닫혔어요 — never 문을 닫혔어요. If you still see 을/를 in front of a passive verb, the sentence is broken. The one regular exception is a body part or a possession you were affected through: 지하철에서 발을 밟혔어요 (I got my foot stepped on)."},pronunciation:"Three of these hide a sound change. 막히다 is [마키다] and 잡히다 is [자피다] — the ㅎ fuses with the stop into an aspirate. 끊기다 is [끈키다] for the same reason. 밟히다 is [발피다].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each active sentence into a passive one: drop the doer and re-mark the subject.",model:"직원이 창문을 열었어요. → 창문이 열렸어요.",items:["직원이 가게 문을 닫았어요. → 가게 문이 _______.","사고가 길을 막았어요. → 길이 _______.","가게에서 그 책을 다 팔았어요. → 그 책이 다 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"문을 닫혔어요.",right:"문이 닫혔어요.",explanation:'In English "the door was closed," the door still feels like the thing being acted on, so learners keep 을/를 on it. A Korean passive verb cannot take an object at all — the affected thing becomes the 이/가 subject. The moment you swap 닫다 for 닫히다, swap 문을 for 문이.'}},{title:'N에/에게 + 피동 — naming what caused it ("by")',func:"Korean usually leaves the doer out of a passive sentence entirely. When you do name it, the particle depends on what it is: a person or an animal takes 에게, or 한테 in speech, while a thing, a force, or an event takes plain 에. Formal writing has a third option, N에 의해(서), but in conversation it sounds like you are reading a news bulletin.",formTable:[{when:"A person or animal caused it",add:"N에게 / N한테 (spoken)",ex:"경찰 → 도둑이 경찰한테 잡혔어요"},{when:"A thing, force, or event caused it",add:"N에",ex:"바람 → 창문이 바람에 닫혔어요"},{when:"Formal writing or news",add:"N에 의해(서)",ex:"국회 → 이 법은 국회에 의해 개정되었다"},{when:"No cause named — the normal case",add:"(just leave it out)",ex:"문이 닫혔어요 (the door shut)"}],examples:[{ko:"도둑이 경찰한테 바로 잡혔어요.",romanization:"dodugi gyeongchalhante baro japyeosseoyo.",en:"The thief was caught by the police right away.",note:"A person did it, so 한테 in speech (에게 if you are writing)."},{ko:"창문이 바람에 쾅 닫혔어요.",romanization:"changmuni barame kwang dachyeosseoyo.",en:"The window slammed shut in the wind.",note:"바람 cannot decide anything, so it takes 에 — 바람에게 is the classic learner error."},{ko:"아기가 아빠한테 안겨서 울음을 그쳤어요.",romanization:"agiga appahante angyeoseo ureumeul geuchyeosseoyo.",en:"The baby stopped crying once it was picked up by its dad.",note:"안다 (to hold) → 안기다. 아빠 is a person, so 한테 again."},{ko:"요즘은 마감에 쫓겨서 점심도 제대로 못 먹어요.",romanization:"yojeumeun magame jjotgyeoseo jeomsimdo jedaero mon meogeoyo.",en:"These days I'm so pressed by deadlines I can't even eat a proper lunch.",note:"시간에 쫓기다 and 마감에 쫓기다 are set phrases. A deadline is not alive, so 에."}],keyPoint:{label:"에게 is only for something alive",body:'The single most common passive error is copying English "by" onto 에게/한테 for everything. 에게 and 한테 attach only to a person or an animal — something that could have acted on purpose. Wind, rain, noise, traffic, time, a deadline, an accident: all take 에. 바람에 닫혔어요, 마감에 쫓겨요, 소음에 시달려요.'},pronunciation:"에게 and 한테 mean the same thing; 한테 is what you say out loud and 에게 is what you write. 잡혔어요 is [자펴써요] and 쫓겨서 is [쫃껴서].",drill:{instruction:"Add the right particle to the cause: 에게 or 한테 for a person or animal, 에 for a thing or a force.",model:"고양이 / 쥐가 잡히다 → 쥐가 고양이한테 잡혔어요.",items:["경찰 / 도둑이 잡히다 → 도둑이 경찰____ 잡혔어요.","바람 / 문이 닫히다 → 문이 바람____ 닫혔어요.","시간 / 요즘 쫓기다 → 요즘 시간____ 쫓겨요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"창문이 바람에게 닫혔어요.",right:"창문이 바람에 닫혔어요.",explanation:'English has one word, "by," for people and for weather alike, so learners map it straight onto 에게/한테. Korean splits them, and 에게/한테 need something animate. 바람, 비, 소음, 시간, 사고 all take plain 에. If you cannot picture it deciding to act, use 에.'}},{title:"V-아/어지다 — it gets done, it turns out that way",func:'The -이/히/리/기- passive exists only for a closed list of verbs. -아/어지다 covers almost everything else: it takes a transitive verb and makes the action something that simply happens to the thing. 지우다 (to erase) → 지워지다 (to get erased). It carries a shade of "it came out this way on its own," which is why Korean uses it for schedules, decisions, and settings.',formTable:[{when:"Verb stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-아지다",ex:"닦다 → 닦아지다 (to come clean)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in any other vowel",add:"-어지다",ex:"지우다 → 지워지다 (to get erased)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"-해지다",ex:"정하다 → 정해지다 (to be decided)"},{when:'On an adjective the meaning changes to "become"',add:"A-아/어지다",ex:"따뜻하다 → 따뜻해지다 (to get warm)"}],examples:[{ko:"칠판에 쓴 글씨가 잘 안 지워져요.",romanization:"chilpane sseun geulssiga jal an jiwojyeoyo.",en:"The writing on the board won't come off properly.",note:"지우다 has no -이/히/리/기- passive, so it uses 지워지다."},{ko:"회식 날짜가 다음 주 금요일로 정해졌어요.",romanization:"hoesik naljjaga daeum ju geumyoillo jeonghaejyeosseoyo.",en:"The date for the team dinner has been set for next Friday.",note:"Office Korean at its most typical: the date got decided, and nobody is named as deciding it."},{ko:"이 빵은 국산 밀가루로만 만들어져요.",romanization:"i ppangeun guksan milgaruroman mandeureojyeoyo.",en:"This bread is made only with domestic flour.",note:"만들다 → 만들어지다 for products, recipes, and materials."},{ko:"휴대폰 화면이 갑자기 꺼졌다가 다시 켜졌어요.",romanization:"hyudaepon hwamyeoni gapjagi kkeojyeotdaga dasi kyeojyeosseoyo.",en:"My phone screen suddenly went dark and then came back on.",note:"끄다 → 꺼지다 and 켜다 → 켜지다: the device does it, not you."}],keyPoint:{label:"-아/어지다 does two different jobs",body:'On a transitive verb it builds a passive: 지우다 (erase) → 지워지다 (get erased). On an adjective it means "become": 따뜻하다 (be warm) → 따뜻해지다 (get warm). That second job is the 좋아졌어요 / 추워졌어요 you already know. Look at the base word, not the ending, to know which reading you have.'},pronunciation:"The 져 in these forms flattens after ㅈ: 지워져요 is [지워저요], 만들어져요 is [만드러저요], 정해졌어요 is [정해저써요]. Never pronounce a full [여] there.",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each active sentence with -아/어지다 so the thing itself becomes the subject.",model:"제가 날짜를 정했어요. → 날짜가 정해졌어요.",items:["제가 글씨를 지웠어요. → 글씨가 _______.","제가 불을 켰어요. → 불이 _______.","공장에서 이 빵을 만들어요. → 이 빵이 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"문이 안 열려져서 한참 서 있었어요.",right:"문이 안 열려서 한참 서 있었어요.",explanation:'열리다 is already the passive of 열다, so -어지다 stacks a second passive on top of it. English "was opened" feels like it needs a helper verb, which is what tempts learners into this. Pick one route: either the -이/히/리/기- verb (열리다, 닫히다, 보이다) or -아/어지다 on the plain verb — never both. The same trap produces 잊혀지다 and 쓰여지다, which careful speakers avoid.'}},{title:"V-게 되다 — how it ended up (circumstances took the decision)",func:'-게 되다 does not move an object into the subject slot. It changes WHY. The same person still performs the action, but circumstances, not their own choice, brought it about. English reaches for "ended up," "came to," or "it turns out that." It is also the polite way to announce your own news without sounding boastful or blaming.',formTable:[{when:"Any verb stem",add:"V-게 되다",ex:"알다 → 알게 되다 (to come to know)"},{when:"Past — by far the most common form",add:"V-게 됐어요",ex:"가다 → 가게 됐어요 (I ended up going)"},{when:"Announcing news politely",add:"V-게 됐습니다",ex:"일하다 → 부산에서 일하게 됐습니다"},{when:"Ended up NOT doing it",add:"못 V-게 되다 / V-지 않게 되다",ex:"가다 → 못 가게 됐어요 (it turns out I can't go)"}],examples:[{ko:"일이 갑자기 생겨서 이번 모임에는 못 가게 됐어요.",romanization:"iri gapjagi saenggyeoseo ibeon moimeneun mot gage dwaesseoyo.",en:"Something came up, so it turns out I can't make this meetup.",note:"The standard polite way to cancel: circumstances, not you, cancelled it."},{ko:"한국 드라마를 보다가 한국어를 배우게 됐어요.",romanization:"hanguk deuramareul bodaga hangugeoreul baeuge dwaesseoyo.",en:"I got into watching Korean dramas and ended up learning Korean.",note:"You are still the one learning — 게 되다 only says it wasn't a plan."},{ko:"다음 달부터 부산에서 일하게 됐습니다.",romanization:"daeum dalbuteo busaneseo ilhage dwaetseumnida.",en:"As it turns out, I'll be working in Busan from next month.",note:"Announcement register. 일합니다 would sound like you arranged it yourself."},{ko:"매일 같은 시간에 마주치다 보니 인사를 하게 됐어요.",romanization:"maeil gateun sigane majuchida boni insareul hage dwaesseoyo.",en:"We kept running into each other at the same time every day, so we ended up saying hello.",note:"A gradual, unplanned change — one of 게 되다's favourite jobs."}],keyPoint:{label:"피동 vs 사동 vs -게 되다",body:"Three jobs that blur together. 피동 (문이 닫혔어요) removes the doer — the thing is simply affected. 사동, the causative chapter's 아이에게 옷을 입혔어요, adds a doer — you make someone else act. -게 되다 keeps the same doer but hands the decision to circumstances (부산에서 일하게 됐어요 — I am the one working, but I did not pick it). Ask yourself: did the doer disappear, get added, or just stop being in charge?"},pronunciation:"게 되다 is spoken as one chunk with no pause: 하게 됐어요 is [하게 돼써요], 못 가게 됐어요 is [몯 까게 돼써요].",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each plain statement so that circumstances, not your decision, brought it about.",model:"부산에서 일해요. → 부산에서 일하게 됐어요.",items:["이번 주 모임에 못 가요. → 이번 주 모임에 못 _______.","한국어를 배워요. → 한국어를 _______.","그 사실을 알아요. → 그 사실을 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"문이 닫게 됐어요.",right:"문이 닫혔어요.",explanation:`Because 되다 means "become," learners treat -게 되다 as a general-purpose passive. It is not. -게 되다 keeps the doer and only says the decision wasn't theirs, so it needs a subject that could in principle decide. A door decides nothing — it needs the real passive 닫히다.`}},{title:"보이다·읽히다 — one form, passive or causative",func:'Several -이/히/리/기- verbs are the passive AND the causative of the same root, and only the particles tell them apart. 보이다 is "be visible" and "show"; 읽히다 is "be read" and "make someone read"; 안기다 is "be held" and "hand over to be held." Read the particles: a 이/가 subject with no object is passive, while a 을/를 object is causative.',formTable:[{when:"보다 → 보이다, passive",add:"N이/가 보이다",ex:"바다 → 여기서 바다가 보여요 (the sea is visible from here)"},{when:"보다 → 보이다, causative",add:"N에게 N을/를 보이다",ex:"손 → 의사에게 손을 보였어요 (I showed the doctor my hand)"},{when:"읽다 → 읽히다, passive",add:"N이/가 읽히다",ex:"소설 → 그 소설이 많이 읽혀요 (that novel gets read a lot)"},{when:"읽다 → 읽히다, causative",add:"N에게 N을/를 읽히다",ex:"동화책 → 아이에게 동화책을 읽혔어요 (I had my child read a storybook)"}],examples:[{ko:"옥상에 올라가면 한강이 다 보여요.",romanization:"oksange ollagamyeon hangangi da boyeoyo.",en:"If you go up to the roof, you can see the whole Han River.",note:"Passive: 한강이 takes 이/가 and there is no object anywhere."},{ko:"병원에서 의사 선생님한테 다친 손을 보였어요.",romanization:"byeongwoneseo uisa seonsaengnimhante dachin soneul boyeosseoyo.",en:"At the hospital I showed the doctor my injured hand.",note:'Causative: 손을 is an object, so this is "show," not "be visible."'},{ko:"그 소설은 요즘도 학생들에게 널리 읽혀요.",romanization:"geu soseoreun yojeumdo haksaengdeurege neolli ilkyeoyo.",en:"That novel is still widely read by students today.",note:"Passive: 학생들에게 is the reader, and the novel is the subject."},{ko:"엄마가 자기 전에 아이에게 동화책을 읽혔어요.",romanization:"eommaga jagi jeone aiege donghwachaegeul ilkyeosseoyo.",en:"Mom had her child read a storybook before bed.",note:"Causative: 동화책을 is an object, so 엄마 makes the child do the reading."}],keyPoint:{label:"The object particle decides",body:'When one form does both jobs, look for 을/를. 사진이 잘 보여요 has no object → passive, "the photo shows up clearly." 사진을 보여 줬어요 has an object → causative, "I showed the photo." 에게/한테 turns up in both, so it can never settle the question; only 을/를 can. And for "show," everyday Korean almost always adds 주다: 보여 주다, not bare 보이다.'},pronunciation:"읽히다 is [일키다]: the ㄺ and the ㅎ fuse into [ㅋ]. So 읽혀요 is [일켜요] and 읽혔어요 is [일켜써요].",drill:{instruction:"Decide whether each sentence is passive or causative, then fill in the missing particle.",model:"여기서 산____ 잘 보여요. → 산이 (passive: nothing is being shown to anyone)",items:["옥상에서 한강____ 다 보여요. → passive or causative?","의사 선생님한테 손____ 보였어요. → passive or causative?","아이에게 동화책____ 읽혔어요. → passive or causative?"]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구에게 사진이 보였어요.",right:"친구에게 사진을 보여 줬어요.",explanation:'Learners learn 보이다 as "be visible," then discover it can also mean "show," and build the "show" sentence with 이/가 out of habit. If YOU are doing the showing, the photo is an object (사진을) and natural Korean adds 주다. Keep 사진이 보여요 for "the photo is visible."'}}],z=[{title:'V/A-대요 — the contracted "they say…"',func:"In real speech the full -다고 해요 squeezes down to -대요. What comes BEFORE the ending does not change at all: a present-tense verb still needs its -ㄴ/는-, an adjective still attaches bare, and any past tense still uses -았/었-. Only the tail is shorter.",formTable:[{when:"Verb, present, stem with no 받침",add:"-ㄴ대요",ex:"가다 → 간대요 (they say he's going)"},{when:"Verb, present, stem with 받침",add:"-는대요",ex:"먹다 → 먹는대요 (they say she eats it)"},{when:"Adjective, present",add:"-대요",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘대요 (they say he's busy)"},{when:"Any past tense",add:"-았/었대요",ex:"보다 → 봤대요 (they say she saw it)"},{when:"Future or plan",add:"-(으)ㄹ 거래요",ex:"가다 → 갈 거래요 (they say he'll go)"}],examples:[{ko:"지훈이 오늘 회식에 못 온대요.",romanization:"jihuni oneul hoesige mot ondaeyo.",en:"Jihoon says he can't make the team dinner today.",note:"오다 has no 받침, so it takes -ㄴ대요 → 온대요."},{ko:"요즘 그 카페는 사람이 진짜 많대요.",romanization:"yojeum geu kapeneun sarami jinjja mantaeyo.",en:"They say that café is really packed these days.",note:"많다 is an adjective, so -대요 attaches bare — no -ㄴ/는-."},{ko:"동생이 어제 시험을 잘 봤대요.",romanization:"dongsaengi eoje siheomeul jal bwatdaeyo.",en:"My younger sibling says he did well on yesterday's exam.",note:"Past tense keeps 았/었 and simply adds -대요."},{ko:"민서는 다음 달에 부산으로 이사 갈 거래요.",romanization:"minseoneun daeum dare busaneuro isa gal georaeyo.",en:"Minseo says she's moving to Busan next month.",note:"A plan reported second-hand: -(으)ㄹ 거라고 해요 → -(으)ㄹ 거래요."}],keyPoint:{label:"-대요 vs -데요 — one letter, a completely different source",body:`-대요 passes on somebody else's words: 그 집 맛있대요 means "they say it's good." -데요 reports what YOU saw or experienced yourself and is short for -더라고요: 그 집 맛있데요 means "I tried it, and it was good." Korean adults mix these two up in writing constantly, so lock it in now: 대 = 다른 사람 말, 데 = 내가 본 것.`},pronunciation:"After a ㅎ-final adjective the two sounds fuse: 많대요 is [만태요] and 좋대요 is [조태요]. 봤대요 tenses to [봗때요].",drill:{instruction:"Contract each full -다고 해요 sentence into its spoken form.",model:"친구가 바쁘다고 해요. → 친구가 바쁘대요.",items:["지훈이가 못 온다고 해요. → 지훈이가 못 _______.","그 카페에 사람이 많다고 해요. → 그 카페에 사람이 _______.","동생이 시험을 잘 봤다고 해요. → 동생이 시험을 잘 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구가 매일 운동하대요.",right:"친구가 매일 운동한대요.",explanation:`With a present-tense VERB, -대요 keeps the -ㄴ/는- that -다고 하다 always carried: 운동한대요, 먹는대요. Only ADJECTIVES attach bare, as in 바쁘대요. English speakers drop the -ㄴ/는- because English draws no line between "he exercises" and "it's busy."`}},{title:"N(이)래요 vs V-(으)래요 — one shape, two jobs",func:'래요 is the contraction of two different things. After a NOUN it comes from -(이)라고 해요 and means "they say it IS N." After a VERB STEM it comes from -(으)라고 해요 and reports a command: "they told (me) to V." Nothing but the word in front of it tells them apart, so Korean listeners rely entirely on that.',formTable:[{when:"Noun with no 받침",add:"N래요",ex:"의사 → 저분이 의사래요 (they say she's a doctor)"},{when:"Noun with 받침",add:"N이래요",ex:"학생 → 옆집 사람이 학생이래요"},{when:"Verb stem with no 받침 — reported command",add:"V-래요",ex:"가다 → 지금 가래요 (they're telling me to go now)"},{when:"Verb stem with 받침 — reported command",add:"V-으래요",ex:"먹다 → 약을 먹으래요 (they say to take the medicine)"},{when:"Negative command",add:"V-지 말래요",ex:"가다 → 오늘은 가지 말래요 (they say not to go today)"}],examples:[{ko:"저분이 이 식당 사장님이래요.",romanization:"jeobuni i sikdang sajangnimiraeyo.",en:"They say that person is the owner of this restaurant.",note:"A noun with 받침, so 이래요 — never 사장님대요."},{ko:"의사 선생님이 이 약을 하루에 두 번 먹으래요.",romanization:"uisa seonsaengnimi i yageul harue du beon meogeuraeyo.",en:"The doctor says to take this medicine twice a day.",note:"먹다 has a 받침, so the reported command is 먹으래요."},{ko:"엄마가 늦으면 꼭 연락하래요.",romanization:"eommaga neujeumyeon kkok yeollakaraeyo.",en:"Mom says to be sure to call if I'm going to be late.",note:"하다 verbs simply become 하래요; the person giving the order is the subject."},{ko:"팀장님이 오늘은 야근하지 말래요.",romanization:"timjangnimi oneureun yageunhaji mallaeyo.",en:"The team lead says not to work late today.",note:"A negative instruction goes through -지 말라고 해요 → -지 말래요."}],keyPoint:{label:"래요 vs ㄹ래요 — whose idea is it?",body:`저는 갈래요 means "I'll go / I want to go" — your own will, and it takes a first-person subject. 가래요 means "they're telling me to go" — someone else's instruction. One ㄹ flips the sentence from your decision to theirs. Quick test: if you could put 저는 in front of it, you need 갈래요.`},pronunciation:"말래요 doubles the ㄹ as [말래요]. 먹으래요 links straight through as [머그래요], and 연락하래요 is [열라카래요].",drill:{instruction:"Turn each full form into its 래요 contraction.",model:"엄마가 연락하라고 해요. → 엄마가 연락하래요.",items:["저분이 사장님이라고 해요. → 저분이 _______.","의사 선생님이 약을 먹으라고 해요. → 의사 선생님이 약을 _______.","팀장님이 야근하지 말라고 해요. → 팀장님이 야근하지 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구가 의사대요.",right:"친구가 의사래요.",explanation:"A noun never takes -대요. The full form behind it is 의사라고 해요, not 의사다고 해요, so the contraction has to be 의사래요 — or 학생이래요 after a 받침. Learners default to -대요 because it is the contraction they met first."}},{title:"V/A-냬요 — passing on someone else's question",func:"-냬요 is -냐고 해요 squeezed down. Use it when you are relaying a question somebody asked, usually to the person who can actually answer it. The question word — 뭐, 언제, 어디, 왜 — stays exactly where it stood in the original question, and the polite 요 of that question disappears.",formTable:[{when:"Verb or adjective, present",add:"-냬요",ex:"가다 → 어디 가냬요 (they're asking where you're going)"},{when:"Any past tense",add:"-았/었냬요",ex:"오다 → 언제 왔냬요 (they're asking when you came)"},{when:"Noun",add:"N(이)냬요",ex:"학생 → 학생이냬요 (they're asking if you're a student)"},{when:"Full form, for writing or careful speech",add:"-냐고 해요 / -냐고 물어봐요",ex:"가다 → 어디 가냐고 물어봐요"}],examples:[{ko:"팀장님이 보고서 언제 끝나냬요.",romanization:"timjangnimi bogoseo eonje kkeunnanaeyo.",en:"The team lead is asking when the report will be done.",note:"언제 keeps its original position; only the ending changes."},{ko:"친구가 이번 주말에 시간 있냬요.",romanization:"chinguga ibeon jumare sigan innaeyo.",en:"My friend is asking whether you're free this weekend.",note:"A yes/no question needs no question word — 있냬요 carries it."},{ko:"손님이 이 자리 앉아도 되냬요.",romanization:"sonnimi i jari anjado doenaeyo.",en:"A customer is asking whether it's okay to sit here.",note:"-아/어도 돼요? becomes -아/어도 되냬요 when relayed."},{ko:"어머니가 밥은 먹었냬요.",romanization:"eomeoniga babeun meogeonnaeyo.",en:"Mom is asking whether you've eaten.",note:"The past stays inside: 먹었냐고 해요 → 먹었냬요."}],keyPoint:{label:"냬요 is for somebody else's question, never your own",body:"If you want to ask the question yourself, just ask it: 언제 끝나요? Use -냬요 only when you are the middleman — A asked, and you are handing the question to B. That is why almost every -냬요 sentence names the asker up front: 팀장님이…, 친구가…, 손님이…"},pronunciation:"The ㄴ of 냬 nasalizes whatever closes the syllable before it: 있냬요 is [인내요], 먹었냬요 is [머건내요], and 끝나냬요 is [끈나내요].",drill:{instruction:"Relay each question with -냬요, naming the person who asked.",model:"친구: '주말에 시간 있어요?' → 친구가 주말에 시간 있냬요.",items:["팀장님: '보고서 언제 끝나요?' → 팀장님이 보고서 언제 _______.","손님: '여기 앉아도 돼요?' → 손님이 여기 앉아도 _______.","어머니: '밥 먹었어?' → 어머니가 밥 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"팀장님이 보고서 언제 끝나요라고 해요.",right:"팀장님이 보고서 언제 끝나냬요.",explanation:"Learners quote the question word for word, the way English uses quotation marks: 'He asked, when is it done?' Korean reported questions strip the polite ending first and run through -냐고 해요, which contracts to -냬요. The 요 of the original question always disappears."}},{title:"V-재요 — passing on someone's suggestion",func:`-재요 is -자고 해요 contracted: somebody proposed doing something together, and you are relaying the proposal. Because 자 is the "let's" ending, -재요 can only ever attach to an action verb — never to an adjective, and never to a noun.`,formTable:[{when:"Any verb stem",add:"-재요",ex:"가다 → 같이 가재요 (they say let's go together)"},{when:"Verb stem with 받침 — the ending does not change",add:"-재요",ex:"먹다 → 점심 먹재요 (they say let's have lunch)"},{when:"Negative proposal",add:"V-지 말재요",ex:"가다 → 오늘은 가지 말재요 (they say let's not go today)"},{when:"Full form, for writing or careful speech",add:"V-자고 해요",ex:"가다 → 같이 가자고 해요"}],examples:[{ko:"친구들이 이번 주말에 등산 가재요.",romanization:"chingudeuri ibeon jumare deungsan gajaeyo.",en:"My friends say let's go hiking this weekend.",note:"The proposers are the subject, and you are only the messenger."},{ko:"동기들이 점심은 구내식당에서 먹재요.",romanization:"donggideuri jeomsimeun gunaesikdangeseo meokjaeyo.",en:"My colleagues say let's eat lunch in the cafeteria.",note:"먹자고 해요 → 먹재요, pronounced [먹째요]."},{ko:"지훈이가 오늘은 술은 마시지 말재요.",romanization:"jihuniga oneureun sureun masiji maljaeyo.",en:"Jihoon says let's not drink tonight.",note:"The negative goes inside: -지 말자고 해요 → -지 말재요."},{ko:"팀에서 회식 날짜를 다음 주로 미루재요.",romanization:"timeseo hoesik naljjareul daeum juro mirujaeyo.",en:"The team says let's push the dinner back to next week.",note:"팀에서 marks a group acting as one, which is common with relayed proposals."}],keyPoint:{label:"The four contractions in one line",body:"-대요 for a statement (간대요 — they say he's going), -래요 for a command or a noun (가래요 — they say to go / 학생이래요 — they say he's a student), -냬요 for a question (가냬요 — they're asking if you're going), -재요 for a proposal (가재요 — they say let's go). They are the same four sentence types you already learned with -다고/-라고/-냐고/-자고 하다, just squeezed."},pronunciation:"After a ㄱ or ㅂ final the ㅈ of 재 tenses: 먹재요 is [먹째요] and 입재요 is [입째요]. A ㄹ-final stem does not tense it — 말재요 stays [말재요].",drill:{instruction:"Relay each suggestion with -재요.",model:"친구: '같이 등산 가자.' → 친구가 같이 등산 가재요.",items:["동기: '점심은 구내식당에서 먹자.' → 동기가 점심은 구내식당에서 _______.","지훈: '오늘은 술 마시지 말자.' → 지훈이가 오늘은 술 마시지 _______.","팀장님: '회식을 다음 주로 미루자.' → 팀장님이 회식을 다음 주로 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"친구가 주말에 한가하재요.",right:"친구가 주말에 한가하대요.",explanation:`-재요 comes from -자고, the "let's" ending, so it only attaches to an action verb. 한가하다 is an adjective — you cannot propose "let's be free" — so a statement about it takes -대요. Rule of thumb: if the English is "let's…", use -재요; if it is "they say it is…", use -대요.`}},{title:"-다던데(요) — hearsay you drop in as background",func:"-다던데요 is -다고 하던데요: you heard it somewhere, you are passing it on, and the 던데요 tail leaves room for the other person to react. It turns hearsay into background information rather than a flat claim, which is exactly why Koreans use it to suggest things without pushing. The casual version is -다더라.",formTable:[{when:"Verb, present",add:"-ㄴ/는다던데요",ex:"오다 → 내일부터 비가 온다던데요"},{when:"Adjective, present",add:"-다던데요",ex:"맛있다 → 거기 맛있다던데요"},{when:"Any past tense",add:"-았/었다던데요",ex:"닫다 → 벌써 문을 닫았다던데요"},{when:"Noun",add:"N(이)라던데요",ex:"맛집 → 거기가 유명한 맛집이라던데요"},{when:"Casual (반말)",add:"-다더라",ex:"편하다 → 그 앱이 제일 편하다더라"}],examples:[{ko:"그 집 칼국수가 그렇게 맛있다던데요. 한번 가 볼까요?",romanization:"geu jip kalguksuga geureoke masitdadeondeyo. hanbeon ga bolkkayo?",en:"I hear the kalguksu there is really something. Shall we try it?",note:"The classic indirect suggestion: state the hearsay, then leave the decision open."},{ko:"내일부터 비가 온다던데요. 우산 챙기세요.",romanization:"naeilbuteo biga ondadeondeyo. usan chaenggiseyo.",en:"I hear it's going to rain from tomorrow. Bring an umbrella.",note:"A present-tense verb keeps its -ㄴ/는- here too: 온다던데요."},{ko:"그 카페 지난달에 문 닫았다던데요.",romanization:"geu kape jinandare mun dadatdadeondeyo.",en:"I heard that café closed last month.",note:"Past hearsay: 닫았다고 하던데요 → 닫았다던데요."},{ko:"요즘 그 앱이 제일 편하다더라. 너도 한번 써 봐.",romanization:"yojeum geu aebi jeil pyeonhadadeora. neodo hanbeon sseo bwa.",en:"I hear that app is the handiest these days. Give it a try.",note:"-다더라 is the 반말 version, used with close friends."}],keyPoint:{label:"Why Koreans suggest things with 던데요",body:`-다던데요 lets you float an idea without owning it. 거기 맛있다던데요 says "apparently it's good" and hands the decision to the listener, whereas 거기 맛있어요 is your own claim and 거기 가요 is a direct proposal. It is the natural move when you are the junior person in the room and do not want to decide for everyone.`},pronunciation:"던데요 is [던데요]. After a past 았/었 the ㄷ tenses: 닫았다던데요 is [다닫따던데요] and 맛있다던데요 is [마싣따던데요].",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each flat fact as softened hearsay with -다던데요.",model:"거기 맛있어요. → 거기 맛있다던데요.",items:["내일부터 비가 와요. → 내일부터 비가 _______.","그 카페가 문을 닫았어요. → 그 카페가 문을 _______.","거기가 유명한 맛집이에요. → 거기가 유명한 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"제가 어제 거기 갔다던데요.",right:"제가 어제 거기 갔는데요.",explanation:"-다던데요 says you heard it from somebody else, so it cannot report your own first-hand experience. Learners reach for it as a general softener because the 던데요 tail sounds friendly. For something you did yourself, use plain -는데요."}}],P=[{title:`V-느라고 — "I was busy V-ing," and what didn't get done`,func:"-느라고 names a time-consuming activity you were absorbed in, and the second clause says what suffered for it. Three conditions have to hold at once: both clauses share the same subject, the first action genuinely eats the time the second one needed, and the second clause is normally something that failed to happen. Only action verbs qualify.",formTable:[{when:"Any action verb stem",add:"-느라고",ex:"쓰다 → 보고서를 쓰느라고 (busy writing the report)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem — the ㄹ drops",add:"-느라고",ex:"놀다 → 노느라고 (busy playing around)"},{when:"Never take a tense marker",add:"V-느라고 (the tense lives in clause 2)",ex:"쓰다 → 썼느라고 ✗ / 쓰느라고 못 잤어요 ✓"},{when:"Thanking someone for their effort",add:"V-느라고 고생하셨어요",ex:"오다 → 오시느라고 고생하셨어요"}],examples:[{ko:"어제 보고서 쓰느라고 한숨도 못 잤어요.",romanization:"eoje bogoseo sseuneurago hansumdo mot jasseoyo.",en:"I was writing a report yesterday, so I didn't sleep a wink.",note:"Same subject in both halves, and the result is a plain failure to sleep."},{ko:"게임하느라고 엄마가 부르는 소리를 못 들었어요.",romanization:"geimhaneurago eommaga bureuneun sorireul mot deureosseoyo.",en:"I was gaming, so I didn't hear my mom calling me.",note:"엄마가 sits inside a relative clause, so the main subject is still 저 in both halves."},{ko:"여기까지 오시느라고 고생하셨어요.",romanization:"yeogikkaji osineurago gosaenghasyeosseoyo.",en:"Thank you for making the trip all the way here.",note:"The warm use of -느라고: naming the effort somebody spent on you."},{ko:"이번 달에는 이사 준비하느라고 정신이 없었어요.",romanization:"ibeon dareneun isa junbihaneurago jeongsini eopseosseoyo.",en:"I was busy getting ready to move this month, so I was all over the place.",note:"정신이 없다 is one of the most common results after -느라고."}],keyPoint:{label:"One subject, one stretch of time, one thing that failed",body:"All three conditions have to hold. 동생이 떠드느라고 제가 못 잤어요 breaks the first one — the noisy person is 동생 and the sleepless one is 저 — so it needs -는 바람에 instead. And 배가 아프느라고 breaks the third: 아프다 is an adjective, not something you spend time doing."},pronunciation:"In fast speech the 고 often drops: 쓰느라 못 잤어요. After a ㄹ-stem the ㄹ disappears entirely — 놀다 → 노느라고, 만들다 → 만드느라고 — the same drop you see in 노는, 만드는.",drill:{instruction:"Give the excuse with -느라고, keeping the tense out of the ending.",model:"보고서를 쓰다 / 못 자다 → 보고서를 쓰느라고 못 잤어요.",items:["게임하다 / 전화를 못 받다 → _______ 전화를 못 받았어요.","이사 준비하다 / 정신이 없다 → _______ 정신이 없었어요.","친구랑 놀다 / 숙제를 못 하다 → 친구랑 _______ 숙제를 못 했어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"어제 보고서를 썼느라고 못 잤어요.",right:"어제 보고서를 쓰느라고 못 잤어요.",explanation:`English marks the past on both halves — "I was writing… so I couldn't sleep" — so learners add -았/었- to 느라고 as well. Korean marks the tense only once, in the final clause. -느라고 always sits on the bare stem: 쓰느라고, 하느라고, 보느라고.`}},{title:"V-는 바람에 — a sudden cause that derailed you",func:"-는 바람에 blames something that happened TO you: abrupt, outside your control, and it wrecked the outcome. Unlike -느라고 the two clauses may have different subjects, and the second clause is almost always past tense and unwelcome. The ending stays in the -는 shape no matter when the event happened.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem, no 받침",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"서다 → 앞차가 갑자기 서는 바람에"},{when:"Verb stem with 받침",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"먹다 → 급하게 먹는 바람에"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem — the ㄹ drops",add:"-는 바람에",ex:"울다 → 아이가 우는 바람에"},{when:"The result clause is past and unwelcome",add:"-는 바람에 + -았/었어요",ex:"고장 나다 → 고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요"},{when:"Never an order or a proposal after it",add:"✗ -는 바람에 + -(으)세요 / -(으)ㅂ시다",ex:"늦는 바람에 서두르세요 ✗"}],examples:[{ko:"앞차가 갑자기 서는 바람에 뒤에서 부딪힐 뻔했어요.",romanization:"apchaga gapjagi seoneun barame dwieseo budichil ppeonhaesseoyo.",en:"The car in front stopped suddenly, so I nearly hit it from behind.",note:"Different subjects — 앞차 caused it, 저 suffered it — which -느라고 could never do."},{ko:"엘리베이터가 고장 나는 바람에 십오 층까지 걸어 올라갔어요.",romanization:"ellibeiteoga gojang naneun barame sibo cheungkkaji georeo ollagasseoyo.",en:"The lift broke down, so I walked all the way up to the fifteenth floor.",note:"A breakdown is the textbook 바람에 cause: sudden and nobody's choice."},{ko:"지하철이 멈추는 바람에 면접에 이십 분이나 늦었어요.",romanization:"jihacheori meomchuneun barame myeonjeobe isip bunina neujeosseoyo.",en:"The subway stopped, so I was a full twenty minutes late to the interview.",note:"이십 분이나 adds the sting; -는 바람에 sentences usually carry some regret."},{ko:"옆에서 아이가 뛰어드는 바람에 커피를 다 쏟았어요.",romanization:"yeopeseo aiga ttwieodeuneun barame keopireul da ssodasseoyo.",en:"A kid darted in beside me, so I spilled my whole coffee.",note:"The child acted, you paid for it — exactly the split -는 바람에 exists for."}],keyPoint:{label:"-는 바람에 vs -느라고 — who is responsible",body:"-느라고 pins it on your own absorbing activity: 게임하느라고 못 들었어요 — you chose to play. -는 바람에 pins it on something that hit you: 엘리베이터가 고장 나는 바람에 늦었어요 — the lift broke, not you. That is also why the subjects may differ with -는 바람에 and never may with -느라고."},pronunciation:"바람에 links as [바라메], and the whole chunk runs together with no pause: 고장 나는 바람에 is [고장나는 바라메].",drill:{instruction:"Blame the sudden cause with -는 바람에.",model:"엘리베이터가 고장 나다 / 걸어 올라가다 → 엘리베이터가 고장 나는 바람에 걸어 올라갔어요.",items:["앞차가 갑자기 서다 / 부딪힐 뻔하다 → 앞차가 갑자기 _______ 부딪힐 뻔했어요.","지하철이 멈추다 / 면접에 늦다 → 지하철이 _______ 면접에 늦었어요.","아이가 뛰어들다 / 커피를 쏟다 → 아이가 _______ 커피를 다 쏟았어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"동생이 떠드느라고 제가 잠을 못 잤어요.",right:"동생이 떠드는 바람에 제가 잠을 못 잤어요.",explanation:"-느라고 demands one subject across both clauses. Here the noisy one is 동생 and the sleepless one is 저, so -느라고 is simply ungrammatical. Whenever somebody else's action is what ruined your plan, switch to -는 바람에."}},{title:"V/A-더니 — what I watched happen, and what came of it",func:"-더니 links something you personally observed in the past to what followed from it. Because you had to be watching, the subject is nearly always somebody or something other than yourself. It also handles then-versus-now contrast: 아까는 춥더니 지금은 괜찮네요.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem — an action you watched",add:"-더니",ex:"야근하다 → 계속 야근하더니"},{when:"Adjective stem — a state you observed",add:"-더니",ex:"맑다 → 아침에는 하늘이 맑더니"},{when:"Then versus now",add:"A/V-더니 지금은 …",ex:"춥다 → 아까는 춥더니 지금은 괜찮네요"},{when:"The subject is normally not 저/제가",add:"(for yourself, use -았/었더니)",ex:"제가 야근하더니 ✗ / 제가 야근했더니 ✓"}],examples:[{ko:"아침에는 하늘이 맑더니 오후에 갑자기 소나기가 쏟아졌어요.",romanization:"achimeneun haneuri makdeoni ohue gapjagi sonagiga ssodajyeosseoyo.",en:"The sky was clear in the morning, and then a shower came down out of nowhere in the afternoon.",note:"An adjective works fine with -더니 when you watched the state yourself."},{ko:"지훈 씨가 요즘 계속 야근하더니 결국 몸살이 났어요.",romanization:"jihun ssiga yojeum gyesok yageunhadeoni gyeolguk momsari nasseoyo.",en:"Jihoon has been working late constantly, and in the end he came down with something.",note:"결국 is a natural partner for -더니 when the outcome was building up."},{ko:"그 가게가 한동안 손님이 없더니 지난달에 문을 닫았어요.",romanization:"geu gagega handongan sonnimi eopdeoni jinandare muneul dadasseoyo.",en:"That shop had no customers for a while, and then it closed last month.",note:"The subject can be a thing, as long as you were the one observing it."},{ko:"동생이 새 신발을 사더니 매일 그것만 신고 다녀요.",romanization:"dongsaengi sae sinbareul sadeoni maeil geugeonman singo danyeoyo.",en:"My younger sibling bought new shoes, and now wears nothing else.",note:"The result clause can stay in the present when the state is still going on."}],keyPoint:{label:"-더니 needs a witness",body:'-더니 says "I saw this happen, and then this followed." Since you have to have been watching, the subject is almost always somebody or something else — 지훈 씨가, 하늘이, 그 가게가. You cannot stand outside yourself and watch yourself, which is exactly why Korean switches to -았/었더니 for your own actions.'},pronunciation:"The ㄷ tenses after a stop: 없더니 is [업떠니] and 맑더니 is [막떠니]. After a vowel it stays plain, as in 사더니 [사더니].",drill:{instruction:"Link what you observed to what followed, using -더니.",model:"지훈 씨가 계속 야근했어요. 그리고 몸살이 났어요. → 지훈 씨가 계속 야근하더니 몸살이 났어요.",items:["아침에 하늘이 맑았어요. 그런데 오후에 소나기가 왔어요. → 아침에는 하늘이 _______ 오후에 소나기가 왔어요.","그 가게에 손님이 없었어요. 그리고 문을 닫았어요. → 그 가게에 손님이 _______ 문을 닫았어요.","동생이 새 신발을 샀어요. 그리고 매일 그것만 신어요. → 동생이 새 신발을 _______ 매일 그것만 신어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"제가 어제 늦게 자더니 오늘 너무 피곤해요.",right:"제가 어제 늦게 잤더니 오늘 너무 피곤해요.",explanation:`-더니 reports something you observed happening to someone else, so it clashes with a 저/제가 subject. Your own past action leading to a present result takes -았/었더니. Learners pick 더니 because English says "I stayed up late and now I'm tired" with no such distinction.`}},{title:"V-았/었더니 — I did it, and then I found out",func:"-았/었더니 reports your own past action and the consequence you discovered from it. The first clause is normally 제가/저는, and the second clause is the result — often a small revelation rather than a plain cause and effect. Like -더니, it cannot be followed by an order or a suggestion.",formTable:[{when:"Your own past action (stem + 았/었)",add:"-았/었더니",ex:"먹다 → 아침을 안 먹었더니"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"-했더니",ex:"등산하다 → 오랜만에 등산했더니"},{when:"The result may be present or past",add:"-았/었더니 + 어요 / 았어요",ex:"가다 → 일찍 갔더니 아무도 없었어요"},{when:"Contrast with -더니 (somebody else)",add:"V-더니 / V-았/었더니",ex:"지훈 씨가 야근하더니 / 제가 야근했더니"}],examples:[{ko:"아침을 안 먹었더니 지금 배가 너무 고파요.",romanization:"achimeul an meogeotdeoni jigeum baega neomu gopayo.",en:"I skipped breakfast, and now I'm starving.",note:"The classic shape: my own past action, and the state I'm in because of it."},{ko:"감기약을 먹었더니 하루 만에 괜찮아졌어요.",romanization:"gamgiyageul meogeotdeoni haru mane gwaenchanajyeosseoyo.",en:"I took cold medicine, and I was fine within a day.",note:"A discovery about what worked, not just a bare cause."},{ko:"오랜만에 등산했더니 다음 날 다리가 너무 아팠어요.",romanization:"oraenmane deungsanhaetdeoni daeum nal dariga neomu apasseoyo.",en:"I went hiking for the first time in ages, and the next day my legs were killing me.",note:"Here both clauses are past, which is perfectly normal."},{ko:"사무실에 일찍 갔더니 아무도 없었어요.",romanization:"samusire iljjik gatdeoni amudo eopseosseoyo.",en:"I got to the office early, and there was nobody there.",note:"A pure discovery: going early is what let you find that out."}],keyPoint:{label:"-았/었더니 vs -아서/어서 — result or discovery",body:'아침을 안 먹어서 배가 고파요 states a cause and its effect, and everyone could have predicted it. 아침을 안 먹었더니 배가 고파요 adds that you noticed the consequence afterwards — "I skipped breakfast, and sure enough…". Use -아서/어서 when the link is obvious and -았/었더니 when you are reporting what you found out by doing it.'},pronunciation:"The ㄷ tenses after 았/었 every time: 먹었더니 is [머걷떠니], 갔더니 is [갇떠니], 했더니 is [핻떠니].",drill:{instruction:"Report your own past action and what you then found, using -았/었더니.",model:"약을 먹다 / 괜찮아지다 → 약을 먹었더니 괜찮아졌어요.",items:["아침을 안 먹다 / 배가 고프다 → 아침을 안 _______ 배가 고파요.","오랜만에 등산하다 / 다리가 아프다 → 오랜만에 _______ 다리가 아팠어요.","사무실에 일찍 가다 / 아무도 없다 → 사무실에 일찍 _______ 아무도 없었어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"약을 먹었더니 푹 쉬세요.",right:"약을 먹었더니 좀 나아졌어요.",explanation:`-았/었더니 reports a result you have already observed, so the clause after it cannot be a command or a suggestion. Learners try to give advice with it because English "once you've taken the medicine, get some rest" is fine. For advice, use -(으)면 or -고 나서 instead.`}},{title:'-느라고 vs -는 바람에 vs -아서/어서 — picking the right "because"',func:"All three give a reason, and they are not interchangeable. -아서/어서 is the neutral default and fits almost anywhere. -느라고 says the reason was your own time-consuming activity and the result is what you failed to do. -는 바람에 says something sudden hit you and spoiled the outcome. Choose by asking who caused it and whether the result was bad.",formTable:[{when:"Neutral reason, any kind of result",add:"V/A-아서/어서",ex:"오다 → 눈이 많이 와서 재택근무를 했어요"},{when:"My own absorbing activity, a result that failed",add:"V-느라고",ex:"준비하다 → 발표 준비하느라고 점심도 못 먹었어요"},{when:"A sudden outside cause, a spoiled result",add:"V-는 바람에",ex:"꺼지다 → 노트북이 꺼지는 바람에 다 날렸어요"},{when:"A welcome result — credit the cause instead",add:"V/A-아서/어서, or N 덕분에",ex:"도착하다 → 일찍 도착한 덕분에 좋은 자리를 잡았어요"}],examples:[{ko:"눈이 많이 와서 오늘은 재택근무를 했어요.",romanization:"nuni mani waseo oneureun jaetaekgeunmureul haesseoyo.",en:"It snowed heavily, so I worked from home today.",note:"Neutral cause and a perfectly fine outcome — plain -아서 is right."},{ko:"발표 준비하느라고 점심도 못 먹었어요.",romanization:"balpyo junbihaneurago jeomsimdo mot meogeosseoyo.",en:"I was preparing my presentation, so I didn't even get lunch.",note:"My own activity, and the result is something that did not happen."},{ko:"노트북이 갑자기 꺼지는 바람에 쓰던 걸 다 날렸어요.",romanization:"noteubugi gapjagi kkeojineun barame sseudeon geol da nallyeosseoyo.",en:"My laptop died out of nowhere, so I lost everything I'd been writing.",note:"Sudden, outside your control, and the result is a loss."},{ko:"일찍 도착한 덕분에 좋은 자리를 잡았어요.",romanization:"iljjik dochakan deokbune joeun jarireul jabasseoyo.",en:"Thanks to arriving early, I got a good seat.",note:"덕분에 is the positive counterpart of 바람에 — never use 바람에 for good news."}],keyPoint:{label:"A three-question test",body:"Ask in this order. Did something fail to happen, and was the cause your own activity? → -느라고. Did something sudden happen to you and spoil it? → -는 바람에. Neither, or the result is fine? → -아서/어서, and 덕분에 when you want to credit the cause for a good outcome."},pronunciation:"-느라고 and -는 바람에 both attach to the plain stem, so nothing in front of them changes shape. Only -아서/어서 reshapes the stem: 오다 → 와서, 먹다 → 먹어서, 도착하다 → 도착해서.",drill:{instruction:"Choose -느라고, -는 바람에, or -아서/어서 for each pair, then complete the sentence.",model:"발표 준비하다 / 점심을 못 먹다 → 발표 준비하느라고 점심을 못 먹었어요.",items:["노트북이 갑자기 꺼지다 / 파일을 다 날리다 → 노트북이 갑자기 _______ 파일을 다 날렸어요.","눈이 많이 오다 / 재택근무를 하다 → 눈이 많이 _______ 재택근무를 했어요.","게임하다 / 전화를 못 받다 → _______ 전화를 못 받았어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"배가 아프느라고 학교에 못 갔어요.",right:"배가 아파서 학교에 못 갔어요.",explanation:"-느라고 only attaches to ACTION verbs, never to adjectives. 아프다 is an adjective, so a reason built on it uses -아서/어서. Learners reach for -느라고 whenever the result is negative, but a negative result is not what licenses it — a deliberate, time-consuming action is."}}],A=[{title:"V-아/어 버리다 — finish it off, for better or worse",func:'버리다 on its own means "to throw away." As an auxiliary after the -아/어 form it means the action was carried all the way through, with nothing left over — and it always leaves an emotional trace. Finish a burden and it feels like relief; use up something you wanted to keep and it feels like loss.',formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-아 버리다",ex:"팔다 → 팔아 버리다 (to sell it off)"},{when:"Stem ends in any other vowel",add:"-어 버리다",ex:"쓰다 → 써 버리다 (to use it all up)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"-해 버리다",ex:"말하다 → 말해 버리다 (to just come out and say it)"},{when:"Lexicalized — written as one word",add:"잊어버리다 / 잃어버리다",ex:"잊다 → 약속을 잊어버렸어요"}],examples:[{ko:"고민하다가 그냥 다 말해 버렸어요.",romanization:"gominhadaga geunyang da malhae beoryeosseoyo.",en:"I agonised over it and then just came out with the whole thing.",note:"그냥 is the giveaway for the impulsive version — you stopped holding back."},{ko:"지훈 씨가 인사도 없이 가 버렸어요.",romanization:"jihun ssiga insado eopsi ga beoryeosseoyo.",en:"Jihoon left without so much as a goodbye.",note:"가 버리다 adds the sting of finality: he's gone, and that's that."},{ko:"밀린 일을 오늘 다 끝내 버렸어요. 속이 다 시원하네요.",romanization:"millin ireul oneul da kkeunnae beoryeosseoyo. sogi da siwonhaneyo.",en:"I knocked out all my backlog today. What a relief.",note:"속이 시원하다 shows this is the relief reading, not regret."},{ko:"월급을 이틀 만에 다 써 버렸어요.",romanization:"wolgeubeul iteul mane da sseo beoryeosseoyo.",en:"I blew my whole salary in two days.",note:"Same ending, opposite feeling — because what got used up was something you wanted."}],keyPoint:{label:"Relief or regret — what got used up decides",body:'-아/어 버리다 always means "and it is completely done." The feeling comes from the object, not the ending. Finish a burden and it is relief (다 끝내 버렸어요); finish something you wanted to keep and it is regret (다 써 버렸어요). Korean adds 그냥 for the impulsive reading and 다 for the exhaustive one.'},pronunciation:"버리다 contracts hard in speech: 해 버렸어요 comes out as [해버려써요] with no gap, and 팔아 버렸어요 as [파라버려써요] with the ㄹ linking through.",drill:{instruction:"Add -아/어 버리다 to show the action was carried right through.",model:"다 먹다 → 다 먹어 버렸어요.",items:["그냥 다 말하다 → 그냥 다 _______.","인사도 없이 가다 → 인사도 없이 _______.","밀린 일을 다 끝내다 → 밀린 일을 다 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"월급을 다 쓰다 버렸어요.",right:"월급을 다 써 버렸어요.",explanation:'버리다 is an auxiliary, so it attaches to the -아/어 form, never to the dictionary form. 쓰다 becomes 써, giving 써 버렸어요. Learners leave the base verb intact because English "spend it all" does not reshape the verb at all.'}},{title:"V-고 말다 — it ended up happening after all",func:"-고 말다 reports an outcome that arrived DESPITE what you wanted or what you tried. It nearly always appears in the past as -고 말았어요, and it carries resignation or regret — never relief. In the future, -고 말겠어요 flips to fierce determination: it WILL happen, whatever stands in the way.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem + 고 말다, usually past",add:"-고 말았어요",ex:"울다 → 울고 말았어요 (I ended up in tears)"},{when:"With 결국 for the build-up",add:"결국 V-고 말았어요",ex:"포기하다 → 결국 포기하고 말았어요"},{when:"Future — determination, not regret",add:"-고 말겠어요",ex:"따다 → 자격증을 따고 말겠어요"},{when:"Casual past",add:"-고 말았어 / -고 말았다",ex:"깨다 → 컵을 깨고 말았어"}],examples:[{ko:"참으려고 했는데 결국 울고 말았어요.",romanization:"chameuryeogo haenneunde gyeolguk ulgo marasseoyo.",en:"I tried to hold it in, but in the end I broke down.",note:"참으려고 했는데 supplies the resistance that -고 말다 needs."},{ko:"조심했는데도 컵을 깨고 말았어요.",romanization:"josimhaenneundedo keobeul kkaego marasseoyo.",en:"I was careful, and I still went and broke the cup.",note:'-는데도 ("even though") is a natural lead-in for this ending.'},{ko:"몇 년을 준비했는데 마지막에 포기하고 말았어요.",romanization:"myeot nyeoneul junbihaenneunde majimage pogihago marasseoyo.",en:"I prepared for years and then gave up right at the end.",note:"The longer the effort, the heavier -고 말다 lands."},{ko:"올해는 꼭 자격증을 따고 말겠어요.",romanization:"olhaeneun kkok jagyeokjeungeul ttago malgesseoyo.",en:"This year I'm getting that certificate, no matter what.",note:"The future form is pure resolve — the only reading with no regret in it."}],keyPoint:{label:"-고 말다 needs something you were resisting",body:"-고 말다 only works when the outcome went against your effort or your wish. 참으려고 했는데 울고 말았어요 makes sense because you were fighting it. An ordinary action cannot take it: 점심을 먹고 말았어요 sounds absurd unless lunch was somehow a defeat. If nothing was being resisted, plain past tense is all you need."},pronunciation:"말았어요 links as [마라써요]. In casual speech it shortens to 말았어 [마라써], and the future 말겠어요 tenses to [말게써요].",drill:{instruction:"Say that it happened in the end, despite your efforts, with -고 말다.",model:"참으려고 했다 / 울다 → 참으려고 했는데 결국 울고 말았어요.",items:["조심했다 / 컵을 깨다 → 조심했는데도 컵을 _______.","몇 년을 준비했다 / 포기하다 → 몇 년을 준비했는데 마지막에 _______.","안 그러려고 했다 / 실수하다 → 안 그러려고 했는데 또 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"어제 점심을 먹고 말았어요.",right:"어제 점심을 먹었어요.",explanation:'-고 말다 marks an outcome you were trying to avoid, so an ordinary action cannot carry it. Learners use it as a general-purpose "ended up" because that is how the English gloss reads. When nothing was being resisted, plain past tense is the whole answer.'}},{title:"V-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다 — the thing that almost happened",func:"-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다 reports a near miss: the event came close and then did NOT happen. It is always past — 뻔했어요, never 뻔해요 — because a near miss is by definition already over. The event is nearly always something bad, and 하마터면 is its fixed partner at the front of the sentence.",formTable:[{when:"Verb stem, no 받침",add:"-ㄹ 뻔했어요",ex:"가다 → 갈 뻔했어요 (I almost went)"},{when:"Verb stem with 받침",add:"-을 뻔했어요",ex:"죽다 → 죽을 뻔했어요 (I almost died)"},{when:"Always past — never 뻔해요",add:"-(으)ㄹ 뻔했어요",ex:"넘어지다 → 넘어질 뻔해요 ✗ / 넘어질 뻔했어요 ✓"},{when:"The set collocation",add:"하마터면 V-(으)ㄹ 뻔했어요",ex:"두다 → 하마터면 지갑을 두고 나올 뻔했어요"}],examples:[{ko:"하마터면 지갑을 두고 나올 뻔했어요.",romanization:"hamateomyeon jigabeul dugo naol ppeonhaesseoyo.",en:"I very nearly walked out and left my wallet behind.",note:"하마터면 signals the near miss before the verb even arrives."},{ko:"계단에서 미끄러져서 크게 다칠 뻔했어요.",romanization:"gyedaneseo mikkeureojyeoseo keuge dachil ppeonhaesseoyo.",en:"I slipped on the stairs and nearly hurt myself badly.",note:"The slipping did happen; only the injury is the near miss."},{ko:"하마터면 회의 시간을 잊어버릴 뻔했는데, 알림 덕분에 살았어요.",romanization:"hamateomyeon hoeui siganeul ijeobeoril ppeonhaenneunde, allim deokbune sarasseoyo.",en:"I nearly forgot the meeting time, but the reminder saved me.",note:"덕분에 credits what rescued you — the natural follow-up to a near miss."},{ko:"너무 졸려서 지하철에서 내릴 역을 지나칠 뻔했어요.",romanization:"neomu jollyeoseo jihacheoreseo naeril yeogeul jinachil ppeonhaesseoyo.",en:"I was so sleepy I almost rode past my stop on the subway.",note:"-(으)ㄹ 뻔했어요 keeps the object particle: 역을 지나칠 뻔했어요."}],keyPoint:{label:"뻔하다 has a completely separate life as an adjective",body:`V-(으)ㄹ 뻔했다 is a near miss: 늦을 뻔했어요 means you almost were late, but you weren't. Standalone 뻔하다 is an unrelated adjective meaning "obvious, predictable": 결말이 너무 뻔해요, 뻔한 변명. The giveaway is the -(으)ㄹ in front — with it, near miss; without it, obvious.`},pronunciation:"뻔했어요 is [뻔해써요]. Because 뻔 already begins with a tense ㅃ, nothing tenses further: 갈 뻔했어요 is simply [갈 뻔해써요].",drill:{instruction:"Report each near miss with -(으)ㄹ 뻔했어요.",model:"지갑을 두고 나오다 → 하마터면 지갑을 두고 나올 뻔했어요.",items:["계단에서 크게 다치다 → 계단에서 미끄러져서 크게 _______.","회의 시간을 잊어버리다 → 하마터면 회의 시간을 _______.","내릴 역을 지나치다 → 너무 졸려서 내릴 역을 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"어제 계단에서 넘어질 뻔해요.",right:"어제 계단에서 넘어질 뻔했어요.",explanation:`A near miss is already over, so -(으)ㄹ 뻔하다 lives in the past: 뻔했어요. Learners write 뻔해요 because English "I almost fall" feels present-shaped while telling the story. Worse, present-tense 뻔해요 is a different word entirely — the adjective meaning "that's so predictable."`}},{title:"A/V-(으)ㄴ/는 척하다 — putting on an act",func:"척하다 says the outward behaviour did not match the inside. The modifier in front follows the ordinary rules you already know: -는 for a verb happening now, -(으)ㄴ for a finished action or for any adjective, and 인 for a noun. -(으)ㄴ/는 체하다 is an interchangeable variant.",formTable:[{when:"Verb — action in progress",add:"V-는 척하다",ex:"자다 → 자는 척하다 (to pretend to be asleep)"},{when:"Verb — action already finished",add:"V-(으)ㄴ 척하다",ex:"듣다 → 못 들은 척하다 (to pretend not to have heard)"},{when:"Adjective",add:"A-(으)ㄴ 척하다",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁜 척하다 (to pretend to be busy)"},{when:"Noun",add:"N인 척하다",ex:"전문가 → 전문가인 척하다"},{when:"Interchangeable variant",add:"-(으)ㄴ/는 체하다",ex:"알다 → 아는 체하다"}],examples:[{ko:"부장님이 지나가시길래 바쁜 척했어요.",romanization:"bujangnimi jinagasigillae bappeun cheokaesseoyo.",en:"The department head was walking past, so I acted busy.",note:"바쁘다 is an adjective, so the modifier is -(으)ㄴ: 바쁜 척."},{ko:"다 들었으면서 못 들은 척하지 마세요.",romanization:"da deureosseumyeonseo mot deureun cheokaji maseyo.",en:"Don't pretend you didn't hear when you heard every word.",note:"The hearing is already finished, so the modifier is -(으)ㄴ: 못 들은 척."},{ko:"속상했지만 아무렇지 않은 척했어요.",romanization:"soksanghaetjiman amureochi aneun cheokaesseoyo.",en:"I was upset, but I acted as if it were nothing.",note:"아무렇지 않다 is an adjective phrase, hence 않은 척."},{ko:"동생이 자는 척하고 있길래 그냥 두고 나왔어요.",romanization:"dongsaengi janeun cheokago itgillae geunyang dugo nawasseoyo.",en:"My sibling was pretending to be asleep, so I just left them be.",note:"자다 is a verb in progress, so this one really does take -는."}],keyPoint:{label:"Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ, not -는",body:'This is where English speakers slip. "Pretend to be sick" is 아픈 척하다, not 아프는 척하다, because 아프다 is an adjective and adjectives take -(으)ㄴ. Only verbs describing an ongoing action take -는: 자는 척하다, 일하는 척하다. Check what the base word is before you pick the modifier.'},pronunciation:"척하다 is [처카다] — the ㄱ of 척 and the ㅎ of 하 fuse into an aspirated [ㅋ]. So 척했어요 is [처캐써요] and 바쁜 척해요 is [바쁜 처캐요].",drill:{instruction:"Say what the person was pretending, choosing -(으)ㄴ or -는.",model:"자다 (동생) → 동생이 자는 척했어요.",items:["바쁘다 → 부장님이 지나가시길래 _______ 척했어요.","못 듣다 (already finished) → 다 들었으면서 _______ 척하지 마세요.","아무렇지 않다 → 속상했지만 _______ 척했어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"사실 아픈데 안 아프는 척했어요.",right:"사실 아픈데 안 아픈 척했어요.",explanation:'아프다 is an adjective, so it takes -(으)ㄴ 척하다: 아픈 척, 안 아픈 척. Learners attach -는 because English "pretending" looks continuous. Keep -는 척하다 for verbs describing an action in progress, like 자는 척 and 일하는 척.'}},{title:"-아/어 버리다 vs -고 말다 — same event, different feeling",func:"Both say the action reached its end and both often carry regret, so learners treat them as interchangeable. They are not. -아/어 버리다 usually reports something YOU did, deliberately, and can just as easily feel like relief. -고 말다 reports an outcome that arrived despite you, and never feels like relief.",formTable:[{when:"You did it on purpose; often relief",add:"V-아/어 버리다",ex:"끝내다 → 다 끝내 버렸어요 (and good riddance)"},{when:"It happened against your will; always regret",add:"V-고 말았어요",ex:"울다 → 결국 울고 말았어요"},{when:"Same verb, two readings",add:"말해 버렸어요 / 말하고 말았어요",ex:"말하다 → 그냥 말해 버렸어요 (I let it out) vs 결국 말하고 말았어요 (it got out despite me)"},{when:"Determination — only -고 말다 does this",add:"V-고 말겠어요",ex:"붙다 → 이번엔 꼭 붙고 말겠어요"}],examples:[{ko:"미련 없이 그 가방을 팔아 버렸어요.",romanization:"miryeon eopsi geu gabangeul para beoryeosseoyo.",en:"I sold that bag off without a second thought.",note:"미련 없이 makes it clear this was your decision and you feel lighter."},{ko:"숨기려고 했는데 결국 말하고 말았어요.",romanization:"sumgiryeogo haenneunde gyeolguk malhago marasseoyo.",en:"I meant to keep it hidden, but in the end it came out.",note:"Same verb as 말해 버렸어요, but here the telling defeated you."},{ko:"밀린 설거지를 다 해 버리니까 속이 시원하네요.",romanization:"millin seolgeojireul da hae beorinikka sogi siwonhaneyo.",en:"Getting all the piled-up dishes done feels wonderful.",note:"-고 말다 could never appear here — there is no defeat in it."},{ko:"이번 시험은 꼭 붙고 말겠어요.",romanization:"ibeon siheomeun kkok butgo malgesseoyo.",en:"I am going to pass this exam, whatever it takes.",note:"The future -고 말겠어요 is resolve, and 버리다 has no equivalent."}],keyPoint:{label:"Ask whether you wanted it",body:"If you chose the ending and feel lighter for it, use -아/어 버리다. If the ending beat you, use -고 말다. And only -고 말다 turns into determination in the future (-고 말겠어요), because that reading comes from the same core idea: the outcome arrives whether anyone likes it or not."},pronunciation:"해 버리니까 runs together as [해버리니까]. 붙고 말겠어요 tenses to [붇꼬 말게써요], since the ㅌ of 붙 closes as [ㄷ] and tenses the following ㄱ.",drill:{instruction:"Choose -아/어 버리다 or -고 말다 to match the feeling described.",model:"미련 없이 팔았고 후련하다 → 그 가방을 팔아 버렸어요.",items:["숨기려고 했는데 말이 나왔다 → 숨기려고 했는데 결국 _______.","밀린 설거지를 다 하고 후련하다 → 밀린 설거지를 다 _______.","이번 시험은 꼭 붙겠다는 각오 → 이번 시험은 꼭 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"일이 다 끝나서 기분 좋게 끝내고 말았어요.",right:"일이 다 끝나서 기분 좋게 끝내 버렸어요.",explanation:'-고 말다 always carries defeat, so pairing it with 기분 좋게 contradicts itself. When you finished something and feel good about it, -아/어 버리다 is the auxiliary you want. Learners swap the two because both come out as "ended up" in English.'}}],S=[{title:"V/A-(으)며 — listing and simultaneity inside one sentence",func:"-(으)며 is the written counterpart of two spoken endings at once: -고 when it lists parallel facts, and -(으)면서 when the two things happen together. It keeps both clauses under a single final ending, which is what makes a report read as connected prose rather than a list of short statements.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel or ㄹ",add:"-며",ex:"비싸다 → 값은 비싸며 (it is expensive, and…)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으며",ex:"좋다 → 품질이 좋으며 (the quality is good, and…)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었으며",ex:"끝나다 → 회의는 끝났으며 (the meeting has ended, and…)"},{when:"Simultaneity — the written -(으)면서",add:"-(으)며",ex:"보다 → 자료를 보며 (while looking at the materials)"},{when:"The spoken forms you are replacing",add:"-고 / -(으)면서",ex:"값이 싸고 품질도 좋아요 → 값이 싸며 품질도 좋다"}],examples:[{ko:"이번 행사는 다음 달에 시작되며 총 사 주간 진행된다.",romanization:"ibeon haengsaneun daeum dare sijakdoemyeo chong sa jugan jinhaengdoenda.",en:"This event begins next month and runs for a total of four weeks.",note:"Two facts about one subject, joined without a second sentence."},{ko:"신청서는 온라인으로 접수하며, 서류는 방문 제출도 가능하다.",romanization:"sincheongseoneun onnaineuro jeopsuhamyeo, seoryuneun bangmun jechuldo ganeunghada.",en:"Applications are received online, and documents may also be submitted in person.",note:"A comma after -(으)며 is normal when the two clauses have different subjects."},{ko:"그는 연구원이며 동시에 대학에서 강의도 맡고 있다.",romanization:"geuneun yeonguwonimyeo dongsie daehageseo gangeuido matgo itda.",en:"He is a researcher and at the same time also teaches at a university.",note:"이다 takes -며 directly: 연구원이며, 학생이며."},{ko:"회의 자료를 보며 다음 일정을 함께 검토했다.",romanization:"hoeui jaryoreul bomyeo daeum iljeongeul hamkke geomtohaetda.",en:"We reviewed the next schedule together while looking at the meeting materials.",note:"The simultaneity reading — here -(으)며 is doing the job of -(으)면서."}],keyPoint:{label:"Two different layers of written Korean",body:"The written connectors you already know — 따라서 and 그러나 — stand BETWEEN sentences and begin a new one. (즉 is the exception that proves the rule: it can start a sentence, but it just as often sits mid-sentence right after the term it defines.) -(으)며 and everything else in this chapter work INSIDE a sentence, joining clauses under one ending. Formal prose uses both at once: 값이 싸며 품질도 좋다. 따라서 수요가 늘고 있다."},pronunciation:"After a ㄹ-stem the ㄹ survives, because 며 begins with ㅁ: 알다 → 알며 [알며], 만들다 → 만들며 [만들며]. 좋으며 is [조으며], with the ㅎ silent between vowels.",drill:{instruction:"Join each pair into one written sentence with -(으)며.",model:"값이 싸다 / 품질도 좋다 → 값이 싸며 품질도 좋다.",items:["행사는 다음 달에 시작되다 / 사 주간 진행되다 → 행사는 다음 달에 _______ 사 주간 진행된다.","품질이 좋다 / 가격도 적당하다 → 품질이 _______ 가격도 적당하다.","그는 연구원이다 / 대학에서 강의도 하다 → 그는 _______ 대학에서 강의도 한다."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"회의를 마치며 자료를 배포했다.",right:"회의를 마친 후 자료를 배포했다.",explanation:'-(으)며 means the two things are true together or happen at the same time — never one after the other. English "and" covers both, so learners reach for -(으)며 to mean "and then." For sequence use -(으)ㄴ 후(에) or -고 나서, and keep -(으)며 for parallel or simultaneous facts.'}},{title:'V/A-(으)나 — the written "but" inside a sentence',func:"-(으)나 is the clause-internal contrast, the written equivalent of -지만. It sets the second clause against the first without breaking the sentence, which is why reports prefer it when the two halves belong to a single point rather than two separate beats.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-나",ex:"비싸다 → 값은 비싸나 (it is expensive, but…)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으나",ex:"좋다 → 품질은 좋으나 (the quality is good, but…)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었으나",ex:"늘다 → 예산은 늘었으나 (the budget grew, but…)"},{when:"이다 — contrast on a noun",add:"N(이)나",ex:"신입 → 그는 신입이나 (he is new, but…)"},{when:"The spoken form you are replacing",add:"-지만",ex:"예산은 늘었지만 → 예산은 늘었으나"}],examples:[{ko:"예산은 늘었으나 인력은 그대로이다.",romanization:"yesaneun neureosseuna illyeogeun geudaeroida.",en:"The budget has grown, but the staffing is unchanged.",note:"One sentence, one point: the growth and the shortfall belong together."},{ko:"신청은 누구나 가능하나 선정은 심사를 거친다.",romanization:"sincheongeun nuguna ganeunghana seonjeongeun simsareul geochinda.",en:"Anyone may apply, but selection goes through a review.",note:"A very common notice shape: an open door, then the condition on it."},{ko:"자료를 여러 번 검토했으나 오류를 찾지 못했다.",romanization:"jaryoreul yeoreo beon geomtohaesseuna oryureul chatji motaetda.",en:"We reviewed the material several times but could not find the error.",note:"Past tense sits inside the ending: 검토했으나."},{ko:"그는 신입이나 업무 이해도가 매우 높다.",romanization:"geuneun sinibina eommu ihaedoga maeu nopda.",en:"He is new, but his grasp of the work is very high.",note:"After a noun the form is N(이)나 — here 신입이나."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)나 vs 그러나 — one sentence or two",body:"그러나 starts a new sentence; -(으)나 keeps both halves inside one. 예산은 늘었다. 그러나 인력은 그대로이다 gives the contrast its own beat. 예산은 늘었으나 인력은 그대로이다 presents it as a single observation. Neither is more correct — choose by how much weight the contrast deserves."},pronunciation:"Because -(으)나 begins with ㄴ, a ㄹ-stem drops its ㄹ: 알다 → 아나, 살다 → 사나. That is the same drop you already know from 압니다 and 사는. 늘었으나 links as [느러쓰나].",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each -지만 sentence in the written register with -(으)나.",model:"예산은 늘었지만 인력은 그대로예요. → 예산은 늘었으나 인력은 그대로이다.",items:["신청은 누구나 가능하지만 선정은 심사를 거쳐요. → 신청은 누구나 _______ 선정은 심사를 거친다.","여러 번 검토했지만 오류를 못 찾았어요. → 여러 번 _______ 오류를 찾지 못했다.","그는 신입이지만 이해도가 높아요. → 그는 _______ 이해도가 높다."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"값은 비싸으나 품질이 좋다.",right:"값은 비싸나 품질이 좋다.",explanation:'비싸다 ends in a vowel, so it takes plain -나. Learners insert 으 everywhere because they memorise the ending as "-으나," but the 으 appears only after a consonant: 좋으나, 늘었으나. After a vowel it is 비싸나, 가나, 하나.'}},{title:'V/A-(으)므로 — the written "because," and why it is not -(으)ㅁ으로',func:"-(으)므로 states a reason in the written register, more logical and less personal than -아서/어서 or -(으)니까. It is the everyday reason ending of notices, regulations, and reports — and unlike -아서/어서 it can be followed by a command or a prohibition.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel or ㄹ",add:"-므로",ex:"하다 → 공사 중이므로 (since it is under construction)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으므로",ex:"좋다 → 상태가 좋으므로 (since the condition is good)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었으므로",ex:"지나다 → 기한이 지났으므로 (since the deadline has passed)"},{when:"A command may follow — unlike -아서/어서",add:"-(으)므로 + 금한다 / 하십시오",ex:"위험하다 → 위험하므로 출입을 금한다"},{when:"NOT the same word: 'by means of'",add:"-(으)ㅁ으로(써)",ex:"노력하다 → 노력함으로써 (by making an effort)"}],examples:[{ko:"관련 규정이 바뀌었으므로 신청 전에 반드시 확인해야 한다.",romanization:"gwallyeon gyujeongi bakkwieosseumeuro sincheong jeone bandeusi hwaginhaeya handa.",en:"The relevant rules have changed, so you must check before applying.",note:"The reason comes first, the obligation second — standard notice order."},{ko:"해당 구역은 공사 중이므로 출입을 금한다.",romanization:"haedang guyeogeun gongsa jungimeuro churibeul geumhanda.",en:"This area is under construction, so entry is prohibited.",note:"A prohibition may follow -(으)므로; it could not follow -아서/어서."},{ko:"제출 기한이 지났으므로 이번 신청은 접수되지 않는다.",romanization:"jechul gihani jinasseumeuro ibeon sincheongeun jeopsudoeji anneunda.",en:"The submission deadline has passed, so this application will not be accepted.",note:"Past inside the ending, present result — the usual shape for a rule."},{ko:"자료가 충분하지 않으므로 결론을 유보한다.",romanization:"jaryoga chungbunhaji aneumeuro gyeolloneul yubohanda.",en:"The material is not sufficient, so we are holding off on a conclusion.",note:"Negation goes inside: -지 않으므로."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)므로 vs -(으)ㅁ으로(써)",body:'One is a reason, the other is a method, and Korean writers confuse them constantly. 노력했으므로 성공했다 means "he succeeded BECAUSE he made an effort." 노력함으로써 성공했다 means "he succeeded BY making an effort." The tell is the ㅁ: -(으)므로 contains no noun, while -(으)ㅁ으로 is the nominal -(으)ㅁ plus the instrumental particle 으로.'},pronunciation:"않으므로 is [아느므로] and 좋으므로 is [조으므로] — the ㅎ goes silent before the 으. 바뀌었으므로 links as [바뀌어쓰므로].",drill:{instruction:"Give the written reason with -(으)므로.",model:"기한이 지났어요 / 접수되지 않아요 → 기한이 지났으므로 접수되지 않는다.",items:["규정이 바뀌었어요 / 확인해야 해요 → 규정이 _______ 반드시 확인해야 한다.","공사 중이에요 / 출입을 금해요 → 해당 구역은 공사 _______ 출입을 금한다.","자료가 충분하지 않아요 / 결론을 유보해요 → 자료가 충분하지 _______ 결론을 유보한다."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"규정이 바뀌었음으로 확인해야 한다.",right:"규정이 바뀌었으므로 확인해야 한다.",explanation:'The reason ending is -(으)므로, with no 음 in it. 바뀌었음으로 literally reads "by the fact of having changed," which is the nominal -(으)ㅁ plus 으로. Korean writers make this slip too, so learn the split rather than copying what you see online: reason = -(으)므로, method = -(으)ㅁ으로써.'}},{title:'V-고자 — "in order to," the written form of -(으)려고',func:"-고자 states a purpose in formal writing and official speech. It behaves exactly like -(으)려고: the person who intends and the person who acts must be the same, and it attaches only to action verbs. In email and announcements, -고자 followed by 연락드렸습니다 or 안내드립니다 is close to a fixed opening formula.",formTable:[{when:"Any action verb stem",add:"-고자",ex:"문의하다 → 문의하고자 (in order to enquire)"},{when:"Stating an intention",add:"V-고자 하다",ex:"도입하다 → 새 제도를 도입하고자 한다"},{when:"The opening line of a formal email",add:"V-고자 연락드렸습니다 / 메일을 드립니다",ex:"안내드리다 → 일정을 안내드리고자 메일을 드립니다"},{when:"The spoken form you are replacing",add:"-(으)려고",ex:"물어보려고 전화했어요 → 문의하고자 연락드렸습니다"}],examples:[{ko:"행사 일정을 안내드리고자 메일을 드립니다.",romanization:"haengsa iljeongeul annaedeurigoja meireul deurimnida.",en:"I am writing to let you know the event schedule.",note:"The standard opening of a Korean business email."},{ko:"본 조사는 이용자의 의견을 파악하고자 실시되었다.",romanization:"bon josaneun iyongjaui uigyeoneul paakagoja silsidoeeotda.",en:"This survey was conducted in order to understand users' opinions.",note:"Report register: the purpose of the study stated in one clause."},{ko:"자세한 내용을 확인하고자 담당 부서에 문의했다.",romanization:"jasehan naeyongeul hwaginhagoja damdang buseoe munuihaetda.",en:"I enquired with the department in charge in order to confirm the details.",note:"One subject throughout — the same person confirms and enquires."},{ko:"다음 학기부터 새로운 제도를 도입하고자 한다.",romanization:"daeum hakgibuteo saeroun jedoreul doipagoja handa.",en:"We intend to introduce a new system from next semester.",note:"-고자 하다 states the intention itself rather than a purpose clause."}],keyPoint:{label:"One subject, and a real intention",body:"-고자 carries the same two restrictions as -(으)려고. The intender and the doer have to be the same, and the verb has to be something you can decide to do — so an adjective or an involuntary event cannot take it. When the purpose belongs to somebody or something else, Korean switches to -도록."},pronunciation:"-고자 behaves like the plain connective -고. After a vowel-final stem nothing changes: 하고자 is [하고자] and 확인하고자 is [화긴하고자]. After an obstruent coda the ㄱ tenses, exactly as in 먹고 [먹꼬] — so 찾고자 is [찯꼬자].",drill:{instruction:"State the purpose in the written register with -고자.",model:"일정을 안내하려고 메일을 써요. → 일정을 안내드리고자 메일을 드립니다.",items:["의견을 파악하려고 조사했어요. → 이용자의 의견을 _______ 조사를 실시했다.","내용을 확인하려고 문의했어요. → 자세한 내용을 _______ 담당 부서에 문의했다.","새 제도를 도입하려고 해요. → 새로운 제도를 _______ 한다."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"회사가 발전하고자 직원들이 노력했다.",right:"회사가 발전하도록 직원들이 노력했다.",explanation:"-고자, like -(으)려고, needs the intender and the doer to be the same. Here the company is what develops but the employees are what act, so the clauses have different subjects. When the goal belongs to someone else, use -도록 instead."}},{title:"Nominal -(으)ㅁ — how notices end their sentences",func:"-(으)ㅁ turns a whole clause into a noun. Two places you meet it constantly: as the subject or object of verbs like 확인되다 and 밝혀지다, and as the closing of a terse notice line, where the sentence simply stops on the noun form instead of taking a polite ending.",formTable:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㅁ",ex:"하다 → 함, 바라다 → 바람"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-음",ex:"있다 → 있음, 없다 → 없음"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem",add:"-ㅁ (written on the ㄹ)",ex:"알다 → 앎, 만들다 → 만듦"},{when:"Closing a notice line",add:"-(으)ㅁ. / -기 바람.",ex:"제출하다 → 기한 내 제출하기 바람"},{when:"-(으)ㅁ vs -기",add:"-(으)ㅁ = a settled fact · -기 = an activity or a not-yet event",ex:"참석했음이 확인되었다 / 참석하기가 어렵다"}],examples:[{ko:"본 행사는 우천 시 취소될 수 있음.",romanization:"bon haengsaneun ucheon si chwisodoel su isseum.",en:"This event may be cancelled in the event of rain.",note:"A notice line closes on the noun form — no 습니다, no 요."},{ko:"신청자는 아래 서류를 기한 내에 제출하기 바람.",romanization:"sincheongjaneun arae seoryureul gihan naee jechulhagi baram.",en:"Applicants are asked to submit the documents below by the deadline.",note:"-기 바람 is the terse written form of -기 바랍니다."},{ko:"추가 안내는 추후 공지 예정임.",romanization:"chuga annaeneun chuhu gongji yejeongim.",en:"Further guidance is scheduled to be announced at a later date.",note:"이다 becomes 임 — one of the most common notice endings of all."},{ko:"응답자의 절반이 제도 변경을 모르고 있음이 확인되었다.",romanization:"eungdapjaui jeolbani jedo byeongyeongeul moreugo isseumi hwagindoeeotda.",en:"It was confirmed that half of the respondents were unaware of the change.",note:"확인되다 reports an established fact, so the clause takes -(으)ㅁ, not -기."}],keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㅁ vs -기 — settled fact or activity",body:"-(으)ㅁ turns a clause into something already established, confirmed, or observed: 사실임이 밝혀졌다, 참석했음을 확인했다. -기 turns it into an activity or something not yet done: 참석하기가 어렵다, 매일 운동하기. Verbs like 밝혀지다, 확인되다, and 드러나다 take -(으)ㅁ; 바라다, 시작하다, and 어렵다 take -기."},pronunciation:"있음 is [이씀] and 없음 is [업씀]. 만들다 → 만듦 keeps the ㄻ in writing but is pronounced [만듬], and 앎 is [암].",drill:{instruction:"Close each notice line with -(으)ㅁ or -기 바람.",model:"우천 시 취소될 수 있어요. → 우천 시 취소될 수 있음.",items:["추가 안내는 나중에 공지할 예정이에요. → 추가 안내는 추후 공지 _______.","서류를 기한 내에 제출해 주세요. → 서류를 기한 내에 _______.","3층은 당분간 이용할 수 없어요. → 3층은 당분간 이용할 수 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"참석하기가 확인되었습니다.",right:"참석했음이 확인되었습니다.",explanation:"확인되다, 밝혀지다, and 드러나다 all report an established fact, so the clause in front of them takes -(으)ㅁ. Learners default to -기 because it is the nominalizer they met first. Keep -기 for activities and things not yet done — 참석하기가 어렵다 — and use -(으)ㅁ for what has been confirmed."}}],N=[{title:"News passives — 밝혀지다, 알려지다, 전해지다",func:"Journalism reports what is known without naming who knows it. Alongside the -이/히/리/기- passives you have already met, news Korean leans on two other families: 되다 attached to a Sino-Korean noun (발표되다, 예상되다, 확인되다), and -아/어지다 attached to a verb of knowing or telling (밝혀지다, 알려지다, 전해지다). The source, when it appears at all, comes in as N에 따르면.",formTable:[{when:"Sino-Korean noun + 되다",add:"N되다",ex:"발표 → 새 정책이 발표됐다 (a new policy was announced)"},{when:"Verb of knowing or telling + -아/어지다",add:"V-아/어지다",ex:"밝히다 → 원인이 밝혀졌다 (the cause was uncovered)"},{when:"Hedged report — 'it is understood that'",add:"-(으)ㄴ/는 것으로 알려졌다",ex:"검토하다 → 검토 중인 것으로 알려졌다"},{when:"Forecast",add:"-(으)ㄹ 것으로 예상된다 / 전망된다",ex:"오르다 → 가격이 오를 것으로 전망된다"},{when:"Naming the source",add:"N에 따르면 … -다고 밝혔다",ex:"정부 → 정부에 따르면 지원이 확대된다고 밝혔다"}],examples:[{ko:"조사 결과 사고의 원인이 정비 불량으로 밝혀졌다.",romanization:"josa gyeolgwa sagoui wonini jeongbi bullyangeuro balkyeojyeotda.",en:"The investigation found that the cause of the accident was poor maintenance.",note:"밝혀지다 reports a finding; nobody is named as having found it."},{ko:"해당 기업은 내년부터 생산 시설을 확대할 것으로 알려졌다.",romanization:"haedang gieobeun naenyeonbuteo saengsan siseoreul hwakdaehal geoseuro allyeojyeotda.",en:"The company is understood to be expanding its production facilities from next year.",note:"-(으)ㄹ 것으로 알려졌다 is the standard hedge: reported, but not confirmed."},{ko:"정부에 따르면 지원 대상은 다음 달부터 확대된다.",romanization:"jeongbue ttareumyeon jiwon daesangeun daeum dalbuteo hwakdaedoenda.",en:"According to the government, the range of recipients will expand from next month.",note:"N에 따르면 is how a source enters a Korean news sentence."},{ko:"전문가들은 올해 물가가 더 오를 것으로 전망된다고 말했다.",romanization:"jeonmungadeureun olhae mulgaga deo oreul geoseuro jeonmangdoendago malhaetda.",en:"Experts said prices are forecast to rise further this year.",note:"전망되다 for forecasts, 예상되다 for expectations — nearly interchangeable in news."}],keyPoint:{label:"The passive is doing editorial work",body:"밝혀졌다 and 알려졌다 are not just grammar — they mark how solid the claim is. 확인됐다 means verified; 밝혀졌다 means uncovered by an investigation; 알려졌다 means reported but not confirmed, and is what a paper writes when it does not want to vouch for the claim. Reading the passive tells you how much the writer is willing to stand behind."},pronunciation:"밝혀지다 is [발켜지다]: the ㄺ of 밝 and the ㅎ of 혀 fuse into an aspirated [ㅋ], exactly as in 읽히다 [일키다]. 알려졌다 is [알려젇따] and 전해졌다 is [전해젇따].",drill:{instruction:"Rewrite each active news sentence as the passive a Korean paper would print.",model:"조사팀이 원인을 밝혔다. → 원인이 밝혀졌다.",items:["정부가 새 정책을 발표했다. → 새 정책이 _______.","기자들이 그 기업의 계획을 알았다. → 그 기업의 계획이 _______.","전문가들이 물가 상승을 예상한다. → 물가가 오를 것으로 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"정부에 의하면 지원이 확대한다.",right:"정부에 따르면 지원이 확대된다.",explanation:"Two slips in one line. 확대하다 is transitive — something expands something else — so a subject like 지원 needs the passive 확대되다. And while 에 의하면 exists, everyday news Korean uses N에 따르면 for 'according to'; 에 의해 is reserved for naming the agent of a passive."}},{title:"-적(的) — turning a noun into a modifier",func:"-적 attaches to a Sino-Korean noun and turns it into something that can describe: 경제 (economy) becomes 경제적 (economic). It has three shapes with different weights — N적 directly before a noun is the terse written form, N적인 is slightly softer, and N적으로 works as an adverb. It attaches only to Sino-Korean roots, never to native Korean words.",formTable:[{when:"Before a noun — terse, written",add:"N적 + N",ex:"경제 → 경제적 부담 (an economic burden)"},{when:"Before a noun — slightly softer",add:"N적인 + N",ex:"구체 → 구체적인 방안 (a concrete plan)"},{when:"As an adverb",add:"N적으로",ex:"사회 → 사회적으로 (socially, in social terms)"},{when:"As a predicate",add:"N적이다",ex:"적극 → 반응이 적극적이다 (the response is enthusiastic)"},{when:"Native Korean words cannot take it",add:"✗ N적",ex:"마음 → 마음적 ✗ / 정신적 ✓"}],examples:[{ko:"이번 조치는 일시적인 대책일 뿐이라는 비판이 나왔다.",romanization:"ibeon jochineun ilsijeogin daechaegil ppuniraneun bipani nawatda.",en:"Criticism emerged that this measure is nothing more than a temporary fix.",note:"일시적 (temporary) is one of the highest-frequency 적 words in news."},{ko:"정부는 보다 구체적 방안을 다음 주에 내놓기로 했다.",romanization:"jeongbuneun boda guchejeok bangeaneul daeum jue naenokiro haetda.",en:"The government decided to put forward a more concrete plan next week.",note:"구체적 방안 with no 인 — the compressed form that headlines and reports prefer."},{ko:"물가 상승은 사회적으로도 큰 부담이 되고 있다.",romanization:"mulga sangseungeun sahoejeogeurodo keun budami doego itda.",en:"Rising prices are becoming a heavy burden socially as well.",note:"-적으로 turns the modifier into an adverb: 'in social terms'."},{ko:"주민들의 반응은 대체로 긍정적이었다.",romanization:"jumindeurui baneungeun daechero geungjeongjeogieotda.",en:"Residents' reactions were on the whole positive.",note:"N적이다 as a full predicate, here in the past: 긍정적이었다."}],keyPoint:{label:"N적 vs N적인 — how much you compress",body:"Both sit in front of a noun and mean the same thing. Bare N적 is denser and belongs to headlines, reports, and academic prose: 경제적 부담, 구체적 방안. N적인 is a little more relaxed and is what you would say out loud: 경제적인 부담. If you are unsure in speech, 적인 is the safer choice; in a written report, drop the 인."},pronunciation:"적 is [적], and before a vowel it links: 경제적인 is [경제저긴], 사회적으로 is [사회저그로], 긍정적이었다 is [긍정저기얻따].",drill:{instruction:"Build the 적 form the sentence needs — modifier, adverb, or predicate.",model:"경제 / 부담이 크다 → 경제적 부담이 크다.",items:["일시 / 대책이다 → 이번 조치는 _______ 대책일 뿐이다.","사회 / 부담이 되다 → 물가 상승은 _______ 큰 부담이 된다.","긍정 / 반응이다 → 주민들의 반응은 대체로 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"마음적 부담이 크다.",right:"정신적 부담이 크다.",explanation:"-적 attaches only to Sino-Korean roots. 마음 is a native Korean word, so 마음적 does not exist; the Sino-Korean equivalent 정신 gives 정신적. When you want a 적 word, check that the base is a Sino-Korean noun — 경제, 사회, 정신, 구체 — not a native one like 마음, 몸, or 일."}},{title:"-화(化) — the '-ization' suffix",func:"-화 turns a noun into a process of becoming: 자동 (automatic) plus 화 gives 자동화 (automation). It usually appears as a verb with 되다 — 자동화되다, 'to become automated' — and it is how Korean names any large social or technical shift, which is why news writing is full of it.",formTable:[{when:"The process itself, as a noun",add:"N화",ex:"자동 → 자동화 (automation)"},{when:"Something undergoes it",add:"N화되다",ex:"고령 → 사회가 고령화되다 (society ages)"},{when:"Someone brings it about",add:"N화하다",ex:"다양 → 상품을 다양화하다 (to diversify the products)"},{when:"As a modifier",add:"N화된 + N",ex:"자동 → 자동화된 설비 (automated equipment)"},{when:"Frequent in the news",add:"고령화 · 국제화 · 다양화 · 본격화",ex:"본격 → 복구 작업이 본격화됐다"}],examples:[{ko:"생산 공정이 자동화되면서 인력 수요가 크게 줄었다.",romanization:"saengsan gongjeongi jadonghwadoemyeonseo illyeok suyoga keuge jureotda.",en:"As the production process became automated, demand for workers fell sharply.",note:"자동화되다 for the thing that changes; the people who automated it go unmentioned."},{ko:"인구 고령화가 빠르게 진행되고 있는 것으로 나타났다.",romanization:"ingu goryeonghwaga ppareuge jinhaengdoego inneun geoseuro natanatda.",en:"Population ageing is found to be progressing rapidly.",note:"-(으)ㄴ/는 것으로 나타나다 is another standard reporting frame."},{ko:"폭우 피해가 커지면서 복구 작업이 본격화됐다.",romanization:"pogu pihaega keojimyeonseo bokgu jageobi bongyeokwadwaetda.",en:"As the storm damage grew, recovery work got fully under way.",note:"본격화되다 — 'to move into full swing' — is a news fixture."},{ko:"회사는 판매 경로를 다양화하기로 결정했다.",romanization:"hoesaneun panmae gyeongnoreul dayanghwahagiro gyeoljeonghaetda.",en:"The company decided to diversify its sales channels.",note:"N화하다 when somebody does it on purpose, N화되다 when it simply happens."}],keyPoint:{label:"N화되다 vs N화하다",body:"The pair works like every other 되다/하다 split you know. N화되다 describes something undergoing the change with no agent named — 사회가 고령화되다, 공정이 자동화되다. N화하다 names somebody doing it deliberately — 상품을 다양화하다. News uses 되다 far more often, because the whole register is built on leaving the actor out."},pronunciation:"화 is [화], but after a ㄱ it aspirates: 본격화 is [본겨콰] and 자동화 stays [자동화]. 고령화 is [고령화], with no change.",drill:{instruction:"Build the 화 form: 되다 when it simply happens, 하다 when somebody does it.",model:"공정 / 자동 → 공정이 자동화됐다.",items:["사회 / 고령 → 사회가 빠르게 _______.","복구 작업 / 본격 → 복구 작업이 _______.","회사가 판매 경로 / 다양 → 회사가 판매 경로를 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"생산 공정이 자동화했다.",right:"생산 공정이 자동화됐다.",explanation:'English says "the process automated," using one verb for both sides. Korean splits them: the thing that undergoes the change takes N화되다, and only a deliberate actor takes N화하다. A production process does not automate itself on purpose, so it needs 자동화됐다.'}},{title:"-성(性) — the '-ness' noun, and what verbs it takes",func:"-성 turns a noun or a root into an abstract quality: 가능 plus 성 gives 가능성, 'possibility'. Unlike -적 it produces a full noun, so it takes particles and pairs with a small, fixed set of predicates — 있다, 없다, 높다, 크다, 제기되다. Getting that pairing right is most of the work.",formTable:[{when:"The quality, as a noun",add:"N성",ex:"가능 → 가능성 (possibility)"},{when:"It exists or does not",add:"N성이 있다 / 없다",ex:"위험 → 위험성이 있다"},{when:"Its degree",add:"N성이 높다 / 크다",ex:"가능 → 가능성이 높다 (it is likely)"},{when:"Someone raises it as an issue",add:"N성이 제기되다",ex:"필요 → 제도 개선의 필요성이 제기됐다"},{when:"Frequent in the news",add:"안정성 · 위험성 · 중요성 · 다양성",ex:"안정 → 시설의 안정성이 확인됐다"}],examples:[{ko:"올해 안에 제도가 바뀔 가능성이 높다고 보도됐다.",romanization:"olhae ane jedoga bakkwil ganeungseongi noptago bododwaetda.",en:"It was reported that the system is likely to change within the year.",note:"가능성이 높다, not 크다 — this pairing is fixed."},{ko:"조사 이후 제도 개선의 필요성이 다시 제기됐다.",romanization:"josa ihu jedo gaeseonui piryoseongi dasi jegidwaetda.",en:"After the investigation, the need for reform was raised again.",note:"필요성이 제기되다 is how news says somebody made the case for something."},{ko:"점검 결과 시설의 안정성에는 문제가 없는 것으로 확인됐다.",romanization:"jeomgeom gyeolgwa siseorui anjeongseongeneun munjega eomneun geoseuro hwagindwaetda.",en:"The inspection confirmed there is no problem with the facility's stability.",note:"Three chapter patterns in one line: 성, a 되다 passive, and 것으로 확인되다."},{ko:"전문가들은 이 방식의 위험성을 여러 차례 경고해 왔다.",romanization:"jeonmungadeureun i bangsigui wiheomseongeul yeoreo charye gyeonggohae watda.",en:"Experts have repeatedly warned of the risks of this approach.",note:"As a full noun, 위험성 takes 을/를 when it is the object."}],keyPoint:{label:"-성 is a noun, -적 is a modifier",body:"The two suffixes attach to the same roots but do opposite jobs. 가능성 is a thing you can have, count, and mark with a particle: 가능성이 높다. 가능적 does not exist, but where a 적 form does — 경제적, 사회적 — it can only describe, never stand alone as a subject. If the slot needs a noun, reach for 성; if it needs a modifier, reach for 적."},pronunciation:"성 links straight into a following particle: 가능성이 is [가능성이], 필요성이 is [피료성이], 안정성에는 is [안정성에는]. 위험성을 is [위험성을].",drill:{instruction:"Pick the predicate that goes with each -성 noun.",model:"가능성 / likely → 가능성이 높다.",items:["필요성 / somebody raised it → 제도 개선의 _______ 제기됐다.","안정성 / no problem with it → 시설의 _______ 문제가 없다.","위험성 / experts warned of it → 전문가들이 이 방식의 _______ 경고했다."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"제도가 바뀔 가능성이 크다고 보도됐다.",right:"제도가 바뀔 가능성이 높다고 보도됐다.",explanation:'가능성 pairs with 높다, not 크다 — Korean measures a probability by height, not size. Learners reach for 크다 because English says "a big possibility." Compare 필요성이 크다, which does take 크다. These pairings are fixed and worth memorising as chunks.'}},{title:"Headline grammar — dropped particles and noun-final sentences",func:"A Korean headline is a sentence with its joints removed. Subject and object particles disappear, the verb is replaced by a bare noun or a nominal form, and a comma after the opening noun marks it as the topic. Once you know what has been deleted, you can put it back and read the headline as an ordinary sentence.",formTable:[{when:"Topic marked by a comma instead of 은/는",add:"N, …",ex:"정부, 지원 대상 확대 → 정부는 지원 대상을 확대한다"},{when:"Object particle dropped",add:"(을/를 deleted)",ex:"요금 인상 검토 → 요금 인상을 검토한다"},{when:"Verb replaced by a bare noun",add:"… 확대 / 검토 / 예상",ex:"반발 예상 → 반발이 예상된다"},{when:"Ellipsis for a second, linked clause",add:"… (separates two clauses)",ex:"요금 인상 검토… 이용객 반발 예상"},{when:"Quoting without 고",add:'N, "…"라며',ex:'정부, "검토 중"이라며 확답 피해'}],examples:[{ko:"정부, 내년부터 지원 대상 확대",romanization:"jeongbu, naenyeonbuteo jiwon daesang hwakdae",en:"Government to expand eligibility from next year",note:"Unpacked: 정부는 내년부터 지원 대상을 확대한다."},{ko:"지하철 요금 인상 검토… 이용객 반발 예상",romanization:"jihacheol yogeum insang geomto… iyonggaek banbal yesang",en:"Subway fare rise under review… backlash from passengers expected",note:"Unpacked: 지하철 요금 인상을 검토한다. 이용객의 반발이 예상된다."},{ko:"폭우 피해 확산, 복구 작업 본격화",romanization:"pogu pihae hwaksan, bokgu jageop bongyeokwa",en:"Storm damage spreads, recovery work moves into full swing",note:"Two noun-final clauses; the comma here joins them rather than marking a topic."},{ko:'전문가들 "물가 더 오를 것"이라며 대책 촉구',romanization:'jeonmungadeul "mulga deo oreul geot"iramyeo daechaek chokgu',en:"Experts urge action, saying prices will rise further",note:"라며 compresses -라고 하며; the quote keeps its own grammar intact."}],keyPoint:{label:"Read a headline by putting the particles back",body:"Work from the end. The final noun is the verb: 확대 means 확대한다 or 확대된다, 예상 means 예상된다, 검토 means 검토한다. Then the noun before it is usually the object, so restore 을/를. Then whatever sits before the comma is the topic, so restore 은/는. 정부, 지원 대상 확대 unpacks to 정부는 지원 대상을 확대한다."},pronunciation:"Headlines are read aloud with a pause where each particle was deleted, not run together: 정부 / 지원 대상 / 확대. The ellipsis … is read as a longer pause, and it usually separates two whole clauses.",drill:{instruction:"Unpack each headline into a full sentence, restoring the particles and the verb.",model:"정부, 지원 대상 확대 → 정부는 지원 대상을 확대한다.",items:["지하철 요금 인상 검토 → 지하철 요금 인상_ _______.","이용객 반발 예상 → 이용객의 반발_ _______.","복구 작업 본격화 → 복구 작업_ _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"정부는 지원 대상 확대한다.",right:"정부는 지원 대상을 확대한다.",explanation:"Headline Korean drops particles, but ordinary Korean does not. Learners who read a lot of headlines start deleting 을/를 in their own writing, which reads as clipped or careless. Treat the dropped particles as a headline convention only, and restore them the moment you write a full sentence."}}],V=[{title:"손이 크다 — the generous hand, and the rest of the 손 idioms",func:"손이 크다 says somebody does things on a generous scale — cooks too much, gives too much, spends freely. It has nothing to do with hand size. Like every idiom in this chapter it is a complete clause, [body part]이/가 [adjective], and you conjugate the adjective at the end.",formTable:[{when:"Word for word",add:"손 (hand) + 이 + 크다 (big)",ex:"literally 'the hand is big'"},{when:"What it actually means",add:"generous, does things on a large scale",ex:"어머니는 손이 크시다 (Mum always makes far too much)"},{when:"How it inflects",add:"손이 커요 / 손이 커서 / 손이 크신",ex:"손이 커서 늘 음식이 남아요"},{when:"The opposite",add:"손이 작다",ex:"저는 손이 작아서 딱 먹을 만큼만 해요"},{when:"Other 손 idioms",add:"손을 떼다 · 손이 빠르다 · 손이 많이 가다",ex:"그 일에서는 손을 뗐어요 (I've stepped away from it)"}],examples:[{ko:"어머니는 손이 크셔서 명절만 되면 음식이 늘 남아요.",romanization:"eomeonineun soni keusyeoseo myeongjeolman doemyeon eumsigi neul namayo.",en:"My mother cooks on such a scale that there are always leftovers at the holidays.",note:"The honorific goes on the adjective: 손이 크시다 → 손이 크셔서."},{ko:"손이 크신 건 좋은데 이번엔 좀 줄이는 게 어때요?",romanization:"soni keusin geon joeunde ibeonen jom jurineun ge eottaeyo?",en:"It's lovely that you do things so generously, but how about scaling back this time?",note:"손이 크신 as a modifier before 건 — the clause keeps its shape inside."},{ko:"그 일에서는 작년에 이미 손을 뗐어요.",romanization:"geu ireseoneun jangnyeone imi soneul tteosseoyo.",en:"I stepped away from that project last year.",note:"손을 떼다 takes 을/를 because 떼다 is a transitive verb, unlike 손이 크다."},{ko:"김치는 손이 많이 가는 음식이라 하루 만에는 못 만들어요.",romanization:"gimchineun soni mani ganeun eumsigira haru maneneun mot mandeureoyo.",en:"Kimchi takes so much work that you can't make it in a day.",note:"손이 많이 가다 = 'requires a lot of hands-on effort', said of tasks and dishes."}],keyPoint:{label:"The shape is a clause, not a phrase",body:"Every idiom of this family is [body part]이/가 [adjective] — a whole clause. English would build a noun phrase ('a generous hand'), which is why learners produce 큰 손이에요. Korean does not work that way here: keep 손이 크다, 발이 넓다, 눈이 높다 intact and conjugate the adjective on the end."},pronunciation:"손을 뗐어요 links as [소늘 떠써요]. 손이 많이 가는 is [소니 마니 가는], with the ㅎ of 많 silent before the 이.",drill:{instruction:"Complete each 손 idiom with the right form.",model:"어머니 / 음식이 남다 → 어머니는 손이 크셔서 음식이 늘 남아요.",items:["그 일 / 작년에 그만두다 → 그 일에서는 작년에 이미 손을 _______.","김치 / 만들기 어렵다 → 김치는 손이 많이 _______ 음식이에요.","저 / 딱 먹을 만큼만 하다 → 저는 손이 _______ 딱 먹을 만큼만 해요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"그분은 큰 손이에요.",right:"그분은 손이 크세요.",explanation:"English would say 'a big hand', a noun phrase, so learners build 큰 손이에요. Korean body-part idioms are always a clause: the body part takes 이/가 and the adjective carries the ending. 손이 크세요, 발이 넓으세요, 눈이 높으세요."}},{title:"발이 넓다 — a wide circle, and where else 발 turns up",func:"발이 넓다 means somebody knows everybody — a wide social circle, not wide feet. 발 also supplies three more idioms you will hear constantly: 발이 묶이다 for being stranded, 발등에 불이 떨어지다 for a deadline finally biting, and 발 벗고 나서다 for pitching in wholeheartedly.",formTable:[{when:"Word for word",add:"발 (foot) + 이 + 넓다 (wide)",ex:"literally 'the foot is wide'"},{when:"What it actually means",add:"well connected, knows a lot of people",ex:"지훈 씨는 발이 넓다 (Jihoon knows everyone)"},{when:"The noun form",add:"마당발",ex:"그 사람은 우리 동네 마당발이에요"},{when:"Stranded — passive",add:"발이 묶이다",ex:"눈 때문에 공항에서 발이 묶였어요"},{when:"Other 발 idioms",add:"발등에 불이 떨어지다 · 발 벗고 나서다",ex:"발등에 불이 떨어져야 시작해요"}],examples:[{ko:"지훈 씨는 발이 넓어서 이 동네에 모르는 사람이 없어요.",romanization:"jihun ssineun bari neolbeoseo i dongnee moreuneun sarami eopseoyo.",en:"Jihoon is so well connected that there's nobody in this neighbourhood he doesn't know.",note:"넓다 is [널따], so 넓어서 links as [널버서]."},{ko:"눈이 많이 와서 어제는 공항에서 발이 묶였어요.",romanization:"nuni mani waseo eojeneun gonghangeseo bari mukkyeosseoyo.",en:"It snowed heavily, so I was stranded at the airport yesterday.",note:"묶이다 is the passive of 묶다 — the snow does the tying, so 발이 takes 이."},{ko:"저는 발등에 불이 떨어져야 시작하는 성격이에요.",romanization:"jeoneun baldeunge buri tteoreojyeoya sijakaneun seonggyeogieyo.",en:"I'm the kind of person who only starts once the deadline is on top of me.",note:"Literally 'fire falls on the top of the foot' — the moment something becomes urgent."},{ko:"동네 사람들이 발 벗고 나서서 도와줬어요.",romanization:"dongne saramdeuri bal beotgo naseoseo dowajwosseoyo.",en:"The neighbours pitched in wholeheartedly and helped out.",note:"발 벗고 나서다 carries warmth: not just helping, but throwing yourself in."}],keyPoint:{label:"발이 넓다 is a compliment; 마당발 is the person",body:"발이 넓다 describes the quality and is safely complimentary — 발이 넓으시네요 is a normal thing to say to someone who just introduced you to three people. 마당발 is the noun for the person themselves: 그 사람은 마당발이에요. Neither implies anything about being pushy; Korean treats a wide network as straightforwardly good."},pronunciation:"넓다 is [널따] and 넓어서 is [널버서] — the ㅂ of ㄼ only surfaces before a vowel. 발 벗고 is [발 벋꼬], and 묶였어요 is [무껴써요].",drill:{instruction:"Choose the 발 idiom each situation calls for.",model:"아는 사람이 아주 많다 → 발이 넓어요.",items:["눈 때문에 공항에서 못 움직였다 → 공항에서 발이 _______.","마감이 코앞에 와야 겨우 시작한다 → 발등에 불이 _______ 시작해요.","동네 사람들이 적극적으로 도왔다 → 동네 사람들이 발 벗고 _______ 도와줬어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"어제 공항에서 발을 묶었어요.",right:"어제 공항에서 발이 묶였어요.",explanation:"발이 묶이다 is passive — the snow, the strike, or the weather strands you. Learners make it active because English says 'I got stuck', which looks like something you did. The thing that traps you is the cause; your foot takes 이/가 and the verb stays 묶이다."}},{title:"눈이 높다 — high standards, and what the eyes do",func:"눈이 높다 means somebody is hard to please — fussy about partners, flats, or clothes. The eyes also give you 눈에 띄다 for standing out, and 눈코 뜰 새 없다 for being flat out. None of them is about eyesight.",formTable:[{when:"Word for word",add:"눈 (eye) + 이 + 높다 (high)",ex:"literally 'the eye is high'"},{when:"What it actually means",add:"has high standards, is hard to please",ex:"눈이 높아서 아직 못 골랐어요"},{when:"Standing out — passive",add:"눈에 띄다",ex:"빨간 표지가 눈에 띄었어요"},{when:"Flat out busy",add:"눈코 뜰 새 없다",ex:"요즘 눈코 뜰 새가 없어요"},{when:"Careful — a different word",add:"눈치가 빠르다 (quick to read a room)",ex:"눈치가 빨라서 분위기를 금방 알아채요"}],examples:[{ko:"눈이 높아서 아직 마음에 드는 집을 못 찾았대요.",romanization:"nuni nopaseo ajik maeume deuneun jibeul mot chajatdaeyo.",en:"They say she's so particular that she still hasn't found a flat she likes.",note:"-대요 from the reported-speech chapter: this is somebody else's assessment."},{ko:"표지가 빨개서 책장에서 바로 눈에 띄었어요.",romanization:"pyojiga ppalgaeseo chaekjangeseo baro nune ttuieosseoyo.",en:"The cover was red, so it stood out on the shelf immediately.",note:"눈에 띄다 takes 에, not 을 — the thing catches the eye by itself."},{ko:"요즘 일이 몰려서 눈코 뜰 새가 없어요.",romanization:"yojeum iri mollyeoseo nunko tteul saega eopseoyo.",en:"Work has piled up lately, so I'm absolutely flat out.",note:"Literally 'no gap to open eyes or nose' — the strongest everyday 'too busy'."},{ko:"눈이 높은 게 아니라 기준이 분명한 거예요.",romanization:"nuni nopeun ge anira gijuni bunmyeonghan geoyeyo.",en:"It's not that she's fussy — she just knows what she wants.",note:"A useful defence, since 눈이 높다 can sound like a mild dig."}],keyPoint:{label:"눈이 높다 is gentler about yourself than about others",body:"Said of yourself, 눈이 높다 is a wry admission: 제가 눈이 좀 높아요. Said of somebody else — especially about dating — it can land as a criticism, close to 'too picky for their own good'. If you mean it warmly, 기준이 분명하다 is the safe alternative."},pronunciation:"높다 is [놉따] and 높아서 is [노파서]. 눈코 뜰 새 is [눈코 뜰 쌔], with the ㅅ tensing after ㄹ.",drill:{instruction:"Fill in the eye idiom that fits.",model:"고르는 기준이 까다롭다 → 눈이 높아요.",items:["빨간 표지가 금방 보였다 → 표지가 빨개서 바로 눈에 _______.","너무 바빠서 쉴 틈이 없다 → 요즘 눈코 뜰 새가 _______.","아직 마음에 드는 집을 못 찾았다 → _______ 아직 못 찾았어요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"빨간 표지가 눈을 띄었어요.",right:"빨간 표지가 눈에 띄었어요.",explanation:"띄다 here is a contraction of 뜨이다, a passive: the thing gets itself noticed. So the eye is where it lands and takes 에. Learners write 눈을 띄다 by analogy with 눈을 뜨다 ('to open one's eyes'), which is a different verb entirely."}},{title:"입이 무겁다 · 귀가 얇다 — the mouth that keeps a secret and the ear that doesn't",func:"입이 무겁다 means somebody can be trusted with a secret; its opposite 입이 가볍다 means they cannot. 귀가 얇다 means they are easily talked into things. The weight-and-thickness metaphor is consistent: a heavy mouth stays shut, a thin ear lets everything through.",formTable:[{when:"Keeps a secret",add:"입이 무겁다",ex:"그 사람은 입이 무거워요 (he's discreet)"},{when:"Cannot keep a secret",add:"입이 가볍다",ex:"입이 가벼운 사람한테는 말하지 마세요"},{when:"Easily persuaded",add:"귀가 얇다",ex:"귀가 얇아서 광고만 보면 사고 싶어져요"},{when:"A picky eater",add:"입이 짧다",ex:"동생은 입이 짧아요"},{when:"Careful — hearing is different",add:"귀가 밝다 / 귀가 어둡다",ex:"할머니가 귀가 어두우세요 (grandma is hard of hearing)"}],examples:[{ko:"그 사람은 입이 무거우니까 말해도 괜찮아요.",romanization:"geu sarameun ibi mugeounikka malhaedo gwaenchanayo.",en:"He's discreet, so it's fine to tell him.",note:"무겁다 is a ㅂ-irregular: 무거우니까, 무거워요."},{ko:"입이 가벼운 사람한테는 비밀을 말하지 마세요.",romanization:"ibi gabyeoun saramhanteneun bimireul malhaji maseyo.",en:"Don't tell your secrets to someone who can't keep them.",note:"입이 가벼운 as a modifier before 사람 — the clause survives intact."},{ko:"저는 귀가 얇아서 광고만 보면 다 사고 싶어져요.",romanization:"jeoneun gwiga yalbaseo gwanggoman bomyeon da sago sipeojyeoyo.",en:"I'm so easily swayed that I want to buy everything I see advertised.",note:"얇다 is [얄따] and 얇아서 is [얄바서]."},{ko:"동생은 입이 짧아서 먹는 게 늘 정해져 있어요.",romanization:"dongsaengeun ibi jjalbaseo meokneun ge neul jeonghaejyeo isseoyo.",en:"My younger sibling is such a picky eater that they always eat the same things.",note:"입이 짧다 is only about food, never about talking briefly."}],keyPoint:{label:"귀가 얇다 is not about hearing",body:"The 얇다/두껍다 pair on 귀 measures how easily influence gets through, not sound. 귀가 얇다 = easily persuaded. For actual hearing Korean uses a completely different pair: 귀가 밝다 for sharp hearing and 귀가 어둡다 for poor hearing. Mixing them up produces a sentence that sounds like nonsense to a Korean ear."},pronunciation:"무겁다 is a ㅂ-irregular, so 무거워요 and 무거우니까 lose the ㅂ. 얇다 is [얄따] and 짧다 is [짤따]; both restore the ㅂ before a vowel — [얄바서], [짤바서].",drill:{instruction:"Pick the mouth or ear idiom the description calls for.",model:"비밀을 잘 지킨다 → 입이 무거워요.",items:["비밀을 못 지킨다 → 그 사람은 입이 _______.","광고를 보면 바로 사고 싶어진다 → 저는 귀가 _______.","먹는 음식이 정해져 있다 → 동생은 입이 _______."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"할머니는 귀가 얇아서 잘 못 들으세요.",right:"할머니는 귀가 어두우셔서 잘 못 들으세요.",explanation:"귀가 얇다 means easily persuaded and says nothing about hearing. Poor hearing is 귀가 어둡다 and sharp hearing is 귀가 밝다. The thin-ear image is about influence getting through, not sound."}},{title:"속담 다섯 — the proverbs you will actually hear",func:"Korean proverbs are fixed strings. You quote them whole and attach the grammar to the outside — 이다, -이라고, or -(이)라더니 — rather than rebuilding them into a sentence. These five turn up in ordinary conversation, not just in writing.",formTable:[{when:"Small amounts add up",add:"티끌 모아 태산",ex:"티끌 모아 태산이라고 조금씩 모았어요"},{when:"Word travels fast",add:"발 없는 말이 천 리 간다",ex:"발 없는 말이 천 리 간다더니 소문이 다 퍼졌어요"},{when:"Someone with a narrow view",add:"우물 안 개구리",ex:"제가 우물 안 개구리였어요"},{when:"Nearly impossible",add:"하늘의 별 따기",ex:"그 표를 구하는 건 하늘의 별 따기예요"},{when:"Extremely easy",add:"식은 죽 먹기",ex:"이 정도는 식은 죽 먹기죠"}],examples:[{ko:"티끌 모아 태산이라고, 매달 조금씩 모았더니 꽤 됐어요.",romanization:"tikkeul moa taesanirago, maedal jogeumssik moatdeoni kkwae dwaesseoyo.",en:"Little by little makes a lot — I put a bit aside every month and it's added up.",note:"-이라고 quotes the proverb, and -았더니 reports what you found by doing it."},{ko:"발 없는 말이 천 리 간다더니 소문이 벌써 다 퍼졌네요.",romanization:"bal eomneun mari cheolli gandadeoni somuni beolsseo da peojyeonneyo.",en:"Word really does travel — the rumour has already gone everywhere.",note:"-다더니 is the standard way to say 'as the saying goes, and sure enough'."},{ko:"그 공연 표를 구하는 건 하늘의 별 따기예요.",romanization:"geu gongyeon pyoreul guhaneun geon haneurui byeol ttagiyeyo.",en:"Getting a ticket to that show is next to impossible.",note:"The proverb behaves as a noun here, so it simply takes 예요."},{ko:"이 정도 문제는 식은 죽 먹기죠.",romanization:"i jeongdo munjeneun sigeun juk meokgijyo.",en:"A problem at this level is a piece of cake.",note:"식은 죽 먹기 also acts as a noun: 먹기 is already a nominal form."}],keyPoint:{label:"Three ways to hang a proverb on your sentence",body:"Attach 이다 when the proverb IS your point: 하늘의 별 따기예요. Attach -(이)라고 when you are invoking it as received wisdom before your own clause: 티끌 모아 태산이라고, …. Attach -다더니 when reality has just confirmed it: 발 없는 말이 천 리 간다더니, …. What you never do is take the proverb apart."},pronunciation:"천 리 is [철리], with the ㄴ assimilating to the ㄹ. 식은 죽 먹기 is [시근 죽 먹끼], and 없는 in 발 없는 말이 is [엄는].",drill:{instruction:"Choose the proverb each situation calls for.",model:"아주 쉬운 일이다 → 식은 죽 먹기예요.",items:["조금씩 모은 돈이 꽤 커졌다 → _______이라고, 조금씩 모았더니 꽤 됐어요.","소문이 순식간에 퍼졌다 → _______더니 소문이 벌써 다 퍼졌네요.","표를 구하기가 거의 불가능하다 → 그 표를 구하는 건 _______예요."]},englishSpeakerPitfall:{wrong:"티끌을 모아서 태산을 만들어요.",right:"티끌 모아 태산이에요.",explanation:"Proverbs are fixed strings, so you quote them rather than rebuild them. 티끌 모아 태산 keeps its bare nouns with no particles and its 모아 with nothing attached. Say the whole thing as one unit and hang 이에요, -이라고, or -다더니 on the outside."}}],E=Object.assign({"../../data/chapters/chapter-40.json":e,"../../data/chapters/chapter-41.json":o,"../../data/chapters/chapter-42.json":n,"../../data/chapters/chapter-43.json":t,"../../data/chapters/chapter-44.json":a,"../../data/chapters/chapter-45.json":i,"../../data/chapters/chapter-46.json":s,"../../data/chapters/chapter-47.json":r,"../../data/chapters/chapter-48.json":h,"../../data/chapters/chapter-49.json":l,"../../data/chapters/chapter-50.json":d,"../../data/chapters/chapter-51.json":u,"../../data/chapters/chapter-52.json":c,"../../data/chapters/chapter-53.json":m,"../../data/chapters/chapter-54.json":g,"../../data/chapters/chapter-55.json":y,"../../data/chapters/chapter-56.json":p,"../../data/chapters/chapter-57.json":w,"../../data/chapters/chapter-58.json":f,"../../data/chapters/chapter-59.json":b,"../../data/chapters/chapter-60.json":k,"../../data/chapters/chapter-61.json":v,"../../data/chapters/chapter-62.json":_,"../../data/chapters/chapter-63.json":x,"../../data/chapters/chapter-64.json":j,"../../data/chapters/chapter-65.json":I,"../../data/chapters/chapter-66.json":T,"../../data/chapters/chapter-67.json":z,"../../data/chapters/chapter-68.json":P,"../../data/chapters/chapter-69.json":A,"../../data/chapters/chapter-70.json":S,"../../data/chapters/chapter-71.json":N,"../../data/chapters/chapter-72.json":V});export{E as notes};
